        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:001",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:001",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7524-2157"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "This dataset provides one of the longest running set of global observations of atmospheric CO2 that is still in operation. The quality of the data retrieval is arguably one of the best in the world. The extent and density of global observational coverage is the best for CO2 of any comparable satellite currently in orbit and with a 16 year data record it can finally be used to reach robust conclusions about the changes in atmospheric CO2 concentrations beyond natural climate oscillations. The ability to conduct long-term monitoring of atmospheric CO2 in regions that are unreachable by humans or in areas where political factors limit access is indispensable for the progress of climate science. These data have also fostered a great deal of international scientific collaboration. It was the impetus for the development of the Total Carbon Column Observing Network (TCCON), which has become the global standard for total column measurements of trace gases (not only CO2). TCCON is used to validate the leading climate models and numerous other satellite-based measurements. As a result, it could also be argued that the OCO mission played a role in the development of the EM27/SUN FTS observation network, the Collaborative Column Carbon Observing Network (COCCON). Furthermore, the possibility to also use the observations from OCO-2 for SIF adds a vast diversity of other possibilities for things that can be learned from this dataset."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator worked on validating these data over high northern latitude regions using ground-based measurement during their PhD research in Fairbanks, Alaska. They continued to work closely with the OCO-2/3 Science Team during their first postdoctoral fellowship at Colorado State University. They worked on vital changes to the retrieval, which improved the quality of and increased the temporal and spatial coverage of retrievals over high northern latitudes, as well as other regions. They are now working at the University of Toronto and are not working as closely with the OCO-2/3 Science Team as before, but they do quite a lot of comparisons between their team’s ground-based urban measurements in Toronto and the OCO-2 and OCO-3 measurements of the city. These data are essential for putting their team’s urban measurements in a global context that can be compared to other urban areas around the world."
        }
      ]
    },
