    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:002",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:002",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:002",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2509-4325"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "NEX-GDDP-CMIP6 (and its CMIP5 version) is one of the most widely used and impactful downscaled climate projections available, partly because the data, methods, and code are publicly available. The downscaled projections have been accessed on the host NASA and AWS servers millions of times, and NEX-GDDP was among the most-requested datasets by Google Earth Engine users before it was added to their public data holdings in 2023. \n\nNEX-GDDP has had wide-reaching impact in academia, government, and the private sector. The projections have been used in more than 700 peer-reviewed journal articles examining climate change impacts, temperature and heat events, precipitation, hydrology, agriculture and food security, extreme events and disasters, regional climate studies, environmental and ecological impacts, and human health and socio-economic impacts. Forty of these papers can be found in the highest-impact families of journals, a number comparable to the number of papers in these journals published with data from NASA’s MODIS satellites over the same period.\n\nNEX-GDDP is used in the commercial sector by Fortune 500 companies and has been a key enabler of at least four climate-oriented start-ups in the United States, contributing to a robust and dynamic U.S. economy. NEX-GDDP also underpins climate risk and decision support tools built and used by a wide variety of government and non-governmental organizations. These include, among others, the U.S. Department of Defense, the Federal Emergency Management Agency, the World Bank, and the United Nations Environment Program. NASA uses NEX-GDDP for its own Applied Remote Sensing Training program (ARSET), which reaches many thousands of students."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "NEX-GDDP is a product of the NASA Earth eXchange, a research and technology team at NASA's Ames Research Center. When the nominator accepted the position of NEX Project Scientists in late-2022, they were aware of NEX-GDDP, but they had no idea how widely-used and impactful it had become. It was extraordinary to learn how many users of NEX-GDDP there are, and that they represent a huge cross-section of commercial, government, and academic users. They also really enjoyed seeing it become a part of the NASA public information content in the Smithsonian Museum of Natural History. Finally, it has been wonderful to present NEX-GDDP at start-up accelerators and other events and see the amazement on people's faces as they begin to comprehend that the data and code are open and accessible to anyone to build from."
        }
      ]
    },
