    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:003",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:003",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:003",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1295-6555"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "OCO-2 provides high-precision measurements of atmospheric CO₂ concentrations over regions where no ground-based observations exist. Since its launch in 2014, it has become one of the longest-running satellite records of global CO₂, offering a decade of continuous, high-resolution coverage. These data are critical for constraining global CO₂ emissions, validating national and regional emission inventories, and holding countries and sectors accountable for their greenhouse gas outputs. Without OCO-2, our understanding of spatial and temporal patterns in CO₂ emissions would be far less accurate, limiting the ability to develop effective climate mitigation strategies.\n\nBeyond CO₂, OCO-2’s Solar-Induced Fluorescence (SIF) product offers unprecedented insight into plant photosynthetic activity at a global scale. This near-real-time, worldwide coverage is nearly impossible to achieve with ground-based measurements alone, providing essential data for monitoring ecosystem health, informing conservation efforts, and improving climate models that link the carbon cycle and vegetation dynamics.\n\nBy enabling these scientific advances, OCO-2 directly contributes to understanding the planet’s climate system, supporting environmental policy, and enhancing global efforts toward sustainability and climate resilience."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator uses the OCO-2 dataset to investigate long-term trends in atmospheric CO₂ across different regions of the world. A key part of their work involves comparing OCO-2 trends with those measured by the ground-based Total Carbon Column Observing Network (TCCON) to assess the accuracy and consistency of satellite-derived CO₂ observations. They also analyze OCO-2 data over urban areas and large point sources to estimate local enhancements in CO₂ mole fractions. The combination of global coverage and high spatial resolution allows them to study both broad-scale carbon cycle patterns and finer-scale anthropogenic emissions, providing insights that would be difficult or impossible to achieve with ground-based measurements alone."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:003",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 4,
      "sourceValue": [
