    {
      "@id": "id:organization:university-of-toronto-60-st-george-street-toronto-ontario-m5s-1a7-canada",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "University of Toronto, 60 St. George Street, Toronto, Ontario, M5S 1A7, Canada"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:004",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:004",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:004",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4924-0377"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The OCO-2 and OCO-3 datasets have provided an unprecedented global picture of sources and sinks of carbon dioxide (CO2). There are two main products from these satellite missions: CO2 abundances in the atmosphere, and solar-induced chlorophyll fluorescence (SIF) signals. The CO2 measurements from OCO-2 and OCO-3 are the best available such measurements from space, and provide near global coverage with a footprint size on the order of 1 km^2, providing a fine spatial resolution that have allowed the research community to assess emissions from facilities down to single stacks (e.g., Nassar et al. 2017; Moeini et al., 2025), on urban scales (e.g., McDonald et al., 2022; Mastrogiacomo et al., 2025), at the continental scale (e.g., Liu et al., 2017), and at the global scale (e.g., Byrne et al., 2023). The SIF product has been shown to be a proxy for gross primary production on various spatiotemporal scales and has provided one of the first clear global pictures of the vegetative sinks of CO2 (e.g., Sun et al., 2017).\n\nThe OCO mission data provides value because of the quality of the measurements, but the longevity of the missions is equally important. OCO-2 was launched in 2014 and has completed its 11th year in orbit; OCO-3 was launched in 2019 and has collected almost 6 years of data. For research on the carbon cycle, this kind of long-term dataset is crucial to pull important signals from the internal variability of the carbon cycle system. To monitor emissions and assess policy, we need long term, uninterrupted data records of high quality (e.g., Byrne et al. 2023), making OCO-2 and OCO-3 critical and highly impactful datasets.\n\nReferences:\nNassar, R., Hill, T. G., McLinden, C. A., Wunch, D., Jones, D. B. A., & Crisp, D. (2017). Quantifying co2 emissions from individual power plants from space. Geophysical Research Letters, 44(19). https://doi.org/10.1002/2017GL074702 \n\nMoeini, O., et al. (2025). Quantifying co2 emissions from smaller anthropogenic point sources using oco‐2 target and oco‐3 snapshot area mapping mode observations. Journal of Geophysical Research: Atmospheres, 130(2), e2024JD042333. https://doi.org/10.1029/2024JD042333 \n\nLiu, J., et al. (2017). Contrasting carbon cycle responses of the tropical continents to the 2015–2016 El Niño. Science, 358(6360), eaam5690. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aam5690 \n\nMacDonald, C. G., et al. (2023). Estimating enhancement ratios of nitrogen dioxide, carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide using satellite observations. Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics, 23(6), 3493–3516. https://doi.org/10.5194/acp-23-3493-2023 \n\nMastrogiacomo, J., et al. (2025). Estimating urban emissions from satellite-derived enhancement ratios of CH4, CO2, and CO. Journal of Geophysical Research: Atmospheres, 130(13), e2025JD043394. https://doi.org/10.1029/2025JD043394 \n\nByrne, B., et al. (2023). National CO2 budgets (2015–2020) inferred from atmospheric CO2 observations in support of the global stocktake. Earth System Science Data, 15(2), 963–1004. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-15-963-2023"
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator has participated in the validation of OCO-2 and OCO-3 CO2 data since 2007, before the launch of the missions. Since the launches, they have continued their validation work using data from the Total Carbon Column Observing Network (TCCON) (e.g., Wunch et al., 2017), and have participated in the improvement of the algorithms for the OCO-2 and OCO-3 retrievals. They have also used the OCO data for scientific studies of urban greenhouse gas emissions (e.g., MacDonald et al., 2022; Mastrogiacomo et al., 2025; referenced in the Justification above).\n\nReference:\n\nWunch, D., et al. (2017). Comparisons of the Orbiting Carbon Observatory-2 (OCO-2) X CO2 measurements with TCCON. Atmospheric Measurement Techniques, 10(6), 2209–2238. https://doi.org/10.5194/amt-10-2209-2017"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
