          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "Fluxnet reflects the power of crowd sourced collective data sharing and the benefits to global ecology from open data. Very few are obligated to share data here and yet most do. What is learned from synthesis far exceeds what is learned from individual sites. It is used in education, it feeds into global assessments for climate for carbon cycle, it is a key benchmark from earth system models and satellite remote sensing of Earth globally, and supports on ground measurements run by technicians all over the world, supporting development worldwide."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "This dataset provides gap-filled and quality controlled data for surface fluxes (energy and carbon) and corresponding meteorology and soil conditions in many sites throughout the Americas. It is in common format and method, and expands the global Fluxnet dataset. \nThe data has many uses: Planet - it is the foundation of many studies of ecological function, the effects of climate change, carbon fluxes, and a key resource in calibration/validation of physical models and remote sensing products that focus on ecosystem function (vegetation, photosynthesis, water stress, carbon fluxes, ecosystem respiration).\nPeople - It is commonly used in classrooms as an example for ecosystem and meteorological observations (I know many of my colleagues in other universities use this in classes of meteorology, ecohydrology, environmental science and climate change). \nProsperity - it provides the foundation of quantification and validation of nature-based solutions for climate change and carbon sequestration by ecosystems (see for example: Burba, G., 2025. Harvesting Carbon: Lakes, Ponds, and Wetlands. A Guide to Utilizing Direct Flux Measurements for Assessment and Verification of Carbon Sequestration and GHG Emission Rates over Areas. LI-COR Environmental, Lincoln, USA, 60 pp.\nISBN: 979-8-9928158-4-9, https://www.licor.com/products/books/harvesting-carbon-guide)"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "3",
          "schema:text": "The FLUXNET dataset is one of the most influential open data resources in Earth and environmental sciences. It provides continuous, high-frequency measurements of carbon dioxide, water vapor, and energy exchanges between ecosystems and the atmosphere—data that form the empirical fundament of our understanding of how the biosphere regulates climate. Without FLUXNET, we would lack the long-term, globally distributed evidence needed to quantify terrestrial carbon sinks, forecast drought and heatwave impacts, and refine Earth system models that inform climate policy and adaptation strategies.\n\nPeople:\nFLUXNET has profoundly shaped scientific education and capacity building. Thousands of early-career researchers and students have been trained using FLUXNET data, gaining skills in micrometeorology, data analysis, and ecosystem modeling. The network’s open-access ethos promotes global equity in science—enabling participation from developing regions and fostering collaboration among scientists across more than 50 countries. Beyond academia, FLUXNET data improve weather and climate forecasting systems that support agriculture, wildfire management, and disaster response—helping communities anticipate and mitigate climate extremes.\n\nPlanet:\nFLUXNET represents the most comprehensive observational record of ecosystem–atmosphere exchanges available. Its measurements reveal how forests, grasslands, wetlands, and croplands breathe—absorbing and releasing carbon and water in response to climate variability. These insights are indispensable for understanding feedbacks between terrestrial ecosystems and the global carbon cycle, evaluating conservation strategies, and monitoring environmental indicators of planetary health. In an era of rapid change, FLUXNET’s standardized, long-term observations serve as a vital benchmark for detecting trends in photosynthesis, respiration, and evapotranspiration—core processes that sustain life on Earth.\n\nProsperity:\nBy improving the accuracy of carbon budgets and ecosystem productivity estimates, FLUXNET supports the global transition to a sustainable, low-carbon economy. Its data underpin climate-smart land management, carbon market verification, and natural climate solution assessments—activities that link environmental integrity with economic resilience. Moreover, by fostering open data sharing, FLUXNET embodies a model of scientific collaboration that strengthens social equity and collective problem-solving.\n\nIn essence, FLUXNET has enabled us to move from isolated, local observations to a coherent, global picture of how the land surface interacts with the atmosphere. Without it, we would lack the empirical foundation to quantify terrestrial carbon sinks, validate satellite observations, or predict how ecosystems will respond to a warming world. The dataset’s enduring legacy is not only scientific—it is humanitarian: empowering people, protecting the planet, and guiding prosperity in a changing climate.",
          "impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "People",
              "schema:text": "FLUXNET has profoundly shaped scientific education and capacity building. Thousands of early-career researchers and students have been trained using FLUXNET data, gaining skills in micrometeorology, data analysis, and ecosystem modeling. The network’s open-access ethos promotes global equity in science—enabling participation from developing regions and fostering collaboration among scientists across more than 50 countries. Beyond academia, FLUXNET data improve weather and climate forecasting systems that support agriculture, wildfire management, and disaster response—helping communities anticipate and mitigate climate extremes."
            },
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "Planet",
              "schema:text": "FLUXNET represents the most comprehensive observational record of ecosystem–atmosphere exchanges available. Its measurements reveal how forests, grasslands, wetlands, and croplands breathe—absorbing and releasing carbon and water in response to climate variability. These insights are indispensable for understanding feedbacks between terrestrial ecosystems and the global carbon cycle, evaluating conservation strategies, and monitoring environmental indicators of planetary health. In an era of rapid change, FLUXNET’s standardized, long-term observations serve as a vital benchmark for detecting trends in photosynthesis, respiration, and evapotranspiration—core processes that sustain life on Earth."
            },
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "Prosperity",
              "schema:text": "By improving the accuracy of carbon budgets and ecosystem productivity estimates, FLUXNET supports the global transition to a sustainable, low-carbon economy. Its data underpin climate-smart land management, carbon market verification, and natural climate solution assessments—activities that link environmental integrity with economic resilience. Moreover, by fostering open data sharing, FLUXNET embodies a model of scientific collaboration that strengthens social equity and collective problem-solving.\n\nIn essence, FLUXNET has enabled us to move from isolated, local observations to a coherent, global picture of how the land surface interacts with the atmosphere. Without it, we would lack the empirical foundation to quantify terrestrial carbon sinks, validate satellite observations, or predict how ecosystems will respond to a warming world. The dataset’s enduring legacy is not only scientific—it is humanitarian: empowering people, protecting the planet, and guiding prosperity in a changing climate."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator is a PI of many towers and their lab uses data from here everyday."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "The nominator contributes to the dataset by providing data from 3 sites - US-UMB, US-UMd, and US-OWC\nThey use it in their classroom at Ohio State University - Enveng 6220 - Graduate data analysis, and Civilen 4218 - Observations and modeling of climate change, as hands-on example for large datasets of climate related observations, and a source of data to test data analysis and modeling approaches on.\nThey use it in publications, they have many papers using AMERIFLUX-FLUXNET-processed data of their sites (e.g., https://doi.org/10.1111/gcb.70053; http://dx.doi.org/10.1029/2024JG008198). Some of their graduate students that develop models are using data from other sites, obtained through the AMERIFLUX-FLUXNET dataset (e.g., https://doi.org/10.1029/2024MS004396; https://doi.org/10.1029/2024JG008721). Graduate students in the nominator’s group were (and continue to be) involved in intersite comparison and will use data from many long-term sites (e.g, https://doi.org/10.3389/ffgc.2021.695269)."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "3",
          "schema:text": "The nominator uses the FLUXNET dataset as a foundational resource for studying the dynamics of terrestrial ecosystems and their responses to climate variability. The high-frequency measurements of carbon, water, and energy fluxes provide a unique window into the functioning of ecosystems across biomes and timescales. In their research, they analyze FLUXNET data to quantify ecosystem productivity, respiration, and evapotranspiration, and to explore how environmental drivers such as temperature, radiation, and soil moisture influence these fluxes.\n\nIn addition, they use FLUXNET data for the validation of remote sensing products, including satellite-derived estimates of gross primary productivity, evapotranspiration, and surface energy balance. The precise, ground-based measurements from FLUXNET sites are indispensable for assessing the accuracy and robustness of satellite models, helping to bridge the gap between local observations and global Earth system monitoring.\n\nWorking with FLUXNET has deepened their understanding of ecosystem processes and strengthened their ability to connect field observations with large-scale environmental patterns. It also exemplifies the value of open, collaborative data in advancing both scientific discovery and global climate understanding."
        }
      ]
    },
