        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Margaret Torn mstorn@lbl.gov\nSébastien Biraud scbiraud@lbl.gov\nYou-Wei Cheah ycheah@lbl.gov\nTrevor Keenan trevorkeenan@berkeley.edu\nChristin Buechner cbuechner@lbl.gov\nStephen Chan SWChan@lbl.gov\nDanielle Christianson, DSChristianson@lbl.gov\nHousen Chu, hchu@lbl.gov \nSigrid Dengel, sdengel@lbl.gov\nSy-Toan Ngo, SyToanNgo@lbl.gov\nFianna O’Brien, flobrien@lbl.gov\nGilberto Pastorello, GZPastorello@lbl.gov\nBrian Wang, BrianWang@lbl.gov"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The FLUXNET dataset is a global, community-driven synthesis of measurements from micrometeorological “eddy covariance” towers that quantify the exchanges of carbon dioxide (CO₂), water vapour and energy between terrestrial ecosystems and the atmosphere. At each tower site, high-temporal-resolution measurements of these fluxes and ancillary variables (such as air temperature, precipitation and radiation) are made; these local measurements are then processed, quality-controlled, standardised and aggregated into a consistent global dataset. With more than 1,500 site-years of data from over 200 sites (in the FLUXNET2015 release) and numerous earlier versions (e.g., La Thuile 2007), the dataset spans many ecosystems and climate regimes, enabling biome-to-global scale analyses. The FLUXNET dataset underpins research in ecosystem physiology, carbon cycling, climate-ecosystem feedbacks, and remote sensing validation, and is freely accessible under an open-data policy that emphasises international cooperation and broad scientific reuse.\n\nReference URL: https://fluxnet.org/dat"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nFluxnet reflects the power of crowd sourced collective data sharing and the benefits to global ecology from open data. Very few are obligated to share data here and yet most do. What is learned from synthesis far exceeds what is learned from individual sites. It is used in education, it feeds into global assessments for climate for carbon cycle, it is a key benchmark from earth system models and satellite remote sensing of Earth globally, and supports on ground measurements run by technicians all over the world, supporting development worldwide.\n\nNominator 2:\nThis dataset provides gap-filled and quality controlled data for surface fluxes (energy and carbon) and corresponding meteorology and soil conditions in many sites throughout the Americas. It is in common format and method, and expands the global Fluxnet dataset. \nThe data has many uses: Planet - it is the foundation of many studies of ecological function, the effects of climate change, carbon fluxes, and a key resource in calibration/validation of physical models and remote sensing products that focus on ecosystem function (vegetation, photosynthesis, water stress, carbon fluxes, ecosystem respiration).\nPeople - It is commonly used in classrooms as an example for ecosystem and meteorological observations (I know many of my colleagues in other universities use this in classes of meteorology, ecohydrology, environmental science and climate change). \nProsperity - it provides the foundation of quantification and validation of nature-based solutions for climate change and carbon sequestration by ecosystems (see for example: Burba, G., 2025. Harvesting Carbon: Lakes, Ponds, and Wetlands. A Guide to Utilizing Direct Flux Measurements for Assessment and Verification of Carbon Sequestration and GHG Emission Rates over Areas. LI-COR Environmental, Lincoln, USA, 60 pp.\nISBN: 979-8-9928158-4-9, https://www.licor.com/products/books/harvesting-carbon-guide)\n\nNominator 3:\nThe FLUXNET dataset is one of the most influential open data resources in Earth and environmental sciences. It provides continuous, high-frequency measurements of carbon dioxide, water vapor, and energy exchanges between ecosystems and the atmosphere—data that form the empirical fundament of our understanding of how the biosphere regulates climate. Without FLUXNET, we would lack the long-term, globally distributed evidence needed to quantify terrestrial carbon sinks, forecast drought and heatwave impacts, and refine Earth system models that inform climate policy and adaptation strategies.\n\nPeople:\nFLUXNET has profoundly shaped scientific education and capacity building. Thousands of early-career researchers and students have been trained using FLUXNET data, gaining skills in micrometeorology, data analysis, and ecosystem modeling. The network’s open-access ethos promotes global equity in science—enabling participation from developing regions and fostering collaboration among scientists across more than 50 countries. Beyond academia, FLUXNET data improve weather and climate forecasting systems that support agriculture, wildfire management, and disaster response—helping communities anticipate and mitigate climate extremes.\n\nPlanet:\nFLUXNET represents the most comprehensive observational record of ecosystem–atmosphere exchanges available. Its measurements reveal how forests, grasslands, wetlands, and croplands breathe—absorbing and releasing carbon and water in response to climate variability. These insights are indispensable for understanding feedbacks between terrestrial ecosystems and the global carbon cycle, evaluating conservation strategies, and monitoring environmental indicators of planetary health. In an era of rapid change, FLUXNET’s standardized, long-term observations serve as a vital benchmark for detecting trends in photosynthesis, respiration, and evapotranspiration—core processes that sustain life on Earth.\n\nProsperity:\nBy improving the accuracy of carbon budgets and ecosystem productivity estimates, FLUXNET supports the global transition to a sustainable, low-carbon economy. Its data underpin climate-smart land management, carbon market verification, and natural climate solution assessments—activities that link environmental integrity with economic resilience. Moreover, by fostering open data sharing, FLUXNET embodies a model of scientific collaboration that strengthens social equity and collective problem-solving.\n\nIn essence, FLUXNET has enabled us to move from isolated, local observations to a coherent, global picture of how the land surface interacts with the atmosphere. Without it, we would lack the empirical foundation to quantify terrestrial carbon sinks, validate satellite observations, or predict how ecosystems will respond to a warming world. The dataset’s enduring legacy is not only scientific—it is humanitarian: empowering people, protecting the planet, and guiding prosperity in a changing climate."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nThe nominator is a PI of many towers and their lab uses data from here everyday.\n\nNominator 2:\nThe nominator contributes to the dataset by providing data from 3 sites - US-UMB, US-UMd, and US-OWC\nThey use it in their classroom at Ohio State University - Enveng 6220 - Graduate data analysis, and Civilen 4218 - Observations and modeling of climate change, as hands-on example for large datasets of climate related observations, and a source of data to test data analysis and modeling approaches on.\nThey use it in publications, they have many papers using AMERIFLUX-FLUXNET-processed data of their sites (e.g., https://doi.org/10.1111/gcb.70053; http://dx.doi.org/10.1029/2024JG008198). Some of their graduate students that develop models are using data from other sites, obtained through the AMERIFLUX-FLUXNET dataset (e.g., https://doi.org/10.1029/2024MS004396; https://doi.org/10.1029/2024JG008721). Graduate students in the nominator’s group were (and continue to be) involved in intersite comparison and will use data from many long-term sites (e.g, https://doi.org/10.3389/ffgc.2021.695269).\n\nNominator 3:\nThe nominator uses the FLUXNET dataset as a foundational resource for studying the dynamics of terrestrial ecosystems and their responses to climate variability. The high-frequency measurements of carbon, water, and energy fluxes provide a unique window into the functioning of ecosystems across biomes and timescales. In their research, they analyze FLUXNET data to quantify ecosystem productivity, respiration, and evapotranspiration, and to explore how environmental drivers such as temperature, radiation, and soil moisture influence these fluxes.\n\nIn addition, they use FLUXNET data for the validation of remote sensing products, including satellite-derived estimates of gross primary productivity, evapotranspiration, and surface energy balance. The precise, ground-based measurements from FLUXNET sites are indispensable for assessing the accuracy and robustness of satellite models, helping to bridge the gap between local observations and global Earth system monitoring.\n\nWorking with FLUXNET has deepened their understanding of ecosystem processes and strengthened their ability to connect field observations with large-scale environmental patterns. It also exemplifies the value of open, collaborative data in advancing both scientific discovery and global climate understanding."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Pastorello, G., et al. (2020). The FLUXNET2015 dataset and the ONEFlux processing pipeline for eddy covariance data. Scientific Data, 7(1), 225. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-020-0534-3"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nGoogle scholar shows 29,000 results with Fluxnet, 12000 for Ameriflux, 3000 for Fluxnet2015.\n\nNominator 2:\nIt has been used in hundreds of studies. In the last few years, data from Ameriflux-Fluxnet is cited in at least 100 papers per year.\n\nSome examples: Understanding how changes in ecosystem management (e.g. Nature-Based Climate Solutions) impact carbon uptake and storage, albedo, evapotranspiration, and other water and energy storage terms (e.g., Zhang et al. 2022, Duman et al. 2021 https://doi.org/10.1016/j.agrformet.2020.108246, Gough et al. 2021 https://doi.org/10.1002/eap.2417, Gough et al, 2022 https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecolind.2022.109004, Arias‐Ortiz et al. 2021 https://doi.org/10.1029/2021JG006573, Yang et al 2024 https://doi.org/10.1016/j.agrformet.2024.110054). \n\nDiagnosing the impacts of natural disturbances like fire, heat waves, droughts, and insect outbreaks (e.g., Rastogi et al. 2022 https://doi.org/10.1029/2022GL097959). \n\nExploring how topographic gradients in temperature and water availability impact ecosystem productivity and hydrologic cycling (e.g., Shveyster et al., 2024 https://doi.org/10.1029/2022WR033757)\n\nUnderstanding how spatial heterogeneity and land-atmosphere coupling impacts a wide range of boundary-layer processes (e.g. Butterworth et al 2021 https://doi.org/10.1175/BAMS-D-19-0346.1, Butterworth et al 2024 https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2023.1251138; Wanner et al., 2024 https://doi.org/10.1007/s10546-024-00868-8). \n\nScaling to regions, including quantifying lateral fluxes, terrestrial-aquatic linkages, and other carbon transfers required for full carbon accounting and model-data and top down-bottom-up comparison (e.g., Metzger et al., 2021 https://doi.org/10.5194/amt-14-6929-2021, Zhang et a; 2023 https://doi.org/10.1111/2041-210X.14188)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nAnkur Desai\n\nNominator 2:\nGil Bohrer\n\nNominator 3:\nGerbrand Koren"
        },
