      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "FLUXNET Coordination Project / AmeriFlux Management Project / ICOS"
        }
      ],
      "description": "The FLUXNET2015 dataset is a globally harmonized collection of ecosystem–atmosphere exchange measurements from hundreds of eddy-covariance towers spanning major climate zones and biome types. It provides half-hourly to daily time series of carbon dioxide, water and energy fluxes—net ecosystem exchange (NEE), latent and sensible heat (LE, H), radiation components, and meteorology—processed with uniform, community-vetted methods. Core steps include rigorous quality control, u* filtering, gap-filling, and flux partitioning to derive gross primary productivity (GPP) and ecosystem respiration (Reco), with accompanying uncertainty estimates and flags. The dataset also includes standardized site metadata (location, instrumentation, disturbance/land-use history, plant functional type) to enable cross-site synthesis. By harmonizing processing across networks (e.g., AmeriFlux, ICOS, OzFlux, TERN, AsiaFlux, ChinaFlux, NEON), FLUXNET2015 supports robust intercomparison and model benchmarking from plot to global scales. Over the past decade it has underpinned research on carbon-climate feedbacks, drought and heatwave impacts, remote-sensing, modeling and machine learning frameworks linking towers to satellite observations. Openly available in tiered licenses with clear citation and fair-use guidance, FLUXNET2015 serves as a reference standard for terrestrial ecosystem science, education, and applications that inform climate assessments, carbon accounting, and land management.",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "Pastorello, G., et al. (2020). The FLUXNET2015 dataset and the ONEFlux processing pipeline for eddy covariance data. Scientific Data, 7(1), 225. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-020-0534-3",
          "identifier": [
            {
              "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
              "propertyID": "DOI",
              "value": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-020-0534-3"
            }
          ],
          "schema:url": [
            "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-020-0534-3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "The data in FLUXNET 2015 has been used in thousands of publications. It has also been re-used in multiple impactful products. Some examples include:"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "Upscaled/global products trained or validated with FLUXNET2015\nMultiple upscaled products exist. The best known is FLUXCOM, but there are dozens of others.\nFLUXCOM (global GPP, NEE, ET/LE, H, Rn): a suite of machine-learning products that upscale eddy-covariance fluxes using satellite and meteorological predictors (e.g., Jung et al. 2019); widely used for carbon-cycle analyses and model evaluation. \nFLUXCOM-X (next-gen variants): expands FLUXCOM with flexible ML configurations and predictors; products (e.g., X-BASE GPP) are distributed via the ICOS Carbon Portal. \nmeta.icos-cp.eu See the recent X-Base data paper on global energy fluxes and uncertainty quantification by Nelson et al. (2024):"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "Nelson, J. A., et al. (2024). X-BASE: The first terrestrial carbon and water flux products from an extended data-driven scaling framework, FLUXCOM-X. https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-2024-165",
          "schema:url": [
            "https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-2024-165"
          ],
          "identifier": [
            "https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-2024-165"
          ]
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "Benchmarking & evaluation platforms that incorporate flux-tower constraints\nILAMB (International Land Model Benchmarking): community system that compares land-model outputs to >60 observational datasets, computes standardized scores (bias, RMSE, seasonal metrics), and generates reproducible diagnostics; formal method and software described in Collier et al. (JAMES) and the ILAMB documentation/portal:"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "Collier, N., Hoffman, F. M., Lawrence, D. M., Keppel‐Aleks, G., Koven, C. D., Riley, W. J., Mu, M., & Randerson, J. T. (2018). The international land model benchmarking (Ilamb) system: Design, theory, and implementation. Journal of Advances in Modeling Earth Systems, 10(11), 2731–2754. https://doi.org/10.1029/2018MS001354",
          "schema:url": [
            "https://doi.org/10.1029/2018MS001354"
          ],
          "identifier": [
            "https://doi.org/10.1029/2018MS001354"
          ]
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "https://modelevaluation.org: a web application used for standardized model evaluation/benchmarking with curated datasets and shared scoring workflows.",
          "schema:url": [
            "https://modelevaluation.org"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3347-0258",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
