      "name": "Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, UC Berkeley, 1 Cyclotron Road, Berkeley, CA 94720"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:006",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:006",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:006",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3347-0258"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "Without FLUXNET2015, the scientific community would lack the only globally harmonized, observation-based record that directly measures how entire ecosystems exchange carbon, water, and energy with the atmosphere at sub-daily resolution. It would not be possible to reliably separate gross primary productivity from ecosystem respiration across biomes, diagnose how droughts and heatwaves alter carbon sinks, or ground-truth satellites and models that inform climate policy, conservation, and risk management. FLUXNET2015 turns point measurements into a common language for Earth system science, enabling rigorous synthesis from plot to planet.\n\nPeople — education, training, prediction.\nFLUXNET2015 underpins modern training in environmental data science and micrometeorology. Students and early-career researchers learn QA/QC, u* filtering, gap-filling, uncertainty analysis, and flux partitioning on real time series—skills that transfer to weather, air-quality, hydrology, and climate analytics. For prediction, the high-frequency fluxes provide essential targets for model parameterization, data assimilation and machine-learning approaches that forecast ecosystem stress, evapotranspiration, and surface energy partitioning.\n\nPlanet — geophysical phenomena, climate, indicators.\nFLUXNET2015 resolves diurnal to interannual dynamics that cannot be inferred from inventories alone: photosynthetic seasonality, respiration responses, energy balance closure, and land–atmosphere coupling. It is the benchmark for terrestrial model development and evaluation, a calibration source for satellite indicators (e.g., productivity, evapotranspiration, SIF), and a reference for climatologic anomalies to surface processes. The dataset reveals where and when ecosystems amplify or damp climate variability and extremes, improving representation of carbon–water feedbacks in Earth system models.\n\nProsperity — economic good, equity, resilience.\nReliable surface flux estimates reduce uncertainty across sectors sensitive to weather and climate: agriculture (irrigation scheduling, yield risk), energy (demand and renewable forecasts), and water resources (basin-scale ET diagnostics). Open licensing and uniform processing lower barriers for teams worldwide, broadening participation in high-level environmental analytics and supporting workforce development. By improving the fidelity of models and satellite products used in planning and risk management, FLUXNET2015 strengthens community resilience to heat, drought, and compounding extremes.\n\nIn sum, at its 10th anniversary, FLUXNET2015 is foundational infrastructure: it anchors education, accelerates discovery of how the land surface operates under variability and extremes, and enhances decision-relevant predictions across water, energy, and climate applications."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator engages with FLUXNET2015 as both a user and a steward. As Chair of the FLUXNET Community Council, Director of the FLUXNET Coordination Project, and co-PI of the AmeriFlux Management Project, they help lead community engagement, the dataset-development initiative, site onboarding, and updates to common processing (QA/QC, u* filtering, gap-filling, flux partitioning). In their research group, they use FLUXNET2015 daily to diagnose ecosystem function and responses to heat and drought, and to benchmark land-surface and Earth-system models. They fuse tower records with satellite observations (e.g., SIF, thermal, GEDI) and meteorological reanalyses to build upscaled products and machine-learning frameworks, and to test hypotheses about land–atmosphere coupling, water-use efficiency, and carbon–climate feedbacks. They also rely on the dataset for education and training: they run hands-on labs on time-series methods, uncertainty propagation, and reproducible workflows. Finally, they work with network partners and site PIs to ensure attribution, licensing compliance, and sustained curation, and they advocate for resources to keep the record growing and interoperable with next-generation flux products. In short, FLUXNET2015 is central to how their group learns from, teaches with, and advances understanding of the terrestrial biosphere."
        }
      ]
    },
