        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The FLUXNET2015 dataset is a globally harmonized collection of ecosystem–atmosphere exchange measurements from hundreds of eddy-covariance towers spanning major climate zones and biome types. It provides half-hourly to daily time series of carbon dioxide, water and energy fluxes—net ecosystem exchange (NEE), latent and sensible heat (LE, H), radiation components, and meteorology—processed with uniform, community-vetted methods. Core steps include rigorous quality control, u* filtering, gap-filling, and flux partitioning to derive gross primary productivity (GPP) and ecosystem respiration (Reco), with accompanying uncertainty estimates and flags. The dataset also includes standardized site metadata (location, instrumentation, disturbance/land-use history, plant functional type) to enable cross-site synthesis. By harmonizing processing across networks (e.g., AmeriFlux, ICOS, OzFlux, TERN, AsiaFlux, ChinaFlux, NEON), FLUXNET2015 supports robust intercomparison and model benchmarking from plot to global scales. Over the past decade it has underpinned research on carbon-climate feedbacks, drought and heatwave impacts, remote-sensing, modeling and machine learning frameworks linking towers to satellite observations. Openly available in tiered licenses with clear citation and fair-use guidance, FLUXNET2015 serves as a reference standard for terrestrial ecosystem science, education, and applications that inform climate assessments, carbon accounting, and land management."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Without FLUXNET2015, the scientific community would lack the only globally harmonized, observation-based record that directly measures how entire ecosystems exchange carbon, water, and energy with the atmosphere at sub-daily resolution. It would not be possible to reliably separate gross primary productivity from ecosystem respiration across biomes, diagnose how droughts and heatwaves alter carbon sinks, or ground-truth satellites and models that inform climate policy, conservation, and risk management. FLUXNET2015 turns point measurements into a common language for Earth system science, enabling rigorous synthesis from plot to planet.\n\nPeople — education, training, prediction.\nFLUXNET2015 underpins modern training in environmental data science and micrometeorology. Students and early-career researchers learn QA/QC, u* filtering, gap-filling, uncertainty analysis, and flux partitioning on real time series—skills that transfer to weather, air-quality, hydrology, and climate analytics. For prediction, the high-frequency fluxes provide essential targets for model parameterization, data assimilation and machine-learning approaches that forecast ecosystem stress, evapotranspiration, and surface energy partitioning.\n\nPlanet — geophysical phenomena, climate, indicators.\nFLUXNET2015 resolves diurnal to interannual dynamics that cannot be inferred from inventories alone: photosynthetic seasonality, respiration responses, energy balance closure, and land–atmosphere coupling. It is the benchmark for terrestrial model development and evaluation, a calibration source for satellite indicators (e.g., productivity, evapotranspiration, SIF), and a reference for climatologic anomalies to surface processes. The dataset reveals where and when ecosystems amplify or damp climate variability and extremes, improving representation of carbon–water feedbacks in Earth system models.\n\nProsperity — economic good, equity, resilience.\nReliable surface flux estimates reduce uncertainty across sectors sensitive to weather and climate: agriculture (irrigation scheduling, yield risk), energy (demand and renewable forecasts), and water resources (basin-scale ET diagnostics). Open licensing and uniform processing lower barriers for teams worldwide, broadening participation in high-level environmental analytics and supporting workforce development. By improving the fidelity of models and satellite products used in planning and risk management, FLUXNET2015 strengthens community resilience to heat, drought, and compounding extremes.\n\nIn sum, at its 10th anniversary, FLUXNET2015 is foundational infrastructure: it anchors education, accelerates discovery of how the land surface operates under variability and extremes, and enhances decision-relevant predictions across water, energy, and climate applications."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator engages with FLUXNET2015 as both a user and a steward. As Chair of the FLUXNET Community Council, Director of the FLUXNET Coordination Project, and co-PI of the AmeriFlux Management Project, they help lead community engagement, the dataset-development initiative, site onboarding, and updates to common processing (QA/QC, u* filtering, gap-filling, flux partitioning). In their research group, they use FLUXNET2015 daily to diagnose ecosystem function and responses to heat and drought, and to benchmark land-surface and Earth-system models. They fuse tower records with satellite observations (e.g., SIF, thermal, GEDI) and meteorological reanalyses to build upscaled products and machine-learning frameworks, and to test hypotheses about land–atmosphere coupling, water-use efficiency, and carbon–climate feedbacks. They also rely on the dataset for education and training: they run hands-on labs on time-series methods, uncertainty propagation, and reproducible workflows. Finally, they work with network partners and site PIs to ensure attribution, licensing compliance, and sustained curation, and they advocate for resources to keep the record growing and interoperable with next-generation flux products. In short, FLUXNET2015 is central to how their group learns from, teaches with, and advances understanding of the terrestrial biosphere."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Pastorello, G., et al. (2020). The FLUXNET2015 dataset and the ONEFlux processing pipeline for eddy covariance data. Scientific Data, 7(1), 225. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-020-0534-3"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "The data in FLUXNET 2015 has been used in thousands of publications. It has also been re-used in multiple impactful products. Some examples include:\n\nUpscaled/global products trained or validated with FLUXNET2015\nMultiple upscaled products exist. The best known is FLUXCOM, but there are dozens of others.\nFLUXCOM (global GPP, NEE, ET/LE, H, Rn): a suite of machine-learning products that upscale eddy-covariance fluxes using satellite and meteorological predictors (e.g., Jung et al. 2019); widely used for carbon-cycle analyses and model evaluation. \nFLUXCOM-X (next-gen variants): expands FLUXCOM with flexible ML configurations and predictors; products (e.g., X-BASE GPP) are distributed via the ICOS Carbon Portal. \nmeta.icos-cp.eu See the recent X-Base data paper on global energy fluxes and uncertainty quantification by Nelson et al. (2024): \n\nNelson, J. A., et al. (2024). X-BASE: The first terrestrial carbon and water flux products from an extended data-driven scaling framework, FLUXCOM-X. https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-2024-165 \n\nBenchmarking & evaluation platforms that incorporate flux-tower constraints\nILAMB (International Land Model Benchmarking): community system that compares land-model outputs to >60 observational datasets, computes standardized scores (bias, RMSE, seasonal metrics), and generates reproducible diagnostics; formal method and software described in Collier et al. (JAMES) and the ILAMB documentation/portal: \n\nCollier, N., Hoffman, F. M., Lawrence, D. M., Keppel‐Aleks, G., Koven, C. D., Riley, W. J., Mu, M., & Randerson, J. T. (2018). The international land model benchmarking (Ilamb) system: Design, theory, and implementation. Journal of Advances in Modeling Earth Systems, 10(11), 2731–2754. https://doi.org/10.1029/2018MS001354 \n\nhttps://modelevaluation.org: a web application used for standardized model evaluation/benchmarking with curated datasets and shared scoring workflows."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Trevor Keenan"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Email Address",
          "schema:value": "trevorkeenan@berkeley.edu"
        },
        {
