      },
      "schema:email": "dylan.jones@utoronto.ca",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:university-of-toronto-60-st-george-street-toronto-ontario-m5s-1a7-canada"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:007",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:007",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:007",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8599-4555",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2198-0768",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4573-9998",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0619-3045",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4924-0377",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0274-8050",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8306-0516",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2213-0044",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1935-3725"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The TCCON data are a key means to link satellite and in situ observations of greenhouse gases. Without these data, that connection would be much less precise, making it difficult or impossible to use in situ and satellite data together to constrain changes in the carbon cycle and the resulting current and future impact on climate. The TCCON data are also used directly in carbon cycle and urban emission studies. In the former, these data are commonly used as withheld data for validation of model inversions that assimilate in situ and/or satellite data. The global reach and total column nature of these observations provides a valuable validation source for such models to verify that their assimilation produces an atmospheric state in better agreement to a consistent but independent dataset."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "The TCCON dataset for example its greenhouse gas (GHG) measurements are used to validate satellite measured XCO2, XCH4 and XCO. These satellites provide crucial information on the concentration of these GHGs worldwide of critical importance for climate research. However, they need to be well calibrated as the accuracy required is extremely high and demanding. The TCCON netwerk with its global network of 30+ stations provides the final calibration for those GHG-satellites. As such the TCCON data is at the basis of the success of those satellite missions. In addition, the data is being used for studies on the Carbon cycle and anthropogenic emissions."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "3",
          "schema:text": "Space-based, remote sensing observations of atmospheric carbon dioxide (CO2) and methane (CH4) are now providing key insights into global carbon cycle and its response to human activities and climate change. High-spectral-resolution observations of reflected sunlight are being analyzed detect on and quantify the sources emitting these critical greenhouse gases (GHGs) into the atmosphere and the natural sinks that remove them on spatial scales spanning individual facilities to the globe. These data are now providing a transparent, observation-based approach for assessing the quality and completeness of the GHG inventories submitted to the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC). \n\nThe principal challenge of this space-based remote sensing measurement approach is the need for unprecedented precision and accuracy. Even the most intense sources and sinks rarely produce changes in the column-averaged atmospheric dry air mole fractions of these gases exceeding 0.5% on spatial scales larger than a few square kilometers. To meet this challenge, the space-based CO2 and CH4 products must be validated against accurate in situ measurements collected at the surface and from airborne platforms. The Total Carbon Column Observing Network (TCCON) provides the transfer standard needed to relate space-based CO2 and CH4 estimates to these in situ standards. \n\nTCCON is a global network of about two dozen stations distributed at latitudes between Lauder, New Zealand (45 S) and Eureka, Canada (80 N). Each station incorporates a high-spectral-resolution Fourier transform spectrometer (FTS) and a solar tracker to collect spectra of direct sunlight. TCCON FTS spectra are analyzed to estimate the column-average dry air mole fractions of CO2, CH4, N2O, HF, CO, H2O , and HDO (c.f., Laughner et al., https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-16-2197-2024). TCCON CO2 and CH4 estimates are regularly validated against in situ measurements of these gases acquired above the sites by instruments deployed on high-altitude fixed-wing aircraft or balloons. \n\nTo assess the precision and accuracy of the space-based GHG products, estimates of the CO2 and CH4 column-averaged dry air mole fractions derived from TCCON observations are compared to those derived from near-simultaneous, space-based observations acquired near the stations. This validation approach has played critical role in the identification and correction of systematic biases in the space-based estimates of CO2 and CH4 by Japan’s Greenhouse gases Observing SATellites, GOSAT and GOSAT-2, NASA’s Orbiting Carbon Observatories, OCO-2 and OCO-3, and the Copernicus Sentinel 5 Precursor TROPOMI instruments. TCCON data are expected to play an increasingly important role in the validation of products from these and future space-based GHG sensor as nations use these products to manage their carbon emissions to mitigate their impacts on climate change."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "4",
          "schema:text": "The Total Carbon Column Observing Network (TCCON) is a globally distributed, PI-led network of ground-based Fourier-transform spectrometers that measure column-averaged greenhouse gases with exceptional precision. This dataset has proven itself as an invaluable reference for many satellites, including OCO-2, GOSAT, and Sentinel-5P, ensuring the accuracy needed to detect small but important variations in carbon dioxide and methane that reveal underlying sources and sinks. Without TCCON, satellite greenhouse gas data would lack the validation framework necessary for credibility. In addition, the network serves as a training ground for students and early-career scientists, offering hands-on opportunities to collect and process TCCON site data and to use these measurements in combination with the broader network for environmental research. It represents a global collaboration working toward shared scientific goals and provides a scientifically rigorous and trusted reference."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "5",
          "schema:text": "The Total Carbon Column Observing Network (TCCON) has provided a consistent, long-term, high-quality record of atmospheric total column abundances of carbon dioxide, methane, carbon monoxide, and other trace gases for over 20 years. For climate and carbon cycle studies, long-term records are essential to allow us to pull robust signals out of the large interannual variability in the Earth system, and therefore TCCON serves as an important long-term eye on the global carbon cycle. Without it, we could not quantify the impact of wildfires in offsetting forest sequestration (e.g., Byrne et al., 2024), or long-term trends in urban methane emissions (e.g., Wunch et al., 2016).\n\nAside from its value in carbon cycle studies, it has also played a central role in the validation of satellite measurements of carbon dioxide, methane, and carbon monoxide. Because of its long record, the TCCON measurements have been used in the validation of many satellite instruments from several space agencies, including SCIAMACHY, MOPITT, GOSAT, OCO-2, TROPOMI, FY-3D, GOSAT-2, OCO-3, MicroCarb, GOSAT-GW, Sentinel-5. With continued funding, it will be available to support CO2M, MERLIN, and other missions in the future. The TCCON measurements have been used to identify biases in the satellite measurements related to surface properties, aerosol loading, and airmass dependences among other issued (D. Wunch et al., 2011), and have helped to ensure that our valuable space assets measure as precisely and accurately as possible.\n\nTCCON measurements have also been useful for the development and improvement of spectroscopic models of the interaction between light and atmospheric trace gases. The TCCON spectra have been used to develop and improve the state-of-the-art HITRAN line lists, used widely in the atmospheric retrieval community (e.g., Gordon et al., 2022), and in the development of the spectroscopy used for satellite retrievals (e.g., Payne et al., 2020; Scheepmaker et al., 2013).\n\nRefs:\nByrne, B., et al. (2024). Carbon emissions from the 2023 Canadian wildfires. Nature, 633(8031), 835–839. doi:10.1038/s41586-024-07878-z\nGordon, I. E., et al. (2022). The HITRAN2020 molecular spectroscopic database. JQSRT, 277, 107949. doi:10.1016/j.jqsrt.2021.107949\nPayne, V. H., et al. (2020). Absorption coefficient (ABSCO) tables for the Orbiting Carbon Observatories: Version 5.1. JQSRT, 255, 107217. doi:10.1016/j.jqsrt.2020.107217\nScheepmaker, R. A., et al. (2013). Improved water vapour spectroscopy in the 4174–4300 cm−1 region and its impact on SCIAMACHY HDO/H2 O measurements. AMT, 6(4), 879–894. doi:10.5194/amt-6-879-2013\nWunch, D., et al. (2011). A method for evaluating bias in global measurements of CO2 total columns from space. ACP, 11(23), 12317–12337. doi:10.5194/acp-11-12317-2011\nWunch, D., et al. (2016). Quantifying the loss of processed natural gas within California’s South Coast Air Basin using long-term measurements of ethane and methane. ACP, 16(22), 14091–14105. doi:10.5194/acp-16-14091-2016"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "6",
          "schema:text": "This is a stunning achievement of cooperation from many atmospheric-science researchers around the world to set up about 30+ monitoring stations that each uses an FTS instrument to look up at the sun and retrieve important greenhouse gases. These data offer a gold-standard of calibration for a number of satellite-remote-sensing datasets in NASA's Earth Observing System. I have most familiarity with the Orbiting Carbon Observatory (OCO) datasets, and I been on the OCO Science Team since 2010. The nominator's responsibilities on the Science Team are to chacterize the statistical distribution of atmospheric carbon dioxide viewed through OCO's camera and retrieved using a Levenberg-Marquardt retrieval algorithm. Carbon dioxide is a leading greenhouse gas that must be monitored for the planet to have a chance to self-regulate. The retrievals listed as 'target mode' are matched with the TCCON target, and a statistical regression gives the all-important coefficients that allow the OCO data to be calibrated."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "7",
          "schema:text": "The Total Carbon Column Observing Network (TCCON) is a valuable data source for validating satellite-based measurements of column-averaged atmospheric carbon dioxide (XCO2) and other species. This global network of ground-based Fourier Transform Spectrometers provides highly precise and accurate measurements of greenhouse gases over diverse locations worldwide. The TCCON dataset's global coverage spans local, urban, and coastal regions, enabling comprehensive validation of satellite observations from local to regional scales.\n\nNot having the TCCON dataset would introduce significant challenges in providing accurate, ground-truth XCO2 measurements, which are necessary to verify and calibrate satellite data. Satellite instruments heavily rely on TCCON as a \"gold standard\" ground-based reference. The TCCON dataset's high precision and well-maintained calibration support robust validation and error quantification of satellite observations, in addition to its coverage of complex areas where satellite retrievals may face greater uncertainty. TCCON supports improved understanding of the carbon cycle and enhances confidence in satellite climate data products by bridging global satellite sensing with accurate ground-level measurements."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "8",
          "schema:text": "TCCON data are critical for monitoring the health of the planet. They provide information about diurnal, synoptic, seasonal, interannual, and multidecadal variations in important greenhouse gases. TCCON data are a unique source of information about the diurnal cycle in greenhouse gas processes, especially CO2 uptake and release by the biosphere and by anthropogenic sources. Because the observations measure the \"total column\" and are not confined to the boundary layer, the diurnal variations in TCCON data is not complicated by diurnal patterns of boundary layer mixing. Thus, data have shown that diurnal patterns in TCCON data can both be used to quantify local sources of fossil CO2 in urban areas and to constrain local biospheric carbon uptake or release across the diurnal cycle. No other method permits this type of local CO2 analysis at diurnal scales. These data also underpin greenhouse gas monitoring satellite missions, since they provide the most important validation source. Without TCCON data, NASA's OCO missions would not have been able to meet their accuracy or precision goals, since comparison against TCCON has been used to identify sources of error and improve the space-based retrieval algorithm."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "9",
          "schema:text": "Initially established for the validation of greenhouse gas (GHG) measurements from satellite instruments such as the Orbiting Carbon Observatory (OCO) and the Greenhouse Gases Observing Satellite (GOSAT), the TCCON data have proved invaluable for validating a broad range of GHG observations, such as from OCO-2, OCO-3, GOSAT-2, the Chinese Carbon Dioxide Observation Satellite Mission (TanSat), and carbon monoxide (CO) measurements from the Measurement Of Pollution In The Troposphere (MOPITT) and the TROPOspheric Monitoring Instrument (TROPOMI). TCCON data are critical for validation of observations of gases such as CO2 and CH4 because small biases in the satellite observations can have deleterious impacts on the utility of the data, which are essential for understanding the implications for the climate system and the planet of the changing global carbon cycle due to anthropogenic and natural processes. \n\nAlthough the data have been invaluable for validation of the satellites, they are also playing a key role for the evaluation of inverse modelling analyses that employ the satellite data to quantify surface fluxes of GHGs. TCCON data are ideally suited for this task because the measurements are sensitive to variations in trace gases throughout the depth of lower atmosphere and thus, due to the influence of atmospheric transport, the measurements can have footprints that are larger than the grid box of the models. Consequently, there are no representativeness errors associated with comparison of the models with TCCON data. In contrast, with in situ surface observations, the representativeness errors can be quite large when comparing models with the in situ point measurements.\n\nTCCON data are also providing valuable complementary information to the satellite observations of GHGs. Space-based GHG observations are mainly from low Earth orbiting satellites which provide global observational coverage, but only one, or at most two, observations per day at a given location. TCCON, in contrast, gives almost continuous observational coverage, weather permitting. Integrating the temporal information from the TCCON data with the spatial information of the satellite measurements enhances our ability to constrain highly variable processes, such as wildfires, which has implications for societal prosperity and people. For example, inverse model estimates of CO emissions from the unprecedented 2023 Canadian wildfires suggested that the TCCON data, when combined with observations from TROPOMI, gave more constraints on the day-to-day variability of the emissions than can be obtained from only the satellite data. TCCON also provides coincident measurements of other combustion-related gases such as formaldehyde (H2CO) and hydrogen cyanide (HCN), which when combined with CO, CO2, and CH4 can offer process-based information on the fires. Given the increasing severity of wildfires, TCCON data will continue to be a valuable dataset for understanding the impact of these fires."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator is one of the primary developers for the algorithm that is used to generate the level 2 column data as well as a significant part of the data pipeline that delivers the data to public users. They have spent the last ~7 years of their career working to first deliver the current product with accurate and precise ties to the WMO metrological scales, then to continue to refine the precision of the data to meet the needs of the next generation of GHG observing satellites. As part of that, they regularly answer user questions about the data."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "The nominator's team uses the data primarily to validate the satellite measuremenst of XCO2, XCH4 and XCO."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "3",
          "schema:text": "Prior to their retirement from the NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, the nominator served as the the Principal Investigator of the NASA Orbiting Carbon Observatory (OCO) and the Science Team Lead for NASA’s OCO-2 and OCO-3 missions. TCCON was an integral part of these missions and served a critical role in their data validation strategies. It also provided the primary method for cross-validating the OCO-2 and OCO-3 products with the GHG products collected by Japan’s Greenhouse Gas Observing SATellite missions, GOSAT and GOSAT-2. \n\nThey also served as the Greenhouse Gas lead for Committee on Earth Observations (CEOS) Atmospheric Composition-Virtual Constellation (AC-VC). In this context, TCCON became the primary standard for cross-validating GHG results from all CEOS GHG missions against the relevant World Meteorological Organization (WMO) in situ standards for CO2 and CH4. This validation approach contributed to rapid improvements in the quality of the data products returned by all of these space-based GHG sensors and accelerated their acceptance by the carbon cycle science and policy communities."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "4",
          "schema:text": "The nomiantor's personal use of TCCON data has been in evaluating the performance of atmospheric carbon dioxide and carbon monoxide inversion analyses to estimate regional-scale sources and sinks. While emissions are optimized using satellite-based retrievals, the TCCON dataset provides an independent set of observations to compare against simulated posterior concentrations. This independent validation is crucial for confirming that emission estimates generalize beyond the data used directly in the inversion and adds an additional layer of confidence. Without TCCON, there would be few options for independent validation, particularly outside regions with dense in situ sampling."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "5",
          "schema:text": "The nominator is the current Chair of the TCCON, has previously served as Deputy Chair, and is an active member of the network. They are also the PI of the East Trout Lake TCCON station located in Saskatchewan, Canada. They have used the TCCON data to validate the Greenhouse Gases Observing Satellite and the Orbiting Carbon Observatory-2, and to perform studies on urban greenhouse gas emissions."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "6",
          "schema:text": "The nominator has published two peer-reviewed papers on how the calibration of OCO-2 data is carried out with valid uncertainty quantification. They have also been a co-architect of a Bayesian flux-inversion method called WOMBAT, which allows researchers and decision-makers to see where carbon dioxide is coming from or being absorbed at Earth's surface. TCCON is being used to match forward simulation of the fluxes, and their team has participated in two MIPs that use the TCCON data for this purpose."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "7",
          "schema:text": "The TCCON dataset has been very valuable throughout the nominator's postdoctoral research experience at the Jet Propulsion Laboratory, California Institute of Technology. They extensively utilized this ground-based dataset to validate satellite-based measurements of the dry-air mole fractions of carbon dioxide (XCO2) from the Orbiting Carbon Observatory-2 (OCO-2) and Orbiting Carbon Observatory-3 (OCO-3) missions. The TCCON dataset serves as the gold standard and primary validation for the OCO-2/3 missions, as well as for numerous other satellite instruments. Due to its exceptional precision, accuracy, and global coverage, the TCCON dataset served as the truth dataset in validating the v11.1 OCO-2 XCO2 data (ttps://doi.org/10.1029/2024EA003935). As an assistant research scientist, they plan to continue utilizing this invaluable dataset to validate not only satellite instrument data but also measurements from other upcoming ground-based measurement networks."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "8",
          "schema:text": "As a PI at the University of Michigan, the nominator's group regularly uses TCCON data to probe carbon-climate interactions. They have used TCCON data to identify the importance of mesoscale weather systems on CO2 variability, to quantify interannual variability in carbon cycling and link it to El Nino impacts in the tropics, to evaluate prognostic models such as the Community Earth System Model, and to examine diurnal variations."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "9",
          "schema:text": "The nominator’s research is focused on data assimilation and inverse modelling of emissions of environmentally important gases, with a particular focus on satellite observations of greenhouse gases. Previously, they used the TCCON data mainly to evaluate the inversion analyses, however, their group is also integrating the TCCON data with satellite observations in their inversion studies to exploit their complementarity with the satellite observations. They are also using the TCCON data to evaluate emission factors in bottom-up wildfire emission inventories."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:007",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 8,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Atmospheric Science, Space Weather"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "2020 TCCON Data Release"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Total Carbon Column Observing Network (TCCON) Team"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://tccondata.org/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "CaltechData"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.14291/TCCON.GGG2020"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Tom Morrell, Coleen Roehl, Josh Laughner"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The TCCON release contains data from 30+ ground based FTIRs located in over a dozen nations around the world. The files include total column averages of atmospheric trace gases, including but not limited to CO2, CH4, CO, N2O, HF, H2O, HDO, and O2 along with the ancillary information necessary to compare the column amounts to in situ, space-based, or model observations of the same gases. The TCCON data are widely used for satellite validation and carbon cycle studies."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nThe TCCON data are a key means to link satellite and in situ observations of greenhouse gases. Without these data, that connection would be much less precise, making it difficult or impossible to use in situ and satellite data together to constrain changes in the carbon cycle and the resulting current and future impact on climate. The TCCON data are also used directly in carbon cycle and urban emission studies. In the former, these data are commonly used as withheld data for validation of model inversions that assimilate in situ and/or satellite data. The global reach and total column nature of these observations provides a valuable validation source for such models to verify that their assimilation produces an atmospheric state in better agreement to a consistent but independent dataset.\n\nNominator 2:\nThe TCCON dataset for example its greenhouse gas (GHG) measurements are used to validate satellite measured XCO2, XCH4 and XCO. These satellites provide crucial information on the concentration of these GHGs worldwide of critical importance for climate research. However, they need to be well calibrated as the accuracy required is extremely high and demanding. The TCCON netwerk with its global network of 30+ stations provides the final calibration for those GHG-satellites. As such the TCCON data is at the basis of the success of those satellite missions. In addition, the data is being used for studies on the Carbon cycle and anthropogenic emissions.\n\nNominator 3:\nSpace-based, remote sensing observations of atmospheric carbon dioxide (CO2) and methane (CH4) are now providing key insights into global carbon cycle and its response to human activities and climate change. High-spectral-resolution observations of reflected sunlight are being analyzed detect on and quantify the sources emitting these critical greenhouse gases (GHGs) into the atmosphere and the natural sinks that remove them on spatial scales spanning individual facilities to the globe. These data are now providing a transparent, observation-based approach for assessing the quality and completeness of the GHG inventories submitted to the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC). \n\nThe principal challenge of this space-based remote sensing measurement approach is the need for unprecedented precision and accuracy. Even the most intense sources and sinks rarely produce changes in the column-averaged atmospheric dry air mole fractions of these gases exceeding 0.5% on spatial scales larger than a few square kilometers. To meet this challenge, the space-based CO2 and CH4 products must be validated against accurate in situ measurements collected at the surface and from airborne platforms. The Total Carbon Column Observing Network (TCCON) provides the transfer standard needed to relate space-based CO2 and CH4 estimates to these in situ standards. \n\nTCCON is a global network of about two dozen stations distributed at latitudes between Lauder, New Zealand (45 S) and Eureka, Canada (80 N). Each station incorporates a high-spectral-resolution Fourier transform spectrometer (FTS) and a solar tracker to collect spectra of direct sunlight. TCCON FTS spectra are analyzed to estimate the column-average dry air mole fractions of CO2, CH4, N2O, HF, CO, H2O , and HDO (c.f., Laughner et al., https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-16-2197-2024). TCCON CO2 and CH4 estimates are regularly validated against in situ measurements of these gases acquired above the sites by instruments deployed on high-altitude fixed-wing aircraft or balloons. \n\nTo assess the precision and accuracy of the space-based GHG products, estimates of the CO2 and CH4 column-averaged dry air mole fractions derived from TCCON observations are compared to those derived from near-simultaneous, space-based observations acquired near the stations. This validation approach has played critical role in the identification and correction of systematic biases in the space-based estimates of CO2 and CH4 by Japan’s Greenhouse gases Observing SATellites, GOSAT and GOSAT-2, NASA’s Orbiting Carbon Observatories, OCO-2 and OCO-3, and the Copernicus Sentinel 5 Precursor TROPOMI instruments. TCCON data are expected to play an increasingly important role in the validation of products from these and future space-based GHG sensor as nations use these products to manage their carbon emissions to mitigate their impacts on climate change.\n\nNominator 4: \nThe Total Carbon Column Observing Network (TCCON) is a globally distributed, PI-led network of ground-based Fourier-transform spectrometers that measure column-averaged greenhouse gases with exceptional precision. This dataset has proven itself as an invaluable reference for many satellites, including OCO-2, GOSAT, and Sentinel-5P, ensuring the accuracy needed to detect small but important variations in carbon dioxide and methane that reveal underlying sources and sinks. Without TCCON, satellite greenhouse gas data would lack the validation framework necessary for credibility. In addition, the network serves as a training ground for students and early-career scientists, offering hands-on opportunities to collect and process TCCON site data and to use these measurements in combination with the broader network for environmental research. It represents a global collaboration working toward shared scientific goals and provides a scientifically rigorous and trusted reference.\n\nNominator 5:\nThe Total Carbon Column Observing Network (TCCON) has provided a consistent, long-term, high-quality record of atmospheric total column abundances of carbon dioxide, methane, carbon monoxide, and other trace gases for over 20 years. For climate and carbon cycle studies, long-term records are essential to allow us to pull robust signals out of the large interannual variability in the Earth system, and therefore TCCON serves as an important long-term eye on the global carbon cycle. Without it, we could not quantify the impact of wildfires in offsetting forest sequestration (e.g., Byrne et al., 2024), or long-term trends in urban methane emissions (e.g., Wunch et al., 2016).\n\nAside from its value in carbon cycle studies, it has also played a central role in the validation of satellite measurements of carbon dioxide, methane, and carbon monoxide. Because of its long record, the TCCON measurements have been used in the validation of many satellite instruments from several space agencies, including SCIAMACHY, MOPITT, GOSAT, OCO-2, TROPOMI, FY-3D, GOSAT-2, OCO-3, MicroCarb, GOSAT-GW, Sentinel-5. With continued funding, it will be available to support CO2M, MERLIN, and other missions in the future. The TCCON measurements have been used to identify biases in the satellite measurements related to surface properties, aerosol loading, and airmass dependences among other issued (D. Wunch et al., 2011), and have helped to ensure that our valuable space assets measure as precisely and accurately as possible.\n\nTCCON measurements have also been useful for the development and improvement of spectroscopic models of the interaction between light and atmospheric trace gases. The TCCON spectra have been used to develop and improve the state-of-the-art HITRAN line lists, used widely in the atmospheric retrieval community (e.g., Gordon et al., 2022), and in the development of the spectroscopy used for satellite retrievals (e.g., Payne et al., 2020; Scheepmaker et al., 2013).\n\nRefs:\nByrne, B., et al. (2024). Carbon emissions from the 2023 Canadian wildfires. Nature, 633(8031), 835–839. doi:10.1038/s41586-024-07878-z\nGordon, I. E., et al. (2022). The HITRAN2020 molecular spectroscopic database. JQSRT, 277, 107949. doi:10.1016/j.jqsrt.2021.107949\nPayne, V. H., et al. (2020). Absorption coefficient (ABSCO) tables for the Orbiting Carbon Observatories: Version 5.1. JQSRT, 255, 107217. doi:10.1016/j.jqsrt.2020.107217\nScheepmaker, R. A., et al. (2013). Improved water vapour spectroscopy in the 4174–4300 cm−1 region and its impact on SCIAMACHY HDO/H2 O measurements. AMT, 6(4), 879–894. doi:10.5194/amt-6-879-2013\nWunch, D., et al. (2011). A method for evaluating bias in global measurements of CO2 total columns from space. ACP, 11(23), 12317–12337. doi:10.5194/acp-11-12317-2011\nWunch, D., et al. (2016). Quantifying the loss of processed natural gas within California’s South Coast Air Basin using long-term measurements of ethane and methane. ACP, 16(22), 14091–14105. doi:10.5194/acp-16-14091-2016\n\nNominator 6:\nThis is a stunning achievement of cooperation from many atmospheric-science researchers around the world to set up about 30+ monitoring stations that each uses an FTS instrument to look up at the sun and retrieve important greenhouse gases. These data offer a gold-standard of calibration for a number of satellite-remote-sensing datasets in NASA's Earth Observing System. I have most familiarity with the Orbiting Carbon Observatory (OCO) datasets, and I been on the OCO Science Team since 2010. The nominator's responsibilities on the Science Team are to chacterize the statistical distribution of atmospheric carbon dioxide viewed through OCO's camera and retrieved using a Levenberg-Marquardt retrieval algorithm. Carbon dioxide is a leading greenhouse gas that must be monitored for the planet to have a chance to self-regulate. The retrievals listed as 'target mode' are matched with the TCCON target, and a statistical regression gives the all-important coefficients that allow the OCO data to be calibrated. \n\nNominator 7:\nThe Total Carbon Column Observing Network (TCCON) is a valuable data source for validating satellite-based measurements of column-averaged atmospheric carbon dioxide (XCO2) and other species. This global network of ground-based Fourier Transform Spectrometers provides highly precise and accurate measurements of greenhouse gases over diverse locations worldwide. The TCCON dataset's global coverage spans local, urban, and coastal regions, enabling comprehensive validation of satellite observations from local to regional scales.\n\nNot having the TCCON dataset would introduce significant challenges in providing accurate, ground-truth XCO2 measurements, which are necessary to verify and calibrate satellite data. Satellite instruments heavily rely on TCCON as a \"gold standard\" ground-based reference. The TCCON dataset's high precision and well-maintained calibration support robust validation and error quantification of satellite observations, in addition to its coverage of complex areas where satellite retrievals may face greater uncertainty. TCCON supports improved understanding of the carbon cycle and enhances confidence in satellite climate data products by bridging global satellite sensing with accurate ground-level measurements.\n\nNominator 8:\nTCCON data are critical for monitoring the health of the planet. They provide information about diurnal, synoptic, seasonal, interannual, and multidecadal variations in important greenhouse gases. TCCON data are a unique source of information about the diurnal cycle in greenhouse gas processes, especially CO2 uptake and release by the biosphere and by anthropogenic sources. Because the observations measure the \"total column\" and are not confined to the boundary layer, the diurnal variations in TCCON data is not complicated by diurnal patterns of boundary layer mixing. Thus, data have shown that diurnal patterns in TCCON data can both be used to quantify local sources of fossil CO2 in urban areas and to constrain local biospheric carbon uptake or release across the diurnal cycle. No other method permits this type of local CO2 analysis at diurnal scales. These data also underpin greenhouse gas monitoring satellite missions, since they provide the most important validation source. Without TCCON data, NASA's OCO missions would not have been able to meet their accuracy or precision goals, since comparison against TCCON has been used to identify sources of error and improve the space-based retrieval algorithm. \n\nNominator 9:\nInitially established for the validation of greenhouse gas (GHG) measurements from satellite instruments such as the Orbiting Carbon Observatory (OCO) and the Greenhouse Gases Observing Satellite (GOSAT), the TCCON data have proved invaluable for validating a broad range of GHG observations, such as from OCO-2, OCO-3, GOSAT-2, the Chinese Carbon Dioxide Observation Satellite Mission (TanSat), and carbon monoxide (CO) measurements from the Measurement Of Pollution In The Troposphere (MOPITT) and the TROPOspheric Monitoring Instrument (TROPOMI). TCCON data are critical for validation of observations of gases such as CO2 and CH4 because small biases in the satellite observations can have deleterious impacts on the utility of the data, which are essential for understanding the implications for the climate system and the planet of the changing global carbon cycle due to anthropogenic and natural processes. \n\nAlthough the data have been invaluable for validation of the satellites, they are also playing a key role for the evaluation of inverse modelling analyses that employ the satellite data to quantify surface fluxes of GHGs. TCCON data are ideally suited for this task because the measurements are sensitive to variations in trace gases throughout the depth of lower atmosphere and thus, due to the influence of atmospheric transport, the measurements can have footprints that are larger than the grid box of the models. Consequently, there are no representativeness errors associated with comparison of the models with TCCON data. In contrast, with in situ surface observations, the representativeness errors can be quite large when comparing models with the in situ point measurements.\n\nTCCON data are also providing valuable complementary information to the satellite observations of GHGs. Space-based GHG observations are mainly from low Earth orbiting satellites which provide global observational coverage, but only one, or at most two, observations per day at a given location. TCCON, in contrast, gives almost continuous observational coverage, weather permitting. Integrating the temporal information from the TCCON data with the spatial information of the satellite measurements enhances our ability to constrain highly variable processes, such as wildfires, which has implications for societal prosperity and people. For example, inverse model estimates of CO emissions from the unprecedented 2023 Canadian wildfires suggested that the TCCON data, when combined with observations from TROPOMI, gave more constraints on the day-to-day variability of the emissions than can be obtained from only the satellite data. TCCON also provides coincident measurements of other combustion-related gases such as formaldehyde (H2CO) and hydrogen cyanide (HCN), which when combined with CO, CO2, and CH4 can offer process-based information on the fires. Given the increasing severity of wildfires, TCCON data will continue to be a valuable dataset for understanding the impact of these fires."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1: \nThe nominator is one of the primary developers for the algorithm that is used to generate the level 2 column data as well as a significant part of the data pipeline that delivers the data to public users. They have spent the last ~7 years of their career working to first deliver the current product with accurate and precise ties to the WMO metrological scales, then to continue to refine the precision of the data to meet the needs of the next generation of GHG observing satellites. As part of that, they regularly answer user questions about the data.\n\nNominator 2:\nThe nominator's team uses the data primarily to validate the satellite measuremenst of XCO2, XCH4 and XCO.\n\nNominator 3:\nPrior to their retirement from the NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, the nominator served as the the Principal Investigator of the NASA Orbiting Carbon Observatory (OCO) and the Science Team Lead for NASA’s OCO-2 and OCO-3 missions. TCCON was an integral part of these missions and served a critical role in their data validation strategies. It also provided the primary method for cross-validating the OCO-2 and OCO-3 products with the GHG products collected by Japan’s Greenhouse Gas Observing SATellite missions, GOSAT and GOSAT-2. \n\nThey also served as the Greenhouse Gas lead for Committee on Earth Observations (CEOS) Atmospheric Composition-Virtual Constellation (AC-VC). In this context, TCCON became the primary standard for cross-validating GHG results from all CEOS GHG missions against the relevant World Meteorological Organization (WMO) in situ standards for CO2 and CH4. This validation approach contributed to rapid improvements in the quality of the data products returned by all of these space-based GHG sensors and accelerated their acceptance by the carbon cycle science and policy communities.\n\nNominator 4:\nThe nomiantor's personal use of TCCON data has been in evaluating the performance of atmospheric carbon dioxide and carbon monoxide inversion analyses to estimate regional-scale sources and sinks. While emissions are optimized using satellite-based retrievals, the TCCON dataset provides an independent set of observations to compare against simulated posterior concentrations. This independent validation is crucial for confirming that emission estimates generalize beyond the data used directly in the inversion and adds an additional layer of confidence. Without TCCON, there would be few options for independent validation, particularly outside regions with dense in situ sampling.\n\nNominator 5:\nThe nominator is the current Chair of the TCCON, has previously served as Deputy Chair, and is an active member of the network. They are also the PI of the East Trout Lake TCCON station located in Saskatchewan, Canada. They have used the TCCON data to validate the Greenhouse Gases Observing Satellite and the Orbiting Carbon Observatory-2, and to perform studies on urban greenhouse gas emissions.\n\nNominator 6:\nThe nominator has published two peer-reviewed papers on how the calibration of OCO-2 data is carried out with valid uncertainty quantification. They have also been a co-architect of a Bayesian flux-inversion method called WOMBAT, which allows researchers and decision-makers to see where carbon dioxide is coming from or being absorbed at Earth's surface. TCCON is being used to match forward simulation of the fluxes, and their team has participated in two MIPs that use the TCCON data for this purpose. \n\nNominator 7:\nThe TCCON dataset has been very valuable throughout the nominator's postdoctoral research experience at the Jet Propulsion Laboratory, California Institute of Technology. They extensively utilized this ground-based dataset to validate satellite-based measurements of the dry-air mole fractions of carbon dioxide (XCO2) from the Orbiting Carbon Observatory-2 (OCO-2) and Orbiting Carbon Observatory-3 (OCO-3) missions. The TCCON dataset serves as the gold standard and primary validation for the OCO-2/3 missions, as well as for numerous other satellite instruments. Due to its exceptional precision, accuracy, and global coverage, the TCCON dataset served as the truth dataset in validating the v11.1 OCO-2 XCO2 data (ttps://doi.org/10.1029/2024EA003935). As an assistant research scientist, they plan to continue utilizing this invaluable dataset to validate not only satellite instrument data but also measurements from other upcoming ground-based measurement networks.\n\nNominator 8:\nAs a PI at the University of Michigan, the nominator's group regularly uses TCCON data to probe carbon-climate interactions. They have used TCCON data to identify the importance of mesoscale weather systems on CO2 variability, to quantify interannual variability in carbon cycling and link it to El Nino impacts in the tropics, to evaluate prognostic models such as the Community Earth System Model, and to examine diurnal variations.\n\nNominator 9:\nThe nominator’s research is focused on data assimilation and inverse modelling of emissions of environmentally important gases, with a particular focus on satellite observations of greenhouse gases. Previously, they used the TCCON data mainly to evaluate the inversion analyses, however, their group is also integrating the TCCON data with satellite observations in their inversion studies to exploit their complementarity with the satellite observations. They are also using the TCCON data to evaluate emission factors in bottom-up wildfire emission inventories."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Laughner, J. L., Toon, G. C., Mendonca, J., Petri, C., Roche, S., Wunch, D., Blavier, J.-F., Griffith, D. W. T., Heikkinen, P., Keeling, R. F., Kiel, M., Kivi, R., Roehl, C. M., Stephens, B. B., Baier, B. C., Chen, H., Choi, Y., Deutscher, N. M., DiGangi, J. P., … Wennberg, P. O. (2024). The total carbon column observing network’s ggg2020 data version. Earth System Science Data, 16(5), 2197–2260. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-16-2197-2024 \n\nWunch, D., et al. (2011). The total carbon column observing network. Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A: Mathematical, Physical and Engineering Sciences, 369(1943), 2087–2112. https://doi.org/10.1098/rsta.2010.0240 \n\nZammit-Mangion, A., Bertolacci, M., Fisher, J., Stavert, A., Rigby, M., Cao, Y., & Cressie, N. (2022). WOMBAT v1.0: A fully Bayesian global flux-inversion framework. Geoscientific Model Development, 15(1), 45–73. https://doi.org/10.5194/gmd-15-45-2022"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "https://tccon-wiki.caltech.edu/Main/Publications \n\nNominator 4:\npersonal use: https://essd.copernicus.org/articles/15/963/2023/. Full bibliography for the dataset: https://tccon-wiki.caltech.edu/Main/Publications\n\nNominator 6: \nThe TCCON dataset was an essential component of contribution to the global stocktake mandated by the 2015 Paris Agreement: \nByrne, B. et al. (with 60 co-authors including Cressie, N.). (2023). National CO2 budgets (2015-2020) inferred from atmospheric CO2 observations in support of the global stocktake. Earth System Science Data, 15, 963-1004 (doi:10.5194/essd-15-963-2023).\n\nNominator 7: \nhttps://tccon-wiki.caltech.edu/Main/Publications\n\nNominator 8:\nKeppel-Aleks, G., Randerson, J. T., Lindsay, K., Stephens, B. B., Keith Moore, J., Doney, S. C., et al. (2013). Atmospheric Carbon Dioxide Variability in the Community Earth System Model: Evaluation and Transient Dynamics during the Twentieth and Twenty-First Centuries. J. Climate, 26(13), 4447–4475. https://doi.org/10.1175/JCLI-D-12-00589.1\n \nKeppel-Aleks, G., Wennberg, P. O., Washenfelder, R. A., Wunch, D., Schneider, T., Toon, G. C., et al. (2012). The imprint of surface fluxes and transport on variations in total column carbon dioxide. Biogeosciences, 9(3), 875–891. Retrieved from http://www.biogeosciences.net/9/875/2012/\n \nWunch, D., Wennberg, P. O., Toon, G. C., Keppel-Aleks, G., & Yavin, Y. G. (2009). Emissions of greenhouse gases from a North American megacity. Geophys. Res. Lett., 36, L15810. https://doi.org/10.1029/2009GL039825\n \nTorres, A. D., Doney, S. C., Fendrock, M., Luis, K., & Keppel-Aleks, G. (n.d.). The imprint of mesoscale transport systems on X_(CO2) from OCO-2 satellite data. J. Geophys. Res.-Atmos.\n\nGuan, Y., Keppel-Aleks, G., Doney, S. C., Petri, C., Pollard, D., Wunch, D., et al. (2023). Characteristics of interannual variability in space-based XCO2 global observations. Acp, 23(9), 5355–5372. https://doi.org/10.5194/acp-23-5355-2023"
        },
        {
