    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:008",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:008",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:008",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4847-3440",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6192-3157",
        "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-4476-044X",
        "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-4204-1673",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3858-8308",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0698-0658"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "There are many reasons why IMERG is uniquely useful and impactful. \nFirst and foremost it is because it is a global dataset describing with high temporal and spatial resolution precipitation. Precipitation is a critical component of the water cycle, and it has a direct connection to society, the environment and natural hazards. The urgency to make progress in this field is evident as the availability and access to freshwater is at risk in many parts of the world, and floods and droughts become more frequent and more severe. Accordingly, the United Nations (UN) has declared an “International Decade for Action on Water for Sustainable Development” (2018-2028; https://www.un.org/en/events/waterdecade). The facts and figures are frightening and demonstrate a clear need to better understand, document and predict the hydrological cycle including the precipitation component:\n• 2.1 billion people lack access to safely managed drinking water services.\n• Water scarcity already affects four out of every 10 people.\n• 90% of all natural disasters are water-related.\nA non-exhaustive list of the unique characteristics of IMERG that make it invaluable follows:\n• Precipitation varies on high temporal and spatial scales. Thus, the 30 minute and 0.1° resolution of IMERG is a real game-changer in terms of representation and utility.\n• GLOBAL coverage. There are precipitation data sets for parts of the world that have equivalent resolution but no observationally-derived dataset has equivalent fidelity and/or resolution.\n• Long period of data availability: 1998- near real-time.\n• IMERG has been the focus of sustained product development and improvement based on feedback from the community and truly innovative data processing (e.g. introduction of Quality Index and Probability of Liquid Precipitation).\n• IMERG has 3 products with varying latency to accommodate the needs of different user communities all can be seamlessly accessed via https://disc.gsfc.nasa.gov/datasets?keywords=IMERG&page=1 \n• All data are freely available and provided in multiple formats. Critically for ‘time-to-science’ sub-setting is available by parameter and location and aggregation is also supported. So users can get data volumes that they can handle and that are appropriate to their information need.\n• IMERG is backed by outstanding user support and is extremely well-documented.\nCitations alone do indeed not per se demonstrate impact, but a simple google search for the terms IMERG and precipitation yields 13,000 hits. Importantly results from that search also indicates is that IMERG has enable research on hydroclimate issues in virtually every country in the world. Further IMERG has been employed for an incredibly diverse range of topics including; (1) advancing fundamental climate science (how is the climate evolving?), (2) advancing process-level understanding of land-atmosphere feedbacks and hydroclimatic responses, (3) development of flash-flood warning systems to enhance resilience and many more.The NASA supported IMERG dataset web-interface plus the clear documentation means it is also invaluable as an educational tool. As one example, students in my Meteorological Observations and Instruments course use IMERG to learn how to access, download and manipulate large data sets in the context of studying extreme precipitation events."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "IMERG is a multi-sensor precipitation data of global coverage at high frequency (hourly) and resolution (km scale). It represents a crowning jewel achievement of decades of satellite based precipitation estimation as a community (that culminated in the Global Precipitation Mission) with a convergence and confluence of tested methods to yield a usable precipitation product that is also accessible for research and societal applications. As a user and researcher of water resources to solve water-related problems, IMERG more than a product. It has facilitated numerous stories of hope for farmers from the Indus to the Bengal delta and farther (to the Mekong). It has allowed efficient irrigation for these farmers, efficient monitoring of reservoirs for many countries for better planning and reservoir operations strategy"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "3",
          "schema:text": "The IMERG dataset has fundamentally changed how we understand and are able to respond to precipitation events around the world. It feels critical to identify gaps in regions where other satellite-derived data, and especially surface weather observations, are sparse. \n\nWhat makes IMERG so impactful isn't just that it provides precipitation data - it's that it provides globally consistent, high-quality precipitation data to places and applications that never had it before. IMERG can be used for global-scale applications and is especially useful over regions with sparse or no reliable surface observations. The fine spatial and temporal resolution of IMERG data allows them to be accumulated to the scale of the application for increased skill.\n\nIt is well recognized that long-term precipitation measurements are necessary for understanding and monitoring regional precipitation characteristics. This includes characteristics crucial for monitoring water resources and hazards, like droughts and flood events. IMERG provides this monitoring capability globally, consistently, and continuously - something that simply wasn't possible before.\n\nDuring humanitarian crises, understanding rainfall patterns can mean the difference between life and death. Refugee movements often follow water availability. Disease outbreaks are influenced by precipitation patterns. Food security crises develop over months as droughts intensify.\n\nWithout IMERG's detailed precipitation monitoring, agricultural planning would rely on sparse, inconsistent data. Farmers in data-poor regions would face increased crop failures, food security would deteriorate, and agricultural insurance systems would lack the precise risk assessment data needed to operate effectively.\n\nMany critical ecosystems exist in regions with sparse ground-based monitoring. Without IMERG, conservation efforts would operate without understanding precipitation-driven ecological changes, making it impossible to track habitat degradation, predict species migration patterns, or design effective protected area management strategies.\n\nIn addition to the near-real-time data availability for monitoring, historical data availability of IMERG is also of great importance. It is critical to have consistent historical data for comparison to typical and long-term statistics - IMERG does just that."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "4",
          "schema:text": "The IMERG dataset is a highly valuable tool for environmental monitoring, hydraulic design of infrastructure, and disaster risk management.\nReliable precipitation data is important for developing meteorological, hydrological, and water management applications. Being able to estimate rainfall with enough accuracy also helps to describe and anticipate physical processes at different spatial scales, such as floods, landslides, soil erosion, and severe storms.\nRain gauge measurements, however, are still limited in number and unevenly distributed around the world. Satellite-based precipitation products offer a practical alternative. One advantage of satellite estimates is that they can be used in areas without rain gauges or weather radar coverage. They also help improve rainfall information in regions where gauges are available. In addition, they provide relatively consistent historical data with daily or sub-daily resolution.\nOn February 27, 2014, the Global Precipitation Measurement (GPM) mission was launched by NASA and JAXA. It was designed to estimate rainfall and snowfall globally, addressing some of the limitations of earlier missions. The main GPM product, called IMERG (Integrated Multi-satellitE Retrievals for GPM), combines observations from multiple satellite sensors.\nIMERG is available in three versions: Early, Late, and Final Run. Each version reflects a different stage of data processing and has a different delay between observation and availability, known as latency. The Early Run is available after about 2 hours, the Late Run after 14 hours, and the Final Run after more than 3 months. Processing quality improves from Early to Final.\nLatency and temporal resolution are two factors to consider when choosing which version to use. For example, when using precipitation as input for hydrological models in infrastructure design, where the analysis is done once (a one-time task) and not in real time, the Final Run is usually more suitable. In these cases, having a longer and more consistent dataset is more relevant than receiving the data quickly. Long records of short time step precipitation are needed for statistical analysis, such as estimating rainfall intensities for different durations and return periods.\nThe IMERG dataset covers more than 20 years, which is long enough for designing systems such as urban drainage networks (where design return periods are usually less than 20 years). The Final Run is also recommended for research purposes, where the focus is on accuracy rather than timing.\nOn the other hand, in operational contexts such as real-time flood or landslide monitoring, latency becomes more important. In these cases, precipitation data must be available soon after it is observed, within a time frame that fits the modeling step and the response time of the basins involved.\nThe IMERG dataset supports a range of applications that align with broader societal and environmental goals. It contributes to People by improving training, education, and preparedness for natural hazards through more accessible and timely precipitation data. It enhances our understanding of Planet by providing consistent information on rainfall patterns, which is useful for studying climate, environmental indicators, and geophysical processes. Finally, it supports Prosperity by helping to reduce the impact of extreme weather on vulnerable communities, informing risk management strategies, and supporting efforts in humanitarian response and infrastructure planning."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "5",
          "schema:text": "Precipitation is the Earth science variable most requested by users. IMERG is the dataset of choice for many users as it meets their needs for resolution and latency, complete coverage of the globe, and a long record. Furthermore, unlike variables such as temperature, precipitation is a challenge for numerical models and reanalyses, making observation-based products the preferred source for experts. These attributes allow IMERG users to focus on applying the data, accelerating progress in research and applications. \n\nA key success for IMERG is improving our scientific knowledge of precipitation systems, water cycle variability, global freshwater availability, and water-related hazard impacts. A few examples enabled by IMERG are:\n• new studies of the life cycles and climatology of Tropical mesoscale systems;\n• analyses of heavy/extreme precipitation in low and midlatitudes; and\n• new depictions of storm systems at higher latitudes.\nAs well, IMERG is used as a reference to evaluate numerical models and reanalyses.\n\nAbout 30% of users rely on near-real-time IMERG products for time-sensitive applications, which typically don’t appear in the refereed literature. A few include:\n• Operational Weather Analysis : Several agencies, including the United States Air Force (USAF), Environment and Climate Change Canada, and the Australian Bureau of Meteorology, use IMERG in data analysis and NWP validation. The USAF’s global land surface analysis is provided across the military, agricultural, and research sectors.\n• Water Resources, Agricultural Forecasting and Food Security: IMERG enables hydrological and land surface models to characterize land-atmosphere interactions, water resources, and agricultural output. Public and private organizations such as USDA and Nutrien Ag Solutions use IMERG Early to evaluate extreme precipitation to prepare for disruptions to the global supply chain. The PANI system provides irrigation advice to water resource managers and farmers in Pakistan using IMERG Late.\n• Disaster Response and Insurance\n1. IMERG’s user-friendly formats support decision-support systems for FEMA, the U.S. Army Geospatial Intelligence Unit, the National Geospatial Agency, the State Department, and private companies, among others. IMERG provides critical near-real-time input to the Landslide Hazard Assessment for Situational Awareness system (https://landslides.nasa.gov). One LHASA user is the World Food Programme. \n2. IMERG data are used by reinsurance companies, including MiCRO, Agvesto, and Swiss Re, to develop drought and rainfall indices. MiCRO alone has over 5.2 million beneficiaries across Guatemala, El Salvador, and Colombia. \n• User Support: We provide trainings, interviews, workshops, webinars, and educational outreach that introduce global precipitation in general and IMERG specifically. And, we answer questions, usually within 24 hours.\n\nSee https://gpm.nasa.gov/sites/default/files/gpm-highlights/GPM IMERG Highlight Examples.pdf for selected application highlights."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "6",
          "schema:text": "While IMERG is certainly not the only satellite-derived precipitation product, it has become an invaluable flagship product for research and applications. This is due in part to the characteristics of the dataset: 30 minute, < 10km resolution, with real-time data delivery and a 25+ year retrospective record. It is also due to the data-rich approach to production, which incorporates multiple satellite sensors and ground data. The dataset is also very high quality, performing well in evaluations over many parts of the globe. Both the quality and the influence of the dataset are also a reflection of the team that has contributed to its production and continued development. IMERG is a collaborative effort of researchers who have, in the past, developed their own precipitation products. IMERG benefits from the experience and diverse expertise of these contributors.\n\nAs the flagship product of the Global Precipitation Measurement Mission, IMERG enables a vast array of research, education, and application activities that would not be nearly as powerful in the absence of the product. This includes monitoring tropical cyclones and other extreme weather events, contributing to forecasts, disaster response, and model evaluation,. It also includes studies of climate variability and trends in remote parts of the world for which reliable in situ precipitation observations are not available. This makes IMERG a critical input for models of crops, ecology, water resources, disease, and other natural and human systems. In an era of global change, IMERG's retrospective record has also provided an invaluable tool for characterizing and understanding variability and trends in precipitation and in the systems impacted by precipitation."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "Quantifying intensification/suppression of precipitation over urban areas relative to their rural surroundings can inform efforts to reduce urban flooding. Our research sought to systematically address whether urban areas exhibit a higher/lower probability of precipitation and/or higher/lower annual total precipitation and/or intensification/weakening of intense precipitation events relative to nearby rural areas across a range of hydroclimate conditions and 47 conurbations across North America during 2005-2022.\nImportantly for our research, the IMERG algorithm employs satellite-borne remote sensor channels for retrievals over land that are “surface-blind”. They detect solid hydrometeors in the upper portions of clouds (passive microwave) or cloud tops (infrared) rather than surface details such as landcover. Thus, there is no reason to expect the fidelity of IMERG V07 precipitation rates to vary between urban and rural locations. The Final Run additionally includes scaling to the Global Precipitation Climatology Centre monthly surface gauge reports which enhances fidelity. IMERG V07 is uniquely skillful relative to other products and revisions to the IMERG algorithm consistently reduced biases, particularly for extreme events. Further the high temporal resolution was essential.\nPryor et al. (2025): Re-examining urban rainfall enhancement over North America. Environmental Research Letters 20 034035 doi: 10.1088/1748-9326/adb507"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "The nominator’s team uses IMERG regularly for many agencies around the world to help them generate advisories on how to grow more food with less water, on tracking reservoirs for better water sharing and management, for improving operations of dams to create more eco friendly habitat for fish. Because IMERG is global at high frequency and spatial resolution, the precipitation data from satellites can now be used confidently at operational timescales for decision making while also learn from past multi-decadal patterns for developing useful data-driven policy for water management"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "3",
          "schema:text": "The nominator's team at Adiabat ingests IMERG data multiple times per day. This dataset is one of their primary sources for global precipitation estimates for a variety of research, development, and solution projects. Their back-end processes automate the data collection, analysis, and delivery of IMERG for a variety of internal and client-based projects, ranging from verification and validation of overseas precipitation modeling and fueling global flood, drought, and snow analysis and forecasting. This interaction then leads to precipitation statistics, data visualization, and inclusion of IMERG data in various climate and weather reports."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "4",
          "schema:text": "The nominator uses the IMERG Late Run product with daily precipitation accumulations primarily to calculate the Antecedent Precipitation Index (API), a gridded proxy for root-zone soil moisture. Weekly API percentiles enable regional monitoring of hydrological conditions across southern South America.\nSome of IMERG’s most useful features include its user-friendly format (The nominator works with the GIS version, in GeoTIFF format) and its long historical record, spanning over 20 years, which supports robust statistical analyses. Its spatial resolution and relatively low latency also make it well-suited for near-real-time monitoring and early warning applications.\nAnother project I’m developing is a shallow landslide risk estimation tool that uses the API as a proxy for soil moisture to assess landslide potential in areas that are not classified as \"unconditionally stable\" or \"unconditionally unstable\", that is, areas where slope and soil characteristics alone don’t determine stability, but where soil moisture conditions also play a decisive role."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "5",
          "schema:text": "The nominator leads the development team for IMERG. Their team develop concepts, build and test the code, work with the processing and archive sites, and support users across the science and applications communities. IMERG embodies numerous lessons learned about user needs (including from a predecessor dataset), such as providing global coverage at relatively fine scales (1/2 hour, 0.1° lat./long.) for as long a period as feasible (1998-present), using nearby-in-time satellite data to improve estimates, and computing the dataset three times at increasing latencies (with progressively more data) to support short-fuse, next-day, and research users. Critically, each Run is computed for the entire period of record to provide a complete climatology specific to its attributes. Subsequently, we have added parameters that users requested: a Quality Index and a Probability of Liquid Precipitation. Starting from the native HDF5 files of half-hourly data, we have worked with the archive sites to add multiple accumulation periods, several file formats, and subsetting by parameter and location to accommodate user requests to simplify their own computational burden. We publish an Algorithm Theoretical Basis Document and a detailed Technical Document, as well as Release Notes for each version upgrade. The team answers numerous user queries, which come to us by: personal interaction, such as at conferences; e-mail; the “contact us” hot link on the GPM web site; and referrals from the PPS and GES DISC sites."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "6",
          "schema:text": "The nominator uses IMERG primarily as an input for models of climate impacts. This includes land surface models used for hydrological monitoring and forecasting in regions around the world, as well as statistical and process-based crop models, and models of infectious disease risk (dengue, malaria, and cholera). IMERG is also used to evaluate the performance of regional climate models and as a tool for diagnostic studies of extreme climate events like flash droughts."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:008",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 9,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Atmospheric Science, Space Weather"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "Integrated Multi-satellitE Retrievals for the Global Precipitation Measurement (GPM) mission (IMERG)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "George J. Huffman, David T. Bolvin, Robert Joyce, Eric J. Nelkin, Erich F. Stocker, Jackson Tan"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "This nomination is for the suite of IMERG products (currently in Version 07). As such, the *native format* datasets are: Early Run half-hourly doi:10.5067/GPM/IMERG/3B-HH-E/07; Late Run half-hourly doi:10.5067/GPM/IMERG/3B-HH-L/07; Final Run half-hourly doi:10.5067/GPM/IMERG/3B-HH/07; Final Run monthly doi:10.5067/GPM/IMERG/3B-MONTH/07"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "Goddard Earth Science Data and Information Services Center (GES DISC)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "https://disc.gsfc.nasa.gov/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Andrey K. Savtchenko (andrey.k.savtchenko@nasa.gov), Zhong Liu (zhong.liu-1@nasa.gov) , Jacob R. Schaperow <jacob.r.schaperow@nasa.gov> and Brandon O. Mitchell <brandon.o.mitchell@nasa.gov>"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "See https://disc.gsfc.nasa.gov/datasets/GPM_3IMERGHH_07/summary?keywords=IMERG\n\nIMERG is NASA’s open-source observational global precipitation product. It is the unified U.S. GPM algorithm that provides precipitation products at half-hour 0.1°x0.1° globally for 1998-present. The estimates from the available precipitation-relevant satellite passive microwave (PMW) sensors are computed, intercalibrated, and merged. Merged geo-IR estimates are computed, intercalibrated to the PMW, and input to a Morphing-Kalman Filter along with the PMW. Motion vectors for the quasi-Lagrangian PMW morphing are computed from numerical reanalysis (Final) and numerical forecast (Early and Late) data fields. \n\nThe IMERG system is run twice in near-real time:\n• \"Early\" multi-satellite product ~4 hr after observation (forward morphing only) and\n• \"Late\" multi-satellite product ~14 hr after observation (forward and backward morphing),\nand once after real time:\n• \"Final\", satellite-gauge product ~4 months after the observation month, (forward and backward morphing, monthly gauge analyses).\n\nEach new version of IMERG reprocesses the entire 1998–present record for all three Runs to ensure consistency regardless of which Run a user employs. Moreover, NASA produces value-added products that answer user needs, such as daily accumulations, GeoTIFF formats, and on-demand parameter and geographical subsetting. Data fields include the complete precipitation field, Quality Index, and Probability of Liquid Precipitation."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nThere are many reasons why IMERG is uniquely useful and impactful. \nFirst and foremost it is because it is a global dataset describing with high temporal and spatial resolution precipitation. Precipitation is a critical component of the water cycle, and it has a direct connection to society, the environment and natural hazards. The urgency to make progress in this field is evident as the availability and access to freshwater is at risk in many parts of the world, and floods and droughts become more frequent and more severe. Accordingly, the United Nations (UN) has declared an “International Decade for Action on Water for Sustainable Development” (2018-2028; https://www.un.org/en/events/waterdecade). The facts and figures are frightening and demonstrate a clear need to better understand, document and predict the hydrological cycle including the precipitation component:\n• 2.1 billion people lack access to safely managed drinking water services.\n• Water scarcity already affects four out of every 10 people.\n• 90% of all natural disasters are water-related.\nA non-exhaustive list of the unique characteristics of IMERG that make it invaluable follows:\n• Precipitation varies on high temporal and spatial scales. Thus, the 30 minute and 0.1° resolution of IMERG is a real game-changer in terms of representation and utility.\n• GLOBAL coverage. There are precipitation data sets for parts of the world that have equivalent resolution but no observationally-derived dataset has equivalent fidelity and/or resolution.\n• Long period of data availability: 1998- near real-time.\n• IMERG has been the focus of sustained product development and improvement based on feedback from the community and truly innovative data processing (e.g. introduction of Quality Index and Probability of Liquid Precipitation).\n• IMERG has 3 products with varying latency to accommodate the needs of different user communities all can be seamlessly accessed via https://disc.gsfc.nasa.gov/datasets?keywords=IMERG&page=1 \n• All data are freely available and provided in multiple formats. Critically for ‘time-to-science’ sub-setting is available by parameter and location and aggregation is also supported. So users can get data volumes that they can handle and that are appropriate to their information need.\n• IMERG is backed by outstanding user support and is extremely well-documented.\nCitations alone do indeed not per se demonstrate impact, but a simple google search for the terms IMERG and precipitation yields 13,000 hits. Importantly results from that search also indicates is that IMERG has enable research on hydroclimate issues in virtually every country in the world. Further IMERG has been employed for an incredibly diverse range of topics including; (1) advancing fundamental climate science (how is the climate evolving?), (2) advancing process-level understanding of land-atmosphere feedbacks and hydroclimatic responses, (3) development of flash-flood warning systems to enhance resilience and many more.The NASA supported IMERG dataset web-interface plus the clear documentation means it is also invaluable as an educational tool. As one example, students in my Meteorological Observations and Instruments course use IMERG to learn how to access, download and manipulate large data sets in the context of studying extreme precipitation events.\n\nNominator 2:\nIMERG is a multi-sensor precipitation data of global coverage at high frequency (hourly) and resolution (km scale). It represents a crowning jewel achievement of decades of satellite based precipitation estimation as a community (that culminated in the Global Precipitation Mission) with a convergence and confluence of tested methods to yield a usable precipitation product that is also accessible for research and societal applications. As a user and researcher of water resources to solve water-related problems, IMERG more than a product. It has facilitated numerous stories of hope for farmers from the Indus to the Bengal delta and farther (to the Mekong). It has allowed efficient irrigation for these farmers, efficient monitoring of reservoirs for many countries for better planning and reservoir operations strategy\n\nNominator 3:\nThe IMERG dataset has fundamentally changed how we understand and are able to respond to precipitation events around the world. It feels critical to identify gaps in regions where other satellite-derived data, and especially surface weather observations, are sparse. \n\nWhat makes IMERG so impactful isn't just that it provides precipitation data - it's that it provides globally consistent, high-quality precipitation data to places and applications that never had it before. IMERG can be used for global-scale applications and is especially useful over regions with sparse or no reliable surface observations. The fine spatial and temporal resolution of IMERG data allows them to be accumulated to the scale of the application for increased skill.\n\nIt is well recognized that long-term precipitation measurements are necessary for understanding and monitoring regional precipitation characteristics. This includes characteristics crucial for monitoring water resources and hazards, like droughts and flood events. IMERG provides this monitoring capability globally, consistently, and continuously - something that simply wasn't possible before.\n\nDuring humanitarian crises, understanding rainfall patterns can mean the difference between life and death. Refugee movements often follow water availability. Disease outbreaks are influenced by precipitation patterns. Food security crises develop over months as droughts intensify.\n\nWithout IMERG's detailed precipitation monitoring, agricultural planning would rely on sparse, inconsistent data. Farmers in data-poor regions would face increased crop failures, food security would deteriorate, and agricultural insurance systems would lack the precise risk assessment data needed to operate effectively.\n\nMany critical ecosystems exist in regions with sparse ground-based monitoring. Without IMERG, conservation efforts would operate without understanding precipitation-driven ecological changes, making it impossible to track habitat degradation, predict species migration patterns, or design effective protected area management strategies.\n\nIn addition to the near-real-time data availability for monitoring, historical data availability of IMERG is also of great importance. It is critical to have consistent historical data for comparison to typical and long-term statistics - IMERG does just that. \n\nNominator 4:\nThe IMERG dataset is a highly valuable tool for environmental monitoring, hydraulic design of infrastructure, and disaster risk management.\nReliable precipitation data is important for developing meteorological, hydrological, and water management applications. Being able to estimate rainfall with enough accuracy also helps to describe and anticipate physical processes at different spatial scales, such as floods, landslides, soil erosion, and severe storms.\nRain gauge measurements, however, are still limited in number and unevenly distributed around the world. Satellite-based precipitation products offer a practical alternative. One advantage of satellite estimates is that they can be used in areas without rain gauges or weather radar coverage. They also help improve rainfall information in regions where gauges are available. In addition, they provide relatively consistent historical data with daily or sub-daily resolution.\nOn February 27, 2014, the Global Precipitation Measurement (GPM) mission was launched by NASA and JAXA. It was designed to estimate rainfall and snowfall globally, addressing some of the limitations of earlier missions. The main GPM product, called IMERG (Integrated Multi-satellitE Retrievals for GPM), combines observations from multiple satellite sensors.\nIMERG is available in three versions: Early, Late, and Final Run. Each version reflects a different stage of data processing and has a different delay between observation and availability, known as latency. The Early Run is available after about 2 hours, the Late Run after 14 hours, and the Final Run after more than 3 months. Processing quality improves from Early to Final.\nLatency and temporal resolution are two factors to consider when choosing which version to use. For example, when using precipitation as input for hydrological models in infrastructure design, where the analysis is done once (a one-time task) and not in real time, the Final Run is usually more suitable. In these cases, having a longer and more consistent dataset is more relevant than receiving the data quickly. Long records of short time step precipitation are needed for statistical analysis, such as estimating rainfall intensities for different durations and return periods.\nThe IMERG dataset covers more than 20 years, which is long enough for designing systems such as urban drainage networks (where design return periods are usually less than 20 years). The Final Run is also recommended for research purposes, where the focus is on accuracy rather than timing.\nOn the other hand, in operational contexts such as real-time flood or landslide monitoring, latency becomes more important. In these cases, precipitation data must be available soon after it is observed, within a time frame that fits the modeling step and the response time of the basins involved.\nThe IMERG dataset supports a range of applications that align with broader societal and environmental goals. It contributes to People by improving training, education, and preparedness for natural hazards through more accessible and timely precipitation data. It enhances our understanding of Planet by providing consistent information on rainfall patterns, which is useful for studying climate, environmental indicators, and geophysical processes. Finally, it supports Prosperity by helping to reduce the impact of extreme weather on vulnerable communities, informing risk management strategies, and supporting efforts in humanitarian response and infrastructure planning.\n\nNominator 5:\nPrecipitation is the Earth science variable most requested by users. IMERG is the dataset of choice for many users as it meets their needs for resolution and latency, complete coverage of the globe, and a long record. Furthermore, unlike variables such as temperature, precipitation is a challenge for numerical models and reanalyses, making observation-based products the preferred source for experts. These attributes allow IMERG users to focus on applying the data, accelerating progress in research and applications. \n\nA key success for IMERG is improving our scientific knowledge of precipitation systems, water cycle variability, global freshwater availability, and water-related hazard impacts. A few examples enabled by IMERG are:\n• new studies of the life cycles and climatology of Tropical mesoscale systems;\n• analyses of heavy/extreme precipitation in low and midlatitudes; and\n• new depictions of storm systems at higher latitudes.\nAs well, IMERG is used as a reference to evaluate numerical models and reanalyses.\n\nAbout 30% of users rely on near-real-time IMERG products for time-sensitive applications, which typically don’t appear in the refereed literature. A few include:\n• Operational Weather Analysis : Several agencies, including the United States Air Force (USAF), Environment and Climate Change Canada, and the Australian Bureau of Meteorology, use IMERG in data analysis and NWP validation. The USAF’s global land surface analysis is provided across the military, agricultural, and research sectors.\n• Water Resources, Agricultural Forecasting and Food Security: IMERG enables hydrological and land surface models to characterize land-atmosphere interactions, water resources, and agricultural output. Public and private organizations such as USDA and Nutrien Ag Solutions use IMERG Early to evaluate extreme precipitation to prepare for disruptions to the global supply chain. The PANI system provides irrigation advice to water resource managers and farmers in Pakistan using IMERG Late.\n• Disaster Response and Insurance\n1. IMERG’s user-friendly formats support decision-support systems for FEMA, the U.S. Army Geospatial Intelligence Unit, the National Geospatial Agency, the State Department, and private companies, among others. IMERG provides critical near-real-time input to the Landslide Hazard Assessment for Situational Awareness system (https://landslides.nasa.gov). One LHASA user is the World Food Programme. \n2. IMERG data are used by reinsurance companies, including MiCRO, Agvesto, and Swiss Re, to develop drought and rainfall indices. MiCRO alone has over 5.2 million beneficiaries across Guatemala, El Salvador, and Colombia. \n• User Support: We provide trainings, interviews, workshops, webinars, and educational outreach that introduce global precipitation in general and IMERG specifically. And, we answer questions, usually within 24 hours.\n\nSee https://gpm.nasa.gov/sites/default/files/gpm-highlights/GPM IMERG Highlight Examples.pdf for selected application highlights.\n\nNominator 6:\nWhile IMERG is certainly not the only satellite-derived precipitation product, it has become an invaluable flagship product for research and applications. This is due in part to the characteristics of the dataset: 30 minute, < 10km resolution, with real-time data delivery and a 25+ year retrospective record. It is also due to the data-rich approach to production, which incorporates multiple satellite sensors and ground data. The dataset is also very high quality, performing well in evaluations over many parts of the globe. Both the quality and the influence of the dataset are also a reflection of the team that has contributed to its production and continued development. IMERG is a collaborative effort of researchers who have, in the past, developed their own precipitation products. IMERG benefits from the experience and diverse expertise of these contributors.\n\nAs the flagship product of the Global Precipitation Measurement Mission, IMERG enables a vast array of research, education, and application activities that would not be nearly as powerful in the absence of the product. This includes monitoring tropical cyclones and other extreme weather events, contributing to forecasts, disaster response, and model evaluation,. It also includes studies of climate variability and trends in remote parts of the world for which reliable in situ precipitation observations are not available. This makes IMERG a critical input for models of crops, ecology, water resources, disease, and other natural and human systems. In an era of global change, IMERG's retrospective record has also provided an invaluable tool for characterizing and understanding variability and trends in precipitation and in the systems impacted by precipitation."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nQuantifying intensification/suppression of precipitation over urban areas relative to their rural surroundings can inform efforts to reduce urban flooding. Our research sought to systematically address whether urban areas exhibit a higher/lower probability of precipitation and/or higher/lower annual total precipitation and/or intensification/weakening of intense precipitation events relative to nearby rural areas across a range of hydroclimate conditions and 47 conurbations across North America during 2005-2022.\nImportantly for our research, the IMERG algorithm employs satellite-borne remote sensor channels for retrievals over land that are “surface-blind”. They detect solid hydrometeors in the upper portions of clouds (passive microwave) or cloud tops (infrared) rather than surface details such as landcover. Thus, there is no reason to expect the fidelity of IMERG V07 precipitation rates to vary between urban and rural locations. The Final Run additionally includes scaling to the Global Precipitation Climatology Centre monthly surface gauge reports which enhances fidelity. IMERG V07 is uniquely skillful relative to other products and revisions to the IMERG algorithm consistently reduced biases, particularly for extreme events. Further the high temporal resolution was essential.\nPryor et al. (2025): Re-examining urban rainfall enhancement over North America. Environmental Research Letters 20 034035 doi: 10.1088/1748-9326/adb507\n\nNominator 2:\nThe nominator’s team uses IMERG regularly for many agencies around the world to help them generate advisories on how to grow more food with less water, on tracking reservoirs for better water sharing and management, for improving operations of dams to create more eco friendly habitat for fish. Because IMERG is global at high frequency and spatial resolution, the precipitation data from satellites can now be used confidently at operational timescales for decision making while also learn from past multi-decadal patterns for developing useful data-driven policy for water management\n\nNominator 3:\nThe nominator's team at Adiabat ingests IMERG data multiple times per day. This dataset is one of their primary sources for global precipitation estimates for a variety of research, development, and solution projects. Their back-end processes automate the data collection, analysis, and delivery of IMERG for a variety of internal and client-based projects, ranging from verification and validation of overseas precipitation modeling and fueling global flood, drought, and snow analysis and forecasting. This interaction then leads to precipitation statistics, data visualization, and inclusion of IMERG data in various climate and weather reports. \n\nNominator 4:\nThe nominator uses the IMERG Late Run product with daily precipitation accumulations primarily to calculate the Antecedent Precipitation Index (API), a gridded proxy for root-zone soil moisture. Weekly API percentiles enable regional monitoring of hydrological conditions across southern South America.\nSome of IMERG’s most useful features include its user-friendly format (The nominator works with the GIS version, in GeoTIFF format) and its long historical record, spanning over 20 years, which supports robust statistical analyses. Its spatial resolution and relatively low latency also make it well-suited for near-real-time monitoring and early warning applications.\nAnother project I’m developing is a shallow landslide risk estimation tool that uses the API as a proxy for soil moisture to assess landslide potential in areas that are not classified as \"unconditionally stable\" or \"unconditionally unstable\", that is, areas where slope and soil characteristics alone don’t determine stability, but where soil moisture conditions also play a decisive role.\n\nNominator 5:\nThe nominator leads the development team for IMERG. Their team develop concepts, build and test the code, work with the processing and archive sites, and support users across the science and applications communities. IMERG embodies numerous lessons learned about user needs (including from a predecessor dataset), such as providing global coverage at relatively fine scales (1/2 hour, 0.1° lat./long.) for as long a period as feasible (1998-present), using nearby-in-time satellite data to improve estimates, and computing the dataset three times at increasing latencies (with progressively more data) to support short-fuse, next-day, and research users. Critically, each Run is computed for the entire period of record to provide a complete climatology specific to its attributes. Subsequently, we have added parameters that users requested: a Quality Index and a Probability of Liquid Precipitation. Starting from the native HDF5 files of half-hourly data, we have worked with the archive sites to add multiple accumulation periods, several file formats, and subsetting by parameter and location to accommodate user requests to simplify their own computational burden. We publish an Algorithm Theoretical Basis Document and a detailed Technical Document, as well as Release Notes for each version upgrade. The team answers numerous user queries, which come to us by: personal interaction, such as at conferences; e-mail; the “contact us” hot link on the GPM web site; and referrals from the PPS and GES DISC sites. \n\nNominator 6:\nThe nominator uses IMERG primarily as an input for models of climate impacts. This includes land surface models used for hydrological monitoring and forecasting in regions around the world, as well as statistical and process-based crop models, and models of infectious disease risk (dengue, malaria, and cholera). IMERG is also used to evaluate the performance of regional climate models and as a tool for diagnostic studies of extreme climate events like flash droughts."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Huffman, G. J., Bolvin, D. T., Braithwaite, D., Hsu, K.-L., Joyce, R. J., Kidd, C., Nelkin, E. J., Sorooshian, S., Stocker, E. F., Tan, J., Wolff, D. B., & Xie, P. (2020). Integrated multi-satellite retrievals for the global precipitation measurement (Gpm) mission(Imerg). In V. Levizzani, C. Kidd, D. B. Kirschbaum, C. D. Kummerow, K. Nakamura, & F. J. Turk (Eds.), Satellite Precipitation Measurement (Vol. 67, pp. 343–353). Springer International Publishing. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-24568-9_19 [Using Version 06, but still generally applicable.]\n\nHuffman, G.J., D.T. Bolvin, R. Joyce, E.J. Nelkin, J. Tan, D. Braithwaite, K. Hsu, O.A. Kelley, P. Nguyen, S. Sorooshian, D.C. Watters, B.J. West, P. Xie (2023). Algorithm Theoretical Basis Document (ATBD) NASA Global Precipitation Measurement (GPM) Integrated Multi-satellitE Retrievals for GPM (IMERG) Version 07. PPS, last updated 12 July 2023. 52 pp. https://gpm.nasa.gov/resources/documents/imerg-v07-atbd \n\nPortier, A., Kirschbaum, D., Gebremichael, M., Kemp, E., Kumar, S., Llabres, I., Snodgrass, E., & Wegiel, J. (2023). Nasa’s global precipitation measurement mission: Leveraging stakeholder engagement & applications activities to inform decision-making. Remote Sensing Applications: Society and Environment, 29, 100853. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rsase.2022.100853"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nhttps://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/pp/prodview-frbkukcrtbxwk\nhttps://developers.google.com/earth-engine/datasets/catalog/NASA_GPM_L3_IMERG_V07\n\nNominator 2:\nYou can check our own systems that serve society on a very large scale with most of them relying on IMERG - https://depts.washington.edu/saswe\n\nNominator 5:\nSelected derived data products:\n• https://appliedsciences.nasa.gov/search?keys=GPM\n• https://science.nasa.gov/science-research/earth-science/gpm-celebrates-ten-years-of-observing-precipitation-for-science-and-society/\n• Portier, A., D.B. Kirschbaum, M. Gebremichael, E. Kemp, S. Kumar, I. Llabres, E. Snodgrass, J. Wegiel, 2023: NASA’s Global Precipitation Measurement Mission: Leveraging Stakeholder Engagement & Applications Activities to Inform Decision-making. Remote Sensing Applications: Society and Environment, 29, 100853. doi:10.1016/j.rsase.2022.100853.\n• Insurance: https://gpm.nasa.gov/applications/disasters/nasa-satellites-help-farmers-central-americas-dry-corridor\n• LHASA: Stanley, T.A., D.B. Kirschbaum, G. Benz, R.A. Emberson, P.M. Amatya, W. Medwedeff, M.K. Clark, 2021: Data-driven landslide nowcasting at the global scale. Frontiers in Earth Science, 9, 640043. doi: 10.3389/feart.2021.640043\n• PANI: https://paaniproject.org/\n• USAF (now using IMERG, vs. CMORPH at publication time for both):\n1. Eylander, J., S.Kumar, C. Peters-Lidard, T. Lewiston, C. Franks, J. Wegiel, 2022: History and Development of the USAF Agriculture Meteorology Modeling System and Resulting USAF–NASA Strategic Partnership. Weather and Forecasting, 37, 2293–2312. doi:10.1175/WAF-D-22-0064.1\n2. Kemp, E.Mm, J.W. Wegiel, S.V. Kumar, J.V. Geiger, D.M. Mocko, J.P. Jacob, C.D. Peters-Lidard, 2022: A NASA-Air Force Precipitation Analysis for Near-Real-Time Operations. Journal of Hydrometeorology, 23, 965-989. doi:10.1175/JHM-D-21-0228.1.\n• Water Resources and Agriculture: https://gpm.nasa.gov/applications/water/satellite-data-empowers-farmers\n• Weather and Climate: https://gpm.nasa.gov/applications/disasters/building-climate-resilience-satellite-data \n\nKnown secondary archives:\n• https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/pp/prodview-frbkukcrtbxwk\n• https://developers.google.com/earth-engine/datasets/catalog/NASA_GPM_L3_IMERG_V07\n• https://data.ucar.edu/nl/dataset/gpm-imerg-final-precipitation-l3-1-month-0-1-degree-x-0-1-degree-v07\n• https://data.ucar.edu/nl/dataset/gpm-imerg-final-precipitation-l3-half-hourly-0-1-degree-x-0-1-degree-v07\n\nNominator 6:\nThe IMERG reference publication has been cited 668 times (as of October 4, 2025). These citations describe a tremendous range of studies, from research to evaluate IMERG performance, to the use of IMERG as an input for other precipitation and climate monitoring products, to applications across the globe. It would be possible to assemble extensive bibliographies characterized by the different ways in which the studies have used the data. They are not going to attempt that right now, but a representative list could probably be assembled quite quickly."
        },
