    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:009",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:009",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:009",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3662-9439"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The Improved Italian - Rainfall Extreme Dataset (I²-RED) is a unique, high-quality dataset of annual maximum rainfall depths across Italy, covering more than a century of observations (1916 - present) from over 5000 gauges. Its impact spans scientific research, climate adaptation and policy making.\n\nPeople Dimension - I²-RED directly enhances the ability of scientists, educators, and practitioners to understand and communicate the characteristics of extreme rainfall in Italy. It enables robust analyses of frequency, intensity, and variability of extreme events, which are crucial for the design of infrastructure, flood risk management, and the development of reliable early-warning systems. Without I²-RED, access to such information would remain fragmented across regional authorities, with many series still locked in historical yearbooks. The dataset provides a basis for hydrological analyses in one of Europe’s most hazard-prone regions.\n\nPlanet Dimension - On the environmental side, I²-RED allows for detailed investigations into how extreme precipitation responds to climate variability and change. By offering a century-long record, it supports the detection of shifts in intensity, frequency, and seasonality of rainfall extremes, which in turn inform studies on flood hazards. Without I²-RED, it would be nearly impossible to evaluate these processes consistently at the national scale, and to link Italian case studies to broader Mediterranean and global climate research.\n\nProsperity Dimension - Reliable rainfall extremes are essential for the safe design of dams, levees, transportation networks, and urban drainage systems, directly influencing economic stability and public safety. The dataset also empowers local and national authorities to adopt evidence-based design rules and adaptation strategies, reducing future costs of flood damage and humanitarian relief.\n\nIn summary, without I²-RED, Italy and the broader scientific community would lack a coherent, quality-controlled foundation for studying and managing rainfall extremes. Its existence fills a long-standing data gap, transforms inaccessible paper archives into a shared resource, and provides an indispensable tool for advancing hydrological science."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator is the author of I²-RED, which was the focus of their very first research project during their PhD. At the time, they were motivated by the lack of a homogeneous and quality-controlled national archive of rainfall extremes in Italy, and they dedicated their doctoral work to building, validating, and documenting this dataset. Developing I²-RED was a formative experience: it required not only technical skills in data quality control, georeferencing, and consistency testing, but also extensive collaboration with regional agencies to access and integrate dispersed hydrological records.\nSince its creation, they have continued to work with I²-RED in a series of subsequent publications, applying it to the analysis of depth–duration–frequency relationships, the impact of orography on rainfall extremes, the evaluation of climate signals in extreme rainfall, and the validation of convection-permitting climate models."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:009",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 10,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Atmospheric Science, Space Weather"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "Improved Italian - Rainfall Extreme Dataset (I2-RED)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Paola Mazzoglio, Ilaria Butera, Pierluigi Claps"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4065266"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "Zenodo"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4065266"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Paola Mazzoglio"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "Obtaining hydrological information in Italy is challenging. After the dismantlement of the national hydrological service performed more than 30 years ago, data collection and management were transferred to 21 regional authorities. Each adopted different data policies, despite national law requiring public data to be openly available for research and non-profit purposes. As of 2025, some agencies still do not publish rainfall data online and release them only upon request, often in tables or PDFs. Moreover, most historical series remain available solely in printed Hydrological Yearbooks.\nThe Improved Italian - Rainfall Extreme Dataset (I²-RED) was developed to address this gap by providing a homogeneous, nationwide archive to support analyses of frequency, spatial distribution, and long-term evolution of extreme rainfall. It collects annual maximum rainfall depths for 1, 3, 6, 12, and 24 hours from more than 5000 gauges operating in Italy since 1916. Station coordinates and altitudes were checked against the EU-DEM elevation model, with large inconsistencies corrected. Internal consistency tests ensured logical duration relationships (e.g., 1h ≤ 3h ≤ 6h maxima), identified duplicates, and flagged series affected by undocumented relocations. Additional quality control targeted outliers and “super-extremes”, helping detect errors like misplaced decimal points.\nI²-RED is deposited on Zenodo with restricted access, while metadata and a descriptive paper are openly available. Legal agreements with regional agencies limit full access to the authors’ research group and to users already cleared by the relevant regional authorities."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The Improved Italian - Rainfall Extreme Dataset (I²-RED) is a unique, high-quality dataset of annual maximum rainfall depths across Italy, covering more than a century of observations (1916 - present) from over 5000 gauges. Its impact spans scientific research, climate adaptation and policy making.\n\nPeople Dimension - I²-RED directly enhances the ability of scientists, educators, and practitioners to understand and communicate the characteristics of extreme rainfall in Italy. It enables robust analyses of frequency, intensity, and variability of extreme events, which are crucial for the design of infrastructure, flood risk management, and the development of reliable early-warning systems. Without I²-RED, access to such information would remain fragmented across regional authorities, with many series still locked in historical yearbooks. The dataset provides a basis for hydrological analyses in one of Europe’s most hazard-prone regions.\n\nPlanet Dimension - On the environmental side, I²-RED allows for detailed investigations into how extreme precipitation responds to climate variability and change. By offering a century-long record, it supports the detection of shifts in intensity, frequency, and seasonality of rainfall extremes, which in turn inform studies on flood hazards. Without I²-RED, it would be nearly impossible to evaluate these processes consistently at the national scale, and to link Italian case studies to broader Mediterranean and global climate research.\n\nProsperity Dimension - Reliable rainfall extremes are essential for the safe design of dams, levees, transportation networks, and urban drainage systems, directly influencing economic stability and public safety. The dataset also empowers local and national authorities to adopt evidence-based design rules and adaptation strategies, reducing future costs of flood damage and humanitarian relief.\n\nIn summary, without I²-RED, Italy and the broader scientific community would lack a coherent, quality-controlled foundation for studying and managing rainfall extremes. Its existence fills a long-standing data gap, transforms inaccessible paper archives into a shared resource, and provides an indispensable tool for advancing hydrological science."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator is the author of I²-RED, which was the focus of their very first research project during their PhD. At the time, they were motivated by the lack of a homogeneous and quality-controlled national archive of rainfall extremes in Italy, and they dedicated their doctoral work to building, validating, and documenting this dataset. Developing I²-RED was a formative experience: it required not only technical skills in data quality control, georeferencing, and consistency testing, but also extensive collaboration with regional agencies to access and integrate dispersed hydrological records.\nSince its creation, they have continued to work with I²-RED in a series of subsequent publications, applying it to the analysis of depth–duration–frequency relationships, the impact of orography on rainfall extremes, the evaluation of climate signals in extreme rainfall, and the validation of convection-permitting climate models."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Mazzoglio, P., Butera, I., & Claps, P. (2020). I2-red: A massive update and quality control of the Italian annual extreme rainfall dataset. Water, 12(12), 3308. https://doi.org/10.3390/w12123308"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Influence of orography in the spatial variability of rainfall extremes\n- Mazzoglio et al. (2023). A local regression approach to analyze the orographic effect on the spatial variability of sub-daily rainfall annual maxima. Geomatics, Natural Hazards and Risk, 14(1), 2205000, https://doi.org/10.1080/19475705.2023.2205000.\n- Mazzoglio et al. (2022). The role of morphology in the spatial distribution of short-duration rainfall extremes in Italy. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 26, 1659–1672, https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-26-1659-2022.\n\nRegional rainfall frequency analysis\n- Magnini et al. (2024). AI-driven morphoclimatic regional frequency modelling of sub-daily rainfall-extremes. Journal of Hydrology, 631, 130808. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2024.130808\n- Magnini et al. (in review). Informativeness of teleconnections in local and regional frequency analysis of rainfall extremes. EGUsphere. https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-2024-3261\n\nTrends in rainfall extremes\n- Mazzoglio et al. (2025). Mapping the uneven temporal changes in ordinary and extraordinary rainfall extremes in Italy. Journal of Hydrology: Regional Studies, 58, 102287. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejrh.2025.102287.\n- Mazzoglio et al. (2022). Long-term spatial and temporal rainfall trends over Italy. Environmental Sciences Proceedings, 21, 28. https://doi.org/10.3390/environsciproc2022021028.\n\nImpact of rainfall variability on flood frequencies\n- Cafiero et al. (2025). How changes in future precipitation impact flood frequencies: a quantile-quantile mapping approach. Water Resources Research, 61, e2024WR038471. https://doi.org/10.1029/2024WR038471.\n\nInfrastructure safety\n- Evangelista et al. (2023). Flood attenuation potential of Italian Dams: sensitivity on geomorphic and climatological factors. Water Resources Management. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11269-023-03649-z.\n\nValidation of convection-permitting models\n- Mazzoglio et al (2025). Orographic and land-sea contrast effects in convection-permitting simulations of extreme sub-daily precipitation. Weather and Climate Extremes, 100798. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.wace.2025.100798.\n\nCreation of other datasets\n- Evangelista et al. (2025). Features of Italian large dams and their upstream catchments. Earth System Science Data, 17, 1407–1426. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-17-1407-2025.\n- Claps et al. (2024). FOCA: a new quality-controlled database of floods and catchment descriptors in Italy. Earth System Science Data, 16, 1503–1522. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-16-1503-2024.\n- Mazzoglio et al. (2024). Rainfall data augmentation in Northern Italy through daily extremes and the Hershfield factor. Proceedings of IAHS, 385, 147–153, https://doi.org/10.5194/piahs-385-147-2024."
        },
