        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "DOI https://doi.org/10.11888/Atmos.tpdc.302088."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Repository data managers Xiaoduo Pan (panxd@itpcas.ac.cn); Curator: Xin Li (xinli@itpcas.ac.cn)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "China Meteorological Forcing Data (CMFD) is a high-precision, high-resolution, long-term dataset developed to support land surface, hydrology, and ecology research over China. The newly released CMFD 2.0 provides near-surface temperature, pressure, specific humidity, wind speed, downward shortwave and longwave radiation, and precipitation, with a temporal resolution of 3 hours, spatial resolution of 0.1°, and a 74-year span (1951–2024) covering 70°E–140°E, 15°N–55°N. CMFD 2.0 integrates ERA5 reanalysis with Chinese meteorological station observations, and applies AI-based products for radiation and precipitation, significantly improving accuracy over previous versions. CMFD 2.0 also provides derived variables (e.g., relative humidity, rain–snow separated precipitation)."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The China Meteorological Forcing Dataset (CMFD) is a transformative resource for both the scientific and broader community. Since its release, it has opened new frontiers in research, education, and applications across disciplines, serving as an exemplar of impactful open data.\nCMFD is widely used in education and training, with thousands of graduate students relying on it as a free and accessible dataset for studies in atmospheric science, hydrology, and ecology. The development team provides workshops and tutorials that lower barriers for early-career scientists. Beyond academia, CMFD contributes directly to disaster response and prediction. Its accurate meteorological inputs support early warning systems and improve forecasts of floods, droughts, and heatwaves, enabling communities to prepare and respond more effectively.\nCMFD has become indispensable for advancing understanding of geophysical and environmental phenomena. Since its 2020 publication in Scientific Data, it has been cited more than 1,200 times, including in leading journals such as the Nature series, Science Advances, Review of Geophysics, and PNAS. The dataset underpins diverse research, from extreme event attribution and regional climate variability to ecological restoration, carbon sink/source assessments, and frozen soil dynamics. It also serves as a standard input to land surface, hydrological, and atmospheric chemistry models, making it foundational to studies of conservation, climate change, and environmental indicators.\nCMFD’s rich applications guide adaptation planning, infrastructure resilience, and humanitarian relief, reinforcing the social and economic fabric of vulnerable regions. Its free availability ensures equitable access to high-quality meteorological information, narrowing the gap between well-resourced institutions and emerging research groups. By integrating state-of-the-art artificial intelligence with reanalysis and station data, it further improves accuracy, supporting decision-making at local, regional, and transboundary scales.\nWithout CMFD, the research community would lack a unified, long, and high-resolution meteorological record over one of the world’s most dynamic and climatically complex regions, limiting disaster risk assessment, ecological and climate studies, and the training of new scientists. With CMFD, we gain not only an invaluable dataset, but also an enduring platform for innovation, collaboration, and resilience in the face of global environmental challenges."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "During the nominator’s PhD research on hydroclimate in High Mountain Asia (HMA), they constantly struggled with the lack of reliable meteorological observations. The terrain is extremely complex, and station records were sparse, difficult to obtain, and often restricted from sharing. Each time they submitted a paper, reviewers would raise concerns about the quality and representativeness of the data. This limitation not only undermined the confidence in their results but also constrained the scope of questions they could address.\nThe release of the CMFD fundamentally changed this situation. Its integration of multiple data sources, advanced machine-learning techniques, and rigorous quality control provided a high-resolution, trustworthy dataset freely available to the entire community. For the nominator, CMFD became a reliable foundation for analyzing climate variability and hydrological processes in HMA without the constant barrier of data access and quality concerns.\nIn their view, CMFD set a benchmark that open access to high-quality datasets can transform research productivity, foster equity, and empower scientists everywhere. It has not only elevated their own work but also helped democratize hydroclimate research in data-scarce regions."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "He, J., Yang, K., Tang, W., Lu, H., Qin, J., Chen, Y., & Li, X. (2020). The first high-resolution meteorological forcing dataset for land process studies over China. Scientific Data, 7(1), 25. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-020-0369-y"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator has selected five representative references to highlight the diverse ways this dataset has been reused across climate and environmental research:\n\nZhang, G., Nan, Z., Hu, N., Yin, Z., Zhao, L., Cheng, G., & Mu, C. (2022). Qinghai‐tibet plateau permafrost at risk in the late 21st century. Earth’s Future, 10(6), e2022EF002652. https://doi.org/10.1029/2022EF002652 \n\nWang, Y., Xiao, J., Ma, Y., Ding, J., Chen, X., Ding, Z., & Luo, Y. (2023). Persistent and enhanced carbon sequestration capacity of alpine grasslands on Earth’s Third Pole. Science Advances, 9(20), eade6875. https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.ade6875 \n\nLi, X., Liu, F., Ma, C., Hou, J., Zheng, D., Ma, H., Bai, Y., Han, X., Vereecken, H., Yang, K., Duan, Q., & Huang, C. (2024). Land data assimilation: Harmonizing theory and data in land surface process studies. Reviews of Geophysics, 62(1), e2022RG000801. https://doi.org/10.1029/2022RG000801 \n\nXu, F., Zhang, G., Woolway, R. I., Yang, K., Wada, Y., Wang, J., & Crétaux, J.-F. (2024). Widespread societal and ecological impacts from projected Tibetan Plateau lake expansion. Nature Geoscience, 17(6), 516–523. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41561-024-01446-w \n \nLi, X., Guo, H., Cheng, G., Song, X., Ran, Y., Feng, M., Che, T., Li, X., Wang, L., Duan, A., Shangguan, D., Chen, D., Jin, R., Deng, J., Su, J., & Cao, B. (2025). Polar regions are critical in achieving global sustainable development goals. Nature Communications, 16(1), 3879. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-59178-3"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Wenhao Dong"
        },
        {
