    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:011",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:011",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:011",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4784-4502",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1376-438X"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The World Ozone and Ultraviolet Radiation Data Centre (WOUDC) is one of the world’s longest-standing and most comprehensive archives of ozone and UV radiation observations, with records extending back to the 1920s. The WOUDC archive is central to monitoring and understanding the recovery of the ozone layer under the Montreal Protocol, one of the most successful environmental treaties ever enacted. It provides long-term, globally distributed records that are indispensable for studying stratospheric ozone variability, tropospheric ozone pollution, and surface UV radiation."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "The World Ozone and Ultraviolet Radiation Data Centre (WOUDC) stands as one of the longest-operating and most globally integrated atmospheric observation archives in existence. Its value to science, society, and policy cannot be overstated. In my opinion, its impact spans across education, climate understanding, public health, and international environmental governance—making it a prime candidate for AGU recognition.\n\nWithout WOUDC, we would lose a cornerstone dataset that enables the long-term tracking of stratospheric ozone, which is essential for understanding the recovery of the ozone layer in the wake of the Montreal Protocol. This historical perspective is critical not only for evaluating past interventions but also for assessing present and future risks—especially in light of climate–ozone interactions.\n\nPeople: The WOUDC dataset supports education and training of atmospheric scientists worldwide. Many graduate theses and research projects begin with this openly accessible repository. Its long-term, quality-assured data from standardized instruments like the Dobson and Brewer spectrophotometers enable direct engagement with real measurements. Furthermore, understanding UV radiation levels and ozone trends is critical for public health campaigns related to skin cancer prevention and UV-index forecasting—particularly for vulnerable populations.\n\nPlanet: WOUDC provides one of the few continuous, multi-decadal records of total column ozone and vertical ozone profiles. This makes it indispensable for assessing geophysical phenomena such as stratospheric dynamics, polar ozone depletion, and the coupling between ozone and climate. It is used as a benchmark for satellite validation, reanalysis models, and global assessments by organizations like WMO and UNEP. Without it, our ability to quantify environmental indicators such as UV exposure, atmospheric composition changes, and the effectiveness of international treaties would be greatly diminished.\n\nProsperity: From a societal perspective, the accessibility of WOUDC data strengthens scientific equity and capacity-building, especially in developing countries. Institutions without extensive in situ measurement capabilities can still engage in high-level climate research and policy development using this shared global resource. It also contributes to community resilience by supporting regional air quality and UV monitoring networks that inform both local adaptation measures and global climate mitigation strategies.",
          "impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "People",
              "schema:text": "The WOUDC dataset supports education and training of atmospheric scientists worldwide. Many graduate theses and research projects begin with this openly accessible repository. Its long-term, quality-assured data from standardized instruments like the Dobson and Brewer spectrophotometers enable direct engagement with real measurements. Furthermore, understanding UV radiation levels and ozone trends is critical for public health campaigns related to skin cancer prevention and UV-index forecasting—particularly for vulnerable populations."
            },
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "Planet",
              "schema:text": "WOUDC provides one of the few continuous, multi-decadal records of total column ozone and vertical ozone profiles. This makes it indispensable for assessing geophysical phenomena such as stratospheric dynamics, polar ozone depletion, and the coupling between ozone and climate. It is used as a benchmark for satellite validation, reanalysis models, and global assessments by organizations like WMO and UNEP. Without it, our ability to quantify environmental indicators such as UV exposure, atmospheric composition changes, and the effectiveness of international treaties would be greatly diminished."
            },
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "Prosperity",
              "schema:text": "From a societal perspective, the accessibility of WOUDC data strengthens scientific equity and capacity-building, especially in developing countries. Institutions without extensive in situ measurement capabilities can still engage in high-level climate research and policy development using this shared global resource. It also contributes to community resilience by supporting regional air quality and UV monitoring networks that inform both local adaptation measures and global climate mitigation strategies."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "As a research scientist within ECCC, the nominator worked with many researchers globally on applying WOUDC ozone dataset in ozone recovery studies, satellite validation and verification, and model development."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "As the lead of the Dobson Calibration Center for Europe, the nominator regularly contributes ozone measurements—both from Dobson and Brewer spectrophotometers—to the WOUDC. Their team’s data are rigorously calibrated, quality-assured, and archived in this global repository, where they are made openly accessible to the international community. The nominator also uses WOUDC data extensively in their own research, particularly in long-term trend analysis, instrument intercomparisons, and satellite validation studies. Being able to rely on consistent, traceable datasets from WOUDC has been essential in co-authoring peer-reviewed publications and supporting international assessments. They also point students and collaborators to WOUDC as a model of a well-curated scientific archive that is both technically robust and user-friendly. Their involvement with WOUDC is not only professional, but also personal—they see it as a foundation for collaboration, transparency, and progress in atmospheric science."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:011",
