          "schema:value": "https://woudc.org/en/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "World Ozone and Ultraviolet Radiation Data Centre"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Tom Kralidis"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The World Ozone and Ultraviolet Radiation Data Centre (WOUDC) is one of six World Data Centres which are part of the Global Atmosphere Watch programme of the World Meteorological Organization. The WOUDC data centre is operated by the Meteorological Service of Canada, a branch of Environment and Climate Change Canada.\n\nOzone data set categories include total column ozone and vertical profile data from lidar measurements, ozonesonde flights, and the Umkehr technique. Ultraviolet (UV) radiation data set categories include broadband, multiband, and high resolution spectral data types.\n\nThere are over 500 registered stations in the archive from over 150 contributors . Metadata such as station lists with locations, are available from the WOUDC Website.\n\nValue added data products include total ozone time series graphs and near real-time ozone maps.\n\nhttps://woudc.org/en/about"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nThe World Ozone and Ultraviolet Radiation Data Centre (WOUDC) is one of the world’s longest-standing and most comprehensive archives of ozone and UV radiation observations, with records extending back to the 1920s. The WOUDC archive is central to monitoring and understanding the recovery of the ozone layer under the Montreal Protocol, one of the most successful environmental treaties ever enacted. It provides long-term, globally distributed records that are indispensable for studying stratospheric ozone variability, tropospheric ozone pollution, and surface UV radiation.\n\nNominator 2:\nThe World Ozone and Ultraviolet Radiation Data Centre (WOUDC) stands as one of the longest-operating and most globally integrated atmospheric observation archives in existence. Its value to science, society, and policy cannot be overstated. In my opinion, its impact spans across education, climate understanding, public health, and international environmental governance—making it a prime candidate for AGU recognition.\n\nWithout WOUDC, we would lose a cornerstone dataset that enables the long-term tracking of stratospheric ozone, which is essential for understanding the recovery of the ozone layer in the wake of the Montreal Protocol. This historical perspective is critical not only for evaluating past interventions but also for assessing present and future risks—especially in light of climate–ozone interactions.\n\nPeople: The WOUDC dataset supports education and training of atmospheric scientists worldwide. Many graduate theses and research projects begin with this openly accessible repository. Its long-term, quality-assured data from standardized instruments like the Dobson and Brewer spectrophotometers enable direct engagement with real measurements. Furthermore, understanding UV radiation levels and ozone trends is critical for public health campaigns related to skin cancer prevention and UV-index forecasting—particularly for vulnerable populations.\n\nPlanet: WOUDC provides one of the few continuous, multi-decadal records of total column ozone and vertical ozone profiles. This makes it indispensable for assessing geophysical phenomena such as stratospheric dynamics, polar ozone depletion, and the coupling between ozone and climate. It is used as a benchmark for satellite validation, reanalysis models, and global assessments by organizations like WMO and UNEP. Without it, our ability to quantify environmental indicators such as UV exposure, atmospheric composition changes, and the effectiveness of international treaties would be greatly diminished.\n\nProsperity: From a societal perspective, the accessibility of WOUDC data strengthens scientific equity and capacity-building, especially in developing countries. Institutions without extensive in situ measurement capabilities can still engage in high-level climate research and policy development using this shared global resource. It also contributes to community resilience by supporting regional air quality and UV monitoring networks that inform both local adaptation measures and global climate mitigation strategies."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nAs a research scientist within ECCC, the nominator worked with many researchers globally on applying WOUDC ozone dataset in ozone recovery studies, satellite validation and verification, and model development. \n\nNominator 2:\nAs the lead of the Dobson Calibration Center for Europe, the nominator regularly contributes ozone measurements—both from Dobson and Brewer spectrophotometers—to the WOUDC. Their team’s data are rigorously calibrated, quality-assured, and archived in this global repository, where they are made openly accessible to the international community. The nominator also uses WOUDC data extensively in their own research, particularly in long-term trend analysis, instrument intercomparisons, and satellite validation studies. Being able to rely on consistent, traceable datasets from WOUDC has been essential in co-authoring peer-reviewed publications and supporting international assessments. They also point students and collaborators to WOUDC as a model of a well-curated scientific archive that is both technically robust and user-friendly. Their involvement with WOUDC is not only professional, but also personal—they see it as a foundation for collaboration, transparency, and progress in atmospheric science."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nWeber, M., Arosio, C., Coldewey-Egbers, M., Fioletov, V. E., Frith, S. M., Wild, J. D., Tourpali, K., Burrows, J. P., & Loyola, D. (2022). Global total ozone recovery trends attributed to ozone-depleting substance (Ods) changes derived from five merged ozone datasets. Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics, 22(10), 6843–6859. https://doi.org/10.5194/acp-22-6843-2022 \n\nNominator 2:\nGuide to the WMO/GAW World Ultraviolet Radiation Data Centre, version 6.0, https://library.wmo.int/idurl/4/47197"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nXiaoyi Zhao\n\nNominator 2:\nVoltaire A. Velazco"
        },
        {
