          "schema:value": "This is a lightning dataset from a ground based global network of VLF sensors providing lightning strokes location, time, energy, etc."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The World Wide Lightning Location Network (WWLLN) dataset is one of the most influential and widely utilized global lightning datasets in atmospheric and geophysical research. Since its establishment, WWLLN has enabled continuous, near real-time monitoring of global lightning activity and has supported a broad spectrum of studies spanning atmospheric electricity, severe weather, tropical cyclones, climate variability, ionospheric disturbances, upper atmospheric phenomena, wildfire detection, and space weather applications.\n\nThe scientific impact of the WWLLN dataset is substantial and continues to grow. To date, more than 3,500 peer-reviewed research articles have been published using WWLLN data, demonstrating its extensive adoption across the international research community. In addition, the dataset has contributed to over 40 doctoral and graduate theses worldwide, reflecting its enduring value in advanced academic research and higher education.\n\nWWLLN’s global coverage, long-term continuity, and high temporal resolution have made it an indispensable resource for researchers investigating lightning-related processes on regional and planetary scales. The dataset has also facilitated interdisciplinary collaborations among meteorologists, atmospheric physicists, climate scientists, oceanographers, and space scientists, thereby significantly advancing the understanding of Earth’s coupled atmosphere–ionosphere system.\n\nBeyond its current achievements, WWLLN possesses enormous future potential. The network continues to grow through the addition of new sensors and international collaborations, steadily improving global detection efficiency and location accuracy. Its long-term continuous observations are becoming increasingly valuable for climate trend analysis, disaster early-warning systems, aviation safety, maritime operations, and data-driven forecasting using artificial intelligence and machine learning techniques."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator hosts a sensor station of WWLLN in Bangladesh and conducts research on lightning and its associated geophysical phenomenon with their students and co-researchers. They provide relevant information and warnings to combat disasters to the Ministry of Disaster Management and Relief (MoDMR) in Bangladesh. They conduct awareness campaigns to areas having intense lightning, and regular statistical analysis of this dataset helps them. They carry out regular studies of lightning occurrences at the major airports of Bangladesh and major shipping lanes originating from the Bay of Bengal to the Indian Ocean. They also record the lightning activity associated with major tropical cyclones in the Bay of Bengal from their origination to landfall to advance scientific understanding and contribute to new research."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Rafi, M. H., Holzworth, R. H., & Mostafa, M. G. (2024). Evaluation of detection efficiency of World Wide Lightning Location Network in Southeast Asian region. Journal of Geophysical Research: Atmospheres, 129, e2024JD041160. https://doi.org/10.1029/2024JD041160"
        },
