      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:japan-atomic-energy-agency-765-1-funaishikawa-tokai-mura-naka-gun-ibaraki-319-11",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "Japan Atomic Energy Agency, 765-1 Funaishikawa, Tokai-mura, Naka-gun, Ibaraki 319-1184, Japan"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:014",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:014",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:014",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9902-7083"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "PARMA/EXPACS was nominated because the dataset turns atmospheric cosmic-ray radiation from a specialist topic into shared, auditable infrastructure. Before PARMA, realistic atmospheric fluxes were practical only for experts with large compute resources and Monte Carlo skills. By packaging rigorous physics in an easy-to-use tool, PARMA/EXPACS democratized access and broadened applications well beyond radiation and space physics.\n\nPEOPLE\nAircrew dose rises with altitude; accordingly, flight crews—like ground-based radiation workers—require appropriate monitoring and management. In Japan, tools built on PARMA, such as JISCARD (http://www.jiscard.jp/) (in Japanese) and WASAVIES (https://wasavies.nict.go.jp/index_e.html), are used by airlines to estimate route- and fleet-specific exposures and to maintain crew dose records [1-7]. Beyond aviation, PARMA provides transparent, location- and time-dependent fields for evaluating public background exposure [7-12], and EXPACS was adopted as the fundamental tool for UNSCEAR’s revision of global reference values for cosmic-ray exposure [13].\n\nPLANET\nCosmogenic-nuclide yields in the atmosphere are essential for estimating exposure ages and surface-erosion rates. Earlier work relied on empirical scaling; the PARMA-based Lifton–Sato–Dunai (LSD) model [14] enables physically grounded scaling that accounts for latitude, longitude, altitude, and solar modulation, improving accuracy and inter-site comparability. LSD is implemented in exposure-age calculators, such as CRONUS Earth (https://cronus.cosmogenicnuclides.rocks/2.0/), and is widely used as a production-rate standard. In addition, ionization-rate profiles computed with PARMA are used to examine links between variability in the cosmic-ray environment and climate [15], volcano activity [16], thunderstorm [17], and cosmogenic nuclide yields [18].\n\nPROSPERITY\nPARMA also underpins practical advances in industry and agriculture. In microelectronics, realistic neutron/muon spectra from PARMA are used to estimate cosmic-ray–induced soft error rates [19-21], informing qualification and mitigation strategies that safeguard safety-critical systems—including those used in autonomous driving—against silent data corruption. In agri-hydrology, cosmic-ray neutron sensing (CRNS) infers near-surface soil moisture from above-ground neutron counts; PARMA supplies the site- and season-specific atmospheric neutron context needed to design sensors, set calibration ranges, and normalize networks across fields and climates, improving irrigation management and drought resilience [22-27]. PARMA/EXPACS is also applied to the design of muon-radiography, which can be used for imaging various objects such as pyramid and nuclear reactors [28-30].\n\nThrough PARMA/EXPACS, research domains that were previously unconnected have coalesced into a new community of cosmic-ray engineering applications. The field has broadened beyond a purely scientific pursuit: shared, auditable radiation fields now let these communities design, compare, and operate on common ground, creating links that did not exist before.\n\nReferences are provided in the column of “bibliographies listing instances of reuse”."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator interacts with PARMA/EXPACS as both its principal creator and its ambassador. They conceived, implemented, and validated the model essentially single-handedly—designing the physics, coding the analytical formulations, benchmarking against measurements, and packaging the results so that anyone can compute location-, altitude-, and time-dependent particle spectra, ionization, and dose in seconds. In an era when impactful geophysical datasets are typically produced by large consortia, this largely solo development is noteworthy.\n\nAs a user, the nominator has pushed PARMA/EXPACS into practice: opening clear paths for aviation and public radiation-protection use (e.g., route-specific aircrew dose assessment and national background-dose baselines) and collaborating with researchers worldwide to extend its reach across disciplines. With colleagues, they integrated the model into tools such as JISCARD and WASAVIES for operational exposure management; supported cosmogenic-nuclide scaling (LSD) and community calculators; provided inputs for cosmic-ray neutron sensing (CRNS) normalization; informed muon radiography design; and supplied realistic fields for semiconductor soft-error modeling. They also maintain documentation, train users, and respond to feedback, helping ensure the dataset remains rigorous, reproducible, and genuinely useful beyond the radiation community."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:014",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 15,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Atmospheric Science, Space Weather"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "PARMA/EXPACS"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Tatsuhiko Sato"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://phits.jaea.go.jp/expacs/index.html"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "Japan Atomic Energy Agency"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "PARMA/EXPACS is a globally applicable dataset and calculator of atmospheric cosmic-ray particle spectra and radiation dose fields, available free of charge from Japan Atomic Energy Agency. Built on the PHITS-based Analytical Radiation Model in the Atmosphere (PARMA), EXPACS rapidly returns location-, altitude-, date- and solar-cycle-dependent fluxes of neutrons, protons, heavy ions (Z≤28), muons, electrons/positrons and photons, together with derived quantities such as effective dose, ambient dose equivalent, absorbed dose in air and ion-pair production rates. The underlying model has been extensively validated against measurements over a wide range of geomagnetic cutoffs, altitudes and solar conditions, and successive PARMA releases widened the physics coverage (e.g., zenith-angle dependence) and application domains. Because EXPACS is Excel-based with well-documented inputs/outputs, it lowers the barrier to reproducible analyses for researchers and practitioners across disciplines. The dataset underpins operational aviation dose assessment, public exposure evaluation, geoscience applications (cosmogenic nuclide scaling), near-surface hydrology (cosmic-ray neutron sensing), muon radiography and semiconductor soft-error studies, making it a cross-cutting community resource rather than a niche tool."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "PARMA/EXPACS was nominated because the dataset turns atmospheric cosmic-ray radiation from a specialist topic into shared, auditable infrastructure. Before PARMA, realistic atmospheric fluxes were practical only for experts with large compute resources and Monte Carlo skills. By packaging rigorous physics in an easy-to-use tool, PARMA/EXPACS democratized access and broadened applications well beyond radiation and space physics.\n\nPEOPLE\nAircrew dose rises with altitude; accordingly, flight crews—like ground-based radiation workers—require appropriate monitoring and management. In Japan, tools built on PARMA, such as JISCARD (http://www.jiscard.jp/) (in Japanese) and WASAVIES (https://wasavies.nict.go.jp/index_e.html), are used by airlines to estimate route- and fleet-specific exposures and to maintain crew dose records [1-7]. Beyond aviation, PARMA provides transparent, location- and time-dependent fields for evaluating public background exposure [7-12], and EXPACS was adopted as the fundamental tool for UNSCEAR’s revision of global reference values for cosmic-ray exposure [13].\n\nPLANET\nCosmogenic-nuclide yields in the atmosphere are essential for estimating exposure ages and surface-erosion rates. Earlier work relied on empirical scaling; the PARMA-based Lifton–Sato–Dunai (LSD) model [14] enables physically grounded scaling that accounts for latitude, longitude, altitude, and solar modulation, improving accuracy and inter-site comparability. LSD is implemented in exposure-age calculators, such as CRONUS Earth (https://cronus.cosmogenicnuclides.rocks/2.0/), and is widely used as a production-rate standard. In addition, ionization-rate profiles computed with PARMA are used to examine links between variability in the cosmic-ray environment and climate [15], volcano activity [16], thunderstorm [17], and cosmogenic nuclide yields [18].\n\nPROSPERITY\nPARMA also underpins practical advances in industry and agriculture. In microelectronics, realistic neutron/muon spectra from PARMA are used to estimate cosmic-ray–induced soft error rates [19-21], informing qualification and mitigation strategies that safeguard safety-critical systems—including those used in autonomous driving—against silent data corruption. In agri-hydrology, cosmic-ray neutron sensing (CRNS) infers near-surface soil moisture from above-ground neutron counts; PARMA supplies the site- and season-specific atmospheric neutron context needed to design sensors, set calibration ranges, and normalize networks across fields and climates, improving irrigation management and drought resilience [22-27]. PARMA/EXPACS is also applied to the design of muon-radiography, which can be used for imaging various objects such as pyramid and nuclear reactors [28-30].\n\nThrough PARMA/EXPACS, research domains that were previously unconnected have coalesced into a new community of cosmic-ray engineering applications. The field has broadened beyond a purely scientific pursuit: shared, auditable radiation fields now let these communities design, compare, and operate on common ground, creating links that did not exist before.\n\nReferences are provided in the column of “bibliographies listing instances of reuse”."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator interacts with PARMA/EXPACS as both its principal creator and its ambassador. They conceived, implemented, and validated the model essentially single-handedly—designing the physics, coding the analytical formulations, benchmarking against measurements, and packaging the results so that anyone can compute location-, altitude-, and time-dependent particle spectra, ionization, and dose in seconds. In an era when impactful geophysical datasets are typically produced by large consortia, this largely solo development is noteworthy.\n\nAs a user, the nominator has pushed PARMA/EXPACS into practice: opening clear paths for aviation and public radiation-protection use (e.g., route-specific aircrew dose assessment and national background-dose baselines) and collaborating with researchers worldwide to extend its reach across disciplines. With colleagues, they integrated the model into tools such as JISCARD and WASAVIES for operational exposure management; supported cosmogenic-nuclide scaling (LSD) and community calculators; provided inputs for cosmic-ray neutron sensing (CRNS) normalization; informed muon radiography design; and supplied realistic fields for semiconductor soft-error modeling. They also maintain documentation, train users, and respond to feedback, helping ensure the dataset remains rigorous, reproducible, and genuinely useful beyond the radiation community."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Sato, T. (2016). Analytical model for estimating the zenith angle dependence of terrestrial cosmic ray fluxes. PLOS ONE, 11(8), e0160390. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0160390 \n\nSato, T. (2015). Analytical model for estimating terrestrial cosmic ray fluxes nearly anytime and anywhere in the world: Extension of parma/expacs. PLOS ONE, 10(12), e0144679. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0144679 \n\nSato, T., Yasuda, H., Niita, K., Endo, A., & Sihver, L. (2008). Development of parma: Phits-based analytical radiation model in the atmosphere. Radiation Research, 170(2), 244–259. https://doi.org/10.1667/RR1094.1 \n\nSato, T., & Niita, K. (2006). Analytical functions to predict cosmic-ray neutron spectra in the atmosphere. Radiation Research, 166(3), 544–555. https://doi.org/10.1667/RR0610.1"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "1. Yasuda, H., Sato, T., Yonehara, H., Kosako, T., Fujitaka, K., & Sasaki, Y. (2011). Management of cosmic radiation exposure for aircraft crew in Japan. Radiation Protection Dosimetry, 146(1–3), 123–125. https://doi.org/10.1093/rpd/ncr133 \n\n2. Mares, V., & Yasuda, H. (2010). Aviation route doses calculated with EPCARD.Net and JISCARD EX. Radiation Measurements, 45(10), 1553–1556. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.radmeas.2010.06.015 \n\n3. Andersson, M., Ryufuku, S., & Yasuda, H. (2011). Jiscard gui, a graphical interface application for simple and quick calculation of aviation route doses. Japanese Journal of Health Physics, 46(4), 314–320. https://doi.org/10.5453/jhps.46.314 \n\n4. Sato, T., Kataoka, R., Shiota, D., Kubo, Y., Ishii, M., Yasuda, H., Miyake, S., Park, I. C., & Miyoshi, Y. (2018). Real time and automatic analysis program for wasavies: Warning system for aviation exposure to solar energetic particles. Space Weather, 16(7), 924–936. https://doi.org/10.1029/2018SW001873 \n\n5. Kataoka, R., Sato, T., Miyake, S., Shiota, D., & Kubo, Y. (2018). Radiation dose nowcast for the ground level enhancement on 10–11 september 2017. Space Weather, 16(7), 917–923. https://doi.org/10.1029/2018SW001874 \n\n6. Fujita, M., Sato, T., Saito, S., & Yamashiki, Y. (2021). Probabilistic risk assessment of solar particle events considering the cost of countermeasures to reduce the aviation radiation dose. Scientific Reports, 11(1), 17091. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-95235-9 \n\n7. Almeida Filho, D., Federico, C. A., Da Silva, F. C. A., & Souza-Santos, D. (2023). Evaluation of neutron doses in Brazilian aircrew by the Monte Carlo method. Radiation Measurements, 166, 106959. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.radmeas.2023.106959 \n\n8. Didier, T. S. S., Joel, G. S. C., Saïdou, Samuel, B. G., & Maurice, N. M. (2024). Cosmic-ray exposure assessment using particle and heavy ion transport code system: Case study Douala-Cameroon. Radiation Protection Dosimetry, 200(7), 640–647. https://doi.org/10.1093/rpd/ncae087 \n\n9. Sato, T. (2016). Evaluation of world population-weighted effective dose due to cosmic ray exposure. Scientific Reports, 6(1), 33932. https://doi.org/10.1038/srep33932 \n\n10. Chen, J., Timmins, R., Verdecchia, K., & Sato, T. (2009). An estimation of Canadian population exposure to cosmic rays. Radiation and Environmental Biophysics, 48(3), 317–322. https://doi.org/10.1007/s00411-009-0226-z \n\n11. Cinelli, G., Gruber, V., De Felice, L., Bossew, P., Hernandez-Ceballos, M. A., Tollefsen, T., Mundigl, S., & De Cort, M. (2017). European annual cosmic-ray dose: Estimation of population exposure. Journal of Maps, 13(2), 812–821. https://doi.org/10.1080/17445647.2017.1384934 \n\n12. Cabianca, T. (2024, November 8). UNSCEAR evaluation of public exposure to ionizing radiation. In MEREIA Workshop 2024 (Hybrid), Vienna, Austria.\n\n13. Ulanowski, A., Sato, T., Petoussi-Henss, N., & Balonov, M. (2025). Relationships between protection and operational dosimetric quantities for external exposure to natural background radiation. Radiation and Environmental Biophysics, 64(1), 105–115. https://doi.org/10.1007/s00411-025-01109-3 \n\n14. Lifton, N., Sato, T., & Dunai, T. J. (2014). Scaling in situ cosmogenic nuclide production rates using analytical approximations to atmospheric cosmic-ray fluxes. Earth and Planetary Science Letters, 386, 149–160. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.epsl.2013.10.052 \n\n15. Kataoka, R., Ebisuzaki, T., Miyahara, H., Nimura, T., Tomida, T., Sato, T., & Maruyama, S. (2014). The Nebula Winter: The united view of the snowball Earth, mass extinctions, and explosive evolution in the late Neoproterozoic and Cambrian periods. Gondwana Research, 25(3), 1153–1163. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gr.2013.05.003 \n\n16. Ebisuzaki, T., Miyahara, H., Kataoka, R., Sato, T., & Ishimine, Y. (2011). Explosive volcanic eruptions triggered by cosmic rays: Volcano as a bubble chamber. Gondwana Research, 19(4), 1054–1061. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gr.2010.11.004 \n\n17. Karapetyan, G. G. (2014). Variations of muon flux in the atmosphere during thunderstorms. Physical Review D, 89(9), 093005. https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.89.093005 \n\n18. Schaar, K., Spiegl, T., Sato, T., & Langematz, U. (2025). The impact of ENSO on near-surface Beryllium-7. Journal of Environmental Radioactivity, 282, 107592. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvrad.2024.107592 \n\n19. Sierawski, B. D., et al. (2011). Effects of scaling on muon-induced soft errors. In 2011 International Reliability Physics Symposium (pp. 3C.3.1–3C.3.6). Monterey, CA, USA. https://doi.org/10.1109/IRPS.2011.5784484 \n\n20. Abe, S., & Sato, T. (2016). Shielding effect on secondary cosmic-ray neutron- and muon-induced soft errors. 2016 16th European Conference on Radiation and Its Effects on Components and Systems (RADECS), 1–5. https://doi.org/10.1109/RADECS.2016.8093141 \n\n21. Autran, J.-L., Semikh, S., Munteanu, D., Serre, S., Gasiot, G., & Roche, P. (2012). Soft-error rate of advanced sram memories: Modeling and monte carlo simulation. In M. Andriychuk (Ed.), Numerical Simulation—From Theory to Industry. InTech. https://doi.org/10.5772/50111 \n\n22. Köhli, M., Weimar, J., Schrön, M., Baatz, R., & Schmidt, U. (2021). Soil moisture and air humidity dependence of the above-ground cosmic-ray neutron intensity. Frontiers in Water, 2, 544847. https://doi.org/10.3389/frwa.2020.544847 \n\n23. Köhli, M., Schrön, M., Zacharias, S., & Schmidt, U. (2023). Uranos v1. 0 – the ultra rapid adaptable neutron-only simulation for environmental research. Geoscientific Model Development, 16(2), 449–477. https://doi.org/10.5194/gmd-16-449-2023 \n\n24. Köhli, M., Schrön, M., Zreda, M., Schmidt, U., Dietrich, P., & Zacharias, S. (2015). Footprint characteristics revised for field‐scale soil moisture monitoring with cosmic‐ray neutrons. Water Resources Research, 51(7), 5772–5790. https://doi.org/10.1002/2015WR017169 \n\n25. Weimar, J., Köhli, M., Budach, C., & Schmidt, U. (2020). Large-scale boron-lined neutron detection systems as a 3he alternative for cosmic ray neutron sensing. Frontiers in Water, 2, 16. https://doi.org/10.3389/frwa.2020.00016 \n\n26. Heistermann, M., Francke, T., Schrön, M., & Oswald, S. E. (2024). Technical Note: Revisiting the general calibration of cosmic-ray neutron sensors to estimate soil water content. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 28(4), 989–1000. https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-28-989-2024 \n\n27. Hertle, L., Zacharias, S., Larsen, N., Rasche, D., McJannet, D., & Schrön, M. (2025). Neutron monitor based incoming flux correction for cosmic‐ray neutron sensing of environmental water. Water Resources Research, 61(9), e2025WR040527. https://doi.org/10.1029/2025WR040527 \n\n28. Liu, G., Yao, K., Niu, F., Li, Z., Tian, H., Li, J., Luo, X., Jin, L., Gao, J., Rong, J., Fu, Z., Kang, Y., Zhang, W., Ding, S., Wang, Y., Zeng, J., Luo, X., Tian, X., Shi, W., … Liu, Z. (2024). Deep investigation of muography in discovering geological structures in mineral exploration: A case study of Zaozigou gold mine. Geophysical Journal International, 237(1), 588–603. https://doi.org/10.1093/gji/ggae057 \n\n29. Nakai, Y. (2020). Development of a muography system to survey large scale structures (Doctoral dissertation, Kyushu University). \n\n30. Tripathy, S., et al. (2018). GEANT4 simulation for imaging of high Z materials using cosmic ray muons. In Advanced detectors for nuclear, high energy and astroparticle physics (p. 175)."
