    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:015",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "Indian Subcontinent 2m Temperature Field",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:015",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:atmospheric-science-space-weather"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Siddharth Rout"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/siddharthrout/indian-subcontinent-temperature-field"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:indian-subcontinent-temperature-field"
      ],
      "description": "Temperature at 2m above surface level in Kelvin from 1985-01-01 to 2024-12-31 recorded every 3 hours. Sourced from ERA5 ECMWF reanalysis. Pixel resolution is 257 x 257 and meridional resolution is 0.25 degree. Latitude domain [10° South , 54° North], Longitude domain [50° East, 114° East]."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0865-0916",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Siddharth Rout",
      "identifier": {
        "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
        "propertyID": "ORCID",
        "value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0865-0916"
      },
      "schema:email": "siddharth.rout@ubc.ca",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:university-of-british-columbia-2329-west-mall-vancouver-bc-v6t-1z4-canada"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:university-of-british-columbia-2329-west-mall-vancouver-bc-v6t-1z4-canada",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "University of British Columbia, 2329 West Mall, Vancouver, BC, V6T 1Z4, Canada"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:015",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:015",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:015",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0865-0916"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The dataset is highly impactful because it provides a comprehensive, long-term view of surface temperature across a densely populated and climatically diverse region, enabling insights that would otherwise be difficult or impossible. Without this dataset, educators and researchers would have limited ability to train students or develop models that accurately capture regional climate dynamics, hindering capacity-building in climate science, data-driven meteorology, and disaster prediction. For communities, the absence of such high-resolution historical data would constrain early-warning systems and preparedness strategies for heatwaves, floods, or other temperature-related hazards, directly affecting human safety and resilience.\n\nFrom a planetary perspective, this dataset allows detailed study of geophysical and environmental phenomena such as regional warming trends, monsoon variability, and urban heat islands, which are critical for understanding climate change impacts and informing conservation or adaptation measures. It provides an empirical basis to monitor environmental indicators over three decades, enabling scientists to detect anomalies, model future scenarios, and evaluate the effectiveness of climate mitigation strategies.\n\nIn terms of prosperity, the dataset contributes to economic and social well-being by supporting climate-sensitive decision-making in agriculture, energy, and urban planning. By allowing predictive modeling of temperature extremes, it can help mitigate losses in crops, reduce energy stress, and protect vulnerable populations. The data support equitable access to climate information, empowering local communities and policymakers to respond proactively to environmental risks, enhance humanitarian relief efforts, and build resilience against climate-driven disruptions.\n\nIn essence, this dataset bridges gaps in knowledge, education, and planning: it allows people to learn and innovate, helps the planet to be monitored and managed, and fosters prosperity by supporting informed, equitable, and resilient communities in the face of a changing climate. Without it, regional climate modeling, disaster preparedness, and climate-informed decision-making would remain severely limited, leaving both people and ecosystems more vulnerable to temperature extremes and environmental change."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator interacts with this dataset primarily for research and modeling purposes. They load the gridded temperature fields into Python, typically using libraries like pytorch and numpy, and explore both the spatial and temporal patterns of surface temperature over the Indian subcontinent. They preprocess the data by normalizing, reshaping, or splitting it into training, validation, and test sets, making it suitable for machine learning or climate prediction tasks. They use it to develop and benchmark models that predict temperature fields, analyze seasonal and long-term trends, and detect anomalies. Visualizing the data through heatmaps and temporal plots helps them understand regional variability and the influence of extreme events. In their research, the dataset allows them to experiment with methods for stochastic modeling, uncertainty quantification and statistical analysis, providing hands-on experience with large-scale geophysical data. Overall, working with this dataset has strengthened their understanding of both weather processes and computational modeling techniques while giving them practical insight into the challenges of spatiotemporal forecasting."
        }
      ]
    },
