    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:016",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:016",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:016",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8488-408X"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "SatPM2.5 is an impactful dataset providing accessible data for researchers, policymakers, and communities to better understand global and regional PM2.5 exposure. The data enable analyses that support decision-making and policy, filling in gaps where ground-based monitors are sparse or absent. The data are relied upon by a large and growing health and air quality management community including the American Lung Association (ALA), Air Quality Life Index, Environmental Performance Index, EPA, Global Burden of Disease, HEI State of Global Air, OECD, NOAA, UNDP, WHO, World Bank, UNICEF, US Census Bureau, air quality managers and forecasters, private companies, non-governmental organizations, numerous environmental health scientists, and the general public. These data have thus contributed to the growing awareness of the health and environmental effects of ambient PM2.5. \n\nA specific example involves contributing to the ALA annual State of the Air Report, allowing communities to learn how their local air quality compares to other U.S. counties and the national average. The ALA assigns counties an air quality “report card” using county-level design values calculated from the EPA’s ground-based monitoring network. The SatPM2.5 datasets complement EPA monitoring networks with additional data that supports research and reporting. Satellite-derived data provides PM2.5 estimates for the ~80% of counties without a PM2.5 ground monitor, helping to create a more complete picture of exposure than ground-based data alone. These datasets benefit communities and public health officials by improving understanding of local air quality and guiding air quality management decisions, thus helping to build healthier, more informed communities.\n\nSatPM2.5 data underpin epidemiological studies on the association of PM2.5 with health endpoints. Analyses of these data have contributed to numerous epidemiological assessments of the association of air quality with a variety of health outcomes such as asthma, autoimmune rheumatic disease, birth outcomes, breast cancer, cardiovascular disease, childhood cancer, cognitive development in children, COVID-19 outcomes, dementia, diabetes, lung cancer, mortality, pregnancy, and stroke.\n\nSatPM2.5 data and associated epidemiological studies have informed air quality regulations. They contributed to the body of evidence that supported the establishment of the annual PM2.5 guideline for Canada, of the lowering of the WHO air quality guideline to 5 µg/m3, and of the lowering of the US EPA annual PM2.5 standard to 9 µg/m3."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The Holloway Group at the University of Wisconsin–Madison uses the satellite-derived PM2.5 scientific datasets to support county-level analyses of air pollution exposure. By providing estimates where ground-level monitors are unavailable, the data help inform counties about local PM2.5 exposure and support research and policy decisions related to air quality management. SatPM2.5 datasets have supported recent manuscripts in press at GeoHealth, including Acker et al., “Comparison of Policy-Relevant Air Quality Metrics Calculated with Sparse in Situ Monitoring and Contiguous Satellite-Derived Data”​ and Holloway et al. “Applying Satellite-Derived PM2.5 Data to Policy-Relevant Air Quality Metrics.” The Holloway Group also uses the North American regional composition estimates (V5.NA.05 / V5.NA.05.02) for speciated PM2.5 analysis."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:016",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 17,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Atmospheric Science, Space Weather"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "SatPM2.5 (Satellite-derived PM2.5) with current versions: V6.GL.02.04 (global, 1998–2023, Convolutional Neural Network based calibration), V5.GL.05.02 (global, 1998–2023, Geographically Weighted Regression based calibration), and V5.NA.05/V5.NA.05.02 (North American, 2000–2023, compositional PM2.5)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Global Estimates (V6.GL.02.04): Siyuan Shen, Chi Li, Aaron van Donkelaar, Nathan Jacobs, Chenguang Wang, & Randall V. Martin\n\nGlobal/Regional Estimates (V5.GL.05.02): Aaron van Donkelaar, Melanie S. Hammer, Liam Bindle, Michael Brauer, Jeffery R. Brook, Michael J. Garay, N. Christina Hsu, Olga V. Kalashnikova, Ralph A. Kahn, Colin Lee, Robert C. Levy, Alexei Lyapustin, Andrew M. Sayer, & Randall V. Martin\n\nNorth American Regional Estimates with Composition (V5.NA.05 / V5.NA.05.02): Aaron van Donkelaar, Randall V. Martin, Bonne Ford, Chi Li, Amanda J. Pappin, Siyuan Shen, & Dandan Zhang"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "Satellite-derived PM2.5 website: https://satpm.org/ Alternative access to V6.GL.02.04 through the Registry of Open Data on AWS: https://registry.opendata.aws/surface-pm2-5-v6gl/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "Satellite-derived PM2.5"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Repository Data Managers / Curators: Atmospheric Composition Analysis Group (ACAG) at Washington University in St. Louis, with key data managers Aaron van Donkelaar, Siyuan Shen, Chi Li, and Randall Martin\n\nGroup-name that support the dataset: Atmospheric Composition Analysis Group (ACAG) at Washington University in St. Louis"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "SatPM2.5 is a collection of datasets providing spatially and temporally continuous global and regional estimates of ground-level fine particulate matter (PM2.5). The datasets combine Aerosol Optical Depth (AOD) retrievals from multiple satellite-based instruments (MODIS/Terra, MODIS/Aqua, MISR/Terra, SeaWiFS/SeaStar, VIIRS/SNPP, and VIIRS/NOAA20), with chemical transport model simulations (GEOS-Chem) and ground-based observations. The current data collection includes several scientific versions: V6.GL.02.04 (global, 1998–2023, Convolutional Neural Network based calibration), V5.GL.05.02 (global, 1998–2023, Geographically Weighted Regression based calibration), and V5.NA.05/V5.NA.05.02 (North American, 2000–2023, compositional PM2.5). The datasets are provided at both high spatial resolution (0.01° × 0.01°) and reduced resolution (0.1° × 0.1°), with annual, monthly, and biweekly time-scales. For ease of accessibility, SatPM2.5 includes several processed datasets, including population-weighted estimates derived from the V5.GL.05.02 and V6.GL.02.04 scientific versions. Adapted from SatPM2.5 website: https://www.satpm.org/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "SatPM2.5 is an impactful dataset providing accessible data for researchers, policymakers, and communities to better understand global and regional PM2.5 exposure. The data enable analyses that support decision-making and policy, filling in gaps where ground-based monitors are sparse or absent. The data are relied upon by a large and growing health and air quality management community including the American Lung Association (ALA), Air Quality Life Index, Environmental Performance Index, EPA, Global Burden of Disease, HEI State of Global Air, OECD, NOAA, UNDP, WHO, World Bank, UNICEF, US Census Bureau, air quality managers and forecasters, private companies, non-governmental organizations, numerous environmental health scientists, and the general public. These data have thus contributed to the growing awareness of the health and environmental effects of ambient PM2.5. \n\nA specific example involves contributing to the ALA annual State of the Air Report, allowing communities to learn how their local air quality compares to other U.S. counties and the national average. The ALA assigns counties an air quality “report card” using county-level design values calculated from the EPA’s ground-based monitoring network. The SatPM2.5 datasets complement EPA monitoring networks with additional data that supports research and reporting. Satellite-derived data provides PM2.5 estimates for the ~80% of counties without a PM2.5 ground monitor, helping to create a more complete picture of exposure than ground-based data alone. These datasets benefit communities and public health officials by improving understanding of local air quality and guiding air quality management decisions, thus helping to build healthier, more informed communities.\n\nSatPM2.5 data underpin epidemiological studies on the association of PM2.5 with health endpoints. Analyses of these data have contributed to numerous epidemiological assessments of the association of air quality with a variety of health outcomes such as asthma, autoimmune rheumatic disease, birth outcomes, breast cancer, cardiovascular disease, childhood cancer, cognitive development in children, COVID-19 outcomes, dementia, diabetes, lung cancer, mortality, pregnancy, and stroke.\n\nSatPM2.5 data and associated epidemiological studies have informed air quality regulations. They contributed to the body of evidence that supported the establishment of the annual PM2.5 guideline for Canada, of the lowering of the WHO air quality guideline to 5 µg/m3, and of the lowering of the US EPA annual PM2.5 standard to 9 µg/m3."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The Holloway Group at the University of Wisconsin–Madison uses the satellite-derived PM2.5 scientific datasets to support county-level analyses of air pollution exposure. By providing estimates where ground-level monitors are unavailable, the data help inform counties about local PM2.5 exposure and support research and policy decisions related to air quality management. SatPM2.5 datasets have supported recent manuscripts in press at GeoHealth, including Acker et al., “Comparison of Policy-Relevant Air Quality Metrics Calculated with Sparse in Situ Monitoring and Contiguous Satellite-Derived Data”​ and Holloway et al. “Applying Satellite-Derived PM2.5 Data to Policy-Relevant Air Quality Metrics.” The Holloway Group also uses the North American regional composition estimates (V5.NA.05 / V5.NA.05.02) for speciated PM2.5 analysis."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Global Estimates (V6.GL.02.04):\nShen, S., Li, C., van Donkelaar, A., Jacobs, N., Wang, C., & Martin, R. V. (2024). Enhancing Global Estimation of Fine Particulate Matter Concentrations by Including Geophysical a Priori Information in Deep Learning. ACS ES&T Air, 1(5), 332–345. https://doi.org/10.1021/acsestair.3c00054\n\nNorth American Regional Estimates with Composition (V5.NA.05 / V5.NA.05.02):\nvan Donkelaar, A., Martin, R. V., Ford, B., Li, C., Pappin, A. J., Shen, S., & Zhang, D. (2024). North American Fine Particulate Matter Chemical Composition for 2000–2022 from Satellites, Models, and Monitors: The Changing Contribution of Wildfires. ACS ES&T Air, 1(12), 1589–1600. https://doi.org/10.1021/acsestair.4c00151\n\nGlobal/Regional Estimates (V5.GL.05.02): \nvan Donkelaar, A., Hammer, M. S., Bindle, L., Brauer, M., Brook, J. R., Garay, M. J., Hsu, N. C., Kalashnikova, O. V., Kahn, R. A., Lee, C., Levy, R. C., Lyapustin, A., Sayer, A. M., & Martin, R. V. (2021). Monthly Global Estimates of Fine Particulate Matter and Their Uncertainty. Environmental Science & Technology, 55(22), 15287–15300. https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.est.1c05309"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Links to other reusers are:\n\nThe Global Burden of Disease Project www.healthdata.org/research-analysis/gbd\nThe World Health Organization www.who.int/health-topics/air-pollution\nThe Air Quality Life Index aqli.epic.uchicago.edu/\nThe OECD Regional Well-Being Index www.oecdregionalwellbeing.org/\nThe HEI State of Global Air www.stateofglobalair.org/\nThe Environmental Performance Index epi.yale.edu/\nThe World Bank Sustainable Development Goal tracking prosperitydata360.worldbank.org/en/indicator/\nThe American Lung Association www.lung.org/research/sota\n\nThe Integrated Science Assessment (EPA 2019) and its Supplement (EPA 2022) that underpin the updated EPA PM2.5 standard collectively include over 100 citations describing and applying these satellite-derived PM2.5 data. The final rule itself (89 FR 16202) also includes multiple references to these satellite-derived PM2.5 data and its applications.\n\nEPA, U. (2019). Integrated Science Assessment (ISA) for Particulate Matter (Final Report, Dec 2019): U.S. Environmental Protection Agency, Washington, DC, EPA/600/R-619/188, 2019.\n \nEPA, U. S. (2022). Supplement to the 2019 Integrated Science Assessment for Particulate Matter (Final Report, 2022): U.S. Environmental Protection Agency, Washington, DC, EPA/635/R-622/028, 2022.\n\nOther examples of reuse include\nBrauer, M., et al. (2024), Global burden and strength of evidence for 88 risk factors in 204 countries and 811 subnational locations, 1990&#x2013;2021: a systematic analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study 2021, The Lancet, 403(10440), 2162-2203, doi:10.1016/S0140-6736(24)00933-4.\n\nCenter For International Earth Science Information Network-CIESIN-Columbia University. (2022). Global Annual PM2.5 Grids from MODIS, MISR and SeaWiFS Aerosol Optical Depth (AOD), 1998-2019, V4.GL.03 (Version 4.03) [Data set]. Palisades, NY: NASA Socioeconomic Data and Applications Center (SEDAC). https://doi.org/10.7927/FX80-4N39 \n\nGallagher, C. L., Holloway, T., Tessum, C. W., Jackson, C. M., & Heck, C. (2023). Combining Satellite-Derived PM2.5 Data and a Reduced-Form Air Quality Model to Support Air Quality Analysis in US Cities. GeoHealth, 7(5), e2023GH000788. https://doi.org/10.1029/2023GH000788\n\nShi, L., et al. (2023). \"Incident dementia and long-term exposure to constituents of fine particle air pollution: A national cohort study in the United States.\" Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 120(1): e2211282119.\n\nSoutherland, V. A., Brauer, M., Mohegh, A., Hammer, M. S., van Donkelaar, A., Martin, R. V., Apte, J. S., and Anenberg, S. C.: Global urban temporal trends in fine particulate matter (PM2.5) and attributable health burdens: estimates from global datasets, The Lancet Planetary Health, 6, e139-e146, 10.1016/S2542-5196(21)00350-8, 2022.\n\nWeichenthal, S., Pinault, L., Christidis, T., Burnett, R. T., Brook, J. R., Chu, Y., Crouse, D. L., Erickson, A. C., Hystad, P., Li, C., Martin, R. V., Meng, J., Pappin, A. J., Tjepkema, M., van Donkelaar, A., Weagle, C. L., & Brauer, M. (2022). How low can you go? air pollution affects mortality at very low levels. Science Advances, 8(39). https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abo3381.\n\nWu, X., R. C. Nethery, B. M. Sabath, D. Braun, and F. Dominici (2020), Exposure to air pollution and COVID-19 mortality in the United States, medRxiv, 2020.2004.2005.20054502, doi:10.1101/2020.04.05.20054502."
        },
