      },
      "schema:email": "lizhen@tea.ac.cn",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:institute-of-atmospheric-physics-chinese-academy-of-sciences-beijing-china"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:institute-of-atmospheric-physics-chinese-academy-of-sciences-beijing-china",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "Institute of Atmospheric Physics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, China"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:018",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:018",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:018",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2007-8898"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "This dataset is highly impactful for advancing Earth and environmental science, aligning closely with the \"People, Planet, and Prosperity\" dimensions of sustainable development: \n\nPlanet Dimension \nRH is a key driver of atmospheric processes (e.g., fog/haze formation, evapotranspiration) and ecosystem dynamics. Raw RH data previously contained false decreasing trends due to observation system shifts (manual to automated), misleading climate change assessments. The homogenized dataset corrects these biases, enabling accurate quantification of RH’s long-term variability and its link to global warming—critical for understanding changes in hydrological cycles, atmospheric visibility, and terrestrial ecosystems across China. Without it, researchers would overestimate RH decline, leading to flawed conclusions about climate-driven shifts in water vapor balance and associated environmental impacts (e.g., drought risk, vegetation productivity). \n\nPeople Dimension \nThe dataset directly supports public health and safety research. RH strongly influences human thermal comfort (Jin and Meng, 2017) and air quality (via its role in haze formation). By providing reliable RH data, it informs studies on heatwave resilience, respiratory health risks from poor air quality, and disaster preparedness (e.g., fog-related transportation hazards). For example, accurate RH trends help refine heat index models, guiding public health policies to protect vulnerable populations during extreme weather. Additionally, the dataset serves as a teaching and training resource for climate science students, fostering capacity building in data-driven environmental research. \n\nProsperity Dimension \nReliable RH data underpins evidence-based decision-making for sectors dependent on climate conditions, including agriculture, water resource management, and renewable energy (e.g., hydropower). For instance, accurate evapotranspiration estimates (derived from RH) optimize irrigation scheduling, boosting crop yields and food security. The dataset also supports climate adaptation planning for communities—especially in humid/semi-humid regions prone to haze or flooding—enhancing socioeconomic resilience. Without this dataset, industries and policymakers would rely on biased data, increasing the risk of inefficient resource allocation and reduced climate resilience. \n\nNotably, the dataset addresses a critical gap: it is the first openly available homogenized daily RH dataset for China, enabling reproducible research and global comparisons. Its alignment with ERA5 reanalysis data (and the caveat it raises about reanalysis biases) further strengthens its value for validating climate models, a cornerstone of global climate research."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator’s long-term research focuses on climate data homogenization and regional climate change, and this homogenized daily RH dataset is one of cornerstones of their work. Over the years, supported by projects from the National Natural Science Foundation of China, Ministry of Science and Technology, and Chinese Academy of Sciences, they led the development of this dataset—from curating raw data to applying multi-method homogenization (Pettitt test, moving t-test, MASH). \nThey have used this dataset to clarify RH’s true long-term trends in China, challenging the false decline observed in raw data. It has also supported collaborative studies on urbanization’s impact on RH (e.g., in humid southeastern China) and validated links between RH variability and large-scale climate drivers. Beyond their research, the dataset is widely adopted by domestic meteorological institutions (e.g., National Meteorological Information Center, Beijing Climate Center) and international partners, supporting climate attribution, extreme weather prediction, and haze mechanism studies. For example, it was used to refine regional heatwave risk assessments and inform local adaptation policies. Publishing the dataset in an open repository (Science Data Bank) was a priority to ensure accessibility, and its application in over 50 peer-reviewed studies (including their own work on climate sequence homogenization) confirms its role as a trusted resource for the global climate community."
        }
      ]
    },
