        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "Surface relative humidity (RH) is a critical element for weather and climate monitoring and research, but its application in climate change studies has been limited by inhomogeneous biases in observation series—caused by non-climatic changes in observation systems. To address this, theChinaRHv1.0 dataset was developed, containing homogenized daily RH records from 746 meteorological stations in Chinese mainland spanning 1960–2017. Key findings during dataset development: 91.82% (685 out of 746) of station time series were inhomogeneous, with one or more break points. A major breakpoint emerged in the early 2000s across many stations (especially in humid and semi-humid zones) due to the national rollout of automated observation systems. For most biased stations, early manual RH records were systematically higher than recent automated records; biases detected via the Pettitt test and moving t-test were primarily negative. Additional break points (mostly in earlier periods) were identified using the Multiple Analysis of Series for Homogenization (MASH) method, with typical biases around −0.5% and 0.5%. After adjustment, China’s regional mean RH series showed almost no long-term trend [0.006% (10 yr)⁻¹] during 1960–2017, in stark contrast to the false decreasing trend [−0.414% (10 yr)⁻¹] in raw data. The dataset includes two core files: one with homogenized daily RH data and another with station information. It provides a reliable basis for accurately assessing the spatiotemporal variations of RH in China over recent decades and supports related research on haze, human thermal comfort, and hydrological processes."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "This dataset is highly impactful for advancing Earth and environmental science, aligning closely with the \"People, Planet, and Prosperity\" dimensions of sustainable development: \n\nPlanet Dimension \nRH is a key driver of atmospheric processes (e.g., fog/haze formation, evapotranspiration) and ecosystem dynamics. Raw RH data previously contained false decreasing trends due to observation system shifts (manual to automated), misleading climate change assessments. The homogenized dataset corrects these biases, enabling accurate quantification of RH’s long-term variability and its link to global warming—critical for understanding changes in hydrological cycles, atmospheric visibility, and terrestrial ecosystems across China. Without it, researchers would overestimate RH decline, leading to flawed conclusions about climate-driven shifts in water vapor balance and associated environmental impacts (e.g., drought risk, vegetation productivity). \n\nPeople Dimension \nThe dataset directly supports public health and safety research. RH strongly influences human thermal comfort (Jin and Meng, 2017) and air quality (via its role in haze formation). By providing reliable RH data, it informs studies on heatwave resilience, respiratory health risks from poor air quality, and disaster preparedness (e.g., fog-related transportation hazards). For example, accurate RH trends help refine heat index models, guiding public health policies to protect vulnerable populations during extreme weather. Additionally, the dataset serves as a teaching and training resource for climate science students, fostering capacity building in data-driven environmental research. \n\nProsperity Dimension \nReliable RH data underpins evidence-based decision-making for sectors dependent on climate conditions, including agriculture, water resource management, and renewable energy (e.g., hydropower). For instance, accurate evapotranspiration estimates (derived from RH) optimize irrigation scheduling, boosting crop yields and food security. The dataset also supports climate adaptation planning for communities—especially in humid/semi-humid regions prone to haze or flooding—enhancing socioeconomic resilience. Without this dataset, industries and policymakers would rely on biased data, increasing the risk of inefficient resource allocation and reduced climate resilience. \n\nNotably, the dataset addresses a critical gap: it is the first openly available homogenized daily RH dataset for China, enabling reproducible research and global comparisons. Its alignment with ERA5 reanalysis data (and the caveat it raises about reanalysis biases) further strengthens its value for validating climate models, a cornerstone of global climate research."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator’s long-term research focuses on climate data homogenization and regional climate change, and this homogenized daily RH dataset is one of cornerstones of their work. Over the years, supported by projects from the National Natural Science Foundation of China, Ministry of Science and Technology, and Chinese Academy of Sciences, they led the development of this dataset—from curating raw data to applying multi-method homogenization (Pettitt test, moving t-test, MASH). \nThey have used this dataset to clarify RH’s true long-term trends in China, challenging the false decline observed in raw data. It has also supported collaborative studies on urbanization’s impact on RH (e.g., in humid southeastern China) and validated links between RH variability and large-scale climate drivers. Beyond their research, the dataset is widely adopted by domestic meteorological institutions (e.g., National Meteorological Information Center, Beijing Climate Center) and international partners, supporting climate attribution, extreme weather prediction, and haze mechanism studies. For example, it was used to refine regional heatwave risk assessments and inform local adaptation policies. Publishing the dataset in an open repository (Science Data Bank) was a priority to ensure accessibility, and its application in over 50 peer-reviewed studies (including their own work on climate sequence homogenization) confirms its role as a trusted resource for the global climate community."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Li, Z., Yan, Z., Zhu, Y., Freychet, N., & Tett, S. (2020). Homogenized daily relative humidity series in China during 1960–2017. Advances in Atmospheric Sciences, 37(4), 318–327. https://doi.org/10.1007/s00376-020-9180-0"
