        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "These data are column averages of CO2 and measurements of solar induced fluorescence, an indicator of photosynthetic activity, spanning over 10 years (OCO-2) and 5 years (OCO-3). The OCO-2 data provide global coverage, while the OCO-3 data fall between ~52 S and 52 N due to the inclination of the ISS. Both provide focused observations over specific target locations."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The OCO-2 data, in particular, have become the gold standard for space-based global observations of carbon dioxide. This global view of carbon has enabled a variety of new carbon cycle science, including giving early warning that the carbon uptake in the Amazon is weakening, understanding how the major 2015/16 El Niño impacted the carbon cycle, and enabling top-down constrains of national CO2 emissions through the first global stock take. OCO-3’s unique capability to observe small areas has allowed mapping of city-scale CO2, enabling analyses of CO2 emissions at small, actionable scales. Beyond the immediate scientific impact of their CO2 products, these instruments are expected to play an important role in validating the next generation of CO2 observing satellites. Because the OCOs are so carefully calibrated and validated and have over a decade of algorithm development, comparison of the next generation satellite’s retrievals to the OCO retrievals is expected to be of special importance for those satellites to verify that their CO2 products are accurate over a wide range of conditions.\n\nOn top of the CO2 products, the solar induced fluorescence (SIF) data provide a direct measure of photosynthetic activity. This enables more accurate modeling of carbon uptake compared to respiration at the 1-2 km scale. It can also provide important information about crop health, allowing better prediction of agricultural yields.\n\nCombined, these aspects touch all three dimensions: people and prosperity through better prediction of agriculture yields and planet through the many advances in carbon cycle science enabled by these data."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator primarily supports the algorithm development and validation for both satellites. They develop a component of the level 2 algorithm and the primary dataset used for validation (TCCON). They have worked on these projects for ~7 years, during which time they have had the opportunity to hear many presentations on work benefiting from these data."
        },
