    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:022",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "The Collaborative Carbon Column Observing Network (COCCON)",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:022",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:atmospheric-science-space-weather"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Frank Hase"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Darko Dubravica"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "all of the COCCON PIs"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://www.imkasf.kit.edu/english/COCCON.php"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:institute-of-meteorology-and-climate-research-atmospheric-trace-gases-and-remote"
      ],
      "description": "COCCON is a network of small, portable Fourier-transform infrared spectrometers that measure near-IR solar spectral from which total columns of CO2, CH4, and (in most cases) CO can be derived. The smaller size, lower cost, and robustness of these instruments allows them to be deployed in networks around emission sources or remote locations where more expensive and complex instruments are impractical.",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "Frey, M., et al. (2019). Building the collaborative carbon column observing network (Coccon): Long-term stability and ensemble performance of the em27/sun fourier transform spectrometer. Atmospheric Measurement Techniques, 12(3), 1513–1530. https://doi.org/10.5194/amt-12-1513-2019",
          "identifier": [
            {
              "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
              "propertyID": "DOI",
              "value": "https://doi.org/10.5194/amt-12-1513-2019"
            }
          ],
          "schema:url": [
            "https://doi.org/10.5194/amt-12-1513-2019"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:022",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:022",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:022",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8599-4555"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "COCCON plays an important role in both direct observation of carbon emissions and validation of carbon observing satellites. For direct observation, the small size and relative affordability of the instruments allow multiple instruments to be deployed around an emission source, such as an urban area or landfill. The gradient in column-average CO2 or CH4 between the instruments up- and down- wind of the source provides a top-down constraint on the emissions from that area. The column averages measured by these instruments are mostly insensitive to vertical transport of the emissions; compared to a surface concentration measurement, this reduced uncertainty caused by the potential to miss emissions lofted above the surface. \n\nFor satellite validation, it is vital to have ground-truth column averages of CO2 or CH4 with which to compare the satellite products. Like its sibling network, TCCON, COCCON uses direct-sun measurements which are insensitive to certain key sources of error in nadir-viewing satellite retrievals, such as surface reflectance and aerosols. Whereas TCCON focuses on the highest precision measurements possible in a few dozen locations, the smaller instruments used by COCCON permit their observations to be taken in places where a full-fledged TCCON station is not practical (due to limited local resources). This enables satellite validation across a wider range of surface and aerosol characteristics, vital for ensuring the satellite data have consistent quality across the world. Additionally, the same density that enables the gradient studies described in my first paragraph allows satellites to use COCCON to validate their observed gradients in urban areas or other key emissions sources.\n\nThis data impacts the plant dimension most strongly. The constraints on carbon emissions enabled by the network as well as the improvements in satellite validation play important roles in our ability to understand and predict how our climate will change in the future, driven by carbon fluxes."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator supports the network in their retrieval processing by contributing a priori profiles that their algorithm requires."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:022",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 23,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Atmospheric Science, Space Weather"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "The Collaborative Carbon Column Observing Network (COCCON)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Frank Hase, Darko Dubravica, and all of the COCCON PIs"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://www.imkasf.kit.edu/english/COCCON.php"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "Institute of Meteorology and Climate Research Atmospheric Trace Gases and Remote Sensing"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "COCCON is a network of small, portable Fourier-transform infrared spectrometers that measure near-IR solar spectral from which total columns of CO2, CH4, and (in most cases) CO can be derived. The smaller size, lower cost, and robustness of these instruments allows them to be deployed in networks around emission sources or remote locations where more expensive and complex instruments are impractical."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "COCCON plays an important role in both direct observation of carbon emissions and validation of carbon observing satellites. For direct observation, the small size and relative affordability of the instruments allow multiple instruments to be deployed around an emission source, such as an urban area or landfill. The gradient in column-average CO2 or CH4 between the instruments up- and down- wind of the source provides a top-down constraint on the emissions from that area. The column averages measured by these instruments are mostly insensitive to vertical transport of the emissions; compared to a surface concentration measurement, this reduced uncertainty caused by the potential to miss emissions lofted above the surface. \n\nFor satellite validation, it is vital to have ground-truth column averages of CO2 or CH4 with which to compare the satellite products. Like its sibling network, TCCON, COCCON uses direct-sun measurements which are insensitive to certain key sources of error in nadir-viewing satellite retrievals, such as surface reflectance and aerosols. Whereas TCCON focuses on the highest precision measurements possible in a few dozen locations, the smaller instruments used by COCCON permit their observations to be taken in places where a full-fledged TCCON station is not practical (due to limited local resources). This enables satellite validation across a wider range of surface and aerosol characteristics, vital for ensuring the satellite data have consistent quality across the world. Additionally, the same density that enables the gradient studies described in my first paragraph allows satellites to use COCCON to validate their observed gradients in urban areas or other key emissions sources.\n\nThis data impacts the plant dimension most strongly. The constraints on carbon emissions enabled by the network as well as the improvements in satellite validation play important roles in our ability to understand and predict how our climate will change in the future, driven by carbon fluxes."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator supports the network in their retrieval processing by contributing a priori profiles that their algorithm requires."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Frey, M., et al. (2019). Building the collaborative carbon column observing network (Coccon): Long-term stability and ensemble performance of the em27/sun fourier transform spectrometer. Atmospheric Measurement Techniques, 12(3), 1513–1530. https://doi.org/10.5194/amt-12-1513-2019"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Joshua Laughner"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Email Address",
          "schema:value": "josh.laughner@jpl.nasa.gov"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator ORCID",
          "schema:value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8599-4555"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Affiliation or Organization",
          "schema:value": "Jet Propulsion Laboratory, California Institute of Technology, 4800 Oak Grove Drive, Pasadena, CA 91109, USA"
        }
      ]
