          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The ObsPack framework (Masarie et al., 2014) is designed to bring together direct atmospheric greenhouse gas measurements derived from one or more national or university laboratories, prepare them with specific applications in mind, and package and distribute them in a set of self-documenting files. ObsPack products include a set of prepared data sets and metadata, a summary of included data sets, and an e-mail address list of all data providers. [Copied from https://gml.noaa.gov/ccgg/obspack/]"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "In situ data from ground stations, towers, aircraft, and balloons provide a high-resolution and direct measurement of atmospheric composition. There is a wealth of such data stretching back decades; however, the data format varies depending on the creator and time period during which it was observed. This made using a variety of data sources challenging, as users would need to build custom readers for each dataset and correctly handle any quality filtering needed, which could also differ from one dataset to the next.\n\nThe ObsPack addressed these challenges by collecting an enormous variety of in situ data together into a single, unified format. The data are now stored in modern netCDF files, making it far simpler for users to ingest the full variety of data. The makes a wide range of activities more efficient, from assimilating the atmospheric observations into models to validating remote sensing measurements against the data.\n\nThis makes the ObsPack most impactful in the planet dimension. The data include a number of greenhouse gases. These observations allow direct analysis of changing carbon fluxes as well as more indirect methods, such as model assimilation. Similarly, these data are a critical part of tying remote sensing observations to the same metrological scales as their in situ counterparts, ensuring that there are no false gradients due to systematic biases between different datasets. This is essential for understanding and predicting climate change, as these gradients map directly to carbon fluxes, which are a major driver of such change."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "I used ObsPack data to validate the current version of the TCCON data against in order to tie the TCCON data to the WMO CO2 and CH4 scales. Having the data in a common format and location significantly eased this process, as I otherwise would have had to do significantly more legwork to track down the latest versions of all the relevant datasets and ingest them into my analysis."
        },
