          "schema:value": "Mahabaleshwara Hegde, Suhung Shen, Xiaohua Pan"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "Rienecker et al. 2011 state that “the Modern-Era Retrospective Analysis for Research and Applications (MERRA) was undertaken by NASA’s Global Modeling and Assimilation Office with two primary objectives: to place observations from NASA’s Earth Observing System satellites into a climate context and to improve upon the hydrologic cycle represented in earlier generations of reanalyses. The refined Version 2 of the MERRA dataset, MERRA-2, provides global coverage of meteorological and hydrological variables beginning in 1980 at spatial resolution of 0.5 ° x 0.625° (about 50 km). It was introduced to replace the original MERRA dataset because of the advances made in the assimilation system that enable incorporation of modern hyperspectral radiance and microwave observations, along with GPS-Radio Occultation datasets. MERRA-2 also uses NASA's ozone profile observations that began in late 2004. Additional advances in both the Goddard Earth Observing System (GEOS) model and the Gridpoint Statistical Interpolation (GSI) assimilation system are included in MERRA-2. MERRA-2 is the first long-term global reanalysis to assimilate space-based observations of aerosols and represent their interactions with other physical processes in the climate system. MERRA-2 includes a representation of ice sheets over Greenland and Antarctica.\n\nhttps://gmao.gsfc.nasa.gov/gmao-products/merra-2/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The capability of gaining a more detailed understanding of Earth’s interconnected natural systems – encompassing physical and biological phenomena which take place in the atmosphere, oceans, and diverse land environments ranging from ice sheets to tropical rainforests – and also to examine their multitude of interactions – was markedly changed by satellite remote-sensing data. No longer restricted to data collected in single locations or small regions, researchers extended their viewpoint over altitude, latitude, and longitude, observing large-scale events and processes over their entire range of influence. As an example, detection of the occurrence of tropical cyclones far from land was significantly enhanced, allowing better prediction of potential risk, as well as improved understanding of factors contributing to their strength and movement.\n\nMany variables are likely needed to characterize any Earth system process or phenomenon. Collecting a large number of potentially useful variables in one dataset, however, is a daunting task. Combining satellite remote sensing data with advanced Earth system modeling, to augment insights gained from either alone, is even more daunting, However, this is what the MERRA-2 dataset has accomplished. By combining modeling with observational data, MERRA-2 provides global data at high spatial and temporal resolution for a time range over 40 years long. \n\nMERRA-2 was created by the modeling expertise of the Goddard Modeling and Assimilation Office (GMAO). GMAO authored the GEOS-5 model that incorporates remote sensing data from the Earth Observing System (EOS) satellites, as well as numerous precursor instruments. The MERRA-2 reanalysis includes many algorithms that calculate hundreds of variables.These variables range from the simple and recognizable, such as rainfall amount, surface temperature, atmospheric trace gas concentrations, humidity, windspeed and direction, dust mass, and cloud fraction – to the more complex, such as aerosol optical depth, evapotranspiration, geopotential height, heat and energy fluxes – and to variables that primarily meteorologists love, such as numerous tendencies, various flavors of precipitation, and turbulence effects. \n\nMERRA-2 contributes to scientific understanding and societal applications across a wide range of topicality. The variables can be used for foundational Earth system research, refining knowledge of interconnected geophysical processes isn the atmosphere, hydrosphere, and geosphere. The dataset is also used in numerous fields of application science, where the Earth system interacts with needs of and changes in human society. Research on air quality, infectious diseases, water resources, natural hazards, and energy production are just a few examples of the latter. Furthermore, MERRA-2 provides insight into the effects of climate and environmental change on factors vital to societal stability and geopolitical relationships, oftentimes for diverse regions of the world which would not have been amenable to characterization with in situ measurements only.\n\nEnumerating all of the topics that MERRA-2 data has been used for would require a long book; discussing all of its potential uses would require an even longer tome. As an unrefined evaluation of its impact, a Google Scholar search with the single keyword “MERRA-2” returns approximately 16,700 entries. MERRA-2 is an invaluable dataset for comprehending our changing Earth system."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "In their role as a senior scientist at a data center enabling scientific research, the nominator has recommended MERRA-2 data for Earth science topics and applications. Publications citing the Giovanni (https://giovanni.gsfc.nasa.gov) data analysis system, which provides numerous MERRA-2 variables, demonstrates consistent international usage of the dataset for both fundamental Earth system science investigations and for applied science. \n\nMERRA-2 provides a powerful combination of global high spatial resolution with remarkable temporal resolutions – the latter including 3-hourly, daily, and monthly products over 40 years. It is not uncommon to see utilization of the dataset variables for retrospective analyses for events occurring many years ago. MERRA-2 provides the capability of validating field research, in situ data, and other remotely-sensed data variables. \n\nMERRA-2 windspeed and wind direction data allowed my investigation of a unique phytoplankton bloom in the Pacific Ocean region of Chuuk Lagoon (Micronesia) occurring in 2013. A decade later, a rare sequence of windspeed and direction changes was identified that fostered lagoonal phytoplankton growth, followed by transport into the Pacific Ocean. The versatility and characteristics of the MERRA-2 dataset made this retrospective event analysis feasible, and it is unlikely that any other dataset provides such a capability."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Rienecker, M. M., Suarez, M. J., Gelaro, R., Todling, R., Bacmeister, J., Liu, E., Bosilovich, M. G., Schubert, S. D., Takacs, L., Kim, G.-K., Bloom, S., Chen, J., Collins, D., Conaty, A., Da Silva, A., Gu, W., Joiner, J., Koster, R. D., Lucchesi, R., … Woollen, J. (2011). Merra: Nasa’s modern-era retrospective analysis for research and applications. Journal of Climate, 24(14), 3624–3648. https://doi.org/10.1175/JCLI-D-11-00015.1 \n\nGelaro, R., McCarty, W., Suárez, M. J., Todling, R., Molod, A., Takacs, L., Randles, C. A., Darmenov, A., Bosilovich, M. G., Reichle, R., Wargan, K., Coy, L., Cullather, R., Draper, C., Akella, S., Buchard, V., Conaty, A., Da Silva, A. M., Gu, W., … Zhao, B. (2017). The modern-era retrospective analysis for research and applications, version 2(MERRA-2). Journal of Climate, 30(14), 5419–5454. https://doi.org/10.1175/JCLI-D-16-0758.1"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "James Acker"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Email Address",
          "schema:value": "james.g.acker@nasa.gov"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator ORCID",
          "schema:value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7262-9615"
        },
