    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8150-7556",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Zhong Liu",
      "identifier": {
        "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
        "propertyID": "ORCID",
        "value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8150-7556"
      },
      "schema:email": "zhong.liu@nasa.gov",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:nasa-ges-disc-gmu-nasa-goddard-space-flight-center-greenbelt-md-20771"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:nasa-ges-disc-gmu-nasa-goddard-space-flight-center-greenbelt-md-20771",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "NASA GES DISC (GMU), NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, Greenbelt, MD 20771"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:025",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:025",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:025",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8150-7556"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "Climate research and application activities rely on long-term, consistent, bias-free, timely available, and easy-to-access climate data records (also known as CDR). Without these crucial CDRs, for example, it is difficult to accurately study, understand, and monitor global precipitation such as rainfall distribution patterns, climate phenomena (e.g., ENSO events), and their seasonal and interannual variations and trends. Meanwhile, CDR products are vital for model validation that supports model development efforts.\n\nThe Global Precipitation Climatology Project (GPCP) is a long-term effort to produce such CDR. Details and history regarding the data collection, analysis, and quality control can be found in Huffman et al. (2023). The suite of the GPCP products is the standard of the scientific community and has been used in over 2500 journal articles since they were released. Samples can be found on the product landing page (e.g., https://doi.org/10.5067/MEASURES/GPCP/DATA306). \n\nGPCP data has been used in the studies of global precipitation variability and trends (Gu and Adler 2023). GPCP precipitation data is widely used in monsoon studies to analyze spatial and temporal variations in rainfall associated with major monsoon systems such as the South Asian, East Asian, West African, and North American monsoons. Its global coverage and long-term record allow researchers to assess monsoon onset and withdrawal dates, seasonal intensity, interannual variability, and trends over decades. It is also used to evaluate the performance of climate models and reanalysis products in simulating monsoon behavior and to study the impacts of large-scale drivers like ENSO and the Indian Ocean Dipole on monsoon rainfall. Overall, GPCP data supports both diagnostic studies of past monsoon behavior and predictive modeling efforts.\n\nGPCP precipitation data contributes to community resilience by providing reliable, long-term information on global and regional rainfall patterns, which is essential for planning and adapting to climate variability and change. Communities vulnerable to droughts, floods, or shifting rainfall seasons—such as those in monsoon or semi-arid regions can use GPCP data to understand historical precipitation trends and extremes. This helps inform risk assessments, disaster preparedness, agricultural planning, and water resource management. For example, identifying patterns of decreasing rainfall or increasing rainfall variability can support early warning systems and guide investment in resilient infrastructure. \n\nGPCP data supports climate education and capacity-building in local governments and NGOs, enabling evidence-based decision-making in the face of climate-related hazards. GPCP data is a valuable resource in education for teaching students about precipitation patterns, weather systems, and global hydrological cycles. With a powerful online tool (Giovanni, https://giovanni.gsfc.nasa.gov/giovanni/), users can explore the GPCP products without downloading data and software. For example, Giovanni users can generate precipitation maps, seasonal and interannual time series, Hovmoller diagrams, and more to understand precipitation patterns and climate events. In short, Giovanni allows students to explore real-world climate data, conduct analyses of precipitation trends, and understand the impacts of precipitation variability on different regions. In short, its use in classrooms and research projects encourages data literacy, critical thinking, and engagement with climate science, making it a powerful tool for both secondary and higher education."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "global precipitation distribution patterns, seasonal and interannual variations and trends, as well as large-scale phenomena such as monsoon and ENSO events. When the nominator needs global precipitation climatology information (e.g., research, education and training), they use GPCP products. With a powerful online tool (i.e., Giovanni), they can obtain different plots such as maps, time series, shapefiles, Hovmoller diagrams, provided by 21 plot types in Giovanni, without downloading data and software. \n\nAddressing the CDR standards of consistency and homogeneity is often time-consuming and costly. As a GPCP dataset curator at GES DISC, the nominator interacts with the dataset PIs who have put a lot of effort into developing the GPCP products that meet the CDR standards. Although there are other higher spatial and temporal resolution precipitation products available, the CDR standards may not be addressed properly and therefore may not be suitable for climate research and applications. The data format for the GPCP products is NetCDF, which is a user-friendly format commonly used in many tools (e.g., NASA GISS Panoply) and software packages (e.g., NCO). GES DISC provides user-friendly data services (e.g., https, OPeNDAP, subsetters, and Giovanni) to users with different data needs. Furthermore, GES DISC provides award-winning user services to answer questions ranging from data access to scientific information."
        }
      ]
    },
