        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The ASIA-AQ dataset provides comprehensive observations of atmospheric composition collected during a two-month airborne field study conducted in February-March of 2024. ASIA-AQ employed two research aircraft to sample key Asian megacities (Manila, Philippines; Seoul, South Korea; Bangkok, Thailand; and Kaohsiung, Taiwan). The NASA DC-8 carried a payload of 26 instruments for in situ sampling of gaseous and fine particle pollution accounting for over 100 individual compounds as well as particle number, size distributions, physical properties, and chemical composition. The NASA G-III carried a remote sensing payload that included a UV-Vis spectrometer to map distributions of two key pollutants, nitrogen dioxide and formaldehyde, and a high spectral resolution lidar to measure the 3-D distribution of ozone and fine particle pollution. At least four flight days were dedicated to each location, completing circuits to sample the city and surrounding areas three times per day. The data complements ground-based monitoring and satellite observations from South Korea’s Geostationary Environment Monitoring Spectrometer (GEMS) and local air quality monitoring networks. The dataset also includes observations from two Korean King Air aircraft flown in South Korea, ground-based remote sensors for aerosols and trace gases, and short-term research supersites in Seoul, Korea; Chiang Mai, Thailand; and intensive observation periods from a research network in southwestern Taiwan."
        },
        {
