          "schema:value": "ASIA-AQ fully exercised an integrated observing system for air quality that combined airborne measurements with air quality observations from satellites and regulatory ground-monitoring networks. Satellite observations of air quality on an hourly basis have only recently become available with South Korea’s GEMS instrument providing broad coverage over Asia from geostationary orbit. These satellite observations complement ground-based monitoring, which is spatially limited, but satellites observe the total atmospheric column and determining conditions at the surface can be challenging. Providing airborne observations to sample conditions aloft during a short field intensive provides critical information on how to best interpret satellite observations and use them to extend information from surface networks. The combined dataset also provides the most detailed characterization of the atmosphere for evaluation and improvement of air quality models. \nASIA-AQ supported building a stronger international science community through collaboration and partnering with local stakeholders to apply a unified strategy of multi-perspective observations across several Asian countries that included South Korea, the Philippines, Thailand, and Taiwan. The project's primary aim was to enhance both the specific understanding of localized air quality issues and the broader comprehension of shared challenges in interpreting satellite observations and modeling air quality. The project sought to deepen understanding of the factors influencing local air quality across Asia by integrating diverse observational methods and advanced modeling techniques. Airborne sampling was conducted with sampling strategies designed in close collaboration with local scientists, air quality agencies, and relevant governmental partners. This coordinated effort ensured comprehensive data collection and contextualized analysis. The initiative emphasized transparency and collaboration by openly sharing data throughout all project phases. Joint analyses are ongoing with local scientists and air quality agencies, and findings are being reported to local governments to inform policy and decision-making."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "As the Mission Scientist for ASIA-AQ, the nominator interacts with the ASIA-AQ data on a daily basis, both directly and through the analysis of team members. Routine telecons with the larger team are ongoing and smaller working groups also convene regularly. Results are proving new insights on the diversity of pollution sources, meteorological factors, and transboundary impacts influencing air quality outcomes and how they can be informed by better interpretation of satellite observations, assessment of ground monitoring networks, and data for the testing and improvement of air quality models. Rapid Science Synthesis reports for the public and policy makers are near completion for Thailand and South Korea. Results for the Philippines and Taiwan are under development. These reports will ultimately be supported by peer-reviewed publications that will be used to develop final science reports for each country."
        },
