    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:027",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "Atmospheric Infrared Sounder (AIRS)",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:027",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:atmospheric-science-space-weather"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "AIRS Science Team"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://airs.jpl.nasa.gov/data/get-data/standard-data/"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:nasa-ges-disc"
      ],
      "description": "AIRS is a hyperspectral infrared sounder on the NASA Aqua spacecraft covering the 3.7-15.4 μm infrared spectral region with 2378 channels and spatial resolution of 13.5 km at nadir. AIRS was launched into a 705 km altitude orbit on May 4, 2002, and has been in routine data gathering mode essentially uninterrupted since September 2002. The 1:30 PM ascending node and orbital altitude of the Aqua spacecraft orbit have been accurately maintained (until 2022) and daily (nearly) global coverage is essentially achieved from the ascending and descending orbits. The AIRS infrared radiances are operationally assimilated in all major global weather prediction systems. These radiances are also routinely used to retrieve vertical profiles of atmospheric temperature, water vapor, and key atmospheric constituents as well as cloud and surface parameters. The AIRS long record (since 2002) spans most of the 21st Century (until now) and the AIRS instrument is remarkably stable: with potential instrument drifts in time that are at least one order of magnitude smaller than the physical climate change temporal signals."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5281-9478",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Joao Teixeira",
      "identifier": {
        "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
        "propertyID": "ORCID",
        "value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5281-9478"
      },
      "schema:email": "teixeira@jpl.nasa.gov",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:jet-propulsion-laboratory-caltech-4800-oak-grove-drive-pasadena-ca-91109"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:jet-propulsion-laboratory-caltech-4800-oak-grove-drive-pasadena-ca-91109",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "Jet Propulsion Laboratory, Caltech, 4800 Oak Grove Drive, Pasadena, CA 91109"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:027",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:027",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:027",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5281-9478"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The infrared radiances measured by AIRS are used (as part of data assimilation or retrieval systems) to create three-dimensional depictions of atmospheric temperature and water vapor, as well as to provide a wealth of information on other gases and cloud properties. With 2378 spectral channels, AIRS has a spectral resolution more than 100 times greater than previous infrared sounders and provides more accurate information on the vertical profiles of atmospheric temperature and water vapor.\nA major advantage of the AIRS instrument as compared to weather balloons (the traditional way of providing temperature and water vapor profile observations for weather prediction models) is its global coverage because of its view from space. The coverage obtained from the launch of weather balloons over much of the Earth’s land masses and most of the oceans and ice surfaces is extremely small. In fact, AIRS soundings are equivalent to launching 300,000 weather balloons over the globe each day.\nThe infrared spectra measured by AIRS are also rich in information about clouds and gases such as ozone, carbon monoxide, carbon dioxide, and methane. The remarkably long and stable AIRS data record of the state of the atmosphere will be used as a unique climate data record for future generations to study how our Earth’s climate has changed in the earlier part of the 21st Century.\nThe AIRS mission started as a weather mission and, because of its long duration and remarkable stability, has now become a climate mission. From a weather perspective, AIRS radiances started to be assimilated early in the mission in global weather prediction systems, such as the ones at the European Centre for Medium-range Weather Forecasts (ECMWF), the US National Weather Service (NWS) and the US Navy. The assimilation of AIRS in global weather prediction systems was a major success from the start, so much so as to be considered by some studies as the most impactful single instrument ever in improving the accuracy of weather forecasts in these global prediction systems. Even today, AIRS is at the core of most global weather prediction and reanalysis systems.\nBy significantly improving the forecast accuracy of extreme weather events for more than 20 years, AIRS has had a major positive impact on individuals, in the context of disaster response and prediction, and as such a major impact on socioeconomic systems.\nOver the last 20 years, AIRS data has been utilized to make major scientific discoveries about the physics and composition of our atmosphere, from the discovery of new regional sources of atmospheric gravity waves, to confirming the global and regional trends of atmospheric carbon monoxide.\nBecause of its global nature and long duration, AIRS has had a significant impact on the study of the planet’s physical systems from a climate perspective. The long and stable AIRS data record provides a rich and unique depiction of how our climate is changing during the early 21st Century. In fact, because of its long duration and remarkable stability, AIRS radiance data has been used to accurately determine atmospheric temperature and water vapor trends, and to directly measure the impact of the increase of carbon dioxide in infrared radiances and as such confirm a critical foundation of the science of global warming."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator started using AIRS more than 20 years ago, both from a weather prediction and a more science driven perspective. The impressive AIRS impact in improving weather prediction was clear from the start and the need to understand these improvements drove much research at the time. The detailed AIRS observations of the vertical structure of temperature and water vapor allowed for the first time for a variety of studies focused on better characterizing and understanding global atmospheric thermodynamics. The nominator was involved in this research, in which the team tried to address questions such as what controls the vertical thermodynamic structure of the tropical and subtropical atmosphere? What is the relation between clouds in the planetary boundary layer (PBL) and the vertical thermodynamic structure? What is the scaling behavior of atmospheric thermodynamic properties? How is global atmospheric thermodynamics changing and impacting climate change?\nSince 2011, they have been the AIRS Science Team Lead, and in this role, they have been involved in all aspects of the AIRS mission, from calibration and data validation to community coordination from the science and applications perspectives. They continue to do research using AIRS data in a variety of ways, to advocate for a broader utilization of AIRS data, for the continuity of hyperspectral infrared observations and for potential new missions such as a mission to better observe global PBL thermodynamics."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
