      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:access-nri-australian-national-university-anu-building-69-5-liversidge-st-acton",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "ACCESS-NRI, Australian National University (ANU), Building 69, 5 Liversidge St., Acton ACT 2601 Australia"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:028",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:028",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:028",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9442-5709"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "Datasets are critical to climate research as well as IPCC reports, having direct impact on international policy decisions. The Coupled Model Intercomparison Project 6 (CMIP6) is a widely reused dataset and a very good example of how the global intercomparison design is part of the key value and benefit. No single climate model will be sufficient; strength comes from using many models together. It's an exemplar for international use and reuse as well as collaboration of science and infrastructure for data sharing. The absence of CMIP6 (and future versions) would be detrimental to climate research and downstream climate data and policy work."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator was formerly the data manager/node operator of the Australian ESGF node hosted at NCI Australia. For example, during CMIP6, Australia's ACCESS-ESM1.5 submission to CMIP6 included 323,856 datasets (totalling 670.33 TB) and had over 3 million downloads. The full CMIP6 data holdings at NCI had >400 unique users running 500,000 compute jobs per month directly on the data and 3.5PB of downloads through the NCI ESGF data node. They now work for Australia's Climate Simulator (ACCESS-NRI), a national research infrastructure supporting the next generation of climate software that will be used for the CMIP7 Australia contribution."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:028",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 29,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Atmospheric Science, Space Weather"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "Coupled Model Intercomparison Project 6 (CMIP6)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Various across modelling centres"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://aims2.llnl.gov/search"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "Earth System Grid Federation"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "From: https://www.climatechangeinaustralia.gov.au/en/overview/cmip6-comparison-cmip5/ \n\nGlobal climate models are an important part of the toolbox for examining future climate change. The use of the models for this purpose is facilitated by the Coupled Model Intercomparison Project (CMIP), an international collaboration that brings together global climate modelling from institutes around the world. CMIP has grown to be a vital resource for climate research but also for making projections of the future climate for decisionmakers. The CMIP effort spans several decades, from early more informal science collaborations through to the latest iteration, the Coupled Model Intercomparison Project phase 6 (CMIP6).\n\nThe CMIP6 ensemble of model simulations was produced in 2015-2021 and was a key input to the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) Sixth Assessment Report. It involves many models contributed from numerous countries around the world.\n\nThe CMIP6 simulations relevant to climate projections are the ‘Historical’ simulations and the ‘ScenarioMIP’ simulations. The Historical simulations cover the 1850-2014 period and use observed greenhouse gas concentrations and other forcings. The ScenarioMIP simulations include simulations of the 21st Century for the Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (SSPs), which provide trajectories of future global emissions of greenhouse gases to explore future change. This is a difference from the previous, CMIP5, phase of CMIP, which used Representative Concentration Pathways (RCPs). We have access to up to 50 CMIP6 models for the Historical period, with at least 33 models available for the four most commonly used (‘Tier 1’) SSPs (some individual SSPs have more). The future emissions trajectories for the Tier 1 SSPs range from SSP1-2.6, consistent with a future in which we broadly meet the goals of the Paris Agreement, to SSP5-8.5, reflecting a scenario with extremely high future emissions."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Datasets are critical to climate research as well as IPCC reports, having direct impact on international policy decisions. The Coupled Model Intercomparison Project 6 (CMIP6) is a widely reused dataset and a very good example of how the global intercomparison design is part of the key value and benefit. No single climate model will be sufficient; strength comes from using many models together. It's an exemplar for international use and reuse as well as collaboration of science and infrastructure for data sharing. The absence of CMIP6 (and future versions) would be detrimental to climate research and downstream climate data and policy work."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator was formerly the data manager/node operator of the Australian ESGF node hosted at NCI Australia. For example, during CMIP6, Australia's ACCESS-ESM1.5 submission to CMIP6 included 323,856 datasets (totalling 670.33 TB) and had over 3 million downloads. The full CMIP6 data holdings at NCI had >400 unique users running 500,000 compute jobs per month directly on the data and 3.5PB of downloads through the NCI ESGF data node. They now work for Australia's Climate Simulator (ACCESS-NRI), a national research infrastructure supporting the next generation of climate software that will be used for the CMIP7 Australia contribution."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Eyring, V., Bony, S., Meehl, G. A., Senior, C. A., Stevens, B., Stouffer, R. J., & Taylor, K. E. (2016). Overview of the coupled model intercomparison project phase 6 (Cmip6) experimental design and organization. Geoscientific Model Development, 9(5), 1937–1958. https://doi.org/10.5194/gmd-9-1937-2016"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Kelsey Druken"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Email Address",
          "schema:value": "kelsey.druken@anu.edu.au"
        },
        {
