    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:029",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:029",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:029",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4481-1377",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3095-8960",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3134-0449"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "ARTMIP has a profound impact on the interpretation of AR characteristics in scientific research. \n\nARs are long, narrow, synoptic scale features that are *often but not necessarily* associated with extratropical cyclones. They have the ability to transport large amounts of water vapor across latitudes and are key players in Earth’s hydrological cycle. Yet, they are difficult to define. Ask a scientist or forecaster how to define an AR and you might get an answer like “I’ll know it when I see it.” For gridded datasets such as reanalyses, or model simulations, this vague definition does not easily lend itself to detection code requiring precise instructions for identification. Thus, for each unique science question or purpose, a different algorithm might be developed or used. By 2016, with the exponential growth in AR research, the AR community came together in a grassroots effort to quantify uncertainty across algorithms and make sense of the conflicting ways ARs were being characterized. What types of results were robust across methods? What types of results were not? We found AR frequency of occurrence, one of the most common metrics attached to ARs, is highly sensitive to the method of detection. For climate change projections, detection methods can even influence the sign of the trend. This has enormous consequences for understanding the impact of ARs on localized climates and has real societal relevance for adaptation planning. \n\nARTMIP essentially began the practice of uncertainty quantification across ARDTs, and it continues to show why it matters. Before ARTMIP published its first paper, often, peer-reviewed journal articles simply used one ARDT with no discussion of how results would be different if the AR was identified differently. Because of ARTMIP, articles almost always discuss metric uncertainty based on definition. For climate change studies, the community has recognized that one ARDT may not be sufficient. We have shown that bounding uncertainty is needed, whether that takes the form of threshold sensitivity calculations, or applying consensus or multiple ARDTs to the problem. Also and equally important, ARTMIP has shown that some metrics are robust across methods. For example, almost all ARDTs will detect the strongest and most intense ARs; it is the weak storms where widespread disagreement occurs. \n\nAn ARDT may be suitable for some types of science questions (or regions), but not others – another key ARTMIP finding. For example, many global ARDTs are appropriate for midlatitude research topics, but not appropriate for polar locations. Often polar ARs are different enough from midlatitude ARs that an ARDT designed for global detection may not accurately capture the polar AR. This can be shown by comparing catalogues from polar-designed ARDTs to general global ARDTs."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "According to the Glossary of Meteorology, an Atmospheric River (AR) is 'a long, narrow, and transient corridor of strong horizontal water vapor transport that is typically associated with a low-level jet stream ahead of the cold front of an extratropical cyclone'. What appears to be a precise definition at first glance, actually offers countless ways to identify and track ARs in practise. ARs occur in different meteorological contexts with a broad range of physical and geometrical properties. Algorithmically, their identification can be achieved through rather basic thresholding approaches or more sophisticated methods, e.g., more recently, advanced image processing and machine learning techniques. Each individual configuration and algorithmic approach will yield a distinct collection of ARs, an AR catalog.\n\nThe ARTMIP dataset is a transformative resource for and beyond the AR research community because it provides a coordinated and standardized framework to compare the plethora of these different AR catalogs. Without ARTMIP, the quantification of uncertainties in AR behavior, impacts, and climate change responses would remain largely speculative. Before ARTMIP was brought to life in 2016, this fragmentation had limited the ability to compare results or draw robust conclusions across studies of AR dynamics and impacts.\n\nBy reducing methodological biases through standardization, ARTMIP enables consistent evaluation of what we know (and don't know) about ARs. It is a vital resource for rigorous intercomparison and benchmarking. ARTMIP empowers researchers to systematically assess how ARs contribute to extreme precipitation, floods, and hydrological variability under both current and future climates. Crucially, its documentation and curation also make AR-focused data accessible and attractive to researchers from other fields, such as hydrology, ecology, agriculture, and risk management, fostering interdisciplinary collaboration that would otherwise be hindered.\n\nTo the best of my knowledge and in contrast to the AR community, many other communities (e.g., identification of frontal systems or mesoscale convective systems) lack coordinated, multi-model, standardized datasets as provided by ARTMIP, making it difficult to separate methodological differences from true scientific uncertainty. ARTMIP fills this gap, providing a framework to evaluate and propagate uncertainties systematically, which is essential for both scientific understanding and anticipating AR impacts.\n\nOverall, I believe that ARTMIP enables science that would otherwise be impossible: it provides a shared reference, reduces methodological biases, quantifies uncertainties, and opens AR research to broader, interdisciplinary applications. Its availability really enhances the reproducibility, comparability, and impact of AR studies and sets a benchmark for coordinated datasets in other fields."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "3",
          "schema:text": "The ARTMIP dataset is a foundational resource for studying the dynamics and impacts of atmospheric rivers (ARs) while addressing uncertainty in AR science. \nARs are long, narrow, and transient corridors of intense water vapor transport in the lower atmosphere. They play a crucial role in the global water cycle by carrying moisture from the tropics to the poles, replenishing freshwater in the midlatitudes, and driving extreme weather and disasters. Despite their importance, identifying ARs objectively is challenging because their definition is difficult to translate into an algorithm for automated detection. As a result, numerous AR detection tools (ARDTs) have been developed over the past decade, each tailored to specific regions or research questions, producing a wide variety of approaches.\nThis diversity has expanded our diagnostic capacity and deepened our understanding of AR dynamics and impacts, but it has also introduced uncertainty into AR science. Through a coordinated community effort, ARTMIP has benchmarked and compared most existing ARDTs. The resulting dataset—the largest ensemble of AR catalogues ever produced—serves as a key reference for both research and operational applications.\nBy enabling direct comparison among ARDTs, ARTMIP has built confidence in the large-scale patterns of AR transport and the detection of intense ARs, while revealing discrepancies in weak ARs and in polar and subtropical regions. This ability to systematically assess uncertainty in AR identification has been transformative. ARTMIP data underpin studies of AR climatology, their role in regional weather and hydrology, their contribution to extreme events and disasters, their representation in climate models, and their evolution in past and future climates. The dataset has also expanded research to less-studied regions such as Southeast Asia, Africa, Australia, South America, the Arctic, and Antarctica.\nBeyond advancing scientific understanding, ARTMIP has strengthened education and collaboration within the AR community. Its open and standardized framework supports students and early-career researchers in developing and testing detection methods, fostering transparency, reproducibility, and shared learning. This collective effort has enhanced our ability to forecast and assess the risks of floods, droughts, and other hydrological extremes, directly supporting disaster preparedness, infrastructure resilience, and sustainable water-resource management. By improving our understanding and prediction of AR-driven hazards, ARTMIP builds capacity for communities to adapt to a changing climate.\nThe ARTMIP dataset grew from the idea that how we identify ARs influences what we can learn about them. Over the past decade, this evolving dataset has consistently demonstrated why uncertainty matters in AR science and has provided the foundation for sound, reliable, and impactful research across the global AR community."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator is the primary contact and curator for the ARTMIP database. Bridging both weather and climate communities, ARTMIP was formed in 2016 so that the team could address the critical need for uncertainty quantification across multiple AR definitions, as well as provide context for published results. Since then, they have assumed a leadership role in curating the database, as well as general ARTMIP project management. They interact with many researchers in the AR community across the world, facilitating the use of ARTMIP data, as well as welcome new members into the ARTMIP community. Their team is currently working on a new Tier 2 sensitivity study focused on the polar regions, and are developing an ARTMIP synthesis paper to summarize what has been learned about AR uncertainty across all of their experiments."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "The nominator has been actively working on atmospheric river (AR) science for a bit less than three years now. In their first study, they and their colleagues decided to contribute to the body of existing AR catalogs as they had identified significant opportunities to improve AR detection and tracking at a fully global scale. The ARTMIP dataset has been a vital source of information and a benchmark for this work: the scientific documentation through its website and publications not only offered a great overview of state-of-the-art knowledge on global AR activity, it also helped them to understand which catalogs they should compare to their own AR catalog. Thanks to ARTMIP, they were able to report these important uncertainties in their now published study. The very active ARTMIP community has embraced their contribution and they have now integrated their AR catalog into the ARTMIP dataset. This underlines the value and continuous advancement of ARTMIP as an publicly available, FAIR dataset. As a part of the ARTMIP initiative, the nominator is currently trying to better quantify uncertainties of AR detection in polar regions, a region in which significant changes to AR dynamics are expected due to climate change. The ARTMIP journey goes on."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "3",
          "schema:text": "The nominator is a postdoctoral researcher working on atmospheric river (AR) science. They first became familiar with the ARTMIP dataset during their PhD, which focused on the dynamics and impacts of ARs from a complexity science perspective. The results of the ARTMIP project made them aware of the substantial uncertainty in AR detection and tracking, and how this uncertainty propagates through AR science. Since then, they have systematically included uncertainty assessments in their research by comparing multiple, conceptually distinct AR detection tools (ARDTs).\nTheir research focuses on analyzing ARs as interacting components of Earth’s climate system. For this, it is crucial to have catalogues that trace ARs continuously across space and time, from genesis to termination. ARTMIP helped them recognize that, despite the diversity of ARDTs, few catalogues provided the global, long-term, high-resolution coverage their analyses required. This motivated them to develop their own ARDT, building upon the knowledge curated by ARTMIP. The result is the PIKART catalogue—a global, high-resolution (0.5°, 6-hour) dataset of AR trajectories from 1940 to 2023, providing both Eulerian and Lagrangian descriptions of AR flow.\nThe ARTMIP community has been welcoming and supportive. The PIKART catalogue is now part of the ARTMIP database and contributes to an ongoing study on ARs in polar regions. Beyond its scientific value, ARTMIP offers young scientists an inclusive platform for collaboration, visibility, and career growth."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:029",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 30,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Atmospheric Science, Space Weather"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "ARTMIP Database"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "(Committee and Co-Chairs): Christine Shields, Travis O’Brien, Jonathan Rutz, Allison Collow, Juan Lora,, Paul Ullrich, L. Ruby Leung, F. Martin Ralph, Michael Wehner, Ashley Payne"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://gdex.ucar.edu/datasets/d651012/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "Research Data Archive, NSF NCAR"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "Tier 1 DOI: 10.5065/D62R3QFS; Tier 2 - CMIP DOI: 10.26024/S4P7-PF13; Tier 2 - High Resolution DOI: 10.5065/VMB6-FM59; Tier 2 - ReanalysisDOI: 10.26024/RAWV-YX53; Tier 2 - Paleo DOI: 10.5065/MTDW-0V06"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Christine A. Shields, ARTMIP"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "ARTMIP is the Atmospheric River Tracking Method Intercomparison Project. The goal of ARTMIP is to understand and quantify uncertainties in atmospheric river (AR) science based on choice of detection/tracking methodology. It is a large community effort with contributors across the globe including North and South America, Asia, Africa, and Australia, all of whom have volunteered their time to provide the community with data to understand and interpret scientific results. \nARs are dynamically driven, filamentary structures often associated with extreme storms and heavy precipitation. ARTMIP provides a large set of AR catalogues, each created with a unique AR detection tool (ARDT). These catalogues are designed to be compared; metrics such as frequency, duration, and footprint can be consistently quantified across ARDTs using the same exact dataset. ARTMIP is divided into “Tier 1” and “Tier 2” where Tier 1 is the baseline control and consists of AR catalogues created using data generated from MERRA-2. Tier 2 includes a wide variety of sensitivity datasets and currently includes catalogues created from: (1) ERA5, (2) JRA55 and JRA55-C, (3) 25km climate model output from CAM5 historical and RCP8.5 scenario simulations, (4) select CMIP5/6 historical and high emissions scenario simulations, (5) CESM2 paleoclimate data for low CO2 (mean state) as well as variations on orbital parameters (seasonality). ARTMIP is currently curating ARDT catalogues designed to examine differences in polar regions which will be released soon as Tier 2 Polar\n\nARTMIP website: https://ncar.github.io/ARTMIP/intro.html \n\nClimate Data Guide entry: https://climatedataguide.ucar.edu/climate-data/atmospheric-rivers-atmospheric-river-tracking-method-intercomparison-project-artmip"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nARTMIP has a profound impact on the interpretation of AR characteristics in scientific research. \n\nARs are long, narrow, synoptic scale features that are *often but not necessarily* associated with extratropical cyclones. They have the ability to transport large amounts of water vapor across latitudes and are key players in Earth’s hydrological cycle. Yet, they are difficult to define. Ask a scientist or forecaster how to define an AR and you might get an answer like “I’ll know it when I see it.” For gridded datasets such as reanalyses, or model simulations, this vague definition does not easily lend itself to detection code requiring precise instructions for identification. Thus, for each unique science question or purpose, a different algorithm might be developed or used. By 2016, with the exponential growth in AR research, the AR community came together in a grassroots effort to quantify uncertainty across algorithms and make sense of the conflicting ways ARs were being characterized. What types of results were robust across methods? What types of results were not? We found AR frequency of occurrence, one of the most common metrics attached to ARs, is highly sensitive to the method of detection. For climate change projections, detection methods can even influence the sign of the trend. This has enormous consequences for understanding the impact of ARs on localized climates and has real societal relevance for adaptation planning. \n\nARTMIP essentially began the practice of uncertainty quantification across ARDTs, and it continues to show why it matters. Before ARTMIP published its first paper, often, peer-reviewed journal articles simply used one ARDT with no discussion of how results would be different if the AR was identified differently. Because of ARTMIP, articles almost always discuss metric uncertainty based on definition. For climate change studies, the community has recognized that one ARDT may not be sufficient. We have shown that bounding uncertainty is needed, whether that takes the form of threshold sensitivity calculations, or applying consensus or multiple ARDTs to the problem. Also and equally important, ARTMIP has shown that some metrics are robust across methods. For example, almost all ARDTs will detect the strongest and most intense ARs; it is the weak storms where widespread disagreement occurs. \n\nAn ARDT may be suitable for some types of science questions (or regions), but not others – another key ARTMIP finding. For example, many global ARDTs are appropriate for midlatitude research topics, but not appropriate for polar locations. Often polar ARs are different enough from midlatitude ARs that an ARDT designed for global detection may not accurately capture the polar AR. This can be shown by comparing catalogues from polar-designed ARDTs to general global ARDTs. \n\nNominator 2:\nAccording to the Glossary of Meteorology, an Atmospheric River (AR) is 'a long, narrow, and transient corridor of strong horizontal water vapor transport that is typically associated with a low-level jet stream ahead of the cold front of an extratropical cyclone'. What appears to be a precise definition at first glance, actually offers countless ways to identify and track ARs in practise. ARs occur in different meteorological contexts with a broad range of physical and geometrical properties. Algorithmically, their identification can be achieved through rather basic thresholding approaches or more sophisticated methods, e.g., more recently, advanced image processing and machine learning techniques. Each individual configuration and algorithmic approach will yield a distinct collection of ARs, an AR catalog.\n\nThe ARTMIP dataset is a transformative resource for and beyond the AR research community because it provides a coordinated and standardized framework to compare the plethora of these different AR catalogs. Without ARTMIP, the quantification of uncertainties in AR behavior, impacts, and climate change responses would remain largely speculative. Before ARTMIP was brought to life in 2016, this fragmentation had limited the ability to compare results or draw robust conclusions across studies of AR dynamics and impacts.\n\nBy reducing methodological biases through standardization, ARTMIP enables consistent evaluation of what we know (and don't know) about ARs. It is a vital resource for rigorous intercomparison and benchmarking. ARTMIP empowers researchers to systematically assess how ARs contribute to extreme precipitation, floods, and hydrological variability under both current and future climates. Crucially, its documentation and curation also make AR-focused data accessible and attractive to researchers from other fields, such as hydrology, ecology, agriculture, and risk management, fostering interdisciplinary collaboration that would otherwise be hindered.\n\nTo the best of my knowledge and in contrast to the AR community, many other communities (e.g., identification of frontal systems or mesoscale convective systems) lack coordinated, multi-model, standardized datasets as provided by ARTMIP, making it difficult to separate methodological differences from true scientific uncertainty. ARTMIP fills this gap, providing a framework to evaluate and propagate uncertainties systematically, which is essential for both scientific understanding and anticipating AR impacts.\n\nOverall, I believe that ARTMIP enables science that would otherwise be impossible: it provides a shared reference, reduces methodological biases, quantifies uncertainties, and opens AR research to broader, interdisciplinary applications. Its availability really enhances the reproducibility, comparability, and impact of AR studies and sets a benchmark for coordinated datasets in other fields.\n\nNominator 3:\nThe ARTMIP dataset is a foundational resource for studying the dynamics and impacts of atmospheric rivers (ARs) while addressing uncertainty in AR science. \nARs are long, narrow, and transient corridors of intense water vapor transport in the lower atmosphere. They play a crucial role in the global water cycle by carrying moisture from the tropics to the poles, replenishing freshwater in the midlatitudes, and driving extreme weather and disasters. Despite their importance, identifying ARs objectively is challenging because their definition is difficult to translate into an algorithm for automated detection. As a result, numerous AR detection tools (ARDTs) have been developed over the past decade, each tailored to specific regions or research questions, producing a wide variety of approaches.\nThis diversity has expanded our diagnostic capacity and deepened our understanding of AR dynamics and impacts, but it has also introduced uncertainty into AR science. Through a coordinated community effort, ARTMIP has benchmarked and compared most existing ARDTs. The resulting dataset—the largest ensemble of AR catalogues ever produced—serves as a key reference for both research and operational applications.\nBy enabling direct comparison among ARDTs, ARTMIP has built confidence in the large-scale patterns of AR transport and the detection of intense ARs, while revealing discrepancies in weak ARs and in polar and subtropical regions. This ability to systematically assess uncertainty in AR identification has been transformative. ARTMIP data underpin studies of AR climatology, their role in regional weather and hydrology, their contribution to extreme events and disasters, their representation in climate models, and their evolution in past and future climates. The dataset has also expanded research to less-studied regions such as Southeast Asia, Africa, Australia, South America, the Arctic, and Antarctica.\nBeyond advancing scientific understanding, ARTMIP has strengthened education and collaboration within the AR community. Its open and standardized framework supports students and early-career researchers in developing and testing detection methods, fostering transparency, reproducibility, and shared learning. This collective effort has enhanced our ability to forecast and assess the risks of floods, droughts, and other hydrological extremes, directly supporting disaster preparedness, infrastructure resilience, and sustainable water-resource management. By improving our understanding and prediction of AR-driven hazards, ARTMIP builds capacity for communities to adapt to a changing climate.\nThe ARTMIP dataset grew from the idea that how we identify ARs influences what we can learn about them. Over the past decade, this evolving dataset has consistently demonstrated why uncertainty matters in AR science and has provided the foundation for sound, reliable, and impactful research across the global AR community."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nThe nominator is the primary contact and curator for the ARTMIP database. Bridging both weather and climate communities, ARTMIP was formed in 2016 so that the team could address the critical need for uncertainty quantification across multiple AR definitions, as well as provide context for published results. Since then, they have assumed a leadership role in curating the database, as well as general ARTMIP project management. They interact with many researchers in the AR community across the world, facilitating the use of ARTMIP data, as well as welcome new members into the ARTMIP community. Their team is currently working on a new Tier 2 sensitivity study focused on the polar regions, and are developing an ARTMIP synthesis paper to summarize what has been learned about AR uncertainty across all of their experiments. \n\nNominator 2:\nThe nominator has been actively working on atmospheric river (AR) science for a bit less than three years now. In their first study, they and their colleagues decided to contribute to the body of existing AR catalogs as they had identified significant opportunities to improve AR detection and tracking at a fully global scale. The ARTMIP dataset has been a vital source of information and a benchmark for this work: the scientific documentation through its website and publications not only offered a great overview of state-of-the-art knowledge on global AR activity, it also helped them to understand which catalogs they should compare to their own AR catalog. Thanks to ARTMIP, they were able to report these important uncertainties in their now published study. The very active ARTMIP community has embraced their contribution and they have now integrated their AR catalog into the ARTMIP dataset. This underlines the value and continuous advancement of ARTMIP as an publicly available, FAIR dataset. As a part of the ARTMIP initiative, the nominator is currently trying to better quantify uncertainties of AR detection in polar regions, a region in which significant changes to AR dynamics are expected due to climate change. The ARTMIP journey goes on.\n\nNominator 3:\nThe nominator is a postdoctoral researcher working on atmospheric river (AR) science. They first became familiar with the ARTMIP dataset during their PhD, which focused on the dynamics and impacts of ARs from a complexity science perspective. The results of the ARTMIP project made them aware of the substantial uncertainty in AR detection and tracking, and how this uncertainty propagates through AR science. Since then, they have systematically included uncertainty assessments in their research by comparing multiple, conceptually distinct AR detection tools (ARDTs).\nTheir research focuses on analyzing ARs as interacting components of Earth’s climate system. For this, it is crucial to have catalogues that trace ARs continuously across space and time, from genesis to termination. ARTMIP helped them recognize that, despite the diversity of ARDTs, few catalogues provided the global, long-term, high-resolution coverage their analyses required. This motivated them to develop their own ARDT, building upon the knowledge curated by ARTMIP. The result is the PIKART catalogue—a global, high-resolution (0.5°, 6-hour) dataset of AR trajectories from 1940 to 2023, providing both Eulerian and Lagrangian descriptions of AR flow.\nThe ARTMIP community has been welcoming and supportive. The PIKART catalogue is now part of the ARTMIP database and contributes to an ongoing study on ARs in polar regions. Beyond its scientific value, ARTMIP offers young scientists an inclusive platform for collaboration, visibility, and career growth."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "ARTMIP-organized papers:\n\nExperimental Design: \nShields, C. A., Rutz, J. J., Leung, L.-Y., Ralph, F. M., Wehner, M., Kawzenuk, B., Lora, J. M., McClenny, E., Osborne, T., Payne, A. E., Ullrich, P., Gershunov, A., Goldenson, N., Guan, B., Qian, Y., Ramos, A. M., Sarangi, C., Sellars, S., Gorodetskaya, I., Kashinath, K., Kurlin, V., Mahoney, K., Muszynski, G., Pierce, R., Subramanian, A. C., Tome, R., Waliser, D., Walton, D., Wick, G., Wilson, A., Lavers, D., Prabhat, Collow, A., Krishnan, H., Magnusdottir, G., and Nguyen, P.: Atmospheric River Tracking Method Intercomparison Project (ARTMIP): project goals and experimental design, Geosci. Model Dev., 11, 2455-2474, https://doi.org/10.5194/gmd-11-2455-2018, 2018.\n\nTier 1:\nRutz, J.J, Shields, C.A., Lora, J.M, Payne, A.E., Guan, B., Ullrich, P., O’Brien, T., Leung, L.-Y., Ralph, F.M., Wehner, M., Brands, S., Collow, A., Goldenson, N., Gorodetskaya, I., Griffith, H., Hagos, S., Kashinath, K., Kawzenuk, B., Krishnan, H., Kurlin, V., Lavers, D., Magnusdottir, G., Mahoney, K., McClenny, E., Muszynski, G., Nguyen, P.D., Prabhat, Qian, Y., Ramos, A.M., Sarangi, C., Sellars, S., Shulgina, T., Tome, R., Waliser, D., Walton, D., Wick, G., Wilson, A., Viale, M.: The Atmospheric River Tracking Method Intercomparison Project (ARTMIP): Quantifying Uncertainties in Atmospheric River Climatology, Journal of Geophysical Research-Atmospheres , https://doi.org/10.1029/2019JD030936, 2019.\n\nTier 2 - CMIP Climate Change: \nO’Brien, Travis Allen and Wehner, Michael F and Payne, Ashley E. and Shields, Christine A and Rutz, Jonathan J. and Leung, L. Ruby and Ralph, F. Martin and Marquardt Collow, Allison B. and Guan, Bin and Lora, Juan Manuel and et al., (2022) Increases in Future AR Count and Size: Overview of the ARTMIP Tier 2 CMIP5/6 Experiment. JGR-A https://doi.org/10.1029/2021JD036013.\n\nTier 2 - Reanalysis:\nCollow, A.B., Shields, C.A., Guan, B., Kim, S., Lora, J.M., McClenny, E.E., Nardi, K., Payne, A., Reid, K., Shearer, E. J. , Tome, R., Wille, J.D., Ramos, A.M., Gorodetskaya, I.V., Leung, L.R., O’Brien, T.A., Ralph, F.M., Rutz, J. Ullirich, P.A., Wehner, M., (2022) An Overview of ARTMIP’s Tier 2 Reanalysis Intercomparison: Uncertainty in the Detection of Atmospheric Rivers and their Associated Precipitation, Journal of Geophysical Research, Atmospheres, https://doi.org/10.1029/2021JD036155.\n\nTIer 2 - High Resolution Climate Change:\nShields, C. A., Payne, A. E., Shearer, E. J., Wehner, M. F., O’Brien, T. A., Rutz, J. J., Leung, L.R., Ralph, F. M., Collow, A. B. M., Ullrich, P. A. Ullrich, Dong, Q., Gershunov, A., Griffith, H., Guan, B., Lora, J. M., Lu, M., McClenny, E., Nardi, K. M., Pan, M., Qian, Y., Ramos, A. M. Ramos, Shulgina, T., Viale, M., Sarangi, C., Tomé, R., Zarzycki, C. (2023). Future atmospheric rivers and impacts on precipitation: Overview of the ARTMIP Tier 2 high-resolution global warming experiment. Geophysical Research Letters, 50, e2022GL102091. https://doi.org/10.1029/2022GL102091\n\nTier 2 - Paleoclimate: \nRush, W. D., Lora, J. M., Skinner, C. B., Menemenlis, S. A., Shields, C. A., Ullrich, P., O’Brien, T., A., Brands, S., Guan, B., Mattingly, K.S., McClenny, E., Nardi, K., Nellikkattil, A., Ramos A., Reid, K.J., Shearer, E., Tomé, R., Wille, J.D., Leung, L.R., Ralph, F., M., Rutz, J.J., Wehner, M., Zhang, Z., Lu, M., Quagraine, K.T (2025). Atmospheric river detection under changing seasonality and mean-state climate: ARTMIP tier 2 paleoclimate experiments. Journal of Geophysical Research: Atmospheres, 130, e2024JD042222. https://doi.org/10.1029/2024JD042222."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "There are 30 peer-reviewed publications (thus far) currently highlighted on the ARTMIP project webpage. Researchers who use the ARTMIP database are eligible to submit their publications for highlight, so this number reflects both ARTMIP-organized publications and individual research efforts that use ARTMIP data. Note that the publications listed on our page do not necessarily reflect the number of publications that have indeed used ARTMIP data. Many individual researchers download data from the ARTMIP repository but do not necessarily contact us to be highlighted. \n\nhttps://ncar.github.io/ARTMIP/publications.html"
        },
