    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:030",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "RELAB spectral library at Brown University",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:030",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:earth-planetary-materials"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Carle Pieters, Takehiro Hiroi, Ralph Milliken (Brown U RELAB)"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://sites.brown.edu/relab/relab-spectral-database/"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:https-pds-speclib-rsl-wustl-edu-search-aspx-catalog-relab"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "Takehiro Hiroi, Ralph Milliken (Brown U RELAB)"
        }
      ],
      "description": "Founded by Professor Carle Pieters in the 1980s, the Reflectance Experiment Laboratory (RELAB) at Brown University is supported by NASA as a multi-user spectroscopy facility. The overall purpose of the design and operation of the RELAB bidirectional spectrometer is to obtain high precision, high spectral resolution, bidirectional reflectance spectra of earth and planetary materials. Spectroscopic data acquired in the laboratory are all made publicly available and provide the interpretive foundation upon which compositional information about unexplored or unsampled planetary surfaces is derived from remotely obtained reflectance spectra. Laboratory time can be made available at no charge to investigators who are in funded NASA programs to measure planetary materials with two operational spectrometers: (1) a near-ultraviolet, visible, and near-infrared bidirectional spectrometer and (2) a near-, mid-, and far-infrared FT-IR spectrometer. One of the key elements of its design is the ability to measure samples using viewing geometries specified by the user. This allows investigators to simulate the geometry of natural observing conditions for particulate samples as small as 10 mg. The laboratory has been operational for over 4 decades, its database includes over 20,000 spectra, and it is now administered by Professor Ralph Milliken and Dr. Takehiro Hiroi."
    },
