      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:030",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:030",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2745-3240"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "There are two visible/shortwave infrared databases of earth and planetary materials. Nearly every solar system discovery is based on the prescient work of Roger Clark at USGS and, separately, Carle Pieters at Brown University to curate high quality collections of measurements of what geological materials look like in the visible and near infrared. These are repositories openly available to any investigator to download spectra and compare to what they observe with telescopes, spacecraft, or field and lab studies on Earth. Clay minerals on Mars, sodium carbonates on Ceres, pure sulfur on Io, serpentines on asteroids all relied on these databases. Remote sensing on Earth too uses these for albedo properties. Each has strengths: USGS has separate associated files that contain an independent characterization of composition, including impurities while RELAB lets any research submit a (reasonable #) of samples for measurement, and thus is more diverse in range. Most of the major discoveries of planetary science would not have been possible without these terrestrial-lab libraries. Both over 40 years old, the USGS and NASA/Brown RELAB spectral libraries continue to have influence across remote sensing and laboratory spectroscopy communities alike."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "I first started using as a graduate student when we had odd spectra from Mars. Turned out one set were carbonates, but slightly weird hydrated Mg carbonates rather than a calcite rich limestone. Another set were prehnite, a Ca-Al silicate hydroxide that uniquely indicates subgreenschist metamorphic conditions of formation. Still another set were alunite, an Al sulfate hydroxide that indicates acidic groundwater springs. The pre-existing databases were essential to the discoveries of the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter team"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:030",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 31,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Earth & Planetary Materials"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "RELAB spectral library at Brown University"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Carle Pieters, Takehiro Hiroi, Ralph Milliken (Brown U RELAB)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://sites.brown.edu/relab/relab-spectral-database/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "https://pds-speclib.rsl.wustl.edu/search.aspx?catalog=RELAB"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "doi: 10.17189/1519032"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Takehiro Hiroi, Ralph Milliken (Brown U RELAB)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "Founded by Professor Carle Pieters in the 1980s, the Reflectance Experiment Laboratory (RELAB) at Brown University is supported by NASA as a multi-user spectroscopy facility. The overall purpose of the design and operation of the RELAB bidirectional spectrometer is to obtain high precision, high spectral resolution, bidirectional reflectance spectra of earth and planetary materials. Spectroscopic data acquired in the laboratory are all made publicly available and provide the interpretive foundation upon which compositional information about unexplored or unsampled planetary surfaces is derived from remotely obtained reflectance spectra. Laboratory time can be made available at no charge to investigators who are in funded NASA programs to measure planetary materials with two operational spectrometers: (1) a near-ultraviolet, visible, and near-infrared bidirectional spectrometer and (2) a near-, mid-, and far-infrared FT-IR spectrometer. One of the key elements of its design is the ability to measure samples using viewing geometries specified by the user. This allows investigators to simulate the geometry of natural observing conditions for particulate samples as small as 10 mg. The laboratory has been operational for over 4 decades, its database includes over 20,000 spectra, and it is now administered by Professor Ralph Milliken and Dr. Takehiro Hiroi."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "There are two visible/shortwave infrared databases of earth and planetary materials. Nearly every solar system discovery is based on the prescient work of Roger Clark at USGS and, separately, Carle Pieters at Brown University to curate high quality collections of measurements of what geological materials look like in the visible and near infrared. These are repositories openly available to any investigator to download spectra and compare to what they observe with telescopes, spacecraft, or field and lab studies on Earth. Clay minerals on Mars, sodium carbonates on Ceres, pure sulfur on Io, serpentines on asteroids all relied on these databases. Remote sensing on Earth too uses these for albedo properties. Each has strengths: USGS has separate associated files that contain an independent characterization of composition, including impurities while RELAB lets any research submit a (reasonable #) of samples for measurement, and thus is more diverse in range. Most of the major discoveries of planetary science would not have been possible without these terrestrial-lab libraries. Both over 40 years old, the USGS and NASA/Brown RELAB spectral libraries continue to have influence across remote sensing and laboratory spectroscopy communities alike."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "I first started using as a graduate student when we had odd spectra from Mars. Turned out one set were carbonates, but slightly weird hydrated Mg carbonates rather than a calcite rich limestone. Another set were prehnite, a Ca-Al silicate hydroxide that uniquely indicates subgreenschist metamorphic conditions of formation. Still another set were alunite, an Al sulfate hydroxide that indicates acidic groundwater springs. The pre-existing databases were essential to the discoveries of the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter team"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Bethany Ehlmann"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Email Address",
          "schema:value": "bethany.ehlmann@lasp.colorado.edu"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator ORCID",
          "schema:value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2745-3240"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Affiliation or Organization",
          "schema:value": "University of Colorado Boulder, 3665 Discovery Drive, Boulder, CO 80303"
        }
      ]
