    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:031",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "USGS Spectral Library",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:031",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:earth-planetary-materials"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Roger Clark et al (USGS), Ray Kokaly (USGS), Gregg Swayze (USGS), et al."
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://www.usgs.gov/labs/spectroscopy-lab/usgs-spectral-library"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:https-www-sciencebase-gov-catalog-item-5807a2a2e4b0841e59e3a18d"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "Ray Kokaly (USGS) et al."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Founded by Dr. Roger Clark in the 1980s, researchers at the USGS Spectroscopy Laboratory have measured the spectral reflectance of thousands of materials in the lab and compiled them in the USGS Spectral Library. The spectral library was assembled to facilitate laboratory and field spectroscopy and remote sensing for identifying and mapping minerals, vegetation, and manmade materials. For many of the samples, wavelength coverage spans the ultraviolet, visible, near-infrared, mid-infrared, and far-infrared regions (0.2 to 200 microns). A wide range of materials are included in the library: minerals, soils, coatings on rocks, liquids and frozen volatiles, organic compounds, manmade materials, and vegetation and other biologic materials. Detailed sample descriptions are provided with the spectra, including the results of X-ray Diffraction, Electron Probe Micro-Analysis, and other analytical methods. The library is presently managed by Dr. Ray Kokaly."
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:university-of-colorado-boulder-3665-discovery-drive-boulder-co-80309",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "University of Colorado Boulder, 3665 Discovery Drive, Boulder, CO 80309"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:031",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:031",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:031",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2745-3240"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "There are two visible/shortwave infrared databases of earth and planetary materials. Nearly every solar system discovery is based on the prescient work of Roger Clark at USGS and, separately, Carle Pieters at Brown University to curate high quality collections of measurements of what geological materials look like in the visible and near infrared. These are repositories openly available to any investigator to download spectra and compare to what they observe with telescopes, spacecraft, or field and lab studies on Earth. Clay minerals on Mars, sodium carbonates on Ceres, pure sulfur on Io, serpentines on asteroids all relied on these databases. Remote sensing on Earth too uses these for albedo properties. Each has strengths: USGS has separate associated files that contain an independent characterization of composition, including impurities while RELAB lets any research submit a (reasonable #) of samples for measurement, and thus is more diverse in range. Most of the major discoveries of planetary science would not have been possible without these terrestrial-lab libraries. Both over 40 years old, the USGS and NASA/Brown RELAB spectral libraries continue to have influence across remote sensing and laboratory spectroscopy communities alike."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "I first started using as an undergraduate student in my remote sensing classes when we learned about hyperspectral imagery. I was fascinated with how spectral feature fitting could be used to identify a mineral like that I could hold in my hand from space, using only passive sensing of reflected light. Fast forward to when I was a graduate student when we had new spectra from Mars. Along with RELAB, the USGS spectral library was essential in discovering new minerals on other planets. Particularly important is the fact that the minerals in the USGS spectral library have independent x-ray diffraction measurements so that one can understand how small impurities can affect spectral properties. This dataset has been vital for imaging spectrometers across the solar system, whether discovering minerals on a distant asteroid or mapping the Earth's mineral resources from space."
        }
      ]
    },
