    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:034",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:034",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:034",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5275-0821"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The Indian Landslide Susceptibility Map (ILSM) is the first high-resolution, machine learning–based national-scale landslide susceptibility map of India, developed at ~100 m resolution. In my opinion, this dataset is transformative because it combines multiple geophysical, hydrological, meteorological, and anthropogenic factors into a reproducible, data-driven framework. It addresses long-standing limitations of heuristic or coarse-scale maps that often could not capture localized landslide hazards. \n\nILSM was based on the idea that in developing countries state boundaries play an important role since inventory mapping and all such mapping initiatives are taken by states. However geographical and geological conditions don’t vary largely in neighbouring states. So using big data we can learn landslide susceptibility in data poor regions. Without the ILSM, disaster risk reduction in India would continue to rely on fragmented inventories and low-resolution hazard maps, leaving millions of people in mountainous and hilly regions under-prepared. One major outcome of ILSM was identification of new landslide susceptibility areas which have been previously ignored. ILSM directly benefits people by informing disaster preparedness, guiding infrastructure planning, and supporting education and training for landslide risk assessment. ILSM advances understanding of geophysical phenomena, enabling climate and rainfall-related landslide hazard assessments at scale. ILSM supports social equity and community resilience by identifying vulnerable populations, aiding humanitarian relief planning, and helping policymakers prioritize investments in safer, more resilient infrastructure. \n\nThis dataset also sets a precedent for using Artificial Intelligence and geospatial big data to map extreme events. It empowers both researchers and practitioners by providing open, accessible data through platforms like Google Earth Engine, ensuring its widespread usability for policy, science, and community resilience."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "In their research, the nominator interacts with the ILSM dataset primarily through Google Earth Engine (https://hydrosense.users.earthengine.app/view/ilsm) and ESRI (https://www.arcgis.com/home/item.html?id=43c6aa08a1af43cdbc243b1e36630e56 ). ILSM gives a new meaning to open data. It is not only open source for downstream applications but also open visualization and interaction making its usage easy. For integration with other datasets with settlement and infrastructure datasets to study the relationship between human exposure and slow-moving or rainfall-triggered landslides. The uniform spatial coverage of ILSM allows them to extend analyses across different physiographic and climatic regions of India, something that would not have been possible with fragmented local inventories. Having a high-resolution, openly accessible dataset like ILSM saves significant effort in curating and harmonizing input data, allowing them to focus on advancing modelling techniques and policy applications."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:034",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 35,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Earth Surface, Natural Hazards, Geology, Near Surface Geophysics"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "Indian Landslide Susceptibility Map (ILSM)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Nirdesh Sharma, Manabendra Saharia, G.V. Ramana"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://hydrosense.users.earthengine.app/view/ilsm"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "Zenodo"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "https://zenodo.org/records/10085272"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "Every year landslides claim many lives and cause losses amounting to billions of dollars. Understanding the areas prone to landslides and the factors driving them is critical to reducing the impact of landslides. The framework we have developed relies on machine learning models for their flexibility with big datasets and ease of mapping with higher resolution. \n\nILSM utilizes datasets obtained from satellites and remote sensing devices, to provide a high-resolution map of landslide zones in India. It also ensemble probabilistic estimates for landslide susceptibility which can help develop landslide models. This model accurately detects and locates landslide-prone zones that are previously mapped and also identifies new landslide zones. Therefore, it can serve as a useful tool for landslide monitoring and awareness programs."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The Indian Landslide Susceptibility Map (ILSM) is the first high-resolution, machine learning–based national-scale landslide susceptibility map of India, developed at ~100 m resolution. In my opinion, this dataset is transformative because it combines multiple geophysical, hydrological, meteorological, and anthropogenic factors into a reproducible, data-driven framework. It addresses long-standing limitations of heuristic or coarse-scale maps that often could not capture localized landslide hazards. \n\nILSM was based on the idea that in developing countries state boundaries play an important role since inventory mapping and all such mapping initiatives are taken by states. However geographical and geological conditions don’t vary largely in neighbouring states. So using big data we can learn landslide susceptibility in data poor regions. Without the ILSM, disaster risk reduction in India would continue to rely on fragmented inventories and low-resolution hazard maps, leaving millions of people in mountainous and hilly regions under-prepared. One major outcome of ILSM was identification of new landslide susceptibility areas which have been previously ignored. ILSM directly benefits people by informing disaster preparedness, guiding infrastructure planning, and supporting education and training for landslide risk assessment. ILSM advances understanding of geophysical phenomena, enabling climate and rainfall-related landslide hazard assessments at scale. ILSM supports social equity and community resilience by identifying vulnerable populations, aiding humanitarian relief planning, and helping policymakers prioritize investments in safer, more resilient infrastructure. \n\nThis dataset also sets a precedent for using Artificial Intelligence and geospatial big data to map extreme events. It empowers both researchers and practitioners by providing open, accessible data through platforms like Google Earth Engine, ensuring its widespread usability for policy, science, and community resilience."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "In their research, the nominator interacts with the ILSM dataset primarily through Google Earth Engine (https://hydrosense.users.earthengine.app/view/ilsm) and ESRI (https://www.arcgis.com/home/item.html?id=43c6aa08a1af43cdbc243b1e36630e56 ). ILSM gives a new meaning to open data. It is not only open source for downstream applications but also open visualization and interaction making its usage easy. For integration with other datasets with settlement and infrastructure datasets to study the relationship between human exposure and slow-moving or rainfall-triggered landslides. The uniform spatial coverage of ILSM allows them to extend analyses across different physiographic and climatic regions of India, something that would not have been possible with fragmented local inventories. Having a high-resolution, openly accessible dataset like ILSM saves significant effort in curating and harmonizing input data, allowing them to focus on advancing modelling techniques and policy applications."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Sharma, N., Saharia, M., & Ramana, G.V. (2024). High resolution landslide susceptibility mapping using ensemble machine learning and geospatial big data. Catena, 235, 107653. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.catena.2023.107653"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "ILSM has been cited in the Parliament of India citing landslides need better management. (https://eparlib.sansad.in/bitstream/123456789/2981313/1/569.pdf ) \n\nILSM has been cited over 75 times in 18 months since it has been published.(https://www.researchgate.net/publication/376170489_High_resolution_landslide_susceptibility_mapping_using_ensemble_machine_learning_and_geospatial_big_data/citations?latestCitations=PB%3A395585723 ) \n\nILSM has been used to develop landslide vulnerability and hazard maps that have been published. (https://doi.org/10.1007/s11069-025-07256-6 ) \n\nILSM has been reused as a baseline hazard layer for developing slope stability and rainfall-triggered landslide early warning models. \n\nILSM has been shown in many news articles and blogs \n\nhttps://scroll.in/article/1069886/landslide-susceptibility-map-finds-eastern-ghats-as-risk-prone \n\nhttps://india.mongabay.com/2024/06/eastern-ghats-debuts-in-new-landslide-susceptibility-map/ \n\nhttps://eos.org/thelandslideblog/india-landslide-susceptibility \n\nhttps://www.insightsonindia.com/2024/01/10/indias-first-high-resolution-landslide-susceptibility-map/ \n\nhttps://shaastramag.iitm.ac.in/special-feature/coming-landslide-map-india \n\nhttps://www.thehindu.com/sci-tech/science/iit-delhi-team-first-hi-res-landslide-risk-map-india/article67701918.ece"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Dr. Manabendra Saharia"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Email Address",
          "schema:value": "msaharia@iitd.ac.in"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator ORCID",
          "schema:value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5275-0821"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Affiliation or Organization",
          "schema:value": "Indian Institute of Technology Delhi, Hauz Khas, New Delhi-110016, India"
        }
      ]
