    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:036",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "FDSN Databases",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:036",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:earth-surface-natural-hazards-geology-near-surface-geophysics"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "The International Federation of Digital Seismograph Networks (FDSN)"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://fdsn.org/"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:nsf-sage-data-services-earthscope-eida-nodes-etc"
      ],
      "description": "The International Federation of Digital Seismograph Networks (FDSN) is a global organization. Its membership is comprised of groups responsible for the installation and maintenance of seismographs either within their geographic borders or globally. Membership in the FDSN is open to all organizations that operate more than one broadband station. Members agree to coordinate station siting and provide free and open access to their data. This cooperation helps scientists all over the world to further the advancement of earth science and particularly the study of global seismic activity. The FDSN also holds commission status within IASPEI.\nThe FDSN goals related to station siting and instrumentation are to provide stations with good geographic distribution, recording data with 24 bits of resolution in continuous time series with at least a 20 sample per second sampling rate. The FDSN was also instrumental in development of a universal standard for distribution of broadband waveform data and related parametric information. The Standard for Exchange of Earthquake Data (SEED) format is the result of that effort.\nFrom https://www.fdsn.org/about/",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "Dziewonski, A. M. (1994). The FDSN: history and objectives. Annals of Geophysics, XXXVii(5), 1039–1041."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6778-8080",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Kiwamu Nishida",
      "identifier": {
        "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
        "propertyID": "ORCID",
        "value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6778-8080"
      },
      "schema:email": "knishida@eri.u-tokyo.ac.jp",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:earthquake-research-institute-the-university-of-tokyo-1-1-1-yayoi-bunkyo-ku-toky"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:earthquake-research-institute-the-university-of-tokyo-1-1-1-yayoi-bunkyo-ku-toky",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "Earthquake Research Institute, The University of Tokyo, 1-1-1 Yayoi, Bunkyo-ku, Tokyo, 113-0032, Japan"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:036",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:036",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:036",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6778-8080"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "It is truly remarkable that the International Federation of Digital Seismograph Networks (FDSN) has provided open and continuous seismic data to the world for over three decades. This unprecedented commitment has fueled extensive geophysical research by scientists globally, leading to immense scientific contributions, chiefly in understanding (i) the Earth's interior structure and (ii) the physics of earthquake phenomena.\n\nA key factor was the unified management of broadband seismometer data and other geophysical data (e.g., barometer, ocean pressure gauges), including comprehensive metadata, from the very beginning. This, coupled with the openness of the data, significantly encouraged a large number of researchers to enter the field. The revolutionary move to distribute data from numerous global institutions under a unified standard brought tremendous benefits to data analysis. \n\nThe data openness has also made it possible to realize research ideas that were previously difficult to achieve. It has consistently supported the free and unrestricted creativity of scientists, resulting in the discovery of previously unknown phenomena. Crucially, it has broadened the scope of seismology to include the atmosphere, oceans, and surface environments, truly opening new frontiers.\n\nFurthermore, the quasi-real-time release of much of this data globally is indispensable for disaster mitigation, allowing for a rapid understanding of what transpires immediately after a major earthquake. This has yielded significant results, particularly concerning great earthquakes and their associated tsunamis. More recently, seismic data has begun to play an essential role in monitoring surface environmental phenomena like landslides and droughts."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "More specifically, and highly relevant to my own research, is the critical role FDSN's provision of nearly all continuous data for over three decades has played in the discovery of unknown phenomena, particularly seismic waves of the solid Earth excited by atmospheric and oceanic sources. Specifically, this led to our discovery of the Earth's background free oscillations in 1998 (e.g., Kobayashi and Nishida, 1998). Research on this topic continues today, consistently supported by FDSN data. \nAnother example is the 2022 eruption of Hunga Tonga-Hunga Ha'apai volcano highlighted the excitation of tsunamis by Lamb waves (e.g., Kubota, Saito & Nishida, 2022). While this recent work is significant, the preceding research on Lamb waves (Nishida et al. 2014) was only made possible because FDSN-affiliated (USArray) barometric pressure data deployed in North America were openly available. In this way, FDSN has played a major role in fostering the free and unrestricted thinking of researchers."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:036",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 37,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Earth Surface, Natural Hazards, Geology, Near Surface Geophysics"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "FDSN Databases"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "The International Federation of Digital Seismograph Networks (FDSN)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://fdsn.org/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "NSF SAGE Data Services (EarthScope), EIDA Nodes, etc."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "DOIs are available at https://www.fdsn.org/networks/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The International Federation of Digital Seismograph Networks (FDSN) is a global organization. Its membership is comprised of groups responsible for the installation and maintenance of seismographs either within their geographic borders or globally. Membership in the FDSN is open to all organizations that operate more than one broadband station. Members agree to coordinate station siting and provide free and open access to their data. This cooperation helps scientists all over the world to further the advancement of earth science and particularly the study of global seismic activity. The FDSN also holds commission status within IASPEI.\nThe FDSN goals related to station siting and instrumentation are to provide stations with good geographic distribution, recording data with 24 bits of resolution in continuous time series with at least a 20 sample per second sampling rate. The FDSN was also instrumental in development of a universal standard for distribution of broadband waveform data and related parametric information. The Standard for Exchange of Earthquake Data (SEED) format is the result of that effort.\nFrom https://www.fdsn.org/about/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "It is truly remarkable that the International Federation of Digital Seismograph Networks (FDSN) has provided open and continuous seismic data to the world for over three decades. This unprecedented commitment has fueled extensive geophysical research by scientists globally, leading to immense scientific contributions, chiefly in understanding (i) the Earth's interior structure and (ii) the physics of earthquake phenomena.\n\nA key factor was the unified management of broadband seismometer data and other geophysical data (e.g., barometer, ocean pressure gauges), including comprehensive metadata, from the very beginning. This, coupled with the openness of the data, significantly encouraged a large number of researchers to enter the field. The revolutionary move to distribute data from numerous global institutions under a unified standard brought tremendous benefits to data analysis. \n\nThe data openness has also made it possible to realize research ideas that were previously difficult to achieve. It has consistently supported the free and unrestricted creativity of scientists, resulting in the discovery of previously unknown phenomena. Crucially, it has broadened the scope of seismology to include the atmosphere, oceans, and surface environments, truly opening new frontiers.\n\nFurthermore, the quasi-real-time release of much of this data globally is indispensable for disaster mitigation, allowing for a rapid understanding of what transpires immediately after a major earthquake. This has yielded significant results, particularly concerning great earthquakes and their associated tsunamis. More recently, seismic data has begun to play an essential role in monitoring surface environmental phenomena like landslides and droughts."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "More specifically, and highly relevant to my own research, is the critical role FDSN's provision of nearly all continuous data for over three decades has played in the discovery of unknown phenomena, particularly seismic waves of the solid Earth excited by atmospheric and oceanic sources. Specifically, this led to our discovery of the Earth's background free oscillations in 1998 (e.g., Kobayashi and Nishida, 1998). Research on this topic continues today, consistently supported by FDSN data. \nAnother example is the 2022 eruption of Hunga Tonga-Hunga Ha'apai volcano highlighted the excitation of tsunamis by Lamb waves (e.g., Kubota, Saito & Nishida, 2022). While this recent work is significant, the preceding research on Lamb waves (Nishida et al. 2014) was only made possible because FDSN-affiliated (USArray) barometric pressure data deployed in North America were openly available. In this way, FDSN has played a major role in fostering the free and unrestricted thinking of researchers."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Dziewonski, A. M. (1994). The FDSN: history and objectives. Annals of Geophysics, XXXVii(5), 1039–1041."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Kiwamu Nishida"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Email Address",
          "schema:value": "knishida@eri.u-tokyo.ac.jp"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator ORCID",
          "schema:value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6778-8080"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Affiliation or Organization",
          "schema:value": "Earthquake Research Institute, The University of Tokyo, 1-1-1 Yayoi, Bunkyo-ku, Tokyo, 113-0032, Japan"
        }
      ]
    },
