    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:037",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:037",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:037",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2822-8261"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The Global Lithological Map (GLiM) was nominated because it is the by far highest-resolution portrayal of the Earth’s surface geology. Compiled over six years by a small research team without structured data infrastructure, GLiM unified 90 regional geological maps into a coherent global lithological framework, of more than a million polygons, sometimes digitizing analogue archives, e.g. scanned Soviet paper maps by hand. Each mapped unit was systematically translated into standardized lithological classes sometimes relying on additional literature research. The result is a high-resolution representation of the planet’s rock composition.\nSince publication, GLiM has become a cornerstone dataset directly cited in more than a thousand studies. Through sibling-datasets developed from it, GLiM improves nearly all research of the Earth System that includes aspects of the terrestrial underground.\nGLiM is showcased in a 3D web interface but most importantly as a paper map, published by the Commission for the Geological Map of the World. Both items have been used for public education. \nLithology governs elemental fluxes from rocks to soils, rivers, and oceans. As the lithological foundation of the global permeability maps GLHYMPS (Gleeson et al., 2014), GLiM underpins nearly all modern groundwater and hydrology models, improving our ability to assess water security, drought resilience, and subsurface hazard risks. It informs SoilGrids (Hengl et al., 2017) and global ecosystem classifications (Sayre et al., 2014). GLiM supports estimates of global weathering and CO₂ consumption, marine nutrient inputs, and lithology-controlled isotopic baselines. It even serves as a validation layer for new hyperspectral satellite missions mapping surface mineralogy from space. GLiM is relevant for economic benefit through its usage as input data also for models of landscape stability and rock slides as well as soil fertility and nutrient availability. It also is used for definition of source rock availability in several studies addressing enhanced rock weathering as a carbon dioxide removal method. \nAs global Earth-system and climate models advance toward kilometer- and sub-kilometer-scale resolutions, driven by increasing computational power and increasing resolution of satellite imagery, the limiting factor of global model quality increasingly lies in the quality of base data describing the subsurface. GLiM provides precisely that missing element: a unified, high-resolution, and physically meaningful lithological layer that connects surface observations to subsurface processes. In this sense, it has become a cornerstone enabling how we now quantify and visualize the functioning of the Earth system in modern models. It bridges disciplines, supports education, and supports a vast range of scientific and societal applications—an enduring contribution to how humanity understands its planet."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator made the dataset, so their personal interactions are very special. The dataset basically is a shapefile, but can be interacted with as a published paper map or an ESRI-web map, too. The native interaction would be through a GIS-system, analyzing the data attributes and extracting the information necessary. The interaction can be a bit slow, since the original dataset is larger than 1 GB with more than 1 million polygons. The dataset is built very simply - so interaction is easy as long as people know how to use GIS."
        }
      ]
    },
