    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:038",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "USArray Transportable Array",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:038",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:earth-surface-natural-hazards-geology-near-surface-geophysics"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "IRIS Data Management Center"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://doi.org/10.7914/SN/TA"
      ],
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "DOI",
          "value": "https://doi.org/10.7914/SN/TA"
        }
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:earthscope-data-services"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "EarthScope Data Services"
        }
      ],
      "description": "The USArray Transportable Array was a revolutionary geoscience project that fundamentally changed our understanding of the North American continent's structure and evolution. Its main impact was creating the first high-resolution, continent-scale 3D image of the Earth's crust and upper mantle.\n\nFor over a decade, a dense network of 400 high-quality seismometers was systematically moved across the United States, collecting a massive, uniform dataset of seismic waves from earthquakes around the world. This allowed scientists to create detailed 3D maps of the Earth's interior, revealing geological structures with unprecedented clarity.\n\nThe USArray project created a massive, open-access dataset of unparalleled quality and scale. This \"firehose of data\" spurred a wave of innovation in seismology, allowing researchers worldwide to develop and test new analytical methods. It set a new standard for \"big data\" in the Earth sciences and fostered a more collaborative research environment.\n\nThe dense grid of seismometers allowed for a much more detailed analysis of how seismic waves travel. This improved our understanding of earthquake physics, refined seismic hazard maps, and advanced techniques like ambient noise tomography, where subtle background vibrations of the Earth are used to create images of the crust.",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "https://www.fdsn.org/networks/detail/TA/",
          "schema:url": [
            "https://www.fdsn.org/networks/detail/TA/"
          ]
        },
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "https://doi.org/10.1175/BAMS-D-14-00204.1",
          "identifier": [
            {
              "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
              "propertyID": "DOI",
              "value": "https://doi.org/10.1175/BAMS-D-14-00204.1"
            }
          ],
          "schema:url": [
            "https://doi.org/10.1175/BAMS-D-14-00204.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "https://www.earthscope-program-2003-2018.org/articles/continental_evolution_TA.html",
          "schema:url": [
