    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:038",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:038",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:038",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4981-8340"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The USArray Transportable Array provided benefits both to society and our understanding of the North American continental structure and dynamics.\n\nThe USArray project was a goldmine for science education. Its high-quality, open-access dataset allowed students at all levels, from K-12 to university, to work with real-world scientific data, a rare opportunity in Earth science. The 3D tomographic models generated from the dense array of seismometers across the continent are powerful and intuitive teaching tools. They make abstract geological concepts, like subducting plates and mantle plumes, visible and understandable to a broad audience.\n\nThe project significantly improved our ability to prepare for and respond to seismic events. By providing a much clearer picture of the Earth's crust, including previously unknown faults and structural weaknesses, USArray data helps create more accurate and detailed seismic hazard maps. These maps are critical for developing building codes, planning infrastructure projects, and defining insurance risk.\n\nThe dense network of sensors recorded how seismic waves travel through different types of rock and soil with unprecedented detail. This information is used to refine models that predict how much the ground will shake in different locations during an earthquake, helping engineers design more resilient buildings.\n\nWhile the USArray Transportable Array did not yield answers that would enable the prediction of earthquakes, the project was a massive leap forward in earthquake hazard assessment. By mapping the deep structure, scientists can better understand the forces and stresses acting on faults. This helps identify areas where seismic risk was previously underestimated. The data also provided new insights into the physical processes that lead to earthquakes, such as how stress is transferred through the crust. \n\nFinally, what is important to consider about this massive project, spanning 16 years, is the publicly visible showing of a successful government-funded science experiment that required the cooperation of countless private land owners and institutions to maintain a consistent swath of sensitive instruments that would run for months at a time and then be demobilized for installation elsewhere. This effort required careful planning, logistical tracking, consistent design specifications, and quality controlled data acquisition and storage. The result is a massive dataset of unmatched consistency and quality in the service of geophysical studies and education."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "I am involved as a Data Engineer, Curator, and Provider of the USArray Transportable Array dataset through the EarthScope Consortium."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:038",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 39,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Earth Surface, Natural Hazards, Geology, Near Surface Geophysics"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "USArray Transportable Array"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "IRIS Data Management Center"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.7914/SN/TA"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "EarthScope Data Services"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.7914/SN/TA"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "EarthScope Data Services"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The USArray Transportable Array was a revolutionary geoscience project that fundamentally changed our understanding of the North American continent's structure and evolution. Its main impact was creating the first high-resolution, continent-scale 3D image of the Earth's crust and upper mantle.\n\nFor over a decade, a dense network of 400 high-quality seismometers was systematically moved across the United States, collecting a massive, uniform dataset of seismic waves from earthquakes around the world. This allowed scientists to create detailed 3D maps of the Earth's interior, revealing geological structures with unprecedented clarity.\n\nThe USArray project created a massive, open-access dataset of unparalleled quality and scale. This \"firehose of data\" spurred a wave of innovation in seismology, allowing researchers worldwide to develop and test new analytical methods. It set a new standard for \"big data\" in the Earth sciences and fostered a more collaborative research environment.\n\nThe dense grid of seismometers allowed for a much more detailed analysis of how seismic waves travel. This improved our understanding of earthquake physics, refined seismic hazard maps, and advanced techniques like ambient noise tomography, where subtle background vibrations of the Earth are used to create images of the crust."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The USArray Transportable Array provided benefits both to society and our understanding of the North American continental structure and dynamics.\n\nThe USArray project was a goldmine for science education. Its high-quality, open-access dataset allowed students at all levels, from K-12 to university, to work with real-world scientific data, a rare opportunity in Earth science. The 3D tomographic models generated from the dense array of seismometers across the continent are powerful and intuitive teaching tools. They make abstract geological concepts, like subducting plates and mantle plumes, visible and understandable to a broad audience.\n\nThe project significantly improved our ability to prepare for and respond to seismic events. By providing a much clearer picture of the Earth's crust, including previously unknown faults and structural weaknesses, USArray data helps create more accurate and detailed seismic hazard maps. These maps are critical for developing building codes, planning infrastructure projects, and defining insurance risk.\n\nThe dense network of sensors recorded how seismic waves travel through different types of rock and soil with unprecedented detail. This information is used to refine models that predict how much the ground will shake in different locations during an earthquake, helping engineers design more resilient buildings.\n\nWhile the USArray Transportable Array did not yield answers that would enable the prediction of earthquakes, the project was a massive leap forward in earthquake hazard assessment. By mapping the deep structure, scientists can better understand the forces and stresses acting on faults. This helps identify areas where seismic risk was previously underestimated. The data also provided new insights into the physical processes that lead to earthquakes, such as how stress is transferred through the crust. \n\nFinally, what is important to consider about this massive project, spanning 16 years, is the publicly visible showing of a successful government-funded science experiment that required the cooperation of countless private land owners and institutions to maintain a consistent swath of sensitive instruments that would run for months at a time and then be demobilized for installation elsewhere. This effort required careful planning, logistical tracking, consistent design specifications, and quality controlled data acquisition and storage. The result is a massive dataset of unmatched consistency and quality in the service of geophysical studies and education."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "I am involved as a Data Engineer, Curator, and Provider of the USArray Transportable Array dataset through the EarthScope Consortium."
        },
