    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:039",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:039",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:039",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8879-6514"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The SCEC Community Fault Model (CFM) provides a comprehensive 3D fault geometry dataset covering the entire San Andreas fault system that can be used in a range of simulations related to tectonics, geodesy, earthquake science, and seismic hazard assessments. The CFM is built using data from a wide range of sources to produce geologically and geophysically accurate fault surface representations. This is impactful because without the CFM, computational fault models would likely still mostly rely on simplified rectangular and planar fault representations. It is unlikely that the researchers creating computational simulations of faulting would have the skills, resources, and/or time to create a detailed geometry like the CFM for use in their models. The CFM allows anyone to download a complex state-of-the-science 3D representation for >400 faults in California. \n\nThe CFM also has web-based tools, the CFM Explorer, where users can search, download, visualize, and interact with the model, all with no special software needed. The CFM Explorer lowers the bar of entry to new users and allows potential users to quickly learn if the model is appropriate for their needs. The CFM Explorer also integrates several other datasets to help users evaluate the model including relocated seismicity, other SCEC models, and real-time seismicity from the USGS ComCat.\n\nThe CFM has also been used in state-of-the-art seismic hazard assessments including the past Uniform California Earthquake Rupture Forecasts (UCERF) and the ongoing USGS National Seismic Hazard Model (NSHM) efforts. In the UCERF, the CFM was used as a basis for a simplified 3D fault geometry for southern California. This simplified version of the CFM used in UCEREF3 was then adopted by the recent NSHM23 effort. Therefore, the CFM has directly impacted seismic hazard assessments.\n\nThe CFM is also used in the automated real-time CFM earthquake-to-fault association service that is a collaboration between SCEC, the Southern California Earthquake Data Center (SCEDC) at Caltech, Harvard University and Appalachian State University. For the first time, the information provided by sophisticated, near real-time seismic networks is automatically connected with the comprehensive 3D Community Fault Model and provides statistical estimates (based on Evans et al. 2020) of which faults are most likely associated with all >M3 events in southern California. This service was recently updated to utilize the latest CFM model, and the association service emails now provide direct links to the event in 2D and 3D using the CFM Explorer. Registration for the CFM association service is free and is done through users SCEC account as described on the CFM homepage (https://southern.scec.org/research/cfm). Numerous colleagues that serve in rapid response roles have shared the importance of the CFM association service, especially when speaking to media outlets."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator, their collaborators, and students have used the CFM in 14 publications (dating back to 2006) involving 3D mechanical modeling of faulting in southern California. Many of these publications have focused on the effects of 3D non-planar fault geometry. Without the CFM, these models would have used significantly simplified fault representations and many of these manuscripts simply would not have been possible. Due to their wide usage of the CFM, they joined the development team in 2017 where they have helped to automate and standardize production of new model archives, improve model metadata and documentation, and create the CFM Explorer web-based tools.\n\nThey mainly use the CFM in 3D Boundary Element Method Models of California faulting. To do this, they download the CFM archive from Zenodo, load it into either MATLAB (using scripts that they wrote and are publicly available on the CFM homepage) or CAD/GIS software. To make mathematically-stable meshes, they typically spend considerable time re-meshing the faults for the specific needs of their modeling software, but without the CFM as a starting point, they would not be able to simulate deformation along geologically-constrained 3D fault surfaces. Without the CFM, much of their past research simply would not have been possible."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:039",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 40,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Earth Surface, Natural Hazards, Geology, Near Surface Geophysics"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "The Statewide California Earthquake Center Community Fault Model (CFM)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Andreas Plesch, Scott T. Marshall, John H. Shaw"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://southern.scec.org/research/cfm"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "zenodo.org"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4651667"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Statewide California Earthquake Center, Andreas Plesch (Harvard), Scott T. Marshall (App State), John H. Shaw (Harvard)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The SCEC Community Fault Model (CFM) is an object-oriented, three-dimensional representation of active faults in California and adjacent offshore basins. For each fault object, the CFM provides triangulated surface representations (t-surfs) in several resolutions, fault traces in several different file formats (shape files, GMT plain text, and GoogleEarth kml), and complete metadata including references used to constrain the surfaces. The CFM faults are defined based on all available data including surface traces, seismicity, seismic reflection profiles, well data, geologic cross sections, and various other types of data and models. The CFM serves the Statewide California Earthquake Center (SCEC) as a unified resource for physics-based fault systems modeling, strong ground-motion prediction, and probabilistic seismic hazards assessment (e.g., UCERF3, NSHM). Together with the Community Velocity Model (CVM-H 15.1.0), the CFM comprises SCEC's Unified Structural Representation of the Southern California crust and upper mantle (Shaw et al., 2015)."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The SCEC Community Fault Model (CFM) provides a comprehensive 3D fault geometry dataset covering the entire San Andreas fault system that can be used in a range of simulations related to tectonics, geodesy, earthquake science, and seismic hazard assessments. The CFM is built using data from a wide range of sources to produce geologically and geophysically accurate fault surface representations. This is impactful because without the CFM, computational fault models would likely still mostly rely on simplified rectangular and planar fault representations. It is unlikely that the researchers creating computational simulations of faulting would have the skills, resources, and/or time to create a detailed geometry like the CFM for use in their models. The CFM allows anyone to download a complex state-of-the-science 3D representation for >400 faults in California. \n\nThe CFM also has web-based tools, the CFM Explorer, where users can search, download, visualize, and interact with the model, all with no special software needed. The CFM Explorer lowers the bar of entry to new users and allows potential users to quickly learn if the model is appropriate for their needs. The CFM Explorer also integrates several other datasets to help users evaluate the model including relocated seismicity, other SCEC models, and real-time seismicity from the USGS ComCat.\n\nThe CFM has also been used in state-of-the-art seismic hazard assessments including the past Uniform California Earthquake Rupture Forecasts (UCERF) and the ongoing USGS National Seismic Hazard Model (NSHM) efforts. In the UCERF, the CFM was used as a basis for a simplified 3D fault geometry for southern California. This simplified version of the CFM used in UCEREF3 was then adopted by the recent NSHM23 effort. Therefore, the CFM has directly impacted seismic hazard assessments.\n\nThe CFM is also used in the automated real-time CFM earthquake-to-fault association service that is a collaboration between SCEC, the Southern California Earthquake Data Center (SCEDC) at Caltech, Harvard University and Appalachian State University. For the first time, the information provided by sophisticated, near real-time seismic networks is automatically connected with the comprehensive 3D Community Fault Model and provides statistical estimates (based on Evans et al. 2020) of which faults are most likely associated with all >M3 events in southern California. This service was recently updated to utilize the latest CFM model, and the association service emails now provide direct links to the event in 2D and 3D using the CFM Explorer. Registration for the CFM association service is free and is done through users SCEC account as described on the CFM homepage (https://southern.scec.org/research/cfm). Numerous colleagues that serve in rapid response roles have shared the importance of the CFM association service, especially when speaking to media outlets."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator, their collaborators, and students have used the CFM in 14 publications (dating back to 2006) involving 3D mechanical modeling of faulting in southern California. Many of these publications have focused on the effects of 3D non-planar fault geometry. Without the CFM, these models would have used significantly simplified fault representations and many of these manuscripts simply would not have been possible. Due to their wide usage of the CFM, they joined the development team in 2017 where they have helped to automate and standardize production of new model archives, improve model metadata and documentation, and create the CFM Explorer web-based tools.\n\nThey mainly use the CFM in 3D Boundary Element Method Models of California faulting. To do this, they download the CFM archive from Zenodo, load it into either MATLAB (using scripts that they wrote and are publicly available on the CFM homepage) or CAD/GIS software. To make mathematically-stable meshes, they typically spend considerable time re-meshing the faults for the specific needs of their modeling software, but without the CFM as a starting point, they would not be able to simulate deformation along geologically-constrained 3D fault surfaces. Without the CFM, much of their past research simply would not have been possible."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Plesch, A., Shaw, J. H., Benson, C., Bryant, W. A., Carena, S., Cooke, M., Dolan, J., Fuis, G., Gath, E., Grant, L., Hauksson, E., Jordan, T., Kamerling, M., Legg, M., Lindvall, S., Magistrale, H., Nicholson, C., Niemi, N., Oskin, M., … Yeats, R. (2007). Community fault model (Cfm) for southern california. Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, 97(6), 1793–1802. https://doi.org/10.1785/0120050211 \n\nA new manuscript on the CFM will be submitted for publication by the end of 2025."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Below is a list of 25 publications that have used the CFM. As mentioned earlier, the CFM was used as a basis for the UCERF and NSHM fault models, but the nominator did not include those references here as they used a modified/simplified version of the CFM.\n\nGriffith, W. A. (2004). Mechanical validation of the three-dimensional intersection geometry between the puente hills blind-thrust system and the whittier fault, los angeles, california. Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, 94(2), 493–505. https://doi.org/10.1785/0120030094 \n\nGriffith, W. A., & Cooke, M. L. (2005). How Sensitive are Fault-Slip Rates in the Los Angeles Basin to Tectonic Boundary Conditions?. Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, 95(4), 1263-1275. https://doi.org/10.1785/0120040079 \n\nOlson, E. L., & Cooke, M. L. (2005). Application of three fault growth criteria to the Puente Hills thrust system, Los Angeles, California, USA. Journal of Structural Geology, 27(10), 1765–1777. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jsg.2005.02.005 \n\nCooke, M. L., & Marshall, S. T. (2006). Fault slip rates from three‐dimensional models of the Los Angeles metropolitan area, California. Geophysical Research Letters, 33(21), 2006GL027850. https://doi.org/10.1029/2006GL027850 \n\nMarshall, S. T., Cooke, M. L., & Owen, S. E. (2008). Effects of nonplanar fault topology and mechanical interaction on fault-slip distributions in the ventura basin, california. Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, 98(3), 1113–1127. https://doi.org/10.1785/0120070159 \n\nMeigs, A. J., Cooke, M. L., & Marshall, S. T. (2008). Using vertical rock uplift patterns to constrain the three-dimensional fault configuration in the los angeles basin. Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, 98(1), 106–123. https://doi.org/10.1785/0120060254 \n\nDair, L., & Cooke, M. L. (2009). San Andreas Fault Geometry through the San Gorgonio Pass, California. Geology, 37(2), 119-122. https://doi.org/10.1130/G25101A.1 \n \nMarshall, S. T., Cooke, M. L., & Owen, S. E. (2009). Interseismic deformation associated with three‐dimensional faults in the greater Los Angeles region, California. Journal of Geophysical Research: Solid Earth, 114(B12), 2009JB006439. https://doi.org/10.1029/2009JB006439 \n\nCooke, M. L., & Dair, L. C. (2011). Simulating the recent evolution of the southern big bend of the San Andreas fault, Southern California. Journal of Geophysical Research, 116(B4), B04405. https://doi.org/10.1029/2010JB007835 \n\nHerbert, J. W., & Cooke, M. L. (2012). Sensitivity of the Southern San Andreas Fault System to Tectonic Boundary Conditions and Fault Configurations. Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, 102(5), 1-17. https://doi.org/10.1785/0120110316 \n\nMadden, E. H., & Pollard, D. D. (2012). Integration of surface slip and aftershocks to constrain the 3d structure of faults involved in the m 7. 3 landers earthquake, southern california. Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, 102(1), 321–342. https://doi.org/10.1785/0120110073 \n\nMarshall, S. T., Funning, G. J., & Owen, S. E. (2013). Fault slip rates and interseismic deformation in the western Transverse Ranges, California. Journal of Geophysical Research: Solid Earth, 118(8), 4511–4534. https://doi.org/10.1002/jgrb.50312 \n\nFattaruso, L. A., Cooke, M. L., & Dorsey, R. J. (2014). Sensitivity of uplift patterns to dip of the san andreas fault in the coachella valley, california. Geosphere, 10(6), 1235–1246. https://doi.org/10.1130/GES01050.1 \n\nHerbert, J. W., Cooke, M. L., & Marshall, S. T. (2014). Influence of fault connectivity on slip rates in southern California: Potential impact on discrepancies between geodetic derived and geologic slip rates. Journal of Geophysical Research: Solid Earth, 119(3), 2342–2361. https://doi.org/10.1002/2013JB010472 \n\nHerbert, J. W., Cooke, M. L., Oskin, M., & Difo, O. (2014). How much can off-fault deformation contribute to the slip rate discrepancy within the eastern California shear zone? Geology, 42(1), 71–75. https://doi.org/10.1130/G34738.1 \n\nFattaruso, L. A., Cooke, M. L., Dorsey, R. J., & Housen, B. A. (2016). Response of deformation patterns to reorganization of the southern San Andreas fault system since ca. 1.5 Ma. Tectonophysics, 693, 474–488. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tecto.2016.05.035 \n\nMarshall, S. T., Funning, G. J., Krueger, H. E., Owen, S. E., & Loveless, J. P. (2017). Mechanical models favor a ramp geometry for the Ventura‐pitas point fault, California. Geophysical Research Letters, 44(3), 1311–1319. https://doi.org/10.1002/2016GL072289 \n\nResor, P. G., Cooke, M. L., Marshall, S. T., & Madden, E. H. (2018). Influence of fault geometry on the spatial distribution of long‐term slip with implications for determining representative fault‐slip rates. Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, 108(4), 1837–1852. https://doi.org/10.1785/0120170332 \n\nBeyer, J., Cooke, M. L., & Marshall, S. T. (2018). Sensitivity of deformation to activity along the Mill Creek and Mission Creek strands of the southern San Andreas fault. Geosphere, 14(6), 2296–2310. https://doi.org/10.1130/GES01666.1 \n\nDorsett, J. H., Madden, E. H., Marshall, S. T., & Cooke, M. L. (2019). Mechanical models suggest fault linkage through the imperial valley, california, u. S. A. Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, 109(4), 1217–1234. https://doi.org/10.1785/0120180303 \n\nRollins, C., Avouac, J., Landry, W., Argus, D. F., & Barbot, S. (2018). Interseismic strain accumulation on faults beneath los angeles, california. Journal of Geophysical Research: Solid Earth, 123(8), 7126–7150. https://doi.org/10.1029/2017JB015387 \n\nJohnson, K. M., Hammond, W. C., Burgette, R. J., Marshall, S. T., & Sorlien, C. C. (2020). Present‐day and long‐term uplift across the western transverse ranges of southern california. Journal of Geophysical Research: Solid Earth, 125(8), e2020JB019672. https://doi.org/10.1029/2020JB019672 \n\nHughes, A., Bell, R. E., Mildon, Z. K., Rood, D. H., Whittaker, A. C., Rockwell, T. K., Levy, Y., DeVecchio, D. E., Marshall, S. T., & Nicholson, C. (2020). Three‐dimensional structure, ground rupture hazards, and static stress models for complex nonplanar thrust faults in the ventura basin, southern california. Journal of Geophysical Research: Solid Earth, 125(7), e2020JB019539. https://doi.org/10.1029/2020JB019539 \n\nElston, H., Cooke, M., Loveless, J., & Marshall, S. (2024). A new method to invert for interseismic deep slip along closely spaced faults using surface velocities and subsurface stressing‐rate tensors. Earth and Space Science, 11(1), e2023EA003069. https://doi.org/10.1029/2023EA003069 \n\nDevine, S., Harper, H., & Marshall, S. T. (2022). Mechanical models of fault-slip rates in the transverse and peninsular ranges, california. Seismological Research Letters, 93(6), 3135–3150. https://doi.org/10.1785/0220220182"
        },
