    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:040",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "GeoNet (NZ) Geohazard Monitoring Datasets",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:040",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:earth-surface-natural-hazards-geology-near-surface-geophysics"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "New Zealand Institute for Earth Sciences (ESNZ)"
        }
      ],
      "agu:identifierLiteral": [
        "www.geonet.org.nz"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:earth-sciences-new-zealand"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "Earth Sciences New Zealand, GeoNet project team, Data Manager: Jonathan Hanson, contact: info@geonet.org.nz"
        }
      ],
      "description": "Aotearoa New Zealand is one of the most geologically active countries in the world, and since 2001 GeoNet has been New Zealand’s national land and geohazards monitoring system delivering free and open data and information for geohazard monitoring and research across earthquakes, tsunami, landslides, volcanoes and deformation. \n\nGeoNet operates a multiparametric network of monitoring stations and a data collection, processing and dissemination infrastructure. The GeoNet programme is a conduit for science advice related to geohazard monitoring. “GeoNet data” encompasses a diverse data repository that include raw, processed and highly derived data products, that span from the New Zealand National Earthquake Catalogue and associated raw waveform data, to highly derived science products (e.g. Volcano Alert Levels). \n\nGeoNet datasets overlap in their utility and are truly multi-disciplinary and this range of hazard monitoring data and information are available through the website www.geonet.org.nz, a diverse array of access mechanisms and the GeoNet mobile phone application. \n\nA list of datasets (see https://www.geonet.org.nz/policy for a more comprehensive list and related DOIs) considered under the GeoNet umbrella are: \n- 4 GNSS related datasets\n- 8 seismic related datasets\n- 2 landslide specific datasets\n- 8 volcano specific datasets\n- 2 tsunami specific datasets\n- 2 site and instrument metadata datasets\n\nGeoNet is hosted and led by Earth Sciences New Zealand, and sponsored by the New Zealand Government through its agencies: Natural Hazards Commission Toka Tū Ake (NHC), GNS Science Te Pū Ao, Toitū Te Whenua Land Information New Zealand (LINZ), the National Emergency Management Agency (NEMA) and the Ministry of Business, Innovation and Employment (MBIE).",
      "isReferencedBy": [
