    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:040",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:040",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:040",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3132-6228"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The GeoNet datasets have been transformational in hazard monitoring in Aotearoa New Zealand. They have led to ground-breaking research and vital response information around some significant geohazard events (Christchurch earthquake sequences from 2010-2011, the complex M7.8 Kaikoura earthquake in 2016, and multiple volcanic unrest and eruptive episodes – e.g. the Te Maari eruption in 2012, or eruptions at Whakaari/White Island in 2012/13 and 2019). Additionally, their long-term, open nature and high-quality have led to and supported an incredible depth of plate boundary and tectonics research and attracted numerous international groups that used GeoNet data as a backbone for their studies. Some of the most recent discoveries on plate boundary processes have been enabled by the availability of free and open access geophysical data in NZ, such as the detection of slow-slip earthquakes across the Hikurangi subduction zone margin. GeoNet has played a nationally key role in supporting geohazard event response, supported re-insurance at the national level and underpins the New Zealand National Reference Datum through its GNSS PositioNZ network, all delivering huge economic and risk management impact for NZ.\n\n(excerpts below from GeoNets 20th Birthday Celebration in 2021 - https://www.gns.cri.nz/news/celebrating-20-years-of-geonet/ by Bruce Girdwood, Data Science and Geohazards Monitoring Manager (Earth Sciences New Zealand, then GNS Science)\n\nIn July GeoNet will hit a ground-breaking milestone as it turns 20 years old.\n\nThat’s two decades of growing the network, building data sets, increasing monitoring capabilities and improving awareness of the geohazards we live with here in Aotearoa New Zealand. Originally GeoNet started as a provider of high quality and timely data, supporting emergency management and scientific research. Since then it has grown into a household name with more than a quarter of a million Kiwis using the GeoNet App to access data from our geohazard monitoring.\n\nLike any maturing 20-year-old, GeoNet has gained more responsibility and embraced future technology. In 2018, GNS opened its Geohazards Monitoring Centre with the support from MBIE and NEMA. The data collected by the GeoNet network (sensors, cameras, gauges etc) informs GNS’s science and enables our 24/7 monitoring. It helps us understand more about what’s happening and allows us to provide crucial insights and support to emergency management agencies, allowing them to make informed decisions planning for and responding to geohazard events.\n\nWe are pretty unique in the world in terms of monitoring earthquakes, volcanos, landslides and tsunami under one roof… I’m really proud that as a country we are there to help New Zealanders to stay safer.\n\nGeoNet’s network includes over 1,000 instruments. We believe GeoNet is now a vital piece of Aotearoa New Zealand’s infrastructure.\n\nAnd as impressive as the growing network is, GeoNet isn’t just about the instruments and the data – we’re equally proud of the people at GNS Science who bring this network to life. Their monitoring, maintenance and interpretation has led to a world-leading system that contributes to a safer and more resilient Aotearoa New Zealand.\n\nSo, a big warm thank you to our founders and partners. We thank all the scientists, researchers and data users. We’d like to give a special shout out to all our GeoNet teams (past and present) - the people and communities who host our equipment. Together, you have all made this amazing work possible"
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator is very proud of the GeoNet datasets and their impact, and they and a humbling amount of researchers, scientists and other data users are hugely indebted to the hard-work and vision of GeoNet project members, partners and funders. As Data Manager, they are proud to uphold the open data principles at the core of GeoNet, and the partnerships and excellent research that leverage that data, and, as a Scientist in Charge and member of various peril response panels here at Earth Sciences New Zealand, they’re proud of their key role underpinning geohazard response and related emergency management here in NZ, alongside its partners, both in emergency management and across the science system.\n\nTo invest in open data wasn’t their decision, but it was an inspired one at the time, and has paid off impressively. While the team is always looking to improve, it’s been great to see the focus of FAIR (Findable, Accessible, Interoperable and Reusable) on GeoNet data, formalizing and giving scope to an ethos that already existed. NZ is a great natural laboratory for geoscience, but that is unlocked by the open and vital GeoNet datasets."
        }
      ]
    },
