    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:041",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "Global 1-km Downscaled Urban Land Fraction Grids, SSP-Consistent Projections and Base Year, v1 (2000 - 2100)",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:041",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:earth-surface-natural-hazards-geology-near-surface-geophysics"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Jing Gao, Brian O'Neill, Martino Pesaresi"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://doi.org/10.7910/DVN/0EGDOK"
      ],
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "DOI",
          "value": "https://doi.org/10.7910/DVN/0EGDOK"
        }
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:harvard-dataverse"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "Jing Gao ( jinggao@udel.edu )"
        }
      ],
      "description": "This dataset consists of time-series of global maps showing future spatial distributions of urban lands at the 1-km spatial resolution, under five urbanization scenarios consistent with the Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (SSPs), updated decadally throughout the 21st century (2000-2100). The maps were designed to be inputs to Earth system models for investigating interactions between the Earth and human systems across a wide range of spatial scales from local to global. Before this dataset became available, urban land had usually been assumed to not change over time when modeling large-scope socio-environmental interactions even for long-term futures; some urban land change modeling efforts existed, but previously existing models originated from socioeconomic disciplines where short time horizons (< 30 years) are common, hence the models came with strong assumptions inappropriate for long time horizons. However, for studying how urban systems interact with long-term Earth-system phenomena (e.g., climate change), sound long-term modeling of urban changes is necessary. The authors developed a novel data-driven urban land modeling framework specifically for long time horizons, capturing changes from local to global scales, using unprecedented observational data spanning 40 years, and used it to generate this dataset. The methods were intensively evaluated for their statistical robustness, spatial generalizability, and temporal generalizability, as well as thoroughly compared with previous methods. The associated publication was a Nature Communications Top-50 Article in Earth, Environmental, and Planetary Sciences for 2020 ( https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-020-15788-7 ) ( https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-021-01052-0 ).",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "Gao, J., & O’Neill, B. C. (2020). Mapping global urban land for the 21st century with data-driven simulations and Shared Socioeconomic Pathways. Nature Communications, 11(1), 2302. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-020-15788-7",
          "identifier": [
            {
              "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
              "propertyID": "DOI",
