    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:041",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:041",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:041",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1778-8909"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "Because of the methodological advances underlying the production of this dataset, it is the first, and until now only, urban land change dataset specifically designed for understanding long-term human-Earth system interactions across spatial scales. It has been used broadly by researchers, practitioners, and students globally. As the corresponding author of the dataset, I receive frequent contacts regarding the data from a wide range of people. Recent inquires/users include, for example, a start-up company in Japan, college students in Indonesia, investigative journalists from UK, local government analysts in the U.S., etc. The dataset can be used in coordination with a closely related dataset produced by the same author, \"Global 1-km Downscaled Population Grids, SSP-Consistent Projections and Base Year, v1.01 (2000 - 2100)\" ( https://doi.org/10.7910/DVN/TLJ99B ), to investigate how future urbanization and environmental changes may interact to together affect future populations' exposures to various social and environmental stressors, and how that subsequently influence the wellbeing and resilience of future communities."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator is the lead author and primary contact of the dataset. They use the dataset in their own research examining how coordinated urban design at the regional scale can help moderate undesired climate change impacts on future populations in the long run. They also respond to all inquiries regarding the dataset, providing potential users with guidance and assistance as needed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:041",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 42,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Earth Surface, Natural Hazards, Geology, Near Surface Geophysics"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "Global 1-km Downscaled Urban Land Fraction Grids, SSP-Consistent Projections and Base Year, v1 (2000 - 2100)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Jing Gao, Brian O'Neill, Martino Pesaresi"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.7910/DVN/0EGDOK"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "Harvard Dataverse"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "https://dataverse.harvard.edu/dataverse/geospatial_human_dimensions_data"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Jing Gao ( jinggao@udel.edu )"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "This dataset consists of time-series of global maps showing future spatial distributions of urban lands at the 1-km spatial resolution, under five urbanization scenarios consistent with the Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (SSPs), updated decadally throughout the 21st century (2000-2100). The maps were designed to be inputs to Earth system models for investigating interactions between the Earth and human systems across a wide range of spatial scales from local to global. Before this dataset became available, urban land had usually been assumed to not change over time when modeling large-scope socio-environmental interactions even for long-term futures; some urban land change modeling efforts existed, but previously existing models originated from socioeconomic disciplines where short time horizons (< 30 years) are common, hence the models came with strong assumptions inappropriate for long time horizons. However, for studying how urban systems interact with long-term Earth-system phenomena (e.g., climate change), sound long-term modeling of urban changes is necessary. The authors developed a novel data-driven urban land modeling framework specifically for long time horizons, capturing changes from local to global scales, using unprecedented observational data spanning 40 years, and used it to generate this dataset. The methods were intensively evaluated for their statistical robustness, spatial generalizability, and temporal generalizability, as well as thoroughly compared with previous methods. The associated publication was a Nature Communications Top-50 Article in Earth, Environmental, and Planetary Sciences for 2020 ( https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-020-15788-7 ) ( https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-021-01052-0 )."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Because of the methodological advances underlying the production of this dataset, it is the first, and until now only, urban land change dataset specifically designed for understanding long-term human-Earth system interactions across spatial scales. It has been used broadly by researchers, practitioners, and students globally. As the corresponding author of the dataset, I receive frequent contacts regarding the data from a wide range of people. Recent inquires/users include, for example, a start-up company in Japan, college students in Indonesia, investigative journalists from UK, local government analysts in the U.S., etc. The dataset can be used in coordination with a closely related dataset produced by the same author, \"Global 1-km Downscaled Population Grids, SSP-Consistent Projections and Base Year, v1.01 (2000 - 2100)\" ( https://doi.org/10.7910/DVN/TLJ99B ), to investigate how future urbanization and environmental changes may interact to together affect future populations' exposures to various social and environmental stressors, and how that subsequently influence the wellbeing and resilience of future communities."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator is the lead author and primary contact of the dataset. They use the dataset in their own research examining how coordinated urban design at the regional scale can help moderate undesired climate change impacts on future populations in the long run. They also respond to all inquiries regarding the dataset, providing potential users with guidance and assistance as needed."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Gao, J., & O’Neill, B. C. (2020). Mapping global urban land for the 21st century with data-driven simulations and Shared Socioeconomic Pathways. Nature Communications, 11(1), 2302. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-020-15788-7 \n\nGao, J., & Pesaresi, M. (2021). Downscaling SSP-consistent global spatial urban land projections from 1/8-degree to 1-km resolution 2000–2100. Scientific Data, 8(1), 281. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-021-01052-0"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Rohat, G., Wilhelmi, O., Flacke, J., Monaghan, A., Gao, J., Van Maarseveen, M., & Dao, H. (2021). Assessing urban heat-related adaptation strategies under multiple futures for a major U.S. city. Climatic Change, 164(3–4), 61. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10584-021-02990-9 \n\nBukovsky, M. S., Gao, J., Mearns, L. O., & O'Neill, B. C. (2021). SSP-based land-use change scenarios: A critical uncertainty in future regional climate change projections. Earth's Future, 9, e2020EF001782. https://doi.org/10.1029/2020EF001782 \n\nGao, J., & Bukovsky, M. S. (2023). Urban land patterns can moderate population exposures to climate extremes over the 21st century. Nature Communications, 14(1), 6536. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-42084-x \n\nChakraborty, T. C., Venter, Z. S., Demuzere, M., Zhan, W., Gao, J., Zhao, L., & Qian, Y. (2024). Large disagreements in estimates of urban land across scales and their implications. Nature Communications, 15(1), 9165. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-52241-5 \n\nVan Vliet, J., Yang, H., Benz, N., Cheng, C., Doelman, J., Gao, J., Huang, Q., Koomen, E., Li, X., Niu, L., Schrammeijer, E. A., & Zhou, Y. (2026). Hotspots of disagreement across global urban land projections until 2100. Geography and Sustainability, 7(1), 100403. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.geosus.2025.100403"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Jing Gao"
        },
