    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:042",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "Ground-Based Global Navigation Satellite System (GNSS) Hatanaka-compressed Observation Data (1-second sampling, sub-hourly files)",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:042",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:earth-s-interior-geodesy"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "International GNSS Service (IGS)"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://doi.org/10.5067/GNSS/GNSS_HIGHRATE_D_001"
      ],
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "DOI",
          "value": "https://doi.org/10.5067/GNSS/GNSS_HIGHRATE_D_001"
        }
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:cddis"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "International GNSS Service (IGS)"
        }
      ],
      "description": "This dataset consists of ground-based Global Navigation Satellite System (GNSS) Observation Data (1-second sampling, sub-hourly files) from the NASA Crustal Dynamics Data Information System (CDDIS). GNSS provides autonomous geo-spatial positioning with global coverage. GNSS data sets from ground receivers at the CDDIS consist primarily of the data from the U.S. Global Positioning System (GPS) and the Russian GLObal NAvigation Satellite System (GLONASS). Since 2011, the CDDIS GNSS archive includes data from other GNSS (Europe’s Galileo, China’s Beidou, Japan’s Quasi-Zenith Satellite System/QZSS, the Indian Regional Navigation Satellite System/IRNSS, and worldwide Satellite Based Augmentation Systems/SBASs), which are similar to the U.S. GPS in terms of the satellite constellation, orbits, and signal structure. The sub-hourly GNSS observation files (compact) contain 15 minutes of GPS or multi-GNSS observation (1-second sampling) data in RINEX format from a global permanent network of ground-based receivers, one file per 15 minutes per site.",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/21/21/7374? (references high-rate GNSS data)",
          "schema:url": [
            "https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/21/21/7374?"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9346-1978",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Ross Bagwell",
      "identifier": {
        "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
        "propertyID": "ORCID",
        "value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9346-1978"
      },
      "schema:email": "ross.bagwell@nasa.gov",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:ssai-nasa-10210-greenbelt-road-suite-600-lanham-md-20706"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:ssai-nasa-10210-greenbelt-road-suite-600-lanham-md-20706",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "SSAI/NASA 10210 Greenbelt Road, Suite 600, Lanham, MD 20706"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:042",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:042",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:042",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9346-1978"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "If this dataset were unavailable, it would create significant blind spots across research, operations, and societal applications.\n\nPeople\n- there would be a loss of high-resolution training data for geodesy, geophysics, and Earth observations in academia, reducing the ability to practice with real, operational datasets\n- there would be reduced disaster preparedness, losing the ability to detect rapid ground deformations during earthquakes, volcanic eruptions, landslides, etc in near-real time\n- there would be slower post-event analyses, as emergency managers and researchers would lack second-by-second positioning changes needed to understand event dynamics and improve future warnings\n- we would have less effective early warning systems, as without sub-second or second-level GNSS signals, such things as tsunami and seismic early warnings would be delayed or less accurate\n\nPlanet\n- there would be a weakened ability to monitor tectonic motion and strain accumulation on fault lines, especially short-term precursors detectable only at high temporal resolutions\n- there would be a reduced capacity for ionospheric and atmospheric sensing, as high-rate GNSS observations help model disturbances from such things as solar storms, weather systems, and climate events\n- there would be less precision in tracking cryospheric events such as glacier, ice sheet, and sea-level changes that require high-frequency, long-term GNSS monitoring to capture subtle seasonal and tidal effects\n- there would be a gap in monitoring sudden environmental events, such as calving glaciers, sinkholes, or ground subsidence from groundwater extraction, which occur on short timescales\n\nProsperity\n- there would be lower resilience in infrastructure modeling; such things as bridges, dams, and tall buildings often use high-rate GNSS for structural health monitoring to prevent failures\n- there would be a weakened humanitarian response capability, as GNSS displacement data aids in identifying the hardest-hit areas after a disaster like earthquakes or volcanic eruptions, guiding relief logistics\n- there would be an impact on precision agriculture, offshore engineering, and transportation safety, all of which rely on accurate, real-time GNSS positioning for efficiency and safety\n- there would be a loss of equitable access to open, high-quality global datasets, which can widen the gap between countries or institutions with expensive proprietary data and those without\n\nfinally, we would essentially lose the fine temporal detail that turns GNSS from a mapping tool into a real-time Earth monitoring and hazard detection system, the result of which would be slower science, less accurate predictions, and reduced capacity to protect the people, the planet, and global economics."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "As the Deputy Manager for CDDIS, the nominator is entrusted with shaping the operations for their data center to be able to provide this dataset to the public. The nominator’s team interacts with the International GNSS Service (IGS) - the nominator is also the Data Centre Coordinator for the IGS - and their team constantly relies on the international community to ensure the highest quality of data is available."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:042",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 43,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
