        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "EarthChem Team"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "PetDB, the Petrological Database, is a web-based, free and open access data compilation that provides on-line access to geochemical and petrological data. PetDB is a global synthesis of chemical, isotopic, and mineralogical data for rocks, minerals, and melt inclusions. PetDB's current content focuses on data for igneous and metamorphic rocks from the ocean floor, specifically mid-ocean ridge basalts and abyssal peridotites and xenolith samples from the Earth's mantle and lower crust. PetDB is maintained and continuously updated as part of the EarthChem data collections. \nPetDB was first available online in the late 1990s, and comprises the most comprehensive compilation of laboratory analytical data of rocks and minerals from the oceanic realm, counting 7.4 million data values compiled from over 3800 peer-reviewed journal articles.\nPetDB is part of IEDA2 (https://www.iedadata.org/) hosted and maintained by Columbia University. PetDB data is also available through the EarthChem Portal which provides harmonised access to data from PetDB, the GEOROC database, and the USGS National Geochemical Database simultaneously, as well as content from databases that are no longer actively maintained and accessible (SedDB, NAVDAT, MetPetDB).\n(adapted from https://www.re3data.org/repository/r3d100011235 and https://earthchem.org)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nPetDB was nominated because it is one of the most useful databases for geochemists, or anyone using elemental (major, trace element concentrations) or isotope data. The nominator also used the dataset in training an undergraduate level courses.\n\nNominator 2:\nPetDB is a comprehensive and enduring open-access geochemical compilation of laboratory analytical data of rocks and minerals from the ocean floor and global mantle xenoliths. It contains harmonized chemical and isotopic data for volcanic, igneous and metamorphic rocks, integrating decades of analyses published in the academic literature. PetDB offers a highly customisable web search that has enabled research across disciplines as well as Earth Sciences teaching. PetDB is managed by the EarthChem system, which also provides joint data access with 5 other distinct geochemical databases. \n\nAmongst many other collaborations, EarthChem partnered with the Carleton College to develop a set of robust, freely available teaching resources for Earth Science students as well as the general public (https://serc.carleton.edu/research_education/cyberinfrastructure/listings.html). About 15% of users declare they access data for educational purposes.\n\nThe PetDB data compilation has enabled numerous new and highly impactful research discoveries across the Earth Sciences and adjacent disciplines. PetDB is cited by ca. 1,200 research publications that have accrued over 20,600 citations themselves. The most impactful papers have revolutionised our understanding of the composition of the Earth’s crust and mantle, and by extension the evolution of some of the largest reservoirs of our planet. \nPetDB data has also contributed significantly to economic geology and to environmental studies, in particular of the critical zone. \n\nFinally, PetDB as part of the wider EarthChem and IEDA infrastructure has played a pioneering role in establishing Open and FAIR data principles in geochemistry, and led multiple international efforts to harmonise and democratise data access."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nTo interpret sources and geological processes, comparison data are essential. The nominator incorporates published literature datasets into every publication, whether independently or with students. For training purposes, third-level undergraduate students download a dataset (MORBs of one the oceans) and use it to model TE and isotope variations.\n\nNominator 2:\nThe nominator’s research relies on the mafic geochemical rock record to decipher melting processes in the Earth's mantle, and how dynamics of the upper mantle has evolved through time. As such, they rely on comprehensive global datasets from both the oceans and the continents, and PetDB (and the EarthChem Portal) is an invaluable resource for them. It functions both as a regularly updated \"literature search\" to highlight where appropriate samples exist, as well as providing easy and fast access to the full analyses and source literature of thousands of samples. These data would be impossible to generate within any one research group, and their compilation from the primary literature would take years (and be extremely time consuming to maintain and keep up to date). The highly customisable search options offered by PetDB have so far answered every (data) questions they have had and allowed them to focus more of their time on the interpretation and computational analysis, rather than the compilation, of geochemical data."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Weis, D., Harrison, L. N., McMillan, R., & Williamson, N. M. B. (2020). Fine‐scale structure of earth’s deep mantle resolved through statistical analysis of hawaiian basalt geochemistry. Geochemistry, Geophysics, Geosystems, 21(11), e2020GC009292. https://doi.org/10.1029/2020GC009292 \n\nLehnert, K., Su, Y., Langmuir, C. H., Sarbas, B., & Nohl, U. (2000). A global geochemical database structure for rocks. Geochemistry, Geophysics, Geosystems, 1(5), 1999GC000026. https://doi.org/10.1029/1999GC000026"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Weis, D., Harpp, K. S., Harrison, L. N., Boyet, M., Chauvel, C., Farnetani, C. G., Finlayson, V. A., Lee, K. K. M., Parai, R., Shahar, A., & Williamson, N. M. B. (2023). Earth’s mantle composition revealed by mantle plumes. Nature Reviews Earth & Environment, 4(9), 604–625. https://doi.org/10.1038/s43017-023-00467-0"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nDominique Weis\n\nNominator 2:\nMarthe Klöcking"
        },
