          "schema:value": "The GEOSCOPE network (G) is a global seismological network created in 1982 to respond to the challenge of instrumenting isolated sites all around the world. The GEOSCOPE network is primarily dedicated to research and its data are used for studies of Earth structure and dynamics, seismic sources, time dependent seismology and environmental seismology.\n\nMost of the stations include a very broad band seismometer (STS1 or new generation T360), and the remaining ones a broadband seismometer (STS2 or T120/240). Stations are usually installed in vaults with special equipments aimed at reducing long period noise related to atmospheric pressure variation. \n\nData of all stations are publicly available, both in real-time and after final validation, at the IPGP, Epos-France and Earthscope data centers."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The GEOSCOPE network was the first global seismic network in the 1980s to make its dataset available to the worldwide seismological community. Since then, thanks to the Federation of Digal Seismograph Network (FDSN), a standardized format of archival was defined, seismic data are stored, shared and distributed openly. This policy of free and open data sharing on a global scale has also been adopted in other scientific fields. This international cooperation has made it possible to obtain the best possible global coverage of broadband seismic stations,—something that no single country could have accomplished on its own. GEOSCOPE stations rank among the world’s lowest-noise seismic stations. Their data are widely used on a routine basis for structural and earthquake investigations . More generally, the GEOSCOPE dataset contributes to the monitoring and mitigation of seismic, volcanic, and environmental hazards."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator was one of the first directors of the GEOSCOPE network, and the main creator of the GEOSCOPE data center. At the beginning of 1990s, he also developed the first world wide web site providing easy access to the seismic data. His approach was based on the worldwide free exchange of data, in order to control their quality and reliability through the feedback of users. He was also one of the first beneficiaries of these data, since he used them to derive his own tomographic models of the Earth."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Roult G., Montagner J.P., Stutzmann E., Barbier S., Guiveneux G., The Geoscope program: Its data center, Phys. Earth Planet. Int., 113, 25-43, 1999. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0031-9201(99)00024-2 \n\nLeroy N., Vallée, M., Zigone D. et al., GEOSCOPE network: 40 Yr of global broadband seismic data, Seismol. Res. Lett., 95, 1495–1517, 2024. https://doi.org/10.1785/0220230176"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Geoscope data have been widely used (more than 1700 publications since 1982) in a lot of seismological fields, in particular for deep Earth and earthquake studies.\n\nThe list of publications (years 2004-2023) using GEOSCOPE data is available on : http://geoscope.ipgp.fr/index.php/en/observatory/area-of-documentation/list-of-publications-based-on geoscope-data"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Jean-Paul Montagner"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Email Address",
          "schema:value": "jpmontagner@gmail.com"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator ORCID",
          "schema:value": "orcid.org/0000-0001-9958-3012"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Affiliation or Organization",
          "schema:value": "Université Paris Cité, Institut de Physique du Globe de Paris,\n1 rue Jussieu, 75238 Paris, France"
        }
      ]
    },
