    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:046",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "GEONET",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:046",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:earth-s-interior-geodesy"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "The Geospatial Information Authority of Japan"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://www.gsi.go.jp/ENGLISH/geonet_english.html"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:geonet"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "Geodetic Observation Center, Geospatial Information Authority of Japan"
        }
      ],
      "description": "GEONET is a continuous GNSS network in the Japanese islands, comprising ~1300 sites with an average station coverage of around 20 km. GEONET was established in the mid-1990s and has been the world's densest GNSS network. Over the last ~30 years of operation, GEONET has detected various deformation signals, primarily related to seismic and volcanic phenomena. GEONET has played a significant role in discovering new phenomena. For example, GEONET observed a slow-slip transient in the late 1990s for the first time. Additionally, GEONET advanced our understanding of the Earth's deformation in a plate boundary zone. \n\nIn addition, GEONET contributed to monitoring the troposphere and ionosphere. GEONET has observed various ionospheric disturbances, including those induced by earthquakes and volcanic eruptions. Furthermore, the Japan Meteorological Agency utilizes the tropospheric disturbance measured by GEONET for predicting torrential downpours.",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "Sagiya, T. (2014). A decade of GEONET: 1994–2003 —The continuous GPS observation in Japan and its impact on earthquake studies—. Earth, Planets and Space, 56(8), xxix–xli. https://doi.org/10.1186/BF03353077",
          "identifier": [
            {
              "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
              "propertyID": "DOI",
              "value": "https://doi.org/10.1186/BF03353077"
            }
          ],
          "schema:url": [
            "https://doi.org/10.1186/BF03353077"
          ]
        },
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "Takamatsu, N., Muramatsu, H., Abe, S., Hatanaka, Y., Furuya, T., Kakiage, Y., Ohashi, K., Kato, C., Ohno, K., & Kawamoto, S. (2023). New GEONET analysis strategy at GSI: Daily coordinates of over 1300 GNSS CORS in Japan throughout the last quarter century. Earth, Planets and Space, 75(1), 49. https://doi.org/10.1186/s40623-023-01787-7",
          "identifier": [
            {
              "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
              "propertyID": "DOI",
              "value": "https://doi.org/10.1186/s40623-023-01787-7"
            }
          ],
          "schema:url": [
            "https://doi.org/10.1186/s40623-023-01787-7"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2539-4144",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Yosuke Aoki",
      "identifier": {
        "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
        "propertyID": "ORCID",
        "value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2539-4144"
      },
      "schema:email": "yaoki@eri.u-tokyo.ac.jp",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:earthquake-research-institute-the-university-of-tokyo-1-1-1-yayoi-bunkyo-ku-toky"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:046",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:046",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:046",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2539-4144"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The dataset is impactful in the \"Planet\" category. \n\nGEONET is a continuous GNSS network in the Japanese islands, comprising ~1300 sites with an average station coverage of around 20 km. GEONET was established in the mid-1990s and has been the world's densest GNSS network. Over the last ~30 years of operation, GEONET has detected various deformation signals, primarily related to seismic and volcanic phenomena. GEONET has played a significant role in discovering new phenomena. For example, GEONET observed a slow-slip transient in the late 1990s for the first time. Additionally, GEONET advanced our understanding of the Earth's deformation in a plate boundary zone. \n\nIn addition, GEONET contributed to monitoring the troposphere and ionosphere. GEONET has observed various ionospheric disturbances, including those induced by earthquakes and volcanic eruptions. Furthermore, the Japan Meteorological Agency utilizes the tropospheric disturbance measured by GEONET for predicting torrential downpours. Recent studies have demonstrated that GNSS data from GEONET can enhance our understanding of water transport in the Earth's subsurface. \n\nIn summary, GNSS data from GEONET have made significant contributions to understanding not only the solid part of the Earth but also the Earth's troposphere and ionosphere in the last ~30 years. Therefore, GEONET is worth included in AGU commentary."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator’s research has heavily relied on GNSS data from GEONET. Their first publication on volcano deformation associated with magma intrusion (Aoki et al., 1999, Science) would have been impossible without GEONET. Some studies published during their postdoc (Aoki and Scholz, 2003a, b, JGR) are based solely on the GEONET dataset. Even after they secured a faculty position, they have continued to rely heavily on GEONET. At least one third of their peer-reviewed publications (~100) are directly or indirectly based on the GEONET dataset."
        }
      ]
    },
