          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Ulrike Weis; Brigitte Stoll"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "GeoReM is a database for reference materials of geological and environmental interest, such as rock powders, synthetic and natural glasses as well as mineral, isotopic, biological, river and seawater reference materials. GeoReM contains published analytical data and compilation values (major and trace element concentrations and mass fractions, radiogenic and stale isotope ratios). GeoReM contains all important metadata about the analytical values such as uncertainty, analytical method and laboratory. Sample information and references are also included.\nGeoReM contains about 67,500 analyses of more than 4,100 RM from about 13,900 peer-reviewed publications, certified values of 718 RM and preferred analytical values for 61 RM. GeoReM was accessed more than 34,000 times with 1000 downloads by researchers from 117 countries with most from China, USA, Germany, UK, France and Canada.\nEffective September 11, 2025, the rights to GeoReM and the associated responsibilities have been transferred from the Max Planck Institute for Chemistry in Mainz to the International Association of Geoanalysts (IAG), represented by IAGeo Limited (https://www.geoanalyst.org/). At the time of submission, the database and IT-related peripherals will be transferred to the DFG-funded DIGIS 2.0 project at the University of Göttingen, which will ensure a secure IT structure and curation of the database in collaboration with the IAG. The contact person for this process is Axel Renno (a.renno@hzdr.de)."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The impact of the GeoReM database is predicated on a fundamental principle of chemical analysis in the fields of geochemistry and environmental chemistry: the utilization of (certified) reference materials. These materials form the basis for the traceability and comparability of all data determined by chemical analysis and used for scientific and administrative decision-making processes and economic evaluations. The database contains reference materials and their certified and preferred values, as well as published analytical data on these reference materials. The utilization of the database facilitates a multifaceted evaluation of the analytical methods employed, the technical capabilities of these methods, the sample preparation methods utilized, the laboratories conducting the analyses, and the time period over which the analyses were conducted. The database has been developed to facilitate the development of new analytical methods and the modification of existing ones. It also facilitates the rapid calibration of ongoing analytical processes when used online.\nThe database is employed in the training of geoscientists specializing in chemical analysis and technical laboratory personnel. The data thus obtained facilitate quality assurance and quality management in environmental geochemical investigations, as well as the necessary technical and administrative steps derived from them for the prevention, management, and mitigation of damage.\nThe utilization of the database exerts an indirect influence on all domains of Earth system science that employ geochemical data and models derived from it. The utilization of this material for calibrating geochemical analytical methods and ensuring traceability in deposit evaluation and monitoring of environmental regulations has been demonstrated to result in direct economic benefits."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "Interaction with the database currently takes place via the website (https://georem.mpch-mainz.gwdg.de/) using specific queries for samples, materials, chemical criteria, bibliographic information, methods, or institutions."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Jochum, K. P., Nohl, U., Herwig, K., Lammel, E., Stoll, B., & Hofmann, A. W. (2005). Georem: A new geochemical database for reference materials and isotopic standards. Geostandards and Geoanalytical Research, 29(3), 333–338. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1751-908X.2005.tb00904.x"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Google Scholar gives (as of October 10, 2025) 2540 results for the use of GeoReM (https://scholar.google.de/scholar?hl=de&as_sdt=0%2C5&q=georem+database&oq=GeoReM). \nSome examples of such publications are:\n\nJochum, K. P., Weis, U., Schwager, B., Stoll, B., Wilson, S. A., Haug, G. H., Andreae, M. O., & Enzweiler, J. (2016). Reference values following iso guidelines for frequently requested rock reference materials. Geostandards and Geoanalytical Research, 40(3), 333–350. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1751-908X.2015.00392.x \n\nDe Hoog, J. C. M. & EIMF. (2018). Matrix effects during sims measurement of the lithium mass fractions of silicate glasses: Correction procedures and updated preferred values of reference materials. Geostandards and Geoanalytical Research, 42(4), 513–522. https://doi.org/10.1111/ggr.12237 \n\nKarasiński, J., Tupys, A., Halicz, L., & Bulska, E. (2021). A novel approach for the determination of the ge isotope ratio using liquid–liquid extraction and hydride generation by multicollector inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry. Analytical Chemistry, 93(40), 13548–13554. https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.analchem.1c02491 \n\nBai, J.-H., Liu, F., Zhang, Z.-F., Ma, J.-L., Zhang, L., Liu, Y.-F., Zhong, S.-X., & Wei, G.-J. (2021). Simultaneous measurement stable and radiogenic Nd isotopic compositions by MC-ICP-MS with a single-step chromatographic extraction technique. Journal of Analytical Atomic Spectrometry, 36(12), 2695–2703. https://doi.org/10.1039/D1JA00302J \n\nPotts, P. J., & Webb, P. C. (2022). Simple test for the fitness for purpose of geological reference materials according to the uncertainties associated with their reference values. Geostandards and Geoanalytical Research, 46(3), 477–492. https://doi.org/10.1111/ggr.12439"
