    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:048",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "The GEOROC database",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:048",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:earth-s-interior-geodesy"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Bärbel Sarbas"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Albrecht Hofmann"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "DIGIS team"
        }
      ],
      "agu:identifierLiteral": [
        "georoc.eu"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:georoc-eu"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "Digital Geochemical Infrastructure (DIGIS) team"
        }
      ],
      "description": "The GEOROC Database (Geochemistry of Rocks of the Oceans and Continents) is a comprehensive collection of published datasets with analyses of igneous and metamorphic rocks and minerals. It contains 40.8 million values of major and trace element concentrations, radiogenic and nonradiogenic isotope ratios as well as analytical ages for whole rocks, glasses, minerals and inclusions. Metadata include geospatial and other sample information, analytical method details and source references, which are all fully searchable for downloading custom datasets.\nThe construction of the GEOROC database under the direction of Bärbel Sarbas and Albrecht Hofmann began in January 1998 at the Max Planck Institute for Chemistry, Mainz. GEOROC has been available online since the end of 1999. Since 2006, GEOROC datasets are also available through the EarthChem Portal. The content of the GEOROC database was initially limited to geochemical analyses of ocean island basalts, but was soon expanded to include samples of volcanic rocks from other tectonic environments and the mantle xenoliths they contain as well as associated minerals and a selection of metamorphic rocks. In August 2021, the GEOROC database was formally transferred to the Georg-August-Universität Göttingen, where the new GEOROC group, including Bärbel Sarbas, is continuing to maintain and grow the database. At Göttingen, the database IT-infrastructure is being updated as part of the DIGIS-project to improve the searchability and better adhere to FAIR data principles.",
