    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:048",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:048",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:048",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2613-8086",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6667-2633",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6592-9270",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3317-5697"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "The GEOROC database is one of the most comprehensive and enduring open-access geochemical hard rock compilations in Earth and planetary sciences. It contains harmonized chemical and isotopic data for volcanic, igneous and metamorphic rocks from every tectonic setting on Earth. By integrating over 100 years of published analyses into a consistent, searchable dataset, GEOROC enables reproducible research, long-term data stewardship and global collaboration across disciplines.\nGEOROC supports education and capacity building in geochemistry and Earth System Science worldwide. It is widely used in courses, workshops and training programs, helping students and researchers develop data literacy and quantitative reasoning skills. Its open accessibility fosters equity in education and research, particularly for communities with limited laboratory resources. Beyond academia, GEOROC contributes to geohealth and natural hazard research, improving understanding of geological processes that might affect air and water quality, bedrock radiation and human health. The dataset also strengthens disaster preparedness and promotes geoheritage awareness by linking scientific insight with local landscapes.\nGEOROC underpins major advances in our understanding of plate tectonics, Earth evolution and climate feedback loops. Its global coverage allows scientists to trace mantle heterogeneity, crustal growth and subduction-related recycling, revealing how the solid Earth interacts with the hydrosphere and atmosphere. The dataset is critical for studying volcanic degassing, supervolcano eruption events and global geochemical cycles, which influence both short-term and long-term climate and environmental changes. GEOROC also serves as a baseline for environmental geochemistry, distinguishing natural geochemical signals from anthropogenic pollution and providing insights into deep carbon and volatile cycles that link Earth’s interior to the surface environment.\nGEOROC contributes directly to resource assessment, critical raw material exploration and geothermal energy development for the green energy transition, informing strategies for sustainable resource use and climate change solutions. The dataset is used to identify geochemical fingerprints of ore-forming processes and to evaluate carbon capture, mineral sequestration and enhanced weathering potential in reactive rock systems. \nWithout GEOROC, the scientific community would lack a unified foundation for global geochemical synthesis, limiting our ability to trace Earth’s internal dynamics, assess natural hazards and manage planetary resources responsibly. As a long-standing example of FAIR geodata infrastructure, it advances open science and global prosperity through shared access to reliable, well-curated data. Its sustained accessibility and impact across the dimensions of people, planet and prosperity make GEOROC a cornerstone of modern geoscience."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "As a PhD student who studies alkaline and potassic melts, GEOROC is one of a limited set of resources the nominator can utilize to aid them in their research. On a personal level, they strongly believe that GEOROC has been essential to their success and has been the go-to database for their own and co-author papers. In a practical sense, during a recent review on a paper they submitted, one of the reviewers commended their expert synthesis of a significant and broad range of published data. While they take this praise, they have no issue in clearly stating in the manuscript and in this nomination the quality, completeness and comprehensiveness of the data they used belong to GEOROC and the team who runs this invaluable resource. Even down to the way they attribute sources and format their own data (submitted to the GEOROC repository hosted by the GFZ data services), they can humbly say was demonstrated to them through multiple community workshops on FAIR data best practise by GEOROC curators. The impact GEOROC has had on their career spans back to their 2nd year undergraduate degree, through their 3rd year and Masters degree and now into their PhD. In those early second and 3rd year courses where precompiled and expert GEOROC datasets were used as example sets for exercises, they earnt to see and understand geochemistry. Beyond being a fantastic research resource, GEOROC is also able to serve a tangible benefit in the teaching of future Earth Scientists and illustrate complex concepts. It is truly remarkably good, and it continues to improve"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:048",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 49,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Earth’s Interior, Geodesy"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "The GEOROC database"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Bärbel Sarbas, Albrecht Hofmann and DIGIS team"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "georoc.eu"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "georoc.eu"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Digital Geochemical Infrastructure (DIGIS) team"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The GEOROC Database (Geochemistry of Rocks of the Oceans and Continents) is a comprehensive collection of published datasets with analyses of igneous and metamorphic rocks and minerals. It contains 40.8 million values of major and trace element concentrations, radiogenic and nonradiogenic isotope ratios as well as analytical ages for whole rocks, glasses, minerals and inclusions. Metadata include geospatial and other sample information, analytical method details and source references, which are all fully searchable for downloading custom datasets.\nThe construction of the GEOROC database under the direction of Bärbel Sarbas and Albrecht Hofmann began in January 1998 at the Max Planck Institute for Chemistry, Mainz. GEOROC has been available online since the end of 1999. Since 2006, GEOROC datasets are also available through the EarthChem Portal. The content of the GEOROC database was initially limited to geochemical analyses of ocean island basalts, but was soon expanded to include samples of volcanic rocks from other tectonic environments and the mantle xenoliths they contain as well as associated minerals and a selection of metamorphic rocks. In August 2021, the GEOROC database was formally transferred to the Georg-August-Universität Göttingen, where the new GEOROC group, including Bärbel Sarbas, is continuing to maintain and grow the database. At Göttingen, the database IT-infrastructure is being updated as part of the DIGIS-project to improve the searchability and better adhere to FAIR data principles."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1a-1b-1c:\nThe GEOROC database is one of the most comprehensive and enduring open-access geochemical hard rock compilations in Earth and planetary sciences. It contains harmonized chemical and isotopic data for volcanic, igneous and metamorphic rocks from every tectonic setting on Earth. By integrating decades of published analyses into a consistent, searchable dataset, GEOROC enables reproducible research, long-term data stewardship and global collaboration across disciplines.\nGEOROC supports education and capacity building in geochemistry and Earth System Science worldwide. It is widely used in courses, workshops and training programs, helping students and researchers develop data literacy and quantitative reasoning skills. Its open accessibility fosters equity in education and research, particularly for communities with limited laboratory resources. Beyond academia, GEOROC contributes to geohealth and natural hazard research, improving understanding of geological processes that might affect air and water quality, bedrock radiation and human health. The dataset also strengthens disaster preparedness and promotes geoheritage awareness by linking scientific insight with local landscapes.\nGEOROC underpins major advances in our understanding of plate tectonics, Earth evolution and climate feedback loops. Its global coverage allows scientists to trace mantle heterogeneity, crustal growth and subduction-related recycling, revealing how the solid Earth interacts with the hydrosphere and atmosphere. The dataset is critical for studying volcanic degassing, supervolcano eruption events and global geochemical cycles, which influence both short-term and long-term climate and environmental changes. GEOROC also serves as a baseline for environmental geochemistry, distinguishing natural geochemical signals from anthropogenic pollution and providing insights into deep carbon and volatile cycles that link Earth’s interior to the surface environment.\nGEOROC contributes directly to resource assessment, critical raw material exploration and geothermal energy development for the green energy transition, informing strategies for sustainable resource use and climate change solutions. The dataset is used to identify geochemical fingerprints of ore-forming processes and to evaluate carbon capture, mineral sequestration and enhanced weathering potential in reactive rock systems. \nWithout GEOROC, the scientific community would lack a unified foundation for global geochemical synthesis, limiting our ability to trace Earth’s internal dynamics, assess natural hazards and manage planetary resources responsibly. As a long-standing example of FAIR geodata infrastructure, it advances open science and global prosperity through shared access to reliable, well-curated data. Its sustained accessibility and impact across the dimensions of people, planet and prosperity make GEOROC a cornerstone of modern geoscience.\n\nNominator 2:\nThe GEOROC database is one of the most comprehensive and enduring open-access geochemical hard rock compilations in Earth and planetary sciences. It contains harmonized chemical and isotopic data for volcanic, igneous and metamorphic rocks from every tectonic setting on Earth. By integrating over 100 years of published analyses into a consistent, searchable dataset, GEOROC enables reproducible research, long-term data stewardship and global collaboration across disciplines.\nGEOROC supports education and capacity building in geochemistry and Earth System Science worldwide. It is widely used in courses, workshops and training programs, helping students and researchers develop data literacy and quantitative reasoning skills. Its open accessibility fosters equity in education and research, particularly for communities with limited laboratory resources. Beyond academia, GEOROC contributes to geohealth and natural hazard research, improving understanding of geological processes that might affect air and water quality, bedrock radiation and human health. The dataset also strengthens disaster preparedness and promotes geoheritage awareness by linking scientific insight with local landscapes.\nGEOROC underpins major advances in our understanding of plate tectonics, Earth evolution and climate feedback loops. Its global coverage allows scientists to trace mantle heterogeneity, crustal growth and subduction-related recycling, revealing how the solid Earth interacts with the hydrosphere and atmosphere. The dataset is critical for studying volcanic degassing, supervolcano eruption events and global geochemical cycles, which influence both short-term and long-term climate and environmental changes. GEOROC also serves as a baseline for environmental geochemistry, distinguishing natural geochemical signals from anthropogenic pollution and providing insights into deep carbon and volatile cycles that link Earth’s interior to the surface environment.\nGEOROC contributes directly to resource assessment, critical raw material exploration and geothermal energy development for the green energy transition, informing strategies for sustainable resource use and climate change solutions. The dataset is used to identify geochemical fingerprints of ore-forming processes and to evaluate carbon capture, mineral sequestration and enhanced weathering potential in reactive rock systems. \nWithout GEOROC, the scientific community would lack a unified foundation for global geochemical synthesis, limiting our ability to trace Earth’s internal dynamics, assess natural hazards and manage planetary resources responsibly. As a long-standing example of FAIR geodata infrastructure, it advances open science and global prosperity through shared access to reliable, well-curated data. Its sustained accessibility and impact across the dimensions of people, planet and prosperity make GEOROC a cornerstone of modern geoscience."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1a-1b-1c:\nThe GEOROC datasets can be used through its open, free web portal, which requires no registration, allowing rapid access to geochemical data for igneous and metamorphic rock and mineral samples. Data can be queried by Bibliography, Geological setting, Geography, Chemistry and Petrography along with precompiled search results that are available for geological settings, lithologies, minerals and inclusion data (published through the DIGIS repository). GEOROC also hosts expert datasets, which are curated community compilations of special interest, providing additional high-value resources. An API is currently in development and testing, which will enhance access through coding applications and programmatic workflows. GEOROC is used as a practical example in data-focused courses and workshops run by its managers, the DIGIS team, helping students and researchers gain hands-on experience with geochemical data. Its connection to the GeoReM database for reference materials positions GEOROC at the forefront of developing tools for data quality assessment and standardization in geochemistry, supporting both research and education in a reliable, open-access framework.\n\nNominator 2:\nAs a PhD student who studies alkaline and potassic melts, GEOROC is one of a limited set of resources the nominator can utilize to aid them in their research. On a personal level, they strongly believe that GEOROC has been essential to their success and has been the go-to database for their own and co-author papers. In a practical sense, during a recent review on a paper they submitted, one of the reviewers commended their expert synthesis of a significant and broad range of published data. While they take this praise, they have no issue in clearly stating in the manuscript and in this nomination the quality, completeness and comprehensiveness of the data they used belong to GEOROC and the team who runs this invaluable resource. Even down to the way they attribute sources and format their own data (submitted to the GEOROC repository hosted by the GFZ data services), they can humbly say was demonstrated to them through multiple community workshops on FAIR data best practise by GEOROC curators. The impact GEOROC has had on their career spans back to their 2nd year undergraduate degree, through their 3rd year and Masters degree and now into their PhD. In those early second and 3rd year courses where precompiled and expert GEOROC datasets were used as example sets for exercises, they earnt to see and understand geochemistry. Beyond being a fantastic research resource, GEOROC is also able to serve a tangible benefit in the teaching of future Earth Scientists and illustrate complex concepts. It is truly remarkably good, and it continues to improve"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Lehnert, K., Su, Y., Langmuir, C. H., Sarbas, B., & Nohl, U. (2000). A global geochemical database structure for rocks. Geochemistry, Geophysics, Geosystems, 1(5), 1999GC000026. https://doi.org/10.1029/1999GC000026"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1a-1b-1c:\nFull list of publications citing GEOROC (and PetDB) compiled in July 2024:\n\nKlöcking, M., Lehnert, K., Digital Geochemical Infrastructure for GEOROC (DIGIS), & EarthChem. (2025). GEOROC and PetDB data usage and impact through time. Goldschmidt Conference 2024, Chicago. Zenodo. https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.15341817 \n\nSelected references that address the People, Planet or Prosperity Dimensions:\n\nBezuidenhout, J. (2021). Estimating indoor radon concentrations based on the uranium content of geological units in South Africa. Journal of Environmental Radioactivity, 234, 106647. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvrad.2021.106647 (People: geohealth)\n\nCooper, J. N., Cooper, A. M., Clausen, B. L., & Nick, K. E. (2018). Regional bedrock geochemistry associated with podoconiosis evaluated by multivariate analysis. Environmental Geochemistry and Health, 41(2), 649-665. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10653-018-0158-0 (People: geohealth)\n\nNakano, T., Yamashita, K., Ando, A., Kusaka, S., & Saitoh, Y. (2020). Geographic variation of Sr and S isotope ratios in bottled waters in Japan and sources of Sr and S. Science of The Total Environment, 704, 135449. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.135449 (People: water quality)\n\nTapia-Gatica, J., Selles, I., Bravo, M. A., Tessini, C., Barros-Parada, W., Novoselov, A., & Neaman, A. (2022). Global issues in setting legal limits on soil metal contamination: A case study of Chile. Chemosphere, 290, 133404. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chemosphere.2021.133404 (Planet: environmental remediation)\n\nHartmann, J., West, A. J., Renforth, P., Köhler, P., De La Rocha, C. L., Wolf-Gladrow, D. A., Dürr, H. H., & Scheffran, J. (2013). Enhanced chemical weathering as a geoengineering strategy to reduce atmospheric carbon dioxide, supply nutrients, and mitigate ocean acidification. Reviews of Geophysics, 51(2), 113-149. https://doi.org/10.1002/rog.20004 (Planet: climate)\n\nChiaradia, M. (2013). Copper enrichment in arc magmas controlled by overriding plate thickness. Nature Geoscience, 7(1), 43-46. https://doi.org/10.1038/ngeo2028 (Prosperity: economic good)\n\nAudétat, A., & Li, W. (2017). The genesis of Climax-type porphyry Mo deposits: Insights from fluid inclusions and melt inclusions. Ore Geology Reviews, 88, 436-460. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.oregeorev.2017.05.018 (Prosperity: economic good)\n\nImpactful publications that have significantly advanced knowledge of the composition and evolution of the Earth:\n\nGale, A., Dalton, C. A., Langmuir, C. H., Su, Y., & Schilling, J. (2013). The mean composition of ocean ridge basalts. Geochemistry, Geophysics, Geosystems, 14(3), 489-518. https://doi.org/10.1029/2012gc004334 \n\nHawkesworth, C. J., Dhuime, B., Pietranik, A. B., Cawood, P. A., Kemp, A. I. S., & Storey, C. D. (2010). The generation and evolution of the continental crust. Journal of the Geological Society, 167(2), 229-248. https://doi.org/10.1144/0016-76492009-072 \n\nHerzberg, C., Asimow, P. D., Arndt, N., Niu, Y., Lesher, C. M., Fitton, J. G., Cheadle, M. J., & Saunders, A. D. (2007). Temperatures in ambient mantle and plumes: Constraints from basalts, picrites, and komatiites. Geochemistry, Geophysics, Geosystems, 8(2). https://doi.org/10.1029/2006gc001390 \n\nStracke, A., Hofmann, A. W., & Hart, S. R. (2005). FOZO, HIMU, and the rest of the mantle zoo. Geochemistry, Geophysics, Geosystems, 6(5). https://doi.org/10.1029/2004GC000824 \n\nTurner, S. J., & Langmuir, C. H. (2015). The global chemical systematics of arc front stratovolcanoes: Evaluating the role of crustal processes. Earth and Planetary Science Letters, 422, 182–193. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.epsl.2015.03.056"
        },
