    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:049",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:049",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:049",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3638-1864"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The Australian thermochronology dataset from 2002 and prior provides a valuable resource for assessing the low-temperature (~60–120 °C) history of Australia throughout the Palaeozoic–Cenozoic. This thermal window is key to understanding the behavior and evolution of rocks and minerals in the upper crust in response to broad tectonic and geodynamic forces, including structural reactivation, basin initiation and evolution, palaeoclimate, climate change, and the creation and preservation of mineral and energy resources. These signals are often not preserved in more widely available geochemical and isotopic tools, and thus this dataset helps to fill a key gap in our understanding of lithosphere evolution. \n\nIn an Australian context, low-temperature data is vital for assessing the continental history following amalgamation and widespread orogenesis across the West Australian Craton, North Australian Craton and South Australian Craton over one billion years ago, and in evaluating the impact and duration of thermal perturbations during the more recent crustal accretion during the Palaeozoic–Mesozoic which built the eastern extent of the continent. \n\nDetailed constraints on low-temperature thermal evolution can also be particularly relevant for evaluating formation and preservation potential of economic mineralisation to reduce exploration risk, allowing regions to be triaged for appropriate ancient tectonic activity, and exhumation levels sufficient to expose mineralisation at accessible depths without eroding the deposit. Within onshore and offshore sedimentary basins thermochronology data has been extensively used to constrain basin evolution, with impacts for understanding of rate and nature of material buried in hydrological systems, tectonic drivers of basin formation, and the maturation and preservation of hydrocarbon reserves. In some cases, this data has also been used to infer changing temperatures of groundwater and potentially associated flow dynamics in sedimentary aquifers. Beyond extractive and economic impacts, this thermochronology dataset has been vital for predicting denudation rates across the continent, and identifying areas of structural reactivation in response to complex geodynamic forces. \n\nThe detailed and open thermochronology data covering the breadth of the Australian continent is critical for our understanding of the crustal evolution throughout the previous ~300 million years. Thermal modelling and interpretation of denudation rates and associated tectonic significance was conducted on this dataset by Kohn et al. (2002) over two decades ago, but the recent availability of this data in a reusable format allows for this work to be revisited and enhanced by the addition of new analyses and improved computational tools. For thermochronology studies spanning continental to local contexts, this collection provides a fundamental resource for researchers to contextualise their data within the broader Australian framework to produce high quality, spatially coherent results which build on the wealth of knowledge available across the continent."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "This dataset can be visualised through the EarthBank platform map view to spatially explore aspects including fission track age for each mineral species, mean track lengths, and sample lithologies. Thermal history models can additionally provide information on palaeotemperature and cooling rates at desired past time intervals, which can be coupled with reconstruction of sample locations back in time with tectonic plate models to help reference thermal responses to ancient plate motions. More customizable plotting functionalities are also available for cross-plotting attributes within the compilation. Importantly, these features are not limited to this collection but may be applied across a number of datasets, including new and emerging analyses to assist in geological interpretations. The complete Australian thermochronology dataset for 2002 and prior dataset, including single-grain results, thermal history paths and sample metadata, can also be freely exported in standardised formats for use with external software for specific applications, such as thermal history modelling with alternate software or approaches. Sample data may also be exported as a variety of GIS-ready files for use in geospatial software."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:049",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 50,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Earth’s Interior, Geodesy"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "Australian Thermochronology - 2002 and prior"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Barry Kohn, Andrew Gleadow, Roderick Brown, Kerry Gallagher, Paul O'Sullivan, David Foster, Samuel Boone, Malcolm McMillan"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.58024/agum46bd292f"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "AuScope EarthBank Platform"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.17616/R31NJNAO"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "AuScope Geochemistry Network"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "This dataset contains low-temperature thermochronology analyses derived primarily from Archaean to Palaeozoic igneous rocks and their metamorphic equivalents across the Australian continent from the 1970s to 2002. It includes detailed information on sample locations, lithologies, over 1,800 apatite fission track age determinations, mean confined track length measurements and thermal history models, as well as minor data from titanite and zircon fission track analyses. The collection additionally contains digitised thermal history paths which provide time-temperature predictions for reconstructed intervals and inverse model parameters for each modelled sample, allowing for evaluation of the thermal state of crustal rocks through time. Originally reported and discussed by Kohn et al. (2002) and Gleadow et al. (2002), these data provide key insights into the low-temperature tectono-thermal evolution of Australia’s upper crust over the past ~300 million years, including denudation rates across the continent and the influence of regional tectonic episodes. A subset of this collection including only sample-level ages and mean track lengths is available at the PANGAEA repository (https://doi.org/10.1594/PANGAEA.911861), however, the full collection described here includes much more detailed single-grain and thermal history data provided in a standardised format to allow for data reuse with emerging studies and technology."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The Australian thermochronology dataset from 2002 and prior provides a valuable resource for assessing the low-temperature (~60–120 °C) history of Australia throughout the Palaeozoic–Cenozoic. This thermal window is key to understanding the behavior and evolution of rocks and minerals in the upper crust in response to broad tectonic and geodynamic forces, including structural reactivation, basin initiation and evolution, palaeoclimate, climate change, and the creation and preservation of mineral and energy resources. These signals are often not preserved in more widely available geochemical and isotopic tools, and thus this dataset helps to fill a key gap in our understanding of lithosphere evolution. \n\nIn an Australian context, low-temperature data is vital for assessing the continental history following amalgamation and widespread orogenesis across the West Australian Craton, North Australian Craton and South Australian Craton over one billion years ago, and in evaluating the impact and duration of thermal perturbations during the more recent crustal accretion during the Palaeozoic–Mesozoic which built the eastern extent of the continent. \n\nDetailed constraints on low-temperature thermal evolution can also be particularly relevant for evaluating formation and preservation potential of economic mineralisation to reduce exploration risk, allowing regions to be triaged for appropriate ancient tectonic activity, and exhumation levels sufficient to expose mineralisation at accessible depths without eroding the deposit. Within onshore and offshore sedimentary basins thermochronology data has been extensively used to constrain basin evolution, with impacts for understanding of rate and nature of material buried in hydrological systems, tectonic drivers of basin formation, and the maturation and preservation of hydrocarbon reserves. In some cases, this data has also been used to infer changing temperatures of groundwater and potentially associated flow dynamics in sedimentary aquifers. Beyond extractive and economic impacts, this thermochronology dataset has been vital for predicting denudation rates across the continent, and identifying areas of structural reactivation in response to complex geodynamic forces. \n\nThe detailed and open thermochronology data covering the breadth of the Australian continent is critical for our understanding of the crustal evolution throughout the previous ~300 million years. Thermal modelling and interpretation of denudation rates and associated tectonic significance was conducted on this dataset by Kohn et al. (2002) over two decades ago, but the recent availability of this data in a reusable format allows for this work to be revisited and enhanced by the addition of new analyses and improved computational tools. For thermochronology studies spanning continental to local contexts, this collection provides a fundamental resource for researchers to contextualise their data within the broader Australian framework to produce high quality, spatially coherent results which build on the wealth of knowledge available across the continent."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "This dataset can be visualised through the EarthBank platform map view to spatially explore aspects including fission track age for each mineral species, mean track lengths, and sample lithologies. Thermal history models can additionally provide information on palaeotemperature and cooling rates at desired past time intervals, which can be coupled with reconstruction of sample locations back in time with tectonic plate models to help reference thermal responses to ancient plate motions. More customizable plotting functionalities are also available for cross-plotting attributes within the compilation. Importantly, these features are not limited to this collection but may be applied across a number of datasets, including new and emerging analyses to assist in geological interpretations. The complete Australian thermochronology dataset for 2002 and prior dataset, including single-grain results, thermal history paths and sample metadata, can also be freely exported in standardised formats for use with external software for specific applications, such as thermal history modelling with alternate software or approaches. Sample data may also be exported as a variety of GIS-ready files for use in geospatial software."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Gleadow, A. J. W., Kohn, B. P., Brown, R. W., O’Sullivan, P. B., & Raza, A. (2002). Fission track thermotectonic imaging of the Australian continent. Tectonophysics, 349(1–4), 5–21. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0040-1951(02)00043-4 \n\nKohn, B. P., Gleadow, A. J. W., Brown, R. W., Gallagher, K., O’Sullivan, P. B., & Foster, D. A. (2002). Shaping the Australian crust over the last 300 million years: Insights from fission track thermotectonic imaging and denudation studies of key terranes. Australian Journal of Earth Sciences, 49(4), 697–717. https://doi.org/10.1046/j.1440-0952.2002.00942.x \n\nKohn, B. P., Gleadow, A. J. W., Brown, R. W., Gallagher, K., O’Sullivan, P. B., & Foster, D. A. (2020). Australian-wide apatite fission track data compilation [Text/tab-separated-values]. PANGAEA. https://doi.org/10.1594/PANGAEA.911861"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Dickson, A.J., Hilton, R.G., Prytulak, J., Minisini, D., Eldrett, J.S., Dellinger, M., Stow, M. and Wang, W., 2024. Rhenium isotopes record oxidative weathering intensity in sedimentary rocks. Geochemistry, Geophysics, Geosystems, 25(10), p.e2024GC011795. https://doi.org/10.1029/2024GC011795"
        },
        {
