    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:050",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "Australian Thermochronology - 2002 to 2022",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:050",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:earth-s-interior-geodesy"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Samuel Boone, Malcolm McMillan, Angus Nixon, Ling Chung, Christian Seiler, Stijn Glorie, Barry Kohn, Andrew Gleadow"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://doi.org/10.58024/agumcad7b7cb"
      ],
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "DOI",
          "value": "https://doi.org/10.58024/agumcad7b7cb"
        }
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:auscope-earthbank-platform"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "AuScope Geochemistry Network"
        }
      ],
      "description": "This compilation brings together apatite fission-track (AFT) and (U–Th)/He thermochronology data acquired between 2002 and 2022 from regions spanning Australia and Papua New Guinea, primarily from the University of Melbourne Thermochronology Laboratory. It integrates both published and previously unpublished results derived from diverse igneous, metamorphic, and sedimentary lithologies. The dataset includes detailed sample metadata and lithological descriptions of analysed materials, and a collection of over 1,300 fission-track ages and length distributions, 153 (U–Th)/He age determinations, and 91 digitised thermal history models. Collectively, these data provide an expanded perspective on the low-temperature thermal evolution of the Australasian crust, building upon and enhancing earlier continental-scale thermochronological frameworks."
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:adelaide-university-north-terrace-adelaide-sa-5005-australia",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "Adelaide University, North Terrace, Adelaide SA 5005, Australia"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:050",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:050",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:050",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3638-1864"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "This compilation of Australasian thermochronology acquired between 2002 and 2022 is a valuable resource for assessing the low-temperature (~40–120 °C) history of Australia and Papua New Guinea throughout the last ~300 million years. These temperatures in the shallow crust are key to understanding the evolution of rocks and minerals in response to broad tectonic and geodynamic forces, including orogenesis, structural reactivation, crustal amalgamation, basin initiation and evolution, palaeoclimate, long-term climate change, and the creation and preservation of mineral and energy resources. These effects often occur at temperatures below the sensitivity of more widely available geochemical and isotopic methods, and require lower temperature chronometers such as the apatite fission track and (U-Th)/He systems to resolve these sub-metamorphic events. \n\nThis collection provides expanded thermochronological coverage across Australia and Papua New Guinea, which in combination with the legacy dataset of Kohn et al. (2025) (https://doi.org/10.58024/AGUM46BD292F) offers unprecedented open access data on the regional thermo-tectonic evolution. In areas of potential mineralisation, this thermal history data may be used to aid exploration by constraining the preservation potential of economic mineralisation. Through identifying regions which experienced exhumation levels sufficient to expose target mineralisation at accessible depths, without eroding the deposit, thermochronology data can be added to evaluation of prospective areas to help reduce exploration risk. Within onshore and offshore sedimentary basins, thermochronology data provides a tool for constraining features in sedimentary basins such as the rate and nature of detritus deposited in the basin, tectonic drivers of basin formation, and the maturation and preservation of hydrocarbon reserves. In some cases, low-temperature data has also been used to infer changing temperatures of groundwater and potentially associated flow dynamics in sedimentary aquifers. Beyond extractive and economic impacts, thermochronology data has been vital for predicting denudation rates across the region, and identifying areas of fault reactivation in response to geodynamic forces."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "This dataset can be visualised through the EarthBank platform map view to spatially explore aspects including fission track age for each mineral species, mean track lengths, and sample lithologies. Thermal history models can additionally provide information on palaeotemperature and cooling rates at desired past time intervals, which can be coupled with reconstruction of sample locations back in time with tectonic plate models to help reference thermal responses to ancient plate motions. More customizable plotting functionalities are also available for cross-plotting attributes within the compilation. Importantly, these features are not limited to this collection but may be applied across a number of datasets, including new and emerging analyses to assist in geological interpretations. The complete Australian thermochronology dataset for 2002 and prior dataset, including single-grain results, thermal history paths and sample metadata, can also be freely exported in standardised formats for use with external software for specific applications, such as thermal history modelling with alternate software or approaches. Sample data may also be exported as a variety of GIS-ready files for use in geospatial software."
        }
      ]
    },
