      "name": "University of Maryland, College Park, MD 20742"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:051",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:051",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:051",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3519-1412"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The CEMs offer an integrated, curated overview of many aspects of California that are useful for assessing earthquake hazards. It is composed of several models, each developed by different community members and available in separate repositories, giving the broader community access to a wide range of information relevant for addressing several of the UN’s sustainability goals. \nThe Community Fault Model (CFM) provides a detail and dataset of fault surfaces, constantly updated based on community feedback, aiding in the evaluation of earthquake rupture likelihood—a “people dimension” goal. The Community Velocity Model (CVM) compiles seismic velocity models across various scales and covering different regions centered on the San Andreas Fault System, helping to better understand the region’s subsurface structure and, importantly, to simulate ground-shaking intensity during future earthquakes. This supports the “Prosperity dimension' by enhancing resilience to geoenvironmental hazards. The Community Geodesy Model (CGM) integrates data on the rate at which faults are loaded. The Community Stress Model (CSM) consolidates numerous studies assessing where stress and stressing rates are highest. These models assist in predicting future earthquake locations and, together with the CVM, guide hazard assessments and policy decisions regarding geohazards. The Community Thermal Model (CTM) and Community Rheology Model (CRM) provide essential information for long-term geodynamic simulations of the region, linking practical seismic hazard considerations with fundamental questions about fault interactions and the geological evolution of the area. \nThis collection of models was initiated by community members under the umbrella of SCEC, now known as the Statewide California Earthquake Center. The effort was coordinated by a small team of scientific leaders: Scott Marshall, Liz Hearn, Laurent Montesi, and Patricia Persaud. They recruited individual scientists who contributed data and models to each CEM component. At SCEC, Mei-Hui Su worked on programming the user interfaces that provide easy access to each model, and Edric Pauk developed the website that offers information and links to each model. Tran Huynh and Phil Maechling oversaw the technical and administrative aspects of this project.\nEach dataset in the CEM is available on Zenodo. Additionally, each is accompanied by an “explorer,” a web tool allowing users to interact visually and query each model, expanding access to the models. This newly deployed interface raises awareness of geohazards, supporting another “People Dimension” objective."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "As the principal contact for one component of the CEM, the Community Rheology Model, the nominator often calls upon different CEM components to complement aspects of the model they focus on. For example, they have recently developed methods to use the definition of faults from the CFM to refine the boundaries of the geological blocks displayed in the CRM. They value how all the datasets in the CEM address different aspects of California's geology. In their research group, students have used the CEM to evaluate how the strength of the lithosphere varies across regions and relate this to the locations of major faults. The “explorers” have been essential for understanding data availability and extracting information about specific locations, but the models stored on Zenodo are truly game changers, enabling anyone to build upon the work the geophysics community has conducted in the region over the past several decades."
        }
      ]
    },
