    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:052",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "The National Geologic Map Database (NGMDB)",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:052",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:earth-s-interior-geodesy"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "David Soller, Senior Program Scientist for the National Geologic Map Database,"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "National Cooperative Geologic Mapping Program"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://ngmdb.usgs.gov/"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:u-s-geological-survey-usgs-via-usgs-data-catalog"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "U.S. Geological Survey (USGS) in partnership with the Association of American State Geologists"
        }
      ],
      "description": "The National Geologic Map Database (NGMDB), maintained by the U.S. Geological Survey (USGS) in partnership with the Association of American State Geologists, is the nation’s “go to” central repository and gateway for geoscience map data at all scales. Established by the Geologic Mapping Act of 1992, the NGMDB integrates more than a century of geologic mapping into a dynamic, searchable, and accessible digital platform. It contains tens of thousands of geologic maps and related spatial datasets produced by federal, state, and academic institutions, spanning content such as bedrock and surficial geology, critical materials and energy resources, geologic hazards, hydrogeology, and environmental geology.\n\nDesigned for both specialists and the public, the NGMDB provides vetted data critical for research, education, land-use planning, resource management, and hazard mitigation. By unifying legacy paper maps with modern GIS-ready data, the database not only preserves the scientific heritage of U.S. geology but also enables new applications across disciplines. Its open access ensures that students, educators, policymakers, and emergency managers alike can harness geologic information to make informed decisions about science, infrastructure, energy, water, natural hazards, and conservation. The NGMDB continues to grow and evolve, advancing national priorities for geoscience data interoperability and supporting the broader community with a foundation of trusted, comprehensive, and enduring geologic knowledge.",
