          "schema:value": "U.S. Geological Survey (USGS) in partnership with the Association of American State Geologists"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The National Geologic Map Database (NGMDB), maintained by the U.S. Geological Survey (USGS) in partnership with the Association of American State Geologists, is the nation’s “go to” central repository and gateway for geoscience map data at all scales. Established by the Geologic Mapping Act of 1992, the NGMDB integrates more than a century of geologic mapping into a dynamic, searchable, and accessible digital platform. It contains tens of thousands of geologic maps and related spatial datasets produced by federal, state, and academic institutions, spanning content such as bedrock and surficial geology, critical materials and energy resources, geologic hazards, hydrogeology, and environmental geology.\n\nDesigned for both specialists and the public, the NGMDB provides vetted data critical for research, education, land-use planning, resource management, and hazard mitigation. By unifying legacy paper maps with modern GIS-ready data, the database not only preserves the scientific heritage of U.S. geology but also enables new applications across disciplines. Its open access ensures that students, educators, policymakers, and emergency managers alike can harness geologic information to make informed decisions about science, infrastructure, energy, water, natural hazards, and conservation. The NGMDB continues to grow and evolve, advancing national priorities for geoscience data interoperability and supporting the broader community with a foundation of trusted, comprehensive, and enduring geologic knowledge."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "There is no more important resource to any individual wishing to interact with – or understand – Earth than a geologic map with its underlying topographic basemaps. The NGMDB is the nation’s single most comprehensive archive for this essential knowledge – accessible in a distribution system that is now georeferenced for use with any other map in the US. With it, anyone can tap into over a century of accumulated ‘boots-on-the-ground’ geologic mapping that represents decades of hours of field observations, interpretation and compilation, typically supported by some combination of microscopy, geochemistry, and geochronology. When we employ these maps, we are assured that somewhere, sometime, a geoscientist has literally laid hands on - or even sometimes famously tasted - the soil, sediment, or rock that the map depicts. This is somehow very assuring, knowing that geologists explored and documented with every tool available at the time, the geology and landscapes of an area into a geologic map, an immensely data-rich resource to all who follow. \n\nGeologic maps are the first encounter many students and the public have with technical representations of the physical Earth. They are frequently acknowledged as beautiful in their own right, but they always unambiguously provide a window into otherwise curtained knowledge of the ground beneath our feet. Maps allow educators to illustrate concepts of plate tectonics, natural hazards, mineral resources, and hydrologic systems. They allow emergency responders rapid access to the geologic data of disasters—faults in an earthquake zone, landslide-prone slopes, or unstable floodplains— all crucial information in crises. They provide researchers with new opportunities to interpret and document Earth's processes. Geological maps represent the foundational information upon which most geological research projects are built. Absent the NGMDB, individuals and institutions would be constrained to scattered, inconsistent, or outdated maps, and entire communities would face hazards without knowledge of the underlying geologic risks. \n\nIn a direct way, the NGMDB connects people with practical information about the Earth. More intangible, however, are the personal connections that geologic maps make between science and the public. As soon as a map of a familiar area is viewed, the average person can relate to that map, regardless of the complexity of the geology. Thus, geologic maps hold a special connection between our planet and its people, who necessarily live on it. \n\n The NGMDB is also essential to advancing our basic understanding of Earth’s physical systems, from its most simple to its most complex. Geologic maps comprise the spatial and temporal distribution of rocks, sediments, structures, and geomorphic features—the very evidence by which we reconstruct tectonic, climatic, and environmental processes. Without the NGMDB, the U.S. would lack an integrated baseline for monitoring long-term changes to the planet, from glacial retreat to groundwater depletion to fault reactivation. Importantly, the NGMDB is not static; it continually grows and changes as new digital data becomes available. Thus, the NGMDB enhances our capacity and potential to conserve natural systems, to track climate-driven landscape responses, or to assess geologic controls on ecosystems and soils.\n\nFinally, the NGMDB strengthens socioeconomic systems and community resilience by enabling informed decisions about land use, infrastructure, energy development, and natural resource management. Engineers depend on accurate geologic maps to site roads, tunnels, and reservoirs. Communities rely on them to plan water supply, waste disposal, and urban expansion. Resource industries use them to locate aggregate, groundwater, and energy reserves. Regulators apply them to ensure extraction occurs responsibly and safely. In humanitarian terms, geologic maps are indispensable for rebuilding after disasters and for ensuring equity in how risks and resources are distributed. Without the NGMDB, these decisions would be based on whatever maps individual municipalities could scrape together, amplifying both economic inefficiency and vulnerability. Or worse, they would have to make them from scratch, an economically infeasible task. With NGMDB, essential maps are literally a few clicks away.\n\nIn sum, the NGMDB is not just an archive of maps; it is the backbone of our collective capacity to interpret and inhabit the Earth. If it did not exist, we could not effectively teach Earth systems, we could not track Earth system changes, and we could not plan for society’s future on a dynamic planet. I can think of no more impactful database serving a broader scope of community and society."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1a:\nThe NGMDB dataset has been the backbone of my teaching and research throughout my career. It’s supported courses from Intro to Geology to Advanced Geomorphology and Soil Science, and served as the starting point for nearly all my research—of course each field area always spanning at least four quadrangles! NGMDB also shaped my professional development. My first external grant was an EdMap mapping award, and creating that map with my first PhD student taught me invaluable lessons about our field area and the art and science of mapmaking.\n\nNominator 1b:\nAs a USGS scientist, the NGMDB is a living embodiment of the agency’s mission since 1878: to provide science of the natural system for public benefit. Its strength lies in being federally managed, ensuring spatial and technical consistency across all U.S. maps. Downloads from any location adhere to uniform standards, notation, and metadata. To further its mission, the USGS maintains a dedicated team for troubleshooting, updates, and continuous improvement.\n\nNominators 1c and 1d:\nGeological map production and collaboration with USGS to populate the NGMDB are central to state geological surveys. These programs address societal challenges in energy, minerals, and water. Maps are vital for documenting surficial processes, including those driven by human or climate activity. The NGMDB’s power lies in integrating state and federal mapping efforts into a unified, accessible resource for researchers, industry, government, and the public."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Reference Publication: Soller, D. R., Wardwell, R. S., Garrity, C. P., & Stamm, N. R. (2025). The USGS national geologic map database—Resource for geoscience professionals and the public. Geotechnical Frontiers 2025, 252–259. https://doi.org/10.1061/9780784485989.026"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Lee, C.-T. A., Jiang, H., Ronay, E., Minisini, D., Stiles, J., & Neal, M. (2018). Volcanic ash as a driver of enhanced organic carbon burial in the Cretaceous. Scientific Reports, 8(1), 4197. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-22576-3"
        },
        {
