    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:053",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "International GNSS Service (IGS) Data and Products",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:053",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:earth-s-interior-geodesy"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "IGS components"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://igs.org/data-products-overview/"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:igs-global-data-centers"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "IGS Central Bureau, IGS Operational products coordination components"
        }
      ],
      "description": "The International GNSS Service serves as the premier source of the highest-quality GNSS related standards and conventions, data and products\".\n\nAll IGS data and products are openly available to all user communities, free of charge.\n\n\"The IGS collects, archives, and distributes high-quality GNSS observational data\nsets to satisfy the objectives of a wide range of scientific and other high-end\napplications and experimentation. These data sets are used by the IGS to\ngenerate the following data products:\n ● High accuracy GNSS satellite ephemerides and related information\n ● Earth rotation parameters\n ● Coordinates and velocities of the IGS tracking stations\n ● GNSS satellite and tracking station clock information, timescale products\n ● Ionospheric information\n ● Tropospheric information\"\n\nThe core IGS Data and Products are generated by \"operational components\" and global and regional data and analysis centers. \nThe components are coordinated by the IGS Central Bureau and IGS Governing Board, and various global and regional data repositories store raw data and products produced by different components. One of the global repositories is managed by NASA's Crustal Dynamics Data and Information System (CDDIS), and distributes all IGS data and products via https://files.igs.org/ . \n\nThe quoted part of this description is copied from the IGS Terms of Reference (https://files.igs.org/pub/resource/governance/IGS-Terms-of-Reference-2023.pdf), that provides a detailed overview of IGS components and governance.",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "Johnston, G., Riddell, A., Hausler, G. (2017). The International GNSS Service. Teunissen, Peter J.G., & Montenbruck, O. (Eds.), Springer Handbook of Global Navigation Satellite Systems (1st ed., pp. 967-982). Cham, Switzerland: Springer International Publishing. doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-42928-1",
          "identifier": [
            {
              "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
              "propertyID": "DOI",
              "value": "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-42928-1"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "Nana Yobo L, Holmden C, Brandon AD, Lau KV, Eldrett JS, Bergman S. 2022a. LIP volcanism (not anoxia) tracked by Cr isotopes during Ocean Anoxic Event 2 in the proto-North Atlantic region. Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3043-8604",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Elisabetta D'Anastasio",
      "identifier": {
        "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
        "propertyID": "ORCID",
        "value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3043-8604"
      },
      "schema:email": "e.danastasio@gns.cri.nz",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:earth-sciences-new-zealand-1-fairway-drive-avalon-5011"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:053",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:053",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:053",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3043-8604"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "Modern geophysics and Earth science rely on spatially and temporally referenced digital information. IGS data and products—alongside other 3 geodetic techniques—underpin the International Terrestrial Reference Frame (ITRF), the foundational element of any modern geophysical study required to pinpoint the location of an observation on the Earth’s surface. ITRF is the basis of our coordinate system. \n \nIGS’s commitment to high-quality, open-access geodetic products and standards has enabled researchers around the world to share data, develop processing software, and conduct long-term monitoring of a range of geophysical phenomena. IGS provides daily access to curated GNSS data, including satellite orbits, clock corrections, signal integrity, and atmospheric delays. These products are essential for processing local and global surveys, and are embedded in nearly every study involving ground motion or deformation. \n\nGNSS is used to study plate tectonics, sea level rise, the earthquake cycle, atmospheric dynamics, space weather, and those studies all depend on the availability of robust, consistent and quality controlled IGS data and products. \n\nBeyond science, IGS supports surveying, geomatics, and geo-information users worldwide. Its data improves efficiency and precision in construction, agriculture, mining, exploration, and transport. \n\nIn short, without IGS data and products, our ability to observe, understand, and respond to Earth system changes would be fundamentally compromised."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator first began working with IGS datasets during their PhD, where they used geodetic data—including GNSS—to study ground deformation in Italy. Their research group was establishing a national GNSS network, and IGS data and products were essential for processing raw data into continuous time series to investigate tectonics, the seismic cycle, and long-term velocity fields. Today, they’re honored to serve on the IGS Governing Board. Their current organization continues to rely heavily on IGS data and products to monitor geohazards in New Zealand. They use IGS datasets daily for routine processing and generation of ground deformation time series, and are actively exploring the use of IGS real-time data to support monitoring and rapid response efforts. IGS has been a cornerstone of the nominator’s scientific journey—from research to operational applications—and continues to be central to their team’s work."
        }
      ]
    },
