          "schema:value": "Modern geophysics and Earth science rely on spatially and temporally referenced digital information. IGS data and products—alongside other 3 geodetic techniques—underpin the International Terrestrial Reference Frame (ITRF), the foundational element of any modern geophysical study required to pinpoint the location of an observation on the Earth’s surface. ITRF is the basis of our coordinate system. \n \nIGS’s commitment to high-quality, open-access geodetic products and standards has enabled researchers around the world to share data, develop processing software, and conduct long-term monitoring of a range of geophysical phenomena. IGS provides daily access to curated GNSS data, including satellite orbits, clock corrections, signal integrity, and atmospheric delays. These products are essential for processing local and global surveys, and are embedded in nearly every study involving ground motion or deformation. \n\nGNSS is used to study plate tectonics, sea level rise, the earthquake cycle, atmospheric dynamics, space weather, and those studies all depend on the availability of robust, consistent and quality controlled IGS data and products. \n\nBeyond science, IGS supports surveying, geomatics, and geo-information users worldwide. Its data improves efficiency and precision in construction, agriculture, mining, exploration, and transport. \n\nIn short, without IGS data and products, our ability to observe, understand, and respond to Earth system changes would be fundamentally compromised."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator first began working with IGS datasets during their PhD, where they used geodetic data—including GNSS—to study ground deformation in Italy. Their research group was establishing a national GNSS network, and IGS data and products were essential for processing raw data into continuous time series to investigate tectonics, the seismic cycle, and long-term velocity fields. Today, they’re honored to serve on the IGS Governing Board. Their current organization continues to rely heavily on IGS data and products to monitor geohazards in New Zealand. They use IGS datasets daily for routine processing and generation of ground deformation time series, and are actively exploring the use of IGS real-time data to support monitoring and rapid response efforts. IGS has been a cornerstone of the nominator’s scientific journey—from research to operational applications—and continues to be central to their team’s work."
