    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:054",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:054",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:054",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9452-7975"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "Flood events continue to damage our society, economy and ecosystem. Recent examples are flooding due to hurricanes (e.g., Helene, Milton and Ida) and heavy precipitation (e.g., July 2025 in Texas and New Mexico). These caused damages to our physical infrastructure and led to fatalities. While these immediate impacts of flooding are well understood, documented and modeled, the longer term impacts such as those on indoor mold due to extensive dampness and moisture are not understood well. These impacts are crucial as excess levels of mold in indoor environments can lead to respiratory health issues like asthma. In particular, systematic fine-scale analyses are lacking. Such impacts are expected to become more pronounced due to more frequent and intense flood events as impacted by climate change and sea level rise.\n\nThis dataset provides the first fine-scale (building level) dataset that paves the way for such analyses and understanding causes of mold. The dataset enables development of predictive models that can quantify the impacts of flooding on indoor air quality and human respiratory health. Such models can complement the existing models that estimate flood impacts on structural damages and fatalities. In turn, the dataset enables effective disaster response by planning for preventive actions and strategies to minimize the impacts of flooding on indoor air quality and human health. The dataset can inform insurance firms about overlooked impacts of flood events, thereby enabling more comprehensive assessments of flood impacts. Additionally, the dataset and subsequent analyses can be also used for educating families about how flooding affects their indoor air quality and own respiratory health. Local, state and federal government agencies can utilize the dataset. In turn, the dataset will also show them how their own actions can contribute to the impacts. In the dataset, we noticed greater issues in lower income households with limited resources to maintain their residences and receive health care; this was a substantial issue of environmental justice, social equity and health disparity.\n\nIn summary, this dataset is innovative and impactful to the well-being of our local communities, government, prosperity and planet. The dataset provides benefits to: (1) People – Supporting the well-being through prediction of disaster response and showing the families on what factors cause indoor mold and respiratory health issues; (2) Planet – Advancing the understanding of flood impacts on indoor air quality and human respiratory health; and (3) Prosperity – Contributing to community resilience against flood events and showing the disproportionate impacts of flooding on indoor air quality and human respiratory health."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator was the lead PI of an NSF project that funded the data collection and preliminary analyses. The project was in collaboration with a social worker and public health scientists from Harvard School of Public Health. This dataset was prepared through recruiting volunteer families, survey questionnaires, site inspections, laboratory experiments and machine learning. Since the preparation of this dataset, the nominator has presented the dataset to multiple individuals from academia and federal/state government (e.g., CDC, Florida Asthma Coalition and Louisiana Department of Health). They found the data to be straightforward and transparent to utilize. In a paper led by their PhD student and published in Environment International (https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envint.2025.109319), the team applied the dataset to model the total indoor mold spores in 60 residential buildings affected by Hurricanes Ian and Ida. In another paper currently under review, the team extended the application to classify new symptoms of respiratory health in occupants of affected people by the two hurricanes in South Florida, New Orleans and Northeast region."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:054",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 55,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Education, Geohealth, Society, Education"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "Hurricanes, mold & respiratory health issues"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Ahmadisharaf, Ebrahim; Pakdehi, Maryam; Azimi, Parham; Keshavarz, Zahra; Caballero, Christian; Yan, Zhengxiao; Allen, Joseph"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.17603/ds2-yje5-g692"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "DesignSafe"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.17603/ds2-yje5-g692"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Ebrahim Ahmadisharaf"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "Dampness and mold growth in buildings are some of the common, yet overlooked indirect impacts of floods that adversely affect human respiratory health. There is a lack of understanding on factors that drive mold growth and respiratory health in residential buildings. This is the first comprehensive high-resolution (building level) dataset in the aftermath of two catastrophic hurricane events - Ida and Ian - from the affected residential buildings. The dataset – prepared by survey questionnaires, site inspections, laboratory experiments and machine learning – provides a foundation for detecting key factors – flood characteristics, building and HVAC system properties, human behaviors – driving indoor mold and human respiratory health. The developed empirical database and the identified relationships can be used to establish resilient indoor environments, develop predictive models and develop timely strategies for human health protection. This dataset was applied by Pakdehi et al. (2025; https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envint.2025.109319) to show the first quantitative modeling of the latent impacts of flood events on indoor air quality in terms of mold. The dataset provides benefits to: (1) People – Supporting the well-being through prediction of disaster response; (2) Planet – Advancing the understanding of flood impacts on indoor air quality and human respiratory health; and (3) Prosperity – Contributing to community resilience against flood events."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Flood events continue to damage our society, economy and ecosystem. Recent examples are flooding due to hurricanes (e.g., Helene, Milton and Ida) and heavy precipitation (e.g., July 2025 in Texas and New Mexico). These caused damages to our physical infrastructure and led to fatalities. While these immediate impacts of flooding are well understood, documented and modeled, the longer term impacts such as those on indoor mold due to extensive dampness and moisture are not understood well. These impacts are crucial as excess levels of mold in indoor environments can lead to respiratory health issues like asthma. In particular, systematic fine-scale analyses are lacking. Such impacts are expected to become more pronounced due to more frequent and intense flood events as impacted by climate change and sea level rise.\n\nThis dataset provides the first fine-scale (building level) dataset that paves the way for such analyses and understanding causes of mold. The dataset enables development of predictive models that can quantify the impacts of flooding on indoor air quality and human respiratory health. Such models can complement the existing models that estimate flood impacts on structural damages and fatalities. In turn, the dataset enables effective disaster response by planning for preventive actions and strategies to minimize the impacts of flooding on indoor air quality and human health. The dataset can inform insurance firms about overlooked impacts of flood events, thereby enabling more comprehensive assessments of flood impacts. Additionally, the dataset and subsequent analyses can be also used for educating families about how flooding affects their indoor air quality and own respiratory health. Local, state and federal government agencies can utilize the dataset. In turn, the dataset will also show them how their own actions can contribute to the impacts. In the dataset, we noticed greater issues in lower income households with limited resources to maintain their residences and receive health care; this was a substantial issue of environmental justice, social equity and health disparity.\n\nIn summary, this dataset is innovative and impactful to the well-being of our local communities, government, prosperity and planet. The dataset provides benefits to: (1) People – Supporting the well-being through prediction of disaster response and showing the families on what factors cause indoor mold and respiratory health issues; (2) Planet – Advancing the understanding of flood impacts on indoor air quality and human respiratory health; and (3) Prosperity – Contributing to community resilience against flood events and showing the disproportionate impacts of flooding on indoor air quality and human respiratory health."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator was the lead PI of an NSF project that funded the data collection and preliminary analyses. The project was in collaboration with a social worker and public health scientists from Harvard School of Public Health. This dataset was prepared through recruiting volunteer families, survey questionnaires, site inspections, laboratory experiments and machine learning. Since the preparation of this dataset, the nominator has presented the dataset to multiple individuals from academia and federal/state government (e.g., CDC, Florida Asthma Coalition and Louisiana Department of Health). They found the data to be straightforward and transparent to utilize. In a paper led by their PhD student and published in Environment International (https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envint.2025.109319), the team applied the dataset to model the total indoor mold spores in 60 residential buildings affected by Hurricanes Ian and Ida. In another paper currently under review, the team extended the application to classify new symptoms of respiratory health in occupants of affected people by the two hurricanes in South Florida, New Orleans and Northeast region."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Ahmadisharaf, E., Pakdehi, M., & Yan, Z. (2024). Machine learning modeling: In rapid: understanding the interrelationships among floods, building characteristics, mold growth and occupants’ asthma symptoms in the aftermath of hurricane Ida. Designsafe-CI. https://doi.org/10.17603/DS2-K1EC-4E35"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "The dataset was applied by Pakdehi et al. (2025; https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envint.2025.109319 and 2026; https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jclepro.2026.148247) to estimate indoor mold and detect new symptoms of human respiratory health in residential buildings after two hurricane events.\n\nThe dataset was also discussed in a blog post by Harvard Health (https://www.health.harvard.edu/blog/respiratory-health-harms-often-follow-flooding-taking-these-steps-can-help-202211092848).\n\nIn a recent news article by WIRED (https://www.wired.com/story/after-hurricane-milton-get-ready-for-mold/), the dataset was discussed to the public on how hurricane can lead to indoor air quality in terms of mold and human health issues. The article also discussed how climate change can affect such impacts."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Ebrahim Ahmadisharaf"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Email Address",
          "schema:value": "eahmadisharaf@eng.famu.fsu.edu"
