        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://github.com/UW-SASWE/GRAIN"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "GRAIN v.1.0"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.16786488"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Sarath Suresh, Faisal Hossain"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "GRAIN is the first openly available global map of irrigation canals that carries surface water from rivers and reservoirs to farmland. The dataset contains information of roughly 3.8 million kilometers of canals across 95 countries, covering both major irrigation networks and smaller distributary systems. Each canal segment includes useful attributes such as length, slope, and canal use case classification (agricultural, urban, or navigation) among others. All data are provided under an open CC-BY license in GeoParquet and ESRI Shapefile formats. It was developed by utilising Volunteered Geographic Information (VGI) data from OpenStreetMap (OSM) and enhancing it through a scalable machine learning framework that distinguishes man-made canals from natural rivers, to eliminate the widely prevalent tagging issues in OSM. The dataset is also validated using existing regional scale canal inventories, where it showed strong accuracy for large and primary irrigation networks. By providing a consistent and validated representation of surface-water delivery infrastructure, GRAIN enables researchers, planners, and policymakers to quantify irrigation access, assess canal density relative to cropland, and monitor how effectively water from reservoirs reaches the fields that depend on it."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Despite supporting roughly 40% of the world’s agricultural output, geospatial data on agricultural irrigation canals are surprisingly scarce. Even though canals are vital for the food and water security of billions of people, there has never been an open, globally consistent dataset that maps them, barring a few national and regional scale inventories. GRAIN fills this gap by providing the first standardized, global-scale canal map that is openly available for researchers, planners, and decision makers.\n\nPeople dimension:\nIrrigation canals have transformed arid and semi-arid landscapes into productive farmland, restoring livelihoods in regions once threatened by desertification. By mapping how surface water travels from rivers and reservoirs to fields, GRAIN provides farmers and water managers the information they need to use irrigation water more efficiently. Understanding canal layout can also help improve irrigation scheduling, reduce wastage, and boost crop yields. \n\nPlanet dimension:\nFrom an environmental standpoint, GRAIN makes it possible to monitor how surface water is redistributed by human infrastructure at regional to global scales. It shows where canal networks are most concentrated and helps estimate how much water is lost as it travels from rivers and reservoirs to farms. When combined with popular satellite missions, such as the Surface Water and Ocean Topography (SWOT), and groundwater usage maps, GRAIN helps trace the full journey of water from reservoirs to farms while identifying regions where surface water shortages are increasingly compensated by groundwater extraction. This integration supports a better understanding of the balance between surface and subsurface water resources under changing climate conditions.\n\nProsperity dimension:\nCanal networks form the backbone of irrigated agriculture and rural prosperity. They are considered one of the driving forces behind the success of the Green Revolution. By mapping them consistently across 95 countries, GRAIN provides a valuable resource for governments and development agencies seeking to improve irrigation efficiency and food security. The dataset can reveal regions with inadequate canal coverage or excessive reliance on groundwater pumping, guiding targeted investments in modernization, canal lining, or conjunctive-use planning. It also offers a transparent and reproducible baseline for managing transboundary river basins and promoting equitable water sharing.\n\nWithout GRAIN, it would not be possible to link reservoir operations to actual irrigation command areas at a global scale, compare irrigation access across nations, or connect satellite-based water observations with on the ground delivery networks. GRAIN is an impactful global dataset with the potential to drive interdisciplinary research spanning hydrology, agriculture, climate science, and sustainable development. By enabling these analyses for the first time, GRAIN strengthens our collective capacity to understand and manage water more sustainably."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator developed the GRAIN dataset as part of their Ph.D. research at the University of Washington, and they actively support it by maintaining its codebase, updating source data, and improving its accuracy and usability. Apart from this data curation, they use GRAIN in their Ph.D. research on hydropower, flood risk, and food production. \nSpecifically, they combine GRAIN with global river datasets to identify irrigation command areas downstream of major dams. These regions represent the farmlands that directly depend on water released from the dams. By linking dam operations with vegetation changes as seen from satellites, GRAIN helps them assess how hydropower dams influence agricultural greenness and productivity over time. They also use GRAIN to detect damages caused to cropland due to floods by mapping where excessive releases have flooded irrigated command areas and reduced vegetation recovery. Here, GRAIN acts as a central analytical tool for understanding the complex connections between water infrastructure, agriculture, and climate resilience."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Suresh, S., Hossain, F., Mishra, V., & Hossain, N. (2026). GRAIN– a global registry of agricultural irrigation networks. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-2025-488"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "GRAIN was recently published in Earth System Science Data (ESSD). It has already gained traction within research groups such as the SWOT Lakes and Wetlands working group community. Efforts are underway for downscaling SWOT observations to irrigation canal scales, demonstrating its early scientific relevance and applicability."
        },
