    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:058",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:058",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:058",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2243-2632"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "In order to effectively monitor food security at global scale and reach the Zero Hunger Sustainable Development Goal, having access to reliable information on the proportion of productive agricultural area is key. Global food security is monitored regularly by initiatives such as GEOGLAM, spearheaded by the Group on Earth Observations (GEO), and the Agricultural Market Information System (AMIS), which rely on accurate and up-to-date information on where major crops are grown and their agricultural productivity. These agricultural monitoring systems however currently rely on static information on the spatial extent of cropland, often produced for a past baseline year and at a coarse resolution, with the location of major crop types often based on downscaling of past national and subnational statistics. The ESA funded WorldCereal project is therefore offering an efficient, agile and robust processing system allowing anyone to derive this information at 10 m resolution from satellite imagery, for any region, season and crop of interest. The WorldCereal system has many potential users among national agencies across the globe. In cooperation with international agencies such as the UN Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) and the World Food Program (WFP), they could uptake more crop-specific information to enhance subnational and national agricultural statistics, better predict, estimate and respond to impacts of climate- and human-induced disasters, as well as facilitate reporting to established international processes, such as FAO questionnaires on production and land use statistics, to UNFCCC and to the SDG agenda.\n\nTo generate accurate, high-resolution cropland and crop type maps, high quality in-situ reference data is indispensable for both training classification algorithms and validation of the final products. Although research projects, national and international programs as well as local initiatives constantly gather crop related reference data, each of these initiatives have different views and ways on how and where to publish the resulting data and are using their own data standards and formats, severely limiting data discoverability, reusability and interoperability. Through the continuous collection, harmonization, documentation and publication of such datasets, the WorldCereal initiative is actively promoting open and FAIR data principles in the agricultural monitoring community. In addition, by providing free access to user-friendly and highly automated tools for data discovery, upload and harmonization (https://rdm.esa-worldcereal.org/) the project is effectively lowering the burden for organizations to contribute data and become part of a growing community around agricultural reference data."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "As the program director of GEOGLAM, the nominator’s mission is to ensure that international organizations and national authorities have access to user-friendly tools to facilitate monitoring of agricultural production. The public WorldCereal Reference database serves as an excellent baseline for these stakeholders to create customized EO-based cropland and crop type products through the cloud-based WorldCereal processing system (https://esa-worldcereal.org/en/products/worldcereal-system). Through a dedicated user interface (https://rdm.esa-worldcereal.org/) and API service, users can easily interact with the available data and discover which data is available for their region, season and crops of interest. Having such an overview of reference data availability helps me as a program manager in identifying regions where active partnerships with local agricultural organizations should be established or strengthened and where future agricultural data collection campaigns should be organized in the context of ongoing and future international research programs coordinated by GEOGLAM."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:058",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 59,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Education, Geohealth, Society, Education"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "WorldCereal Harmonized Reference Datasets"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Hendrik Boogaard\nArun Pratihast\nCharlotte Van Haren\nJuan Carlos Laso Bayas\nSantosh Karanam\nSteffen Fritz\nKristof Van Tricht\nJeroen Degerickx"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://zenodo.org/records/18769612"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "WorldCereal Harmonized Reference Datasets – extended and updated, hosted on Zenodo"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "10.5281/zenodo.18769612"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Part of ESA WorldCereal consortium, i.e. Wageningen Environmental Research (WENR), International Institute for Applied Systems Analysis (IIASA) and Flemish Institute for Technological Research (VITO)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The ESA WorldCereal consortium has built a global, community-based and open repository of harmonized reference data on land cover, crop type and irrigation information. Currently covering more than150 datasets across the globe, these data are typically used to construct and validate global to regional high-resolution cropland and crop type maps. We define reference data as all data which can either be used for calibrating classification algorithms or validating the resulting products. As such, reference data should contain location- and time-specific information about land cover and/or crop type. Examples include in-situ field observations gathered through dedicated field surveys, farmers declarations through parcel registration systems, observations derived from visual or automated interpretation of very high-resolution satellite imagery or in-situ photographs, or existing high-quality classified maps based on analysis of satellite imagery. Reference data is typically available in different formats depending on the source of the dataset. To ensure multiple datasets can be seamlessly combined, all datasets have been structured, harmonized, annotated and evaluated according to a standardized protocol, ensuring all data adheres to the same (meta)data standards and formats. Each individual dataset has its own data license defined by the original data holder, which should be respected by any potential user of the data."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "In order to effectively monitor food security at global scale and reach the Zero Hunger Sustainable Development Goal, having access to reliable information on the proportion of productive agricultural area is key. Global food security is monitored regularly by initiatives such as GEOGLAM, spearheaded by the Group on Earth Observations (GEO), and the Agricultural Market Information System (AMIS), which rely on accurate and up-to-date information on where major crops are grown and their agricultural productivity. These agricultural monitoring systems however currently rely on static information on the spatial extent of cropland, often produced for a past baseline year and at a coarse resolution, with the location of major crop types often based on downscaling of past national and subnational statistics. The ESA funded WorldCereal project is therefore offering an efficient, agile and robust processing system allowing anyone to derive this information at 10 m resolution from satellite imagery, for any region, season and crop of interest. The WorldCereal system has many potential users among national agencies across the globe. In cooperation with international agencies such as the UN Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) and the World Food Program (WFP), they could uptake more crop-specific information to enhance subnational and national agricultural statistics, better predict, estimate and respond to impacts of climate- and human-induced disasters, as well as facilitate reporting to established international processes, such as FAO questionnaires on production and land use statistics, to UNFCCC and to the SDG agenda.\n\nTo generate accurate, high-resolution cropland and crop type maps, high quality in-situ reference data is indispensable for both training classification algorithms and validation of the final products. Although research projects, national and international programs as well as local initiatives constantly gather crop related reference data, each of these initiatives have different views and ways on how and where to publish the resulting data and are using their own data standards and formats, severely limiting data discoverability, reusability and interoperability. Through the continuous collection, harmonization, documentation and publication of such datasets, the WorldCereal initiative is actively promoting open and FAIR data principles in the agricultural monitoring community. In addition, by providing free access to user-friendly and highly automated tools for data discovery, upload and harmonization (https://rdm.esa-worldcereal.org/) the project is effectively lowering the burden for organizations to contribute data and become part of a growing community around agricultural reference data."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "As the program director of GEOGLAM, the nominator’s mission is to ensure that international organizations and national authorities have access to user-friendly tools to facilitate monitoring of agricultural production. The public WorldCereal Reference database serves as an excellent baseline for these stakeholders to create customized EO-based cropland and crop type products through the cloud-based WorldCereal processing system (https://esa-worldcereal.org/en/products/worldcereal-system). Through a dedicated user interface (https://rdm.esa-worldcereal.org/) and API service, users can easily interact with the available data and discover which data is available for their region, season and crops of interest. Having such an overview of reference data availability helps me as a program manager in identifying regions where active partnerships with local agricultural organizations should be established or strengthened and where future agricultural data collection campaigns should be organized in the context of ongoing and future international research programs coordinated by GEOGLAM."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "The following publication describes the initial version of the WorldCereal reference data repository:\n\nBoogaard, H., Pratihast, A. K., Laso Bayas, J. C., Karanam, S., Fritz, S., Van Tricht, K., Degerickx, J., & Gilliams, S. (2023). Building a community-based open harmonised reference data repository for global crop mapping. PLOS ONE, 18(7), e0287731. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0287731 \n\nMost recent and up-to-date documentation on this dataset can be found through the WorldCereal documentation portal:\n\nhttps://worldcereal.github.io/worldcereal-documentation/rdm/overview.html"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "WorldCereal global cropland, crop type and irrigation maps 2021:\n\nVan Tricht, K., Degerickx, J., Gilliams, S., Zanaga, D., Battude, M., Grosu, A., Brombacher, J., Lesiv, M., Bayas, J. C. L., Karanam, S., Fritz, S., Becker-Reshef, I., Franch, B., Mollà-Bononad, B., Boogaard, H., Pratihast, A. K., & Szantoi, Z. (2023). WorldCereal: A dynamic open-source system for global-scale, seasonal, and reproducible crop and irrigation mapping. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-2023-184 \n\nThe resulting global cropland layer has been used by the Joint Research Center of the European Commission to establish a new global crop- and grassland base layer for agricultural Early Warning systems:\n\nEuropean Commission. Joint Research Centre. (2024). Development of a new cropland and rangeland area fraction image at 500 m for the ASAP system. Publications Office. https://data.europa.eu/doi/10.2760/32935 \n\nThe dataset is also used as a basis for the production of European-wide, high-resolution crop maps on a yearly basis, as part of the operational Copernicus High Resolution Cropland Layers:\n\nhttps://blog.vito.be/remotesensing/new-copernicus-high-resolution-layer-on-croplands-launched-for-europe, published through the Copernicus Land Monitoring Service (https://land.copernicus.eu/en/products/high-resolution-layer-croplands?tab=crop_types)\n\nThe dataset is featured in a recent review on the use of Earth Observation for crop mapping:\n\nZhang, C., et al. (2025). Remote sensing for crop mapping: A perspective on current and future crop-specific land cover data products. Remote Sensing of Environment, 330, 114995. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rse.2025.114995"
        },
        {
