      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "RTI International"
        }
      ],
      "description": "The gridded U.S. anthropogenic methane greenhouse gas inventory (gridded GHGI) includes spatially and temporally resolved (gridded) maps of annual anthropogenic methane emissions (0.1°×0.1°) for the contiguous United States (CONUS). Total gridded methane emissions for each source sector are consistent with national annual U.S. anthropogenic methane emissions reported in the Inventory of U.S. Greenhouse Gas Emissions and Sinks (U.S. GHGI). These data are for the years for 2012-2022 for 34 emissions source categories. Data are presented on an annual basis and, where available for some sources, with monthly scaling factors.",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "The nominator’s team is working on a new manuscript describing the latest version of the dataset. Here is a publication on the previous release:"
        },
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "Maasakkers, J. D., McDuffie, E. E., Sulprizio, M. P., Chen, C., Schultz, M., Brunelle, L., Thrush, R., Steller, J., Sherry, C., Jacob, D. J., Jeong, S., Irving, B., & Weitz, M. (2023). A gridded inventory of annual 2012–2018 u. S. Anthropogenic methane emissions. Environmental Science & Technology, 57(43), 16276–16288. https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.est.3c05138",
          "identifier": [
            {
              "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
              "propertyID": "DOI",
              "value": "https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.est.3c05138"
            }
          ],
          "schema:url": [
            "https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.est.3c05138"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3924-0277",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Nicholas Kruskamp",
      "identifier": {
        "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
        "propertyID": "ORCID",
        "value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3924-0277"
      },
      "schema:email": "nfkruskamp@gmail.com",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:rti-international-3040-cornwallis-road-research-triangle-park-nc-27709"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:rti-international-3040-cornwallis-road-research-triangle-park-nc-27709",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "RTI International, 3040 Cornwallis Road, Research Triangle Park, NC 27709"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:059",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:059",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:059",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3924-0277"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "This dataset provides the latest updates to the previously released gridded methane emissions estimates consistent with national U.S. UNFCCC reporting, now covering the years 2012 - 2022. Compared to previous gridded methane data products, this update more closely aligns the activity and location data used in gridding with the data used in U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) national inventory estimates and includes 9 new sources - 5 of them in Land Use, Land-Use Change, and Forestry sources. This update now covers 99.99% of anthropogenic methane emissions captured in the inventory. Emissions in this dataset rely on and are consistent with time series of source-specific anthropogenic GHG emissions that are reported annually in the Inventory of U.S. Greenhouse Gas Emissions and Sinks (GHGI), produced by the EPA.\n\nBottom-up spatial disaggregation of methane inventory estimates, such as the data presented here, break down national or regional methane emission totals into finer spatial units using detailed activity data and emission factors. Spatial disaggregation involves assigning these emissions to specific geographic locations based on where the activities occur. This approach is essential for improving the spatial resolution and accuracy of methane inventories which are reported at coarse scales in top-down inventories, and because emissions are largely from fugitive and biological sources which can be more challenging to quantify and locate than other GHG sources.\n\nAccurate spatio-temporal data improves climate and air quality models, enabling better predictions and responses. Furthermore, breaking down emissions by source—such as agriculture, fossil fuel industries, and wetlands—allows for targeted interventions and informed policy design. This spatially explicit approach enables more precise identification of emission hotspots and supports targeted mitigation strategies. It also facilitates integration with atmospheric observations and inverse modeling, which can validate or refine inventory estimates. Comparisons with atmospheric observations can provide information to identify key areas for refinement in future inventories. Moreover, spatial disaggregation is critical for understanding policy and creating safe and healthy environments, enabling communities and policy makers to examine in detail the location of methane emissions, improve estimates of impacted populations, and use a data-driven approach to reducing emissions in the future. In sum, annual methane quantification is a cornerstone of climate science, environmental policy, and global cooperation."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator’s interaction with this dataset started when they were asked to lead the effort to update these gridded datasets for the EPA. An incredible team of scientists had already established the framework for taking greenhouse gas inventory estimates and doing bottom-up disaggregation.\n\nWhile tackling this challenge, they have learned an incredible amount and been exposed to so many new aspects of greenhouse gas inventory estimations. These datasets are a vital part of a large network of research activities related to atmospheric science, global climate change, and decision-making. They especially came to understand this when they presented their preliminary results and approach at the 2024 AGU annual conference. The number of people who approached their poster, were already aware of the existing datasets, and were so excited about an updated data release and the promise of more regular releases were astonishing. It is easy to toil away on major dataset efforts and lose sight of the why, but being part of the larger AGU community and learning about the wide variety of research our work supports gives meaning to the time and effort it takes to create these data.\n\nWorking with these data has also given the nominator a great appreciation for working with a diverse team of researchers, data scientists, and subject matter experts, without whom these types of datasets would not be possible, and they owe a debt of gratitude to everyone who has contributed along the way."
        }
      ]
    },
