        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The gridded U.S. anthropogenic methane greenhouse gas inventory (gridded GHGI) includes spatially and temporally resolved (gridded) maps of annual anthropogenic methane emissions (0.1°×0.1°) for the contiguous United States (CONUS). Total gridded methane emissions for each source sector are consistent with national annual U.S. anthropogenic methane emissions reported in the Inventory of U.S. Greenhouse Gas Emissions and Sinks (U.S. GHGI). These data are for the years for 2012-2022 for 34 emissions source categories. Data are presented on an annual basis and, where available for some sources, with monthly scaling factors."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "This dataset provides the latest updates to the previously released gridded methane emissions estimates consistent with national U.S. UNFCCC reporting, now covering the years 2012 - 2022. Compared to previous gridded methane data products, this update more closely aligns the activity and location data used in gridding with the data used in U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) national inventory estimates and includes 9 new sources - 5 of them in Land Use, Land-Use Change, and Forestry sources. This update now covers 99.99% of anthropogenic methane emissions captured in the inventory. Emissions in this dataset rely on and are consistent with time series of source-specific anthropogenic GHG emissions that are reported annually in the Inventory of U.S. Greenhouse Gas Emissions and Sinks (GHGI), produced by the EPA.\n\nBottom-up spatial disaggregation of methane inventory estimates, such as the data presented here, break down national or regional methane emission totals into finer spatial units using detailed activity data and emission factors. Spatial disaggregation involves assigning these emissions to specific geographic locations based on where the activities occur. This approach is essential for improving the spatial resolution and accuracy of methane inventories which are reported at coarse scales in top-down inventories, and because emissions are largely from fugitive and biological sources which can be more challenging to quantify and locate than other GHG sources.\n\nAccurate spatio-temporal data improves climate and air quality models, enabling better predictions and responses. Furthermore, breaking down emissions by source—such as agriculture, fossil fuel industries, and wetlands—allows for targeted interventions and informed policy design. This spatially explicit approach enables more precise identification of emission hotspots and supports targeted mitigation strategies. It also facilitates integration with atmospheric observations and inverse modeling, which can validate or refine inventory estimates. Comparisons with atmospheric observations can provide information to identify key areas for refinement in future inventories. Moreover, spatial disaggregation is critical for understanding policy and creating safe and healthy environments, enabling communities and policy makers to examine in detail the location of methane emissions, improve estimates of impacted populations, and use a data-driven approach to reducing emissions in the future. In sum, annual methane quantification is a cornerstone of climate science, environmental policy, and global cooperation."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator’s interaction with this dataset started when they were asked to lead the effort to update these gridded datasets for the EPA. An incredible team of scientists had already established the framework for taking greenhouse gas inventory estimates and doing bottom-up disaggregation.\n\nWhile tackling this challenge, they have learned an incredible amount and been exposed to so many new aspects of greenhouse gas inventory estimations. These datasets are a vital part of a large network of research activities related to atmospheric science, global climate change, and decision-making. They especially came to understand this when they presented their preliminary results and approach at the 2024 AGU annual conference. The number of people who approached their poster, were already aware of the existing datasets, and were so excited about an updated data release and the promise of more regular releases were astonishing. It is easy to toil away on major dataset efforts and lose sight of the why, but being part of the larger AGU community and learning about the wide variety of research our work supports gives meaning to the time and effort it takes to create these data.\n\nWorking with these data has also given the nominator a great appreciation for working with a diverse team of researchers, data scientists, and subject matter experts, without whom these types of datasets would not be possible, and they owe a debt of gratitude to everyone who has contributed along the way."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "The nominator’s team is working on a new manuscript describing the latest version of the dataset. Here is a publication on the previous release: \n\nMaasakkers, J. D., McDuffie, E. E., Sulprizio, M. P., Chen, C., Schultz, M., Brunelle, L., Thrush, R., Steller, J., Sherry, C., Jacob, D. J., Jeong, S., Irving, B., & Weitz, M. (2023). A gridded inventory of annual 2012–2018 u. S. Anthropogenic methane emissions. Environmental Science & Technology, 57(43), 16276–16288. https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.est.3c05138"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Nicholas Kruskamp"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Email Address",
          "schema:value": "nfkruskamp@gmail.com"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator ORCID",
          "schema:value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3924-0277"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Affiliation or Organization",
          "schema:value": "RTI International, 3040 Cornwallis Road, Research Triangle Park, NC 27709"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:repository:department-of-energy-and-environment-website-https-doee-dc-gov-climateready",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Catalog",
        "schema:DataCatalog"
      ],
      "name": "Department of Energy and Environment website: https://doee.dc.gov/climateready"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:060",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "Climate Ready DC: What does it mean to be Climate Ready?",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:060",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:geohealth-society-education"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Erin Garnaas-Holmes at the DC Department of Energy and Environment"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/fa9a73fc799249ffb206cbcd90231e8e"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:department-of-energy-and-environment-website-https-doee-dc-gov-climateready"
      ],
      "description": "This is a StoryMap of climate impacts in the District of Columbia. It incorporates engaging graphics, meaningful graphs (like how different generations experience heat in their lifetimes), local photos and a video, easy to digest information, and uplifting real-world solutions being implemented locally."
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:person:oana-leahu-aluas",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Oana Leahu-Aluas",
      "schema:email": "oana.leahu-aluas@dc.gov",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:dc-department-of-energy-and-environment-1200-first-st-ne-washington-dc-20002"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:dc-department-of-energy-and-environment-1200-first-st-ne-washington-dc-20002",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "DC Department of Energy and Environment, 1200 First St NE, Washington, DC 20002"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:060",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:060",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:060",
      "nominator": [
        "id:person:oana-leahu-aluas"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "This dataset is impactful because it centers people in discussing climate impacts. Traditional datasets showing plots on a graph are far removed from the everyday experiences of community members. The Climate Ready StoryMap provides an example to be emulated by jurisdictions around the world. This StoryMap is used to talk to 6th graders about climate impacts in DC and showing the flooding video that is embedded in the StoryMap and asking them to think how old they will be in 2030 or 2050 (corresponding to the chart showing extreme heat days in those years), attracted a lot of attention and engagement from the students. The use of local photos and visuals with people frequently included drives home the point that climate change is not an impact separate from ourselves. Humans are an integral part of it and are impacted by it in very real ways."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator interacts with this dataset when they want to find the graph that shows the number of extreme heat days projected in future years, or the graph of different generations in the same family and their experiences with heat. When they received a request to speak with sixth graders in DC about local climate impacts, they simply turned to this StoryMap rather than trying to create something new or figuring out how to explain climate impacts to this audience."
        }
      ]
    },
