    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:061",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "The Water Conflict Chronology Database",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:061",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:geohealth-society-education"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "The dataset nominated for inclusion is the Water Conflict Chronology, the world’s most comprehensive, open-source database on water and security, created by Dr. Peter Gleick and the Pacific Institute."
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://www.worldwater.org/water-conflict/"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:https-data-mendeley-com-datasets-wjhw4xxgbr-1"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "Pacific Institute for Studies in Development, Environment, and Security (www.pacinst.org and www.worldwater.org)"
        }
      ],
      "description": "In the 1980s, as part of a major effort to define environmental threats to security, the Pacific Institute initiated a project to track and categorize events worldwide where freshwater resources and water systems have been “triggers,” “weapons,” and “casualties” of violence and conflict. The database has been continuously maintained since then. It presents data as a timeline, interactive map, and searchable, downloadable open-access database, with historical information on violence over freshwater categorized by region and type, conflict time and date, locale, and other variables. Detailed sources are included for each entry. An interactive map shows the geographic location of, and summary information about, each event. Data are collected and analyzed from historical records, news and magazine accounts, other conflict databases, military reports, international governmental and non-governmental organizations, social media, and field work. Events are categorized as:\nTrigger: Freshwater as a trigger or root cause of conflict, or underlying cause of ongoing tension that is contributing to conflict, where there is a dispute over the control of water or water systems or where economic or physical access to water, or scarcity of water, triggers violence.\nWeapon: Water as a weapon of conflict, where water resources, or water systems themselves, are used as a tool or weapon in a violent conflict.\nCasualty: Water resources or water systems as a casualty of conflict, where water resources, or water systems, are intentional or incidental casualties or targets of violence.",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "The Water Conflict Chronology is described in detail in:\nGleick, P. H., & Shimabuku, M. (2023). Water-related conflicts: Definitions, data, and trends from the water conflict chronology. Environmental Research Letters, 18(3), 034022. https://doi.org/10.1088/1748-9326/acbb8f",
          "identifier": [
            {
              "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
              "propertyID": "DOI",
              "value": "https://doi.org/10.1088/1748-9326/acbb8f"
            }
          ],
          "schema:url": [
            "https://doi.org/10.1088/1748-9326/acbb8f"
          ]
        },
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "A public article about it was published in the Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists in 2024 (https://thebulletin.org/premium/2024-07/water-and-war/). Specifics about the database are also available online at https://www.worldwater.org/water-conflict/",
          "schema:url": [
            "https://thebulletin.org/premium/2024-07/water-and-war/",
            "https://www.worldwater.org/water-conflict/"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "The WCC has been extensively adopted for research, media, and policy. In 2024 alone, the database, including the online map and data list, received more than 87,000 webpage hits. Many journal articles have used the data in the Water Conflict Chronology in their analyses and case studies, and cited the information collected. The data has been used in briefings for the US Congress and defense and intelligence communities, for example, it was the subject of a briefing at the Office of Net Assessment at the Pentagon in January 2020 and the focus of a briefing to the Global Water Security Caucus of the U.S. Congress in June 2023. A major Los Angeles Times article about the database was published in December 2023: https://www.latimes.com/environment/story/2023-12-28/water-related-conflicts-on-the-rise-worldwide. The WCC is a key reference used in many Wikipedia entries, including “Water Conflict” (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water_conflict) and their main entry on “Water” (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water). Additional examples are provided below.",
          "schema:url": [
            "https://www.latimes.com/environment/story/2023-12-28/water-related-conflicts-on-the-rise-worldwide",
            "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water_conflict",
            "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water"
          ]
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "A few of the groups using the Water Conflict Chronology:\n• The Water, Peace, and Security Partnership: https://waterpeacesecurity.org/info/global-tool-update-december-2023-annual-review\n• The CIMA Research Foundation in Italy.\n• The Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Finland\n• Brot für die Welt\n• Korean Water Resources Association\n• Planetary P&L: Global Water Conflicts: Data Overview Dashboard\n• The Water Scarcity Index: https://waterscarcityatlas.org/data/water-conflict-chronology/",
          "schema:url": [
            "https://waterpeacesecurity.org/info/global-tool-update-december-2023-annual-review",
            "https://waterscarcityatlas.org/data/water-conflict-chronology/"
          ]
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "Many journal articles use the data from the WCC in their research and writings. A partial bibliography is included below.\nPartial Bibliography of Research, Theses, and Testimony Citing the Water Conflict Chronology Database and Data.\nAbbas, Dina, Nelia Forest, Timothy Hildebrandt, et al. 2003. Environmental Change and Security Project Report.\nAlEmadi, Fatima. 2021. “The Water Crisis in the Middle East: Exploring the Relationship Between Water Insecurity and Political Instability.” Georgetown University in Qatar, GU-Q.\nAli, Saleem, Peter H. Gleick, Jahan Tagnova, Syed Mohammad Ali, Yueming Yan, and Ambika Vishwanath. 2025. “Renegotiating The Indus Waters Treaty: Reconciling Science and Transboundary Water Law between Nuclear-Armed Rivals.” SSRN Scholarly Paper No. 5571718. Social Science Research Network, October 6. https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.5571718.\nAngelakis, Andreas N., Mohammad Valipour, Abdelkader T. Ahmed, et al. 2021. “Water Conflicts: From Ancient to Modern Times and in the Future.” Sustainability 13 (8): 4237. https://doi.org/10.3390/su13084237.\nBaripour, Milad, Mohammadsadegh Sadeghian, and Mojtaba Ansarian. 2021. “The Analysis of Water Wars in Order to Achieve Sustainable Water Peace.” Majlis and Rahbord 28 (107): 5–30.\nBemus, Wesley. n.d. “Geopolitical Conflict Dynamics of Valuable Riparian Resources in the Middle East Region.” Bachelor of Arts, Roger Williams University.\nBergamini, Hon Deborah. 2025. Geopolitical and Security Developments in the PAM Regions. Parliamentary Assembly of the Mediterranean.\nBrett Walton. 2022. “War, Drought Cause Spike in Violent Water Conflicts.” Circle of Blue, Where Water Speaks, August. https://www.circleofblue.org/2024/water-policy-politics/stress-conflict-migration/war-drought-cause-spike-in-violent-water-conflicts/.\nBridget R. Scanlon. n.d. Water Resources Podcast. June 12, 2025. With guest David Michel. Rising Global Water Conflicts. Accessed October 9, 2025. https://wrp.beg.utexas.edu/episode/rising-global-water-conflicts.\nBrot für die Welt. 2024. “Water Needs Peace.” Brot für die Welt, March. www.brot-fuer-die-welt.de.\nCassidy, Kristen W. 2015. “China’s Environmental Security Challenge: Policy Options in an Environmental Crisis.”\nCastro, José Esteban. 2016. Água e democracia na América Latina. EDUEPB. https://doi.org/10.7476/9788578794866.\nCentral Water Commission. 2024. News Links on Water Sector. Government of India, Department of Water Resources, River Development & Ganga Rejuvenation. https://cwc.gov.in/sites/default/files/final-international-news-09.09.2024.pdf.\nCranston, Gemma, Jonathan Green, and Hannah Tranter. 2015. Doing Business with Nature. Report by the Natural Capital Leaders Platform. University of Cambridge Institute for Sustainability Leadership (CISL). https://catalogue.unccd.int/521_2015Feb_doing-business-with-nature.pdf.\nCronin, Aidan A, Dinesh Shrestha, Nadine Cornier, Filipe Abdalla, Na‐dine Ezard, and Christine Aramburu. 2008. “A Review of Water and Sanitation Provision in Refugee Camps in Association with Selected Health and Nutrition Indicators–the Need for Integrated Service Provision.” Journal of Water and Health 6 (1): 1–13.\nDaoudy, Marwa. 2020. “Water Weaponization in the Syrian Conflict: Strategies of Domination and Cooperation.” International Affairs 96 (5): 1347–66.\nDavison, Bruce. 2003. “Snow Accumulation in a Distributed Hydrological Model.” University of Waterloo.\nDawson, Marcelle C., Christopher Rosin, and Navé Wald. 2017. Global Resource Scarcity: Catalyst for Conflict or Cooperation? 1st ed. Routledge. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315281612.\nDinko, Dinko Hanaan. 2022. “Scale Matters: A Spatiotemporal Analysis of Freshwater Conflicts from 1900-2019.” Water Resources Management 36 (1): 219–33.\nDombrowsky, Ines, Ramona Hägele, Lukas Behrenbeck, et al. 2022. “Natural Resource Governance in Light of the 2030 Agenda: The Case of Competition for Groundwater in Azraq, Jordan.” In IDOS Studies 106, version 1.0. German Institute of Development and Sustainability. https://doi.org/10.23661/IS106.2022.\nElisie Kåresdotter, Georgia Destouni, Richard B Lammers, Marko Keskinen, Haozhi Pan, and Zahra Kalantari. 2025. “Water Conflicts under Climate Change: Research Gaps and Priorities.” PubMed Central, January 31. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11871258/.\nEuropean Public Sphere. 2022. “The Aqueous Story of War.” European Public Sphere, July. https://www.publicsphere.eu/2022/07/the-aqueous-story-of-war/?lang=en.\nFrédéric Julien. 2006. “RESSOURCES HYDRIQUES ET CONFLITS INTERÉTATIQUES Apports et Limites d’une Approche Quantitative.” UNIVERSITÉ LAVAL.\nFredriksson, Per G, and Satyendra Kumar Gupta. 2020. Irrigation and Culture: Gender Roles and Women’s Rights. https://hdl.handle.net/10419/225005.\nGalgano, Francis A. 2024. “The 1964 Jordan River Diversion Plan: Transboundary Water Basins and Conflict.” Case Studies in the Environment 8 (1): 2305922. https://doi.org/10.1525/cse.2024.2305922.\nGleick, Peter. 2018. “Water, Conflict, and Peace.” Open Rivers: Rethinking Water, Place, and Community Journal, no. 11 (July). https://openrivers.lib.umn.edu/article/water-conflict-and-peace/.\nGleick, Peter. 2019. “Water as a Weapon and Casualty of Armed Conflict: A Review of Recent Water‐related Violence in Iraq, Syria, and Yemen.” Wiley Interdisciplinary Reviews: Water 6 (4): e1351. https://doi.org/10.1002/wat2.1351.\nGleick, Peter H., Charles Iceland, and Morgan Shimabuku. 2019. “Water Security and Society: Data and Definitions for Water Conflict and Cooperation.” In Water Security and the Sustainable Development Goals. UNESCO Publishing.\nGleick, Peter H., and Morgan Shimabuku. 2023. “Water-Related Conflicts: Definitions, Data, and Trends from the Water Conflict Chronology.” Environmental Research Letters 18 (3): 034022. https://doi.org/10.1088/1748-9326/acbb8f.\nGleick, Peter, Viktor Vyshnevskyi, and Serhii Shevchuk. 2023. “Rivers and Water Systems as Weapons and Casualties of the Russia‐Ukraine War.” Earth’s Future 11 (10): e2023EF003910.\nGrech-Madin, Charlotte. 2021. “Water and Warfare: The Evolution and Operation of the Water Taboo.” International Security 45 (4): 84–125. https://doi.org/10.1162/isec_a_00404.\nGreenberg, Michael R. 2009. Water, Conflict, and Hope. November. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2759781/.\nGrünwald, Richard, Yan Feng, and Wenling Wang. 2020. “Reconceptualization of the Transboundary Water Interaction Nexus (TWINS): Approaches, Opportunities and Challenges.” Water International 45 (5): 458–78.\nHack2O. 2024. “Investigating Water Issues: Methods and Principles - Documentation.” Hack₂O, June 25. http://forum.hack2o.eu/t/investigating-water-issues-methods-and-principles/35.\nHarder, Jennifer L, and Jennifer L Harder. 2018. Symposium-The Human Right to Water: Turning Principles Into Action Introduction.\nHill, Alice F., Cholpon K. Minbaeva, Alana M. Wilson, and Rysbek Satylkanov. 2017. “Hydrologic Controls and Water Vulnerabilities in the Naryn River Basin, Kyrgyzstan: A Socio-Hydro Case Study of Water Stressors in Central Asia.” Water 9 (5): 325. https://doi.org/10.3390/w9050325.\nHillenbrand, Thomas, Harald Hiessl, and Stefan Klug. 2013. Herausforderungen einer nachhaltigen Wasserwirtschaft.\nIceland, Charles. 2020. “How to Solve Water-Related Conflicts.” Finding: Freshwater, September 2. https://www.wri.org/insights/how-solve-water-related-conflicts.\nJackson, Steven J. 2008. “Writing the Global Water Crisis.” Technology and Culture 49 (3): 773–78.\nKåresdotter, Elisie, Zipan Cai, Haozhi Pan, and Zahra Kalantari. 2024. “Societal Pathways of Cooperation for Water-Related Conflict Mitigation.” April 1, 7616. https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-egu24-7616.\nKåresdotter, Elisie, Gustav Skoog, Haozhi Pan, and Zahra Kalantari. 2023. “Water-Related Conflict and Cooperation Events Worldwide: A New Dataset on Historical and Change Trends with Potential Drivers.” Science of The Total Environment 868 (Apr