      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The Water Conflict Chronology has been the most influential and impactful dataset raising awareness of the links between water and violence, and most importantly, in stimulating efforts to reduce the risks of water conflicts. Without information on the scope and severity of any problem, it is impossible to stimulate effective policy responses – accurate and comprehensive data and information are central to any public policy. Without, for example, data on atmospheric ozone concentrations over time, no actions would have been taken to protect the ozone layer in the Montreal Protocol. Without the data collected on atmospheric greenhouse gas concentrations, actions on climate change would be harder to justify. Similarly, without the data on water conflicts provided in the Water Conflict Chronology, research on water risks and efforts to develop international protections, international law, more sustainable water policies, and resilient water systems would be far less likely.\nThe WCC has influenced all three dimensions of impact: people, planet, and prosperity: The creation of the database has permitted the education and training of new experts at the intersection of geophysics, hydrology, and the social sciences, and it has brought information to practitioners from academia, the defense and intelligence agencies, policymakers, and the media. The WCC has become the leading environmental indicator of trends in water conflicts and is increasingly useful for linking water risks to both climate change and to broader aspects of water sustainability, including water poverty and the need to accelerate efforts to meet the Sustainable Development Goals. The WCC has led to policies to provide humanitarian relief and the protection of basic water services for populations displaced by conflict, and it has refocused attention on inequitable access to water and conflicts over water access and rights."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The Water Conflict Chronology has been a major project of the Pacific Institute for several decades and has been extremely influential in helping the nominator’s work to try to understand the links between environmental and resource issues and peace and security. The issue of “environmental security” began to take on new importance in the late twentieth century when concerns about the Cold War began to recede, at the same time that there was a growing understanding how geophysical, environmental, and resource factors influenced and were affected by violence, armed conflict, and war – including over energy resources and water. By collecting data on these factors, it has become possible to more clearly assess environmental risks and to press for developing solutions to water problems that can strengthen communities and reduce the risk of violence over water resources. Having these data has permitted the nominator to work directly with academic colleagues, the media and public, and the defense and intelligence communities to educate policymakers on these risks and to push for solutions around water challenges. As described in section 7, below, the data in the WCC has been used and useful for both researchers and for the international policy community."
        }
      ]
    },
