    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:062",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:062",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:062",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5656-6340"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The OpenAQ dataset has become the de facto open standard for global air quality data, serving as an indispensable backbone for equitable environmental research and policy action. Through its transparent aggregation of regulatory, low-cost, and citizen-sensing data, OpenAQ bridges critical information gaps in the Global South, where access to reliable environmental data is often the first barrier to action.\n\nAQAI has built our open-source OpenAQ-Engine (https://github.com/AQ-AI/openaq-engine ) atop this foundation, extending it with UNICEF-backed pilots in Laos, Mongolia, and Belize to model children’s exposure to air pollution and design early-warning tools for health systems. The dataset’s harmonised schema and public API enable cross-country integration with satellite and meteorological layers, creating the first hyperlocal “air-health” intelligence framework now informing both research and humanitarian response.\n\nIf OpenAQ did not exist, much of this interoperability and real-time transparency would be impossible. Environmental data would remain fragmented, siloed behind national or proprietary barriers, leaving frontline communities, local researchers, and policymakers unable to monitor or act at scale.\n\n> OpenAQ empowers the team and helps them connect with educators, students, and civic technologists to provide training on real-world environmental data, while also assisting governments and NGOs in predicting and responding to air-quality-related disasters, such as urban smog events or wildfire episodes.\n\n> Their data underpin methods used to examine geophysical and atmospheric phenomena related to air quality. Based on these data, a GeoAI hybrid hyperlocal air quality model was developed, providing a reliable, versioned reference of historical and nowcast PM2.5 measurements at the global level.\n\n> By democratising access to air quality data, OpenAQ supports social equity, enabling communities and local enterprises to advocate for clean-air policies, and catalysing innovations like AQAI’s low-cost sensing and UNICEF’s community resilience planning.\n\nThus, OpenAQ provides the framework for representing environmental infrastructure transparently. In its absence, it would be difficult to progress toward sustainable air quality management. Especially, data-sparse regions would remain disconnected, inequitable, and scientifically incomplete."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "AQAI, in collaboration with the UNICEF Office of Innovation, required large-scale, harmonised air quality data to train and evaluate deep learning models for pollution forecasting and exposure-risk assessment in data-scarce regions. The OpenAQ dataset served as their core registry and data backbone. The unified schema and open-access API enabled them to attain high-throughput ingestion, preprocessing, and spatial–temporal modelling across multiple countries.\nOpenAQ’s curated data streams, which integrate both regulatory and low-cost sensors, enabled them to train and benchmark their hybrid GeoAI models with consistent quality control and metadata alignment. This structure reduced the engineering overhead typically needed for cross-jurisdictional data harmonisation and significantly accelerated our experimentation cycle.\nFrom a technical perspective, OpenAQ provided the scalable infrastructure they used in their framework to build the OpenAQ-Engine, AQAI’s open-source ML pipeline that fuses satellite (TROPOMI, MODIS) and meteorological data for UNICEF’s deployments in Laos, Mongolia, and Belize. Their sensor partners (like Air Gradient) can now contribute directly to the OpenAQ registry, closing the loop between open data producers and open model users."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:062",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 63,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Education, Geohealth, Society, Education"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "OpenAQ"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "OpenAQ"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://openaq.org/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "explore.openaq.org, api.openaq.org"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "Not Applicable, RoR applied."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Dr. Colleen Rosales, Chris Hagerbaumer, Russ Biggs"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "OpenAQ is the world's largest open-source, open-access platform for harmonized, ground-level ambient air quality data. Its mission is to combat air inequality by making air quality data universally accessible for science, policy, and public empowerment. The platform aggregates and processes real-time and historical measurements from government reference monitors, research institutions, community organizations, and low-cost air sensors worldwide. \n - https://openaq.org/developers/platform-overview/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The OpenAQ dataset has become the de facto open standard for global air quality data, serving as an indispensable backbone for equitable environmental research and policy action. Through its transparent aggregation of regulatory, low-cost, and citizen-sensing data, OpenAQ bridges critical information gaps in the Global South, where access to reliable environmental data is often the first barrier to action.\n\nAQAI has built our open-source OpenAQ-Engine (https://github.com/AQ-AI/openaq-engine ) atop this foundation, extending it with UNICEF-backed pilots in Laos, Mongolia, and Belize to model children’s exposure to air pollution and design early-warning tools for health systems. The dataset’s harmonised schema and public API enable cross-country integration with satellite and meteorological layers, creating the first hyperlocal “air-health” intelligence framework now informing both research and humanitarian response.\n\nIf OpenAQ did not exist, much of this interoperability and real-time transparency would be impossible. Environmental data would remain fragmented, siloed behind national or proprietary barriers, leaving frontline communities, local researchers, and policymakers unable to monitor or act at scale.\n\n> OpenAQ empowers the team and helps them connect with educators, students, and civic technologists to provide training on real-world environmental data, while also assisting governments and NGOs in predicting and responding to air-quality-related disasters, such as urban smog events or wildfire episodes.\n\n> Their data underpin methods used to examine geophysical and atmospheric phenomena related to air quality. Based on these data, a GeoAI hybrid hyperlocal air quality model was developed, providing a reliable, versioned reference of historical and nowcast PM2.5 measurements at the global level.\n\n> By democratising access to air quality data, OpenAQ supports social equity, enabling communities and local enterprises to advocate for clean-air policies, and catalysing innovations like AQAI’s low-cost sensing and UNICEF’s community resilience planning.\n\nThus, OpenAQ provides the framework for representing environmental infrastructure transparently. In its absence, it would be difficult to progress toward sustainable air quality management. Especially, data-sparse regions would remain disconnected, inequitable, and scientifically incomplete."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "AQAI, in collaboration with the UNICEF Office of Innovation, required large-scale, harmonised air quality data to train and evaluate deep learning models for pollution forecasting and exposure-risk assessment in data-scarce regions. The OpenAQ dataset served as their core registry and data backbone. The unified schema and open-access API enabled them to attain high-throughput ingestion, preprocessing, and spatial–temporal modelling across multiple countries.\nOpenAQ’s curated data streams, which integrate both regulatory and low-cost sensors, enabled them to train and benchmark their hybrid GeoAI models with consistent quality control and metadata alignment. This structure reduced the engineering overhead typically needed for cross-jurisdictional data harmonisation and significantly accelerated our experimentation cycle.\nFrom a technical perspective, OpenAQ provided the scalable infrastructure they used in their framework to build the OpenAQ-Engine, AQAI’s open-source ML pipeline that fuses satellite (TROPOMI, MODIS) and meteorological data for UNICEF’s deployments in Laos, Mongolia, and Belize. Their sensor partners (like Air Gradient) can now contribute directly to the OpenAQ registry, closing the loop between open data producers and open model users."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Here are some of the publications that have used OpenAQ datasets: \n1. Pramanik, P., Last, C., et al. “Utilizing Remote Sensing and Machine Learning to Analyze Air Pollution Exposure.” AGU Fall Meeting 2024, Washington DC.\n2. Pramanik, P., Last, C. “Remote Sensing and Machine Learning Models for Air Pollution Exposure.” AGU Fall Meeting 2023, San Francisco.\n3. Last, C., Pramanik, P., et al. “Using Open Data to Understand Air Pollution Exposure.” AGU Fall Meeting 2022, Chicago.\n4. Last, C., Pramanik, P., et al. “Towards an Open Global Air Quality Monitoring Platform to Assess Children’s Exposure to Air Pollutants in the Light of COVID-19 Lockdowns.” SIG CHI 2021, Yokohama.\n5. AQAI's openaq-engine, https://github.com/AQ-AI/openaq-engine"
        },
        {
