    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:063",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "ESA WorldCereal 10 m 2021 v100",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:063",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:geohealth-society-education"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Van Tricht et al."
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://zenodo.org/records/7875105"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:zenodo-org"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "VITO Belgium"
        }
      ],
      "description": "The ESA WorldCereal 10m 2021 dataset stands as the world’s first open-access, global collection of 10-meter resolution crop maps, setting a new standard for agricultural monitoring. This pioneering dataset delivers detailed, seasonal maps of temporary crops, maize, and cereals (wheat, barley, and rye), alongside irrigation and active cropland layers, all processed for 106 agro-ecological zones to ensure regional accuracy. Unlike traditional annual assessments, WorldCereal provides seasonal updates, capturing the dynamic nature of global agriculture and including confidence layers to indicate data reliability.\nBy focusing on major commodity crops and irrigation practices, this dataset addresses critical gaps in global food security and sustainable agriculture. It offers researchers, policymakers, and agricultural professionals unprecedented spatial and temporal detail, enabling data-driven decisions for crop management, resource allocation, and climate resilience.\nThe dataset is freely available for global use, fostering transparency and innovation in agricultural research and policy.",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "Van Tricht, K., Degerickx, J., Gilliams, S., Zanaga, D., Battude, M., Grosu, A., Brombacher, J., Lesiv, M., Bayas, J. C. L., Karanam, S., Fritz, S., Becker-Reshef, I., Franch, B., Mollà-Bononad, B., Boogaard, H., Pratihast, A. K., Koetz, B., & Szantoi, Z. (2023). WorldCereal: A dynamic open-source system for global-scale, seasonal, and reproducible crop and irrigation mapping. Earth System Science Data, 15(12), 5491–5515. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-15-5491-2023",
          "identifier": [
            {
              "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
              "propertyID": "DOI",
              "value": "https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-15-5491-2023"
            }
          ],
          "schema:url": [
            "https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-15-5491-2023"
          ]
        },
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "See, L., Gilliams, S., Conchedda, G. et al. Dynamic global-scale crop and irrigation monitoring. Nat Food 4, 736–737 (2023). https://doi.org/10.1038/s43016-023-00841-7",
          "identifier": [
            {
              "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
              "propertyID": "DOI",
              "value": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s43016-023-00841-7"
            }
          ],
          "schema:url": [
            "https://doi.org/10.1038/s43016-023-00841-7"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "The ESA WorldCereal dataset has rapidly moved beyond research into real-world use. It supports policy and statistics, with FAO benchmarking it against FAOSTAT for national reporting and food security monitoring. Its availability in Google Earth Engine and integration with openEO enable ministries, NGOs, and regional hubs to run scalable crop monitoring pipelines, while in the commercial domain it is repackaged into farm-point GeoParquet products for agritech platforms, insurers, and supply-chain analytics. Furthermore, its global reference datasets are reused for training and benchmarking machine learning models across the EO community. The main WorldCereal paper (Van Tricht et al., 2023, ESSD) has already been cited 65 times on Google Scholar, highlighting its rapid uptake and growing influence as a reference resource in less than two years."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:person:zoltan-szantoi",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Zoltan Szantoi",
      "schema:email": "zoltan.szantoi@esa.int",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:directorate-of-earth-observation-programmes-european-space-agency-esa-frascati-r"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:directorate-of-earth-observation-programmes-european-space-agency-esa-frascati-r",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "Directorate of Earth Observation Programmes, European Space Agency (ESA), Frascati RM, Italy, and (2) Department of Geography and Environmental Studies, Stellenbosch University (SU), Matieland, Stellenbosch, South Africa"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:063",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:063",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:063",
      "nominator": [
        "id:person:zoltan-szantoi"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "This dataset was nominated because the ESA WorldCereal dataset and system mark a transformative advancement in global agricultural monitoring, not only for the data they provide but also for their innovative approach to user empowerment. The dataset is the first open-access collection of 10-meter resolution seasonal crop maps, offering detailed insights into temporary crops, maize, wheat, barley, rye, irrigation, and active cropland across 106 agro-ecological zones. This level of detail and seasonal granularity is unprecedented, enabling researchers, policymakers, and practitioners to monitor food production, water use, and land-use changes with greater accuracy than ever before.\n\nWhat truly distinguishes WorldCereal is its open-source, cloud-agnostic system, which is free to use and built on open data from Copernicus Sentinels. Designed to be accessible and adaptable, the system allows users to train and apply their own crop type models, either by leveraging pre-trained algorithms or customizing them with local field data. This means that even those in resource-limited settings can generate tailored, high-resolution crop maps without needing advanced technical skills or extensive computational resources.\n\nThe system’s flexibility ensures that it can evolve alongside the needs of the global community, supporting a wide range of applications—from local food security initiatives to global climate and economic resilience strategies. By providing both the data and the tools to adapt and expand crop monitoring, WorldCereal is not just a dataset but a platform for innovation in agriculture, environmental sustainability, and equitable development. Its open and user-driven design makes it a vital resource for addressing some of the most pressing challenges in food security and sustainable land management today."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "From the nominator’s perspective, the WorldCereal dataset is a dynamic, living resource designed for direct and powerful interaction. They see users engaging with it in two primary ways: many directly access the pre-generated, 10-meter global cropland maps for 2021 through Zenodo, where download numbers confirm its rapid adoption. For deeper analysis, the seamless integration with Google Earth Engine is key, allowing thousands of users to instantly visualize and interrogate WorldCereal layers alongside other planetary data for large-scale agricultural studies.\n\nMost importantly, they provide the entire open-source, WorldCereal system. This empowers advanced users—researchers, agronomists, and policymakers—to go beyond the base products. Through the Network of Resources or local implementations, they can train custom models with their own data, adapting this powerful tool to specific regional crops and challenges. This transition from a static dataset to an adaptable platform ensures WorldCereal's impact continues to grow, directly supporting global food security and sustainable land management."
        }
      ]
    },
