      "title": "California Climate Projections",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:064",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:global-environmental-change-paleoceanography-and-paleoclimatology-biogeoscience"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Dan Cayan, David Pierce, Stefan Rahimi, Benjamin Bass, Lu Su, Dennis Lettenmaier, Alex Hall"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://analytics.cal-adapt.org/data/"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:cal-adapt-analytics-engine"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "Cal-Adapt Analytics Engine"
        }
      ],
      "description": "Nominators 1a and 1b: \nThe project produced four primary climate projection datasets.\n1. Dynamically Downscaled Data: Eight CMIP6 GCMs were dynamically downscaled using the Weather Research and Forecasting (WRF) model to 9 km over the western U.S. and 3 km over California under SSP3-7.0. The dataset includes 30 hourly variables and additional 6-hourly outputs. Five models were bias-corrected before downscaling.\n2. LOCA2-Hybrid: Fifteen CMIP6 GCMs were statistically downscaled to 3 km over California using dynamically downscaled data to capture weather and climate patterns. The LOCA2 (Localized Constructed Analogues) method (Pierce et al., J. of Hydrometeorology, 2023) applies bias correction throughout the statistical process and includes multiple SSPs and ensemble members from each GCM. Daily variables include temperature, precipitation, wind, humidity, and solar radiation, with hourly temperature downscaling at 32 stations.\n3. Hydrologic Projections: Two hydrology models, Noah-MP (via WRF-Hydro) and VIC, simulated land-surface conditions using four dynamically downscaled and 18 LOCA2-Hybrid datasets. After calibration, snowpack, runoff, and additional surface variables were produced daily.\n4. Hourly Sea Level Projections: Hourly sea-level projections were developed for 13 coastal and Bay-Delta sites using low, medium, and high scenarios from the 2023 State of California Sea Level Rise Guidance. Hourly levels combine predicted tides, meteorological deviations, and long-term sea-level rise; surface wave and run-up effects are excluded.\n\nNominator 2 (for LOCA2-Hybrid):\nLOCA, which stands for Localized Constructed Analogs, is a technique for downscaling climate model projections to regional or local scaleMost climate models have relatively coarse spatial resolution. Which is to say, a single gridcell of a global climate model can cover the distance from San Francisco to Sacramento. If you want to know how temperatures or precipitation might change on finer spatial scales, you need to downscale the climate model output.The localized constructed analogs (LOCA) method is a statistical scheme that produces downscaled estimates suitable for hydrological simulations using a multi-scale spatial matching scheme to pick appropriate analog days from observations.\nThe LOCA2-Hybrid dataset contains projections downscaled from 15 distinct GCMs from CMIP6. The dataset contains multiple ensembles for each GCM. Three CMIP6 scenarios are included in the downscaling (SSP2-4.5, SSP3-7.0 and SSP5-8.5) along with a historical scenario. Across all models, ensembles and scenarios a total of 123 models were downscaled through LOCA2-Hybrid.\n\nThe LOCA2-Hybrid dataset is designed to support decision making across California, primarily focused on the energy sector. Through engagement with key users and by observational constraints a total of 7 variables are available (precipitation, temperature, specific humidity, relative humidity, wind speed, wind direction and shortware radiation flux). Daily values are available for most variables, with a min/max daily available for some variables.\n\nFor more information please see: https://analytics.cal-adapt.org/data/catalog/\n\nFirst, a pool of candidate observed analog days is chosen by matching the model field to be downscaled to observed days over the region that is positively correlated with the point being downscaled, which leads to a natural independence of the downscaling results to the extent of the domain being downscaled. Then the one candidate analog day that best matches in the local area around the grid cell being downscaled is the single analog day used there.\n\nMost grid cells are downscaled using only the single locally selected analog day, but locations whose neighboring cells identify a different analog day use a weighted combination of the center and adjacent analog days to reduce edge discontinuities. By contrast, existing constructed analog methods typically use a weighted average of the same 30 analog days for the entire domain. By greatly reducing this averaging, LOCA produces better estimates of extreme days, constructs a more realistic depiction of the spatial coherence of the downscaled field, and reduces the problem of producing too many light-precipitation days.\n\nThe LOCA method is more computationally expensive than existing constructed analog techniques, but is still practical for downscaling numerous climate model simulations with limited computational resources.\n\nhttps://loca.ucsd.edu/what-is-loca/",
