        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "Nominators 1a and 1b: \nThe project produced four primary climate projection datasets.\n1. Dynamically Downscaled Data: Eight CMIP6 GCMs were dynamically downscaled using the Weather Research and Forecasting (WRF) model to 9 km over the western U.S. and 3 km over California under SSP3-7.0. The dataset includes 30 hourly variables and additional 6-hourly outputs. Five models were bias-corrected before downscaling.\n2. LOCA2-Hybrid: Fifteen CMIP6 GCMs were statistically downscaled to 3 km over California using dynamically downscaled data to capture weather and climate patterns. The LOCA2 (Localized Constructed Analogues) method (Pierce et al., J. of Hydrometeorology, 2023) applies bias correction throughout the statistical process and includes multiple SSPs and ensemble members from each GCM. Daily variables include temperature, precipitation, wind, humidity, and solar radiation, with hourly temperature downscaling at 32 stations.\n3. Hydrologic Projections: Two hydrology models, Noah-MP (via WRF-Hydro) and VIC, simulated land-surface conditions using four dynamically downscaled and 18 LOCA2-Hybrid datasets. After calibration, snowpack, runoff, and additional surface variables were produced daily.\n4. Hourly Sea Level Projections: Hourly sea-level projections were developed for 13 coastal and Bay-Delta sites using low, medium, and high scenarios from the 2023 State of California Sea Level Rise Guidance. Hourly levels combine predicted tides, meteorological deviations, and long-term sea-level rise; surface wave and run-up effects are excluded.\n\nNominator 2 (for LOCA2-Hybrid):\nLOCA, which stands for Localized Constructed Analogs, is a technique for downscaling climate model projections to regional or local scaleMost climate models have relatively coarse spatial resolution. Which is to say, a single gridcell of a global climate model can cover the distance from San Francisco to Sacramento. If you want to know how temperatures or precipitation might change on finer spatial scales, you need to downscale the climate model output.The localized constructed analogs (LOCA) method is a statistical scheme that produces downscaled estimates suitable for hydrological simulations using a multi-scale spatial matching scheme to pick appropriate analog days from observations.\nThe LOCA2-Hybrid dataset contains projections downscaled from 15 distinct GCMs from CMIP6. The dataset contains multiple ensembles for each GCM. Three CMIP6 scenarios are included in the downscaling (SSP2-4.5, SSP3-7.0 and SSP5-8.5) along with a historical scenario. Across all models, ensembles and scenarios a total of 123 models were downscaled through LOCA2-Hybrid.\n\nThe LOCA2-Hybrid dataset is designed to support decision making across California, primarily focused on the energy sector. Through engagement with key users and by observational constraints a total of 7 variables are available (precipitation, temperature, specific humidity, relative humidity, wind speed, wind direction and shortware radiation flux). Daily values are available for most variables, with a min/max daily available for some variables.\n\nFor more information please see: https://analytics.cal-adapt.org/data/catalog/\n\nFirst, a pool of candidate observed analog days is chosen by matching the model field to be downscaled to observed days over the region that is positively correlated with the point being downscaled, which leads to a natural independence of the downscaling results to the extent of the domain being downscaled. Then the one candidate analog day that best matches in the local area around the grid cell being downscaled is the single analog day used there.\n\nMost grid cells are downscaled using only the single locally selected analog day, but locations whose neighboring cells identify a different analog day use a weighted combination of the center and adjacent analog days to reduce edge discontinuities. By contrast, existing constructed analog methods typically use a weighted average of the same 30 analog days for the entire domain. By greatly reducing this averaging, LOCA produces better estimates of extreme days, constructs a more realistic depiction of the spatial coherence of the downscaled field, and reduces the problem of producing too many light-precipitation days.\n\nThe LOCA method is more computationally expensive than existing constructed analog techniques, but is still practical for downscaling numerous climate model simulations with limited computational resources.\n\nhttps://loca.ucsd.edu/what-is-loca/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Nominators 1a and 1b: \nThe dataset was nominated because it represents a comprehensive, state-of-the-art climate data resource for California. A key innovation of this dataset is its hybrid-statistical downscaling approach, which combines the LOCA (Localized Constructed Analogs; Pierce et al., Journal of Hydrometeorology, 2023) statistical method with a pattern library derived from dynamically downscaled simulations that capture both historical and future climate conditions. This hybrid technique reduces the assumption of stationarity, the notion that future weather patterns will be the same as those of the past. By integrating dynamically and statistically downscaled datasets, California agencies, governments, and researchers gain access to high-temporal-resolution data along with projections spanning multiple future scenarios (SSPs), ensemble members, and global climate models (GCMs). Together, these two forms of downscaling provide a foundation for addressing a wide range of climate vulnerability and resilience questions. For example, an energy utility can use hourly data from the dynamically downscaled dataset for load forecasting and then use the statistically downscaled data to estimate the probability of such extreme events across many models and ensembles. The additional hydrologic data enhance water resource planning and support resilience to extreme flood events, while the sea-level data provide crucial insights into the frequency of coastal flooding and recovery intervals for affected communities. To date, 15 utilities and state agencies, 12 consulting firms, five academic institutions, and one private company have used this dataset. Collectively, these California climate projections form a robust, comprehensive resource that enables researchers and decision-makers alike to identify vulnerabilities and develop science-based resilience strategies across the state.\n\nNominator 2 (for LOCA2-Hybrid):\nThe LOCA2-Hybrid dataset is being used by dozens of institutions, researchers, and state agencies to make decisions, understand possible futures, and inform climate policy for over 40 million people across California.\n\nThis dataset is primarily being used to support EPIC grants within CEC (California Energy Commission). These grants are charged to support ratepayers (people who pay for energy via investor-owned utilities) in California and aim to meet the following goals – \nWork to ensure equitable access to safe, affordable, reliable, and environmentally sustainable energy.\n\nAccelerate reaching California’s clean energy goals, including the commitment to a 100 percent clean energy generation system by 2045.\n\nDevelop novel technologies, tools, and strategies related to building decarbonization, energy storage, grid modernization, and more.\n\nEnsure public participation and transparency in its research and development programs.\nEvaluate and measure the benefits of investments, such as cost savings, environmental improvements, and increased grid reliability.\n\nGuided by California Public Utilities Commission (CPUC), multiple investor-owned utilities (IOUs) have used the LOCA2-Hybrid datasets to perform vulnerability assessments, make infrastructure investment decisions, plan for wildfire prevention, understand future risks under a changing climate, and much more. This dataset and associated guidelines regarding its use were published and supported in CPUC’s rule-making Rulemaking 18-04-019 on July 22, 2024.\n\nhttps://docs.cpuc.ca.gov/PublishedDocs/Efile/G000/M536/K272/536272932.PDF\n\nThe development of the LOCA2-Hybrid dataset was informed by many of the people and organizations involved in California’s policy, decision-making, and practice-related fields. Through intentional, social-sciences research, dataset users' needs helped guide decisions about which global climate models, shared socioeconomic pathways, and other associated methodologies were chosen to support LOCA2-Hybrid. Engaging with the community of potential data users enabled the developers of the LOCA2-Hybrid dataset to understand the needs they were trying to address while simultaneously developing cutting-edge science.\nWithout this dataset, planners would need to rely on far coarser data to make crucial decisions. The cost of wildfires in California alone is estimated to be between $250 and $275 billion. Utilities, counties, and agencies are looking to understand the impact of microclimates, climate change, and the crucial interplay between climate, geography, populations, and policy to ameliorate risks and plan for changes in future wildfire events. While not ideal, a lot of planning occurs at a county or, more often, census tract level. If there were only 9km or 45km grids for planning purposes, the differences between urban heat islands and rural locations would be completely lost. This dataset is a ginormous step forward in terms of capturing those nuances.\n\nBeyond the energy sector, this dataset is now part of the Amazon Sustainable Data Initiative (ASDI) and is used by dozens of groups, including IOUs, consultants, agencies, local community groups, insurance companies, individual interested parties, web developers, and more. [ bring in 5th assessment here? ]\n\nBy providing high-quality, high-resolution projections with a large range of ensemble sets, the stage is set for informed decision making, research, and planning for California and the WECC (Western Electricity Coordinating Council) at large."
