          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The U.S. Geological Survey (USGS)’s Land Cover program has leveraged methodologies from legacy land cover projects - National Land Cover Database (NLCD) and Land Change Monitoring, Assessment, and Projection (LCMAP) - together with modern innovations in geospatial deep learning technologies to create the next generation of land cover and land change information. The product suite is called, “Annual NLCD” and includes six annual products that represent land cover and surface change characteristics of the U.S.:\n \nLand Cover\nLand Cover Change\nLand Cover Confidence\nFractional Impervious Surface\nImpervious Descriptor\nSpectral Change Day of Year\nThese land cover science algorithms harness the remotely sensed Landsat data record to provide the land surface change information needed by scientists, resource managers, and decision-makers. Annual NLCD uses a modernized, integrated approach to map, monitor, synthesize, and understand the complexities of land use, cover, and condition change."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The National Land Cover Database (NLCD) was initiated 30 years ago and has continually provided critical land-cover and land-change information for the United States. Originally launched to address the lack of national-scale, moderate-resolution land-cover data, NLCD has evolved from the pioneering 1992 dataset into the latest release, Annual NLCD 1985-2024, providing annual land cover, land cover change, forest canopy, impervious surface, and other land-change variables. A new, innovative AI-based methodology was developed, linking 3 deep learning models to simultaneously produce land cover and impervious surface variables, greatly reducing product latency, consistency, and accuracy.\n\nThroughout its history, NLCD has become an essential resource for scientific research, land management, and policy development. Over 10,300 scientific publications cite NLCD, including over 9,300 peer-reviewed journal articles covering over 1,100 different journal titles, over 280 books and book chapters, and over 600 conference papers. To date, over 970 up and coming scientists have cited NLCD in theses and dissertations. Governments at all levels rely on NLCD, with appearances in over 6,300 policy documents and another 1,700 government technical reports. Over 70% of all US Federal Landsat data use within the sectors of agriculture, forestry, and climate is related to NLCD or an NLCD derivative product. NLCD has even been a staple in the popular press, appearing in over 12,000 popular news stories.\n\nEven as new land-cover products emerge, NLCD continues to distinguish itself through its temporal depth, long-term federal backing, product consistency, and meticulously defined accuracies. For economic applications, NLCD enables analyses of forestry resources, urban expansion, and infrastructure planning, informing investment and land-use decisions. In biodiversity and ecosystem applications, NLCD land cover and tree canopy data help identify critical habitats, track habitat loss, and assess landscape connectivity to guide conservation priorities. For water quality and availability, NLCD supports watershed modeling, pollutant load estimates, and hydrologic simulations by providing inputs on impervious surface extent and vegetation cover. In human health and safety contexts, NLCD data are used to map heat-vulnerable urban areas, track environmental exposure risks, and plan for green space access. Regarding natural hazards and fire, NLCD is used to assess wildfire risk based on vegetation type and fuel continuity and guiding hazard mitigation planning. For climate and weather applications, NLCD informs land-surface models, carbon stock assessments, and climate adaptation planning by providing data of landscape changes that influence energy balance, evapotranspiration, and local climate regulation. Together, these applications highlight NLCD’s role as a foundational dataset for integrated environmental monitoring, resource management, and policy and decision-making."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator is an original developer of the first 1992 NLCD, USGS Branch Chief overseeing NLCD and other projects, and a user of NLCD in their own research. NLCD's use is so pervasive across the scientific community, as a trusted source of information on US land-change information. Outside of their leadership role in USGS, they use NLCD as a core dataset for land-change modeling. Information on the rates, spatial patterns, and temporal trends of change as measured by NLCD are a critical component of their Forecasting Scenarios of land use (FORE-SCE) model. FORE-SCE is used to \"temporally extend\" data such as NLCD, providing both historical and potential future land cover for dates where remote sensing data are unavailable. Using NLCD to drive the model, the nominator’s team developed national scale projections from 1938 to 2100, with multiple future scenarios, as well as several high resolution applications for parts of the US. \n\nAs budgets become more restrictive and the nominator as a leader needs to \"sell\" the value of their team’s work, NLCD is the easiest sales pitch. It's the most widely used Landsat-based product in the world. It touches all sectors, all geographies, and has become deeply ingrained in many of the Nation's land and resource management workflows."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "United States Geological Survey. (2025). Annual national land cover database (Nlcd) collection 1 products(Ver. 1. 1, june 2025) [Pdf,png]. U.S. Geological Survey. https://doi.org/10.5066/P94UXNTS"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Sohl, T., Jin, S., Dewitz, J., Wickham, J., Brown, J., Stehman, S., Herold, N., Schleeweis, K., Tollerud, H., & Deering, C. (2025). Thirty years of the u. S. National land cover database: Impacts and future direction. Photogrammetric Engineering & Remote Sensing, 91(10), 647–659. https://doi.org/10.14358/PERS.25-00121R2 \n\nThere are over 10,000 cited uses of NLCD. There's a table in the above paper that notes cited uses of NLCD for many different sectors. That table is provided here in list form, with major sectors (no following citations) followed by sub-sector and relevant citations of NLCD.\n\nEnergy Development\nBiofuel development Stoof et al. 2015; Baxter and Calvert 2017; Austin et al. 2022\nWind development and siting Kiesecker et al. 2011; Fargione et al. 2012; Dai and Scown 2025 \nHydropower potential at U.S. dams Hadjerioua et al. 2012; Fergus et al. 2021; Rosenlieb et al. 2022\nPower grid vulnerability Alpay et al. 2020; Arora and Ceferino 2023; Watson et al. 2024\nPost-operations reclamation Ferrari et al. 2009; Gurung et al. 2018; Maigret et al. 2019\n\nHuman Health\nMosquitoes and disease risk Bowden et al. 2011; Wiese et al. 2019; Bondo et al. 2022\nLyme disease risk Diuk-Wasser et al. 2012; Ballard and Bone 2021\nCOVID incidence You and Pan 2020; Grima et al. 2021; Li and Managi 2023\nAir-quality effects Cooter et al. 2012; Di et al. 2019a, 2019b; Rehman and Counts 2021\n\nNatural Hazards\nFlooding vulnerability (inland) Qiang et al. 2017; Tate et al. 2021; Banjara et al. 2024\nCoastal storm surge Bunya et al. 2010; Haddad et al. 2015; Yarveysi et al. 2025\nLandslide vulnerability He et al. 2014; Dahigamuwa et al. 2016; Meng et al. 2024\nFire management and risk Alexandre et al. 2015; Radeloff et al. 2018; Gould et al. 2023\n\nBiodiversity and Ecosystem Health\nRegional and national species richness Pidgeon et al. 2007; Culbert et al. 2013; Dertien et al. 2020\nMigratory corridors and behavior Bonter et al. 2009; Guo et al. 2022; Mendgen et al. 2023 \nPollinator health and abundance Gallant et al. 2014; Ballare et al. 2019; Pardee et al. 2023\nSpecies distribution models Ferreira et al. 2011; Kreakie et al. 2012; Sohl 2014\n\nAtmosphere and Land Interactions (Weather)\nConvective precipitation amounts Carrio et al. 2010; Houser et al. 2019; Singh et al. 2020 \nSevere storm severity Stueve et al. 2011; Liu and Li 2016; Frazier et al. 2019 \nSnow characteristics Anderson et al. 2014; Foster et al. 2017; Linares and Ni-Meister 2024 \nUrban heat island Conlon et al. 2020; Xian et al. 2021; Hasan et al. 2022\n\nHydrology\nPollutant modeling McMahon et al. 2019; Saby et al. 2021; Woznicki et al. 2023\nSuspended sediment modeling Renwick et al. 2005; Schwarz 2008; Dow et al. 2024\nHarmful algal blooms Iiames et al. 2021; Pelletier et al. 2024\nUrban runoff Chen et al. 2017; Kandel et al. 2022; Patton et al. 2022\nStreamflow modeling Eddy et al. 2017; Debbage and Shepherd 2018; Chordia et al. 2022\n\nEconomics and Policy\nNational flood policy Patterson and Doyle 2009; FEMA 2022\nClean Water Act analysis Taylor and Druckenmiller 2022; Tomczyk et al. 2023\nNational land accounts Sutton et al. 2019; Warnell et al. 2020; Wentland et al. 2020 \nTourism economics, availability Beeco et al. 2014; Browning et al. 2022 \nConservation Reserve Program Nielson et al. 2008; Riffell et al. 2010; Fleming 2013 \n\nLand Management\nLand use planning Kovacs et al. 2013; Pennington et al. 2017; Bai et al. 2021\nSoil erosion potential Duan et al. 2008; Woznicki et al. 2020; Stribling 2024 \nAgricultural water use Singh et al. 2014; LaHaye et al. 2021; Gedefaw et al. 2022\nWatershed management Ale et al. 2012; Vittorio and Ahiablame 2015; Yang and Best 2015\nEcosystem services valuation Pickard et al. 2015; Narducci et al. 2019; Cochran et al. 2020"
        },
        {
