        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "To facilitate earth system modeling and inventory-based studies, the nominator’s team developed a spatially explicit time-series data set of nitrogen (N) fertilizer use in agricultural land of the continental U.S. during 1850 to 2015. The spatial resolution of this data set is 5km × 5km, and the time step is annually. Through gap-filling, we reconstructed the state-level crop-specific N fertilizer use history by harmonizing national and state-level N fertilizer use data from multiple data sources. They then spatialized and resampled N fertilizer use data to 5km × 5km gridded maps based on historical land cover data of the continental U.S. developed by Yu and Lu (2017). This data indicated that N fertilizer use rates of the U.S. increased by 34 times from 1940 to 2015. Geospatial analysis revealed that the hotspots of N fertilizer use have shifted from the southeastern and eastern US to the Midwest and the Great Plains during the past century."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "This long-term, high-resolution dataset of nitrogen (N) fertilizer use in the contiguous United States fills a critical gap in understanding agricultural practices and their environmental impacts. Before this work, no dataset provided a continuous, spatially explicit, crop-specific record of fertilizer application rates, timing, and chemical forms over more than a century. By offering 5 km × 5 km maps from 1850 to 2015, it enables unprecedented analysis of nitrogen management trends, from the early use of nitrate fertilizers to the modern dominance of ammonium-based products, and reveals regional hotspots of fertilizer use that have shifted over time.\n\nPeople:\nThis dataset is a powerful educational and decision-making tool, helping students, researchers, and practitioners trace 160+ years of agricultural intensification and its environmental consequences. Its detail supports training in geospatial analysis and agricultural modeling and aids development of strategies to predict and prevent water quality issues, harmful algal blooms, and other nitrogen-driven hazards that impact rural communities.\n\nPlanet:\nBy documenting historical and regional patterns of nitrogen use, this resource provides essential inputs for Earth system and biogeochemical models. It helps quantify nitrogen’s role in greenhouse gas emissions, water and air pollution, and ecosystem degradation. With high-resolution mapping of fertilizer timing and form, conservation efforts can more precisely target interventions, and scientists can better evaluate the cumulative nitrogen footprint of U.S. agriculture. Without this dataset, our ability to reconstruct nitrogen’s influence on landscapes, biodiversity, and climate would remain limited.\n\nProsperity:\nThis dataset informs policies and management strategies that reduce fertilizer waste, lower economic losses, and promote resilient farming systems. It supports socially equitable nutrient solutions, guiding efforts to protect water supplies and public health, while also contributing to global food security by improving nitrogen use efficiency.\n\nIn summary, this dataset transforms how we study nitrogen’s role in shaping agricultural systems, ecosystems, and communities over the last 165 years. It offers a comprehensive foundation for modeling, conservation, and sustainable agriculture planning, filling a long-standing gap in spatially explicit fertilizer data. Without it, historical nitrogen management trends would remain fragmented, constraining efforts to design effective solutions for environmental protection and agricultural sustainability."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator uses this dataset extensively in research on agricultural sustainability, focusing on nutrient use efficiency (NUE), nitrogen (N) cycling, and environmental impacts of fertilizer use in the United States. Its high spatial and temporal resolution allows them to quantify long-term trends in fertilizer inputs across different crops and regions, supporting detailed nitrogen budget assessments and evaluation of management practices over more than a century. They rely on these data to investigate how fertilizer timing, chemical form, and application intensity influence nitrogen losses, including ammonia emissions, and to link historical fertilizer patterns to water quality risks and greenhouse gas emissions."
        },
