      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "This dataset represents a major advancement in understanding agricultural phosphorus (P) management across the contiguous United States, providing the first long-term, high-resolution, crop-specific maps of P fertilizer application rates, timing, and methods. Previous datasets were either derived from sales data, offering coarse county-level averages without crop differentiation, or based on farmer surveys that captured crop-level practices but missed spatial variability between states. By reconciling these complementary sources, this work delivers a comprehensive 4 km × 4 km gridded dataset covering 1850–2022, filling a critical gap in quantifying the evolution of phosphorus fertilization practices over more than 170 years.\n\nPeople:\nThis resource supports education, research training, and evidence-based decision-making, enabling students, researchers, and extension specialists to explore how agricultural intensification, fertilizer strategies, and land-use changes have shaped soil fertility and environmental outcomes. By characterizing fertilizer timing and application methods, the dataset aids development of predictive tools to identify areas at risk for nutrient loss, harmful algal blooms, and soil degradation, protecting the health and livelihoods of rural and downstream communities.\n\nPlanet:\nPhosphorus is a non-renewable resource, and its mismanagement contributes significantly to water quality deterioration and ecosystem damage. This dataset provides essential inputs for agricultural, watershed, and Earth system models, allowing scientists to quantify P budgets, trace sources of runoff and eutrophication, and evaluate regional shifts in P management practices over time. Its unprecedented temporal depth and fine spatial resolution enable robust assessments of phosphorus footprints, informing conservation planning and restoration efforts that target nutrient pollution hotspots. Without this dataset, long-term trends in phosphorus inputs and their environmental consequences would remain fragmented and poorly understood.\n\nProsperity:\nThe dataset provides a foundation for more efficient and equitable nutrient management policies, reducing fertilizer waste, economic losses, and environmental costs. By mapping regional and crop-specific fertilization practices, it enables tailored recommendations that enhance farmer profitability, soil health, and climate resilience. It supports long-term planning for sustainable phosphorus use, helping safeguard this critical nutrient for future food security and global agricultural productivity.\n\nIn summary, this dataset delivers an unprecedented view of phosphorus fertilization patterns in the U.S., integrating multiple data sources to provide a century-scale, spatially explicit foundation for advancing sustainable nutrient management. Without it, efforts to optimize phosphorus use efficiency, mitigate water pollution, and sustain agricultural productivity would lack the historical and spatial context needed for transformative change.",
          "impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "People",
              "schema:text": "This resource supports education, research training, and evidence-based decision-making, enabling students, researchers, and extension specialists to explore how agricultural intensification, fertilizer strategies, and land-use changes have shaped soil fertility and environmental outcomes. By characterizing fertilizer timing and application methods, the dataset aids development of predictive tools to identify areas at risk for nutrient loss, harmful algal blooms, and soil degradation, protecting the health and livelihoods of rural and downstream communities."
            },
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "Planet",
              "schema:text": "Phosphorus is a non-renewable resource, and its mismanagement contributes significantly to water quality deterioration and ecosystem damage. This dataset provides essential inputs for agricultural, watershed, and Earth system models, allowing scientists to quantify P budgets, trace sources of runoff and eutrophication, and evaluate regional shifts in P management practices over time. Its unprecedented temporal depth and fine spatial resolution enable robust assessments of phosphorus footprints, informing conservation planning and restoration efforts that target nutrient pollution hotspots. Without this dataset, long-term trends in phosphorus inputs and their environmental consequences would remain fragmented and poorly understood."
            },
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "Prosperity",
              "schema:text": "The dataset provides a foundation for more efficient and equitable nutrient management policies, reducing fertilizer waste, economic losses, and environmental costs. By mapping regional and crop-specific fertilization practices, it enables tailored recommendations that enhance farmer profitability, soil health, and climate resilience. It supports long-term planning for sustainable phosphorus use, helping safeguard this critical nutrient for future food security and global agricultural productivity.\n\nIn summary, this dataset delivers an unprecedented view of phosphorus fertilization patterns in the U.S., integrating multiple data sources to provide a century-scale, spatially explicit foundation for advancing sustainable nutrient management. Without it, efforts to optimize phosphorus use efficiency, mitigate water pollution, and sustain agricultural productivity would lack the historical and spatial context needed for transformative change."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator uses this dataset to conduct detailed phosphorus (P) budget and surplus analyses across the United States, leveraging its fine spatial and temporal resolution to understand long-term trends in fertilizer use and nutrient dynamics. By integrating this dataset with crop production, land use, and soil data, they can quantify regional and national P surpluses, track shifts in fertilizer management practices over time, and identify hotspots of nutrient inefficiency and environmental risk.\n\nIts crop-specific detail and century-scale coverage are essential for evaluating how agricultural intensification, crop choices, and application methods have influenced P balances and runoff potential. This dataset has been central to studies that inform sustainable fertilizer strategies, guide conservation planning, and support policy development for nutrient management. Without this resource, comprehensive P budgeting at the scale needed to inform science-based solutions would be far more limited."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:068",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 69,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Global Environmental Change, Paleoceanography and Paleoclimatology, Biogeoscience"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "Annual crop-specific management history of phosphorus fertilizer input (CMH-P) in the croplands of United States from 1850 to 2022: Application rate, timing, and method"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Cao, Peiyu; Yi, Bo; Bilotto, Franco; Gonzalez Fischer, Carlos; Herrero, Mario; Lu, Chaoqun"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13357690"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "Zenodo"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13357690"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Peiyu Cao"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "This dataset presents spatiotemporal dynamics of phosphorus (P) fertilizer management (application rate, timing, and method) at a 4km × 4 km resolution in agricultural land of the contiguous U.S. from 1850 to 2022. By harmonizing multiple data sources, the county-level crop-specific P fertilizer use history was reconstructed. P fertilizer use data was then spatialized and resampled to 4 km × 4 km gridded maps based on historical U.S. cropland distribution and crop type database developed by Ye et al. (2024).\n\nThis dataset contains (1) P fertilizer total consumption and mean application rate at the national level (Tabular); (2) P fertilizer consumption of 11 crops at the state level (Tabular); (3) P fertilizer consumption of permanent pasture (Tabular); (4) P fertilizer consumption of non-farm at the state level (Tabular); (5) P fertilizer application rate of 11 crop types at the state level (Tabular); (6) P fertilizer application rate of 11 crop types at the county level (Tabular); (7) P fertilizer application timing ratio at the state level (Tabular); (8) P fertilizer application method ratio at the state level (Tabular); (9) Gridded maps of P fertilizer application rate based on state-level data; (10) and (11) Gridded maps of P fertilizer application rate based on county-level data; (12)-(20) Gridded maps of P fertilizer application rate for each crop."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "This dataset represents a major advancement in understanding agricultural phosphorus (P) management across the contiguous United States, providing the first long-term, high-resolution, crop-specific maps of P fertilizer application rates, timing, and methods. Previous datasets were either derived from sales data, offering coarse county-level averages without crop differentiation, or based on farmer surveys that captured crop-level practices but missed spatial variability between states. By reconciling these complementary sources, this work delivers a comprehensive 4 km × 4 km gridded dataset covering 1850–2022, filling a critical gap in quantifying the evolution of phosphorus fertilization practices over more than 170 years.\n\nPeople:\nThis resource supports education, research training, and evidence-based decision-making, enabling students, researchers, and extension specialists to explore how agricultural intensification, fertilizer strategies, and land-use changes have shaped soil fertility and environmental outcomes. By characterizing fertilizer timing and application methods, the dataset aids development of predictive tools to identify areas at risk for nutrient loss, harmful algal blooms, and soil degradation, protecting the health and livelihoods of rural and downstream communities.\n\nPlanet:\nPhosphorus is a non-renewable resource, and its mismanagement contributes significantly to water quality deterioration and ecosystem damage. This dataset provides essential inputs for agricultural, watershed, and Earth system models, allowing scientists to quantify P budgets, trace sources of runoff and eutrophication, and evaluate regional shifts in P management practices over time. Its unprecedented temporal depth and fine spatial resolution enable robust assessments of phosphorus footprints, informing conservation planning and restoration efforts that target nutrient pollution hotspots. Without this dataset, long-term trends in phosphorus inputs and their environmental consequences would remain fragmented and poorly understood.\n\nProsperity:\nThe dataset provides a foundation for more efficient and equitable nutrient management policies, reducing fertilizer waste, economic losses, and environmental costs. By mapping regional and crop-specific fertilization practices, it enables tailored recommendations that enhance farmer profitability, soil health, and climate resilience. It supports long-term planning for sustainable phosphorus use, helping safeguard this critical nutrient for future food security and global agricultural productivity.\n\nIn summary, this dataset delivers an unprecedented view of phosphorus fertilization patterns in the U.S., integrating multiple data sources to provide a century-scale, spatially explicit foundation for advancing sustainable nutrient management. Without it, efforts to optimize phosphorus use efficiency, mitigate water pollution, and sustain agricultural productivity would lack the historical and spatial context needed for transformative change."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator uses this dataset to conduct detailed phosphorus (P) budget and surplus analyses across the United States, leveraging its fine spatial and temporal resolution to understand long-term trends in fertilizer use and nutrient dynamics. By integrating this dataset with crop production, land use, and soil data, they can quantify regional and national P surpluses, track shifts in fertilizer management practices over time, and identify hotspots of nutrient inefficiency and environmental risk.\n\nIts crop-specific detail and century-scale coverage are essential for evaluating how agricultural intensification, crop choices, and application methods have influenced P balances and runoff potential. This dataset has been central to studies that inform sustainable fertilizer strategies, guide conservation planning, and support policy development for nutrient management. Without this resource, comprehensive P budgeting at the scale needed to inform science-based solutions would be far more limited."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Cao, P., Yi, B., Bilotto, F., Gonzalez Fischer, C., Herrero, M., & Lu, C. (2024). Crop-specific management history of phosphorus fertilizer input (Cmh-p) in the croplands of the United States: Reconciliation of top-down and bottom-up data sources. Earth System Science Data, 16(10), 4557–4572. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-16-4557-2024"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Peiyu Cao"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Email Address",
          "schema:value": "caopeyu@gmail.com"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator ORCID",
          "schema:value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5032-1991"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Affiliation or Organization",
          "schema:value": "Cornell University, Ithaca, NY 14853"
        }
      ]
