        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "This dataset presents a global spatially explicit cropland management practices and associated greenhouse gas emissions at a 5 arcminute resolution circa 2020.\n\nBy integrating national and sub-national census surveys, international reports, and literature, we reconstruct synthetic nitrogen fertilizer datasets, depicting the sub-national variability in application rate and fertilizer type across crops. To develop the methodology for estimating the manure application and crop residue handling (incorporation and in-field burning) data, we established a crucial linkage between cropland and livestock production systems through supply-demand of feed and crop nutrients. Our manure application estimates accounted for animal-specific age structure and performance, constrained by feed quality. We determined the usages of crop residues for different purposes by balancing the availability of crop production and feed demand of livestock systems. Additionally, we compiled multiple sources on rainfed and irrigated rice systems to represent global variations in water regime management. The spatial overlap crop-specific physical areas and peatland extent allowed us to quantify the area of drained peatland utilized for crop cultivation. Our integrated cropland management dataset serves as a robust framework to analyze emission variability across countries, crops, and GHG sources, reflecting differences in climatic conditions, land use patterns, and agricultural practices. We applied the IPCC 2019 refinement and IPCC 2006 guidelines to estimate cropland emissions circa 2020 from a variety of sources, including direct and indirect N2O emissions from synthetic fertilizer, manure application, and crop residue incorporation; CO2 emissions from urea application; CH4 emissions from rice paddy; CO2, N2O, and CH4 emissions from drained peatland for cultivation; and CH4 and N2O emissions from crop residue in-field burning."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "This dataset provides the first globally consistent, spatially explicit representation of cropland management practices and their associated greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions at a fine 5-arcminute resolution circa 2020. It integrates a wide range of national and subnational agricultural census data, international reports, and peer-reviewed literature to reconstruct comprehensive, crop-specific estimates of synthetic fertilizer use, manure application, crop residue management, rice water regimes, and cropland peatland extent. By explicitly linking crop production with livestock feed demand and nutrient cycles, the dataset captures essential interactions between crop and livestock systems that are often missing in global emissions inventories.\n\nPeople:\nThis dataset is a foundational resource for education, capacity building, and agricultural decision-making. Its high-resolution representation of cropland management practices allows researchers, students, and policymakers to explore how fertilizer use, manure handling, and crop residue practices vary between countries and regions. It supports training in emissions modeling and agricultural systems analysis, while providing actionable insights for governments and development organizations seeking to meet climate mitigation goals and improve food system sustainability.\n\nPlanet:\nAgriculture is a leading source of GHG emissions, yet emission variability driven by crop choice, livestock integration, climate, and land use remains poorly quantified at fine spatial scales. This dataset addresses that gap by applying the latest IPCC 2019 Refinement and 2006 Guidelines to estimate emissions of CO₂, CH₄, and N₂O from multiple sources, including fertilizer, manure, rice paddies, peatland cultivation, and residue burning. Its consistent, spatially explicit framework enables scientists to better understand emissions drivers, evaluate mitigation opportunities, and quantify agriculture’s role in climate change and ecosystem health. Without this dataset, global assessments of agricultural GHG emissions would remain coarse, fragmented, and unable to reflect regional management differences.\n\nProsperity:\nThe dataset offers a robust evidence base for designing region-specific solutions that enhance agricultural productivity while reducing emissions. By identifying hotspots of inefficient nutrient use, residue burning, or peatland drainage, it informs policies that improve economic outcomes for farmers, strengthen community resilience, and promote sustainable land management. It also supports international reporting, carbon accounting, and climate finance mechanisms by providing harmonized, transparent, and reproducible emissions data.\n\nIn summary, this dataset delivers an unprecedented, high-resolution view of global cropland management and its climate impacts. It is an indispensable tool for researchers, policymakers, and practitioners seeking to develop targeted mitigation strategies, balance food security with environmental goals, and track progress toward global sustainability commitments."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator led the development of this dataset, integrating diverse agricultural, livestock, and environmental data sources to create the first globally consistent, high-resolution representation of cropland management practices and greenhouse gas emissions. They designed and implemented the methodology for linking crop and livestock nutrient cycles, reconstructing manure application rates, fertilizer use, and residue management practices, and incorporated detailed data on rice water regimes and peatland cultivation.\n\nThey also conducted a comprehensive global analysis of cropland emission patterns, quantifying the contributions of multiple sources—including fertilizer, manure, rice paddies, peatlands, and residue burning—across crops, regions, and emission types. This dataset has become the foundation for climate modeling, mitigation scenario assessment, and sustainable agriculture research, and it provides actionable insights to policymakers and researchers working toward emissions reduction and food system resilience."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Cao, P., Bilotto, F., Fischer, C. G., Mueller, N., Carlson, K., Gerber, J., Smith, P., Tubiello, F., West, P., You, L., & Herrero, M. (2025). Mapping greenhouse gas emissions from global cropland circa 2020. https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-6622054/v1"
