        "id:theme:global-environmental-change-paleoceanography-and-paleoclimatology-biogeoscience"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Xueyuan Gao, Peter B. Reich, Jeffrey R. Vincent, Matthew E. Fagan, Robin L. Chazdon, Steffen Fritz, Dmitry Schepaschenko, Matthew D. Potts, Matthew C. Hansen, Martin Jung, Pedro H. S. Brancalion, María Uriarte, Trevor F. Keenan, Thomas W. Crowther, Ralph O. Dubayah, Myroslava Lesiv, Shunlin Liang, Dongdong Wang"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://code.earthengine.google.com/a8ab0a204422bdaf13bd1eff4bc0a5ea"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:google-earth-engine"
      ],
      "description": "The tropical moist forest restoration type map characterizes different forest restoration types on former agricultural lands (croplands and pasturelands) in the global moist tropics during 1982–2015: natural forest regrowth, timber plantations, oil palm plantations, and agroforestry. It, for the first time, provides spatially explicit information with high spatial resolution (100-m) about not only where new trees are growing, but also the types of forest systems being restored in the global moist tropics. \nIt was co-developed by 18 researchers from over 15 universities and research institutes including Princeton University (where the lead author is based in), University of Maryland, University of Michigan, Duke University, University of California, Berkeley, Columbia University, ETH Zurich, and International Institute for Applied Systems Analysis (IIASA). The map and its accompanying research paper (Gao et al. The importance of distinguishing between natural and managed tree cover gains in the moist tropics) were published in Nature Communications in July 2025 and received the 2025 Ecological Society of America Early Career Ecologists Outstanding Paper Award. The map in the original 100-m resolution is hosted on Google Earth Engine and is freely available for viewing and download. \n(dataset website: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-025-59196-1)",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "Gao, X., et al. (2025). The importance of distinguishing between natural and managed tree cover gains in the moist tropics. Nature Communications, 16(1), 6092. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-59196-1",
          "identifier": [
            {
              "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
              "propertyID": "DOI",
              "value": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-59196-1"
            }
          ],
          "schema:url": [
            "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-59196-1"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2997-2332",
