          "schema:value": "The nominated dataset, Tropical moist forest restoration type map, has made an impact on the Plant dimension. \nThe map was developed from 2023-2025 by using a novel satellite-observation-based algorithm capable of distinguishing different types of tree cover restoration—specifically natural regeneration, plantations, and agroforestry—across the global moist tropics. While previous studies have effectively tracked changes in tree cover using satellite imagery, traditional methods have not been able to identify the underlying drivers of tree cover gain. This new map addresses this critical gap.\nThis distinction is essential, as the ecological and social impacts of restoration practices vary widely. For instance, industrial plantations often sequester carbon more rapidly than natural regeneration but can negatively affect biodiversity and habitat quality. By enabling the classification of restoration types, the algorithm and resulted map provide a valuable tool for assessing the effectiveness and ecological integrity of restoration efforts.\nGiven the increasing number of national commitments to forest restoration under Nationally Determined Contributions, the map offers a timely and impactful tool to measure progress toward these goals and evaluate whether restoration efforts are leading to healthier, more resilient ecosystems—which remains the ultimate goal. Researchers in the conservation and restoration communities have started to integrate the map with ground-based documentation to further support the monitoring and evaluation of landscape restoration initiatives.\nAs nature-based carbon removal credits gain traction in carbon markets, the map has also supported the selection of control plots and informed dynamic baselines, helped verify registered reforestation project types, and assisted in estimating potential project leakage. Together, the map has contributed to build greater transparency, accuracy, and environmental integrity in the voluntary carbon market. \nThe map was accessed over 10k times within the first month of its release and is expected to continue making a significant impact within the conservation and restoration communities."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator is the lead author of this dataset and currently a postdoctoral researcher at Princeton University. The development of this dataset began during their PhD studies at the University of Maryland and spanned nearly two years. For them, it was a highly collaborative effort, and it has been a rewarding experience working alongside 17 scientists from over 15 universities and research institutions. To ensure the accuracy of the map, the team conducted an independent accuracy assessment over the course of three months, following the rigorous methodology outlined by Olofsson et al. Since the publication of the map and accompanying work, the team has received a large amount of interest from both the academic research community and stakeholders in the carbon market. At present, the dataset includes a single map representing forest restoration types as of 2015. They plan to expand its temporal coverage by producing additional maps for subsequent years, which will further support the monitoring and evaluation of landscape restoration initiatives. If the dataset was selected in the upcoming AGU commentary, we would be able to get our dataset reach a broader audience and gather more feedback, which would be helpful to build an updated version of this dataset."
        },
