    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:072",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:072",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:072",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8151-4684"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The Cenozoic Global Reference Benthic Carbon and Oxygen Isotope Dataset (CENOGRID) represents a significant advance in the field of climate science, enabling researchers to compile a continuous, high-fidelity record of variations in Earth's climate extending 66 million years into the past. This development marks a remarkable milestone in the field, allowing scientists to make unprecedented observations and advances in their understanding of climate dynamics over long periods of time. The record indicates the presence of four distinct climate states, dubbed Hothouse, Warmhouse, Coolhouse, and Icehouse. More than 5 decades of scientific ocean drilling and collaborative research across multiple international entities was needed to enable the reconstruction of the fine-scale rhythmic variability of past climates. CENOGRID effectively captures natural climate variability and shows that projected anthropogenic warming will significantly exceed it. It shows that Earth’s climate has fluctuated dramatically on scales of millions of years, but we are now entering a new and uncertain phase, with rising anthropogenic greenhouse emissions and shrinking ice sheets. The current rates of change are unprecedented compared to the long-term history of Cenozoic climate.\nThe compiled, continuous, astronomically dated climate record of the past 66 million years shows that the response of the climate to orbital variations depends on factors such as greenhouse gas concentrations and the extent of polar ice sheets. In fact, most of the significant climate transitions that have occurred over the past 66 million years are associated with variations in greenhouse gas concentrations.\nThe CENOGRID climate record provides a valuable framework for many areas of research. It is useful not only for testing climate models, but also for geophysicists studying various aspects of Earth dynamics, and for paleontologists studying how changing environments drive the evolution of species. CENOGRID is a major advance in Earth science and an important legacy of the international scientific ocean drilling programs."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator is the lead author of the paper that published the dataset. In order to understand the timescales and dynamics of how different regions of the Earth respond to, and recover from, anthropogenic climate change, it is necessary to study climate records from the geological past. Scientific ocean drilling, as well as a smaller number of land-based records, provides sediments that have been crucial in reconstructing past ocean environmental conditions, such as temperature, circulation, productivity, oxygenation, pH and carbon storage, over the past ~100 million years. It is crucial to quantify the rates at which the Earth's abiotic and biotic systems respond to and recover from climate shifts. CENOGRID serves as a global reference timekeeper for regional climate records.\nBased on CENOGRID, the geoscience community (currently comprising around 300 active scientists organised into 10 working groups) is launching the Time Integrated Matrix for Earth Sciences (TIMES) initiative. Further background information can be found in the white paper Timing is Everything: https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1029/2024PA004932.\nTIMES is envisaged as an ambitious ten-year effort that aims to recalibrate global climate records from the last 100 million years on a unified, accurate and precise timeline. The ultimate goal is to improve models for future climate pathways by understanding past climate perturbations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:072",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 73,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Global Environmental Change, Paleoceanography and Paleoclimatology, Biogeoscience"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "Cenozoic Global Reference benthic foraminifer carbon and oxygen Isotope Dataset (CENOGRID)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Thomas Westerhold, Norbert Marwan, Anna Joy Drury, Diederik Liebrand, Claudia Agnini, Eleni Anagnostou, James S. K. Barnet, Steven M. Bohaty, David De Vleeschouwer, Fabio Florindo, Thomas Frederichs, David A. Hodell, Ann E. Holbourn, Dick Kroon, Vittoria Lauretano, Kate Littler, Lucas J. Lourens, Mitchell Lyle, Heiko Pälike, Ursula Röhl, Jun Tian, Roy H. Wilkens, Paul A. Wilson, James C. Zachos"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.pangaea.de/10.1594/PANGAEA.917503"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "World Data Center PANGAEA"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "Full data set Cenozoic global reference benthic carbon and oxygen isotope dataset (CENOGRID). PANGAEA (2020); https://doi.pangaea.de/10.1594/PANGAEA.917503; CENOGRID Cenozoic deep-sea benthic foraminifer stable carbon and oxygen isotope reference splice [dataset]. PANGAEA, https://doi.org/10.1594/PANGAEA.917660; CENOGRID Binned and LOESS smoothed Binned and LOESS smoothed Cenozoic deep-sea benthic foraminifer stable carbon and oxygen isotope reference splice [dataset]. PANGAEA, https://doi.org/10.1594/PANGAEA.917717"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "Much of the scientific community’s understanding of Earth’s past climate states comes from measurements of oxygen and carbon isotope variations in deep-sea benthic foraminifera. Yet, major intervals in those records that lack the temporal resolution and/or age control required to identify climate forcing and feedback mechanisms. Here, 66 million years of global climate are documented using a new high-fidelity Cenozoic global reference dataset of benthic carbon and oxygen isotopes (CENOGRID). Using recurrence analysis, Earth’s climate on million-year timescales can be grouped into Hothouse, Warmhouse, Coolhouse, and Icehouse states, separated by transitions related to changing greenhouse gas levels and the growth of polar ice sheets. Each Cenozoic climate state is paced by orbital cycles, but the response to radiative forcing is state dependent."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The Cenozoic Global Reference Benthic Carbon and Oxygen Isotope Dataset (CENOGRID) represents a significant advance in the field of climate science, enabling researchers to compile a continuous, high-fidelity record of variations in Earth's climate extending 66 million years into the past. This development marks a remarkable milestone in the field, allowing scientists to make unprecedented observations and advances in their understanding of climate dynamics over long periods of time. The record indicates the presence of four distinct climate states, dubbed Hothouse, Warmhouse, Coolhouse, and Icehouse. More than 5 decades of scientific ocean drilling and collaborative research across multiple international entities was needed to enable the reconstruction of the fine-scale rhythmic variability of past climates. CENOGRID effectively captures natural climate variability and shows that projected anthropogenic warming will significantly exceed it. It shows that Earth’s climate has fluctuated dramatically on scales of millions of years, but we are now entering a new and uncertain phase, with rising anthropogenic greenhouse emissions and shrinking ice sheets. The current rates of change are unprecedented compared to the long-term history of Cenozoic climate.\nThe compiled, continuous, astronomically dated climate record of the past 66 million years shows that the response of the climate to orbital variations depends on factors such as greenhouse gas concentrations and the extent of polar ice sheets. In fact, most of the significant climate transitions that have occurred over the past 66 million years are associated with variations in greenhouse gas concentrations.\nThe CENOGRID climate record provides a valuable framework for many areas of research. It is useful not only for testing climate models, but also for geophysicists studying various aspects of Earth dynamics, and for paleontologists studying how changing environments drive the evolution of species. CENOGRID is a major advance in Earth science and an important legacy of the international scientific ocean drilling programs."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator is the lead author of the paper that published the dataset. In order to understand the timescales and dynamics of how different regions of the Earth respond to, and recover from, anthropogenic climate change, it is necessary to study climate records from the geological past. Scientific ocean drilling, as well as a smaller number of land-based records, provides sediments that have been crucial in reconstructing past ocean environmental conditions, such as temperature, circulation, productivity, oxygenation, pH and carbon storage, over the past ~100 million years. It is crucial to quantify the rates at which the Earth's abiotic and biotic systems respond to and recover from climate shifts. CENOGRID serves as a global reference timekeeper for regional climate records.\nBased on CENOGRID, the geoscience community (currently comprising around 300 active scientists organised into 10 working groups) is launching the Time Integrated Matrix for Earth Sciences (TIMES) initiative. Further background information can be found in the white paper Timing is Everything: https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1029/2024PA004932.\nTIMES is envisaged as an ambitious ten-year effort that aims to recalibrate global climate records from the last 100 million years on a unified, accurate and precise timeline. The ultimate goal is to improve models for future climate pathways by understanding past climate perturbations."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Westerhold, T., et al. (2020). An astronomically dated record of Earth’s climate and its predictability over the last 66 million years. Science, 369(6509), 1383–1387. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aba6853"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "3 examples – there are many more\n\n1) Comparing Global mean surface temperature (GMST) to CO2 reconstructions\nhttps://paleo-co2.org\nThe CenCO2PIP Consortium. (2023). Towards a Cenozoic History of Atmospheric CO2, Science, 382(6675), eadi5177. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adi5177 \n\n2) Vienna Natural History Museum\nPart of the Hall 6 - The Earth – a dynamic Planet permanent Exhibition \nThe \"fever curve\" of the Earth\nhttps://www.nhm.at/en/exhibitions/permanent_exhibitions/mezzanine_level/hall_6-9_earth_history , then click Rocks as climate archives\n\n3) IPCC Sixth Assessment Report - Climate Change 2021: The Physical Science Basis\n\nIntergovernmental Panel On Climate Change (Ipcc). (2023). Climate Change 2021 – The Physical Science Basis: Working Group I Contribution to the Sixth Assessment Report of the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (1st ed.). Cambridge University Press. https://doi.org/10.1017/9781009157896 \n\nPage 296 Cross-Chapter Box 2.1, Figure 1 \nhttps://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg1/figures/chapter-2/ccbox-2-1-figure-1\nGlobal mean surface temperature (GMST) over the past 60 million years (60 Myr) relative to 1850–1900 shown on three time scales."
        },
