        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "Since 1984, aboveground biomass and plant density have been measured monthly and non-destructively in a salt marsh at North Inlet, South Carolina. This dataset is the centerpiece of an NSF LTREB project that investigates how salt marsh primary production and sediment chemistry respond to both anthropogenic (e.g., eutrophication) and natural (e.g., sea-level rise) drivers of environmental change. Core measurements include plant heights, stem densities, and snail abundances, from which aboveground biomass and productivity are calculated using allometric models and changes in tagged stems in permanent plots. Ancillary data include porewater nutrient concentrations, marsh surface elevation, and flooding frequency. Together, these records provide an unparalleled view of ecosystem processes and plant–sediment feedbacks that regulate marsh resilience, stability, and equilibrium with rising sea level."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "This dataset is unique for its longevity (40 years), frequency (monthly sampling), and integration with ancillary measures that provide a mechanistic understanding of salt marsh dynamics. Few ecological datasets of comparable scale and continuity exist, and without it, many foundational insights into coastal wetland resilience to sea-level rise would not have been possible.\n\nIts impact spans the three AGU “dimensions”:\n\nPlanet: The dataset demonstrates how salt marsh primary production is tightly coupled to relative sea level. Analyses show that feedbacks among vegetation growth, sediment accretion, and flooding maintain marsh elevation within a narrow tidal range. These insights have reshaped understanding of wetland vulnerability, identifying thresholds and tipping points that determine whether marshes persist, migrate, or collapse under rising seas.\n\nPeople: Salt marshes provide essential ecosystem services, including storm buffering, fisheries support, and blue carbon sequestration. This dataset has been central to predictive models used in coastal management, restoration, and climate adaptation planning. Its results are directly relevant to disaster preparedness and the long-term sustainability of coastal communities.\n\nProsperity: By quantifying carbon sequestration and resilience, the work underpins economic valuation of wetlands in carbon markets and informs policy frameworks for conservation, restoration, and climate mitigation.\n\nThe influence of the dataset is evident in Morris et al. (2002), cited over 2,000 times, and in subsequent widely used publications. Core generalities derived include:\n\nFeedbacks between primary production and elevation drive marshes toward equilibrium with mean sea level.\n\nElevation and productivity are constrained by the rate of sea-level rise.\n\nMaximum production and carbon sequestration occur near an optimum elevation, which also represents a tipping point beyond which marshes lose resilience.\n\nThese findings continue to shape research agendas, management strategies, and policy discourse. Without these long-term data, our capacity to predict marsh responses to accelerating sea-level rise—and to safeguard their ecological and societal value—would be fundamentally limited."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator has worked with this dataset for over three decades. It has been central to their research program. Beyond direct analyses, it has informed conceptual models of marsh resilience such as the Marsh Equilibrium Model (Morris et al 2002) that have become widely adopted in coastal science. This dataset is the origin for much of their professional scholarship."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Morris, J.T., & Haskin, B. (1990). A 5-yr record of aerial primary production and stand characteristics of Spartina alterniflora. Ecology, 71(6), 2209–2217. http://www.jstor.org/stable/1938633 \n\nMorris, J.T., Sundareshwar, P.V., Nietch, C.T., Kjerfve, B., & Cahoon, D.R. (2002). Responses of coastal wetlands to rising sea level. Ecology, 83(10), 2869–2877. \nhttps://doi.org/10.1890/0012-9658(2002)083[2869:ROCWTR]2.0.CO;2 \n\nMorris, J.T., Sundberg, K., & Hopkinson, C.S. (2013). Salt marsh primary production and its responses to relative sea level and nutrients in estuaries at Plum Island, Massachusetts, and North Inlet, South Carolina, USA. Oceanography, 26(3), 78–84. https://doi.org/10.5670/oceanog.2013.48 \n\nMorris, J.T., & Sundberg, K.L. (2024). Responses of coastal wetlands to rising sea level revisited: The importance of organic production. Estuaries and Coasts, 47(7), 1735–1749. https://doi.org/10.1007/s12237-023-01313-8 \n\nMorris, J. T., & Sundberg, K. (2025). The orchestration and harmonics of biogeochemical cycles in a southeastern usa saltmarsh. Journal of Geophysical Research: Biogeosciences, 130(12), e2025JG009005. https://doi.org/10.1029/2025JG009005"
        },
