      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The MIRCA-OS dataset fills a critical global knowledge gap by providing spatially and temporally detailed maps of irrigated and rainfed cropped areas for 26 crop classes from 2000–2015 at ~10 km resolution. Without it, researchers, policymakers, and practitioners would lack a consistent, crop-specific record of agricultural water use and production patterns over the early 21st century — a period of major changes in global food systems and water scarcity.\n\nPeople: MIRCA-OS enables advances in agricultural education and training by offering openly accessible, high-resolution data for classroom instruction, student research, and capacity building in resource-limited regions. It informs disaster response and prediction, as changes in irrigated and rainfed agriculture are directly linked to drought vulnerability, food security crises, and humanitarian interventions. By tracking crop calendars and growing areas, it supports early-warning systems for yield losses and supply disruptions.\n\nPlanet: The dataset is indispensable for understanding geophysical and environmental processes at the agriculture–water–climate nexus. It quantifies irrigation’s role in altering freshwater availability, groundwater depletion, and hydrological cycles, while also helping identify regions of sustainable expansion. MIRCA-OS underpins conservation planning by showing where cropland expansion pressures natural ecosystems, and it feeds directly into climate impact models, carbon accounting, and global environmental indicator frameworks.\n\nProsperity: MIRCA-OS contributes to economic good and social equity by enabling better-informed decisions on water allocation, crop choices, and agricultural investments. It supports community resilience by identifying cropping systems and regions most at risk from climate change and resource scarcity, guiding targeted adaptation strategies. Its open-source nature empowers researchers, governments, NGOs, and local planners worldwide to generate custom analyses at any scale, without proprietary barriers.\n\nWithout MIRCA-OS, global-scale models of food production, water scarcity, and climate adaptation would remain reliant on outdated (circa 2000) or coarse-resolution data that fail to capture the complexity and diversity of modern agricultural systems. The dataset’s integration of official statistics with spatial downscaling ensures both accuracy and usability for applications ranging from local irrigation planning to international policy.\n\nIn sum, MIRCA-OS advances knowledge, informs practice, and fosters collaboration across disciplines and sectors — making it a foundational resource for sustainable agriculture, water management, and climate resilience in the 21st century.",
          "impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "People",
              "schema:text": "MIRCA-OS enables advances in agricultural education and training by offering openly accessible, high-resolution data for classroom instruction, student research, and capacity building in resource-limited regions. It informs disaster response and prediction, as changes in irrigated and rainfed agriculture are directly linked to drought vulnerability, food security crises, and humanitarian interventions. By tracking crop calendars and growing areas, it supports early-warning systems for yield losses and supply disruptions."
            },
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "Planet",
              "schema:text": "The dataset is indispensable for understanding geophysical and environmental processes at the agriculture–water–climate nexus. It quantifies irrigation’s role in altering freshwater availability, groundwater depletion, and hydrological cycles, while also helping identify regions of sustainable expansion. MIRCA-OS underpins conservation planning by showing where cropland expansion pressures natural ecosystems, and it feeds directly into climate impact models, carbon accounting, and global environmental indicator frameworks."
            },
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "Prosperity",
              "schema:text": "MIRCA-OS contributes to economic good and social equity by enabling better-informed decisions on water allocation, crop choices, and agricultural investments. It supports community resilience by identifying cropping systems and regions most at risk from climate change and resource scarcity, guiding targeted adaptation strategies. Its open-source nature empowers researchers, governments, NGOs, and local planners worldwide to generate custom analyses at any scale, without proprietary barriers.\n\nWithout MIRCA-OS, global-scale models of food production, water scarcity, and climate adaptation would remain reliant on outdated (circa 2000) or coarse-resolution data that fail to capture the complexity and diversity of modern agricultural systems. The dataset’s integration of official statistics with spatial downscaling ensures both accuracy and usability for applications ranging from local irrigation planning to international policy.\n\nIn sum, MIRCA-OS advances knowledge, informs practice, and fosters collaboration across disciplines and sectors — making it a foundational resource for sustainable agriculture, water management, and climate resilience in the 21st century."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "As a developer of MIRCA-OS, the nominator has been deeply involved in every stage of the dataset’s creation, from assembling municipal-to-global crop statistics to designing and testing the downscaling algorithms that convert administrative records into high-resolution gridded maps. They interact with the dataset both technically and scientifically: compiling and validating diverse input sources, developing open-source code, and generating the monthly and annual products that users now access through HydroShare. They also use MIRCA-OS in their own research to evaluate spatial and temporal trends in irrigation, assess crop-specific water use, and explore links between agriculture, water stress, and climate change. This hands-on engagement has given them an appreciation for the challenges of harmonizing heterogeneous global data and the importance of open, reproducible workflows. Beyond development, they rely on MIRCA-OS to collaborate with colleagues in food and water research and support projects that model crop production impacts on water scarcity risks and the food supply chain. Working with MIRCA-OS has shown them how a carefully constructed dataset can bridge the gap between statistics and actionable knowledge, enabling analyses that were not previously possible and supporting more sustainable approaches to agriculture and water management worldwide."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:075",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 76,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Global Environmental Change, Paleoceanography and Paleoclimatology, Biogeoscience"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "A global open-source dataset of monthly irrigated and rainfed cropped areas (MIRCA-OS) for the 21st century"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Endalkachew Kebede, Kevin Ong’are Oluoch, Stefan Siebert,Piyush Mehta, Sarah Hartman,Jonas Jägermeyr, Deepak Ray, Tariq Ali, Kate A. Brauman, Qinyu Deng, Wei Xie, Kyle Frankel Davis"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://www.hydroshare.org/resource/60a890eb841c460192c03bb590687145/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "HydroShare"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.4211/hs.60a890eb841c460192c03bb590687145"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Endalkachew Abebe Kebede and Kyle Frankel Davis"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The Monthly Irrigated and Rainfed Cropped Area Open-Source (MIRCA-OS) dataset provides global gridded (5-arcminute, ~10 km) maps of irrigated and rainfed harvested areas for 23 crop classes from 2000 to 2015. Built from municipal- to national-scale statistics, updated crop calendars, and global land cover products, MIRCA-OS delivers monthly and annual representations of growing areas, maximum cropped areas, and crop calendars for both irrigated and rainfed systems. It integrates diverse international and national sources (e.g., FAOSTAT, AQUASTAT, EUROSTAT) with an improved downscaling methodology to allocate harvested areas spatially. Products are available in GeoTIFF, NetCDF, and CSV formats, along with input data sources and code, in line with FAIR principles. Supporting applications in agriculture, water resources, climate impact studies, and sustainable development, MIRCA-OS enables detailed insights into global cropland change since 2000 and provides a robust foundation for assessments of land use, food security, and sustainability."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The MIRCA-OS dataset fills a critical global knowledge gap by providing spatially and temporally detailed maps of irrigated and rainfed cropped areas for 26 crop classes from 2000–2015 at ~10 km resolution. Without it, researchers, policymakers, and practitioners would lack a consistent, crop-specific record of agricultural water use and production patterns over the early 21st century — a period of major changes in global food systems and water scarcity.\n\nPeople: MIRCA-OS enables advances in agricultural education and training by offering openly accessible, high-resolution data for classroom instruction, student research, and capacity building in resource-limited regions. It informs disaster response and prediction, as changes in irrigated and rainfed agriculture are directly linked to drought vulnerability, food security crises, and humanitarian interventions. By tracking crop calendars and growing areas, it supports early-warning systems for yield losses and supply disruptions.\n\nPlanet: The dataset is indispensable for understanding geophysical and environmental processes at the agriculture–water–climate nexus. It quantifies irrigation’s role in altering freshwater availability, groundwater depletion, and hydrological cycles, while also helping identify regions of sustainable expansion. MIRCA-OS underpins conservation planning by showing where cropland expansion pressures natural ecosystems, and it feeds directly into climate impact models, carbon accounting, and global environmental indicator frameworks.\n\nProsperity: MIRCA-OS contributes to economic good and social equity by enabling better-informed decisions on water allocation, crop choices, and agricultural investments. It supports community resilience by identifying cropping systems and regions most at risk from climate change and resource scarcity, guiding targeted adaptation strategies. Its open-source nature empowers researchers, governments, NGOs, and local planners worldwide to generate custom analyses at any scale, without proprietary barriers.\n\nWithout MIRCA-OS, global-scale models of food production, water scarcity, and climate adaptation would remain reliant on outdated (circa 2000) or coarse-resolution data that fail to capture the complexity and diversity of modern agricultural systems. The dataset’s integration of official statistics with spatial downscaling ensures both accuracy and usability for applications ranging from local irrigation planning to international policy.\n\nIn sum, MIRCA-OS advances knowledge, informs practice, and fosters collaboration across disciplines and sectors — making it a foundational resource for sustainable agriculture, water management, and climate resilience in the 21st century."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "As a developer of MIRCA-OS, the nominator has been deeply involved in every stage of the dataset’s creation, from assembling municipal-to-global crop statistics to designing and testing the downscaling algorithms that convert administrative records into high-resolution gridded maps. They interact with the dataset both technically and scientifically: compiling and validating diverse input sources, developing open-source code, and generating the monthly and annual products that users now access through HydroShare. They also use MIRCA-OS in their own research to evaluate spatial and temporal trends in irrigation, assess crop-specific water use, and explore links between agriculture, water stress, and climate change. This hands-on engagement has given them an appreciation for the challenges of harmonizing heterogeneous global data and the importance of open, reproducible workflows. Beyond development, they rely on MIRCA-OS to collaborate with colleagues in food and water research and support projects that model crop production impacts on water scarcity risks and the food supply chain. Working with MIRCA-OS has shown them how a carefully constructed dataset can bridge the gap between statistics and actionable knowledge, enabling analyses that were not previously possible and supporting more sustainable approaches to agriculture and water management worldwide."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Kebede, E. A., Oluoch, K. O., Siebert, S., Mehta, P., Hartman, S., Jägermeyr, J., Ray, D., Ali, T., Brauman, K. A., Deng, Q., Xie, W., & Davis, K. F. (2025). A global open-source dataset of monthly irrigated and rainfed cropped areas (Mirca-os) for the 21st century. Scientific Data, 12(1), 208. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-04313-w"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Hao, H., Dong, N., Yang, M., Wei, J., Zhang, X., Xu, S., Yan, D., Ren, L., Leng, G., Chen, L., Zhou, X., Wang, H., Song, L., & Kunstmann, H. (2024). The changing hydrology of an irrigated and dammed yangtze river: Streamflow, extremes, and lake hydrodynamics. Water Resources Research, 60(10), e2024WR037841. https://doi.org/10.1029/2024WR037841 \n\nYue, Z., Zhuo, L., Ji, X., Tian, P., Gao, J., Wang, W., Sun, F., Duan, Y., & Wu, P. (2025). Water-saving irrigated area expansion hardly enhances crop yield while saving water under climate scenarios in China. Communications Earth & Environment, 6(1), 295. https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-025-02279-0 \n\nZhu, W., Baumert, J., Storm, H., Heckelei, T., & Siebert, S. (2025). ECIRA - European crop-specific irrigated area at 1 km resolution annually from 2010 to 2020. Scientific Data, 12(1), 1349. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-025-05628-y \n\nLi, X., Yu, L., Du, Z., & Liu, X. (2025). Crop Statistic to Annual Map: Tracking spatiotemporal dynamics of crop-specific areas through machine learning and statistics disaggregating. Scientific Data, 12(1), 1249. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-025-05572-x \n\nRosa, L., & He, L. (2025). Global multi-model projections of green water scarcity risks in rainfed agriculture under 1.5 °C and 3 °C warming. Agricultural Water Management, 314, 109519. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.agwat.2025.109519 \n\nZhu, W., Yang, T., Wang, J., & Rezaei, E. E. (2025). Decoding cropland mask effects on the explanatory power of remote sensing and reanalyzed climate data on yield anomalies in africa. Earth’s Future, 13(6), e2024EF005443. https://doi.org/10.1029/2024EF005443 \n\nIizumi, T., Ono, Y., Takimoto, T., & Chaogejilatu. (2025). Crop phenology data extraction from research papers using a large language model. Journal of Agricultural Meteorology, 81(2), 112–116. https://doi.org/10.2480/agrmet.D-24-00042"
        },
