        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The Surface Biology and Geology (SBG) High Frequency Time Series (SHIFT) was an airborne and field campaign that occurred over several weeks in 2022 in support of NASA's SBG mission. Its study area included a 640-square-mile (1,656-square-kilometer) region in Santa Barbara County and the coastal Pacific waters. \n\nThe primary goal of the SHIFT campaign was to collect a repeated dense time series of airborne visible to shortwave infrared (VSWIR) airborne imaging spectroscopy data with coincident field measurements in both inland terrestrial and coastal aquatic areas, supported in part by a broad team of research collaborators at academic institutions. \n\nThe SHIFT campaign leveraged NASA's Airborne Visible-Infrared Imaging Spectrometer-Next Generation (AVIRIS-NG) facility instrument to collect approximately weekly VSWIR imagery across the study area. (https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/data/projects/shift)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The Surface Biology and Geology (SBG) High-Frequency Time Series (SHIFT) dataset was nominated because it represents a pivotal advance in Earth observation science, capturing the first-ever dense sub-seasonal hyperspectral record of terrestrial and aquatic ecosystems. Without SHIFT, the empirical foundation needed to understand how biological and biophysical processes evolve at the critical timescales—days to weeks—when ecosystems respond to stress, disturbance, and climate variability would be lacking.\n\nPeople – Education, Training, and Disaster Readiness\nSHIFT stands out as a model for open, inclusive, and capacity-building science. From its inception, the campaign prioritized participation by early career researchers, graduate and undergraduate students, and postdoctoral scholars. More than 40 emerging scientists from universities and research organizations were trained in cutting-edge hyperspectral techniques, field sampling, and cloud-based analytics. This hands-on experience has created a new generation of experts ready to interpret and apply spectroscopy from NASA’s upcoming SBG mission and other global imaging platforms. Beyond research, the dataset’s accessibility through open-source and cloud-based environments democratizes learning and analysis, making advanced remote sensing tools available to all. The weekly timescale of SHIFT also provides a unique training resource for predicting and managing ecological disturbances—such as drought impacts, wildfire recovery, and invasive species spread—through rapid-response science.\n\nPlanet – Understanding Ecosystem and Climate Dynamics\nSHIFT fills a crucial observational gap in our understanding of Earth system processes. By capturing visible-to-shortwave infrared spectroscopy every week across a growing season, the dataset reveals fine-grained ecological rhythms—such as leaf hydration, flowering dynamics, canopy chemistry, and biodiversity shifts—that multispectral sensors cannot resolve. These insights illuminate how plants and landscapes respond to stress, enabling better forecasting of drought resilience, carbon uptake, and post-fire recovery. SHIFT’s integration of terrestrial, coastal, and atmospheric measurements establishes a benchmark for linking high-frequency spectroscopy to broader climate and environmental indicators, directly informing conservation and sustainability science.\n\nProsperity – Economic and Societal Benefit\nBy accelerating the translation of hyperspectral science into actionable information, SHIFT supports sustainable land management, agricultural optimization, and ecosystem service valuation. The data and cloud tools developed through SHIFT are helping build community resilience by enabling more accurate monitoring of water use, vegetation stress, and habitat health—key inputs for local resource managers, policymakers, and humanitarian responders. The project’s open-access framework and cross-sector collaboration embody NASA’s Open Science ideals, ensuring equitable participation in high-value Earth data and advancing global scientific prosperity.\n\nIn essence, the SHIFT dataset bridges the gap between discovery and action. It empowers people through training and open access, enhances planetary stewardship through novel ecological insight, and contributes to prosperity through innovation and resilience. Without SHIFT, our capacity to understand, predict, and manage sub-seasonal ecological change—and to prepare for the future of high-frequency spaceborne spectroscopy—would be fundamentally limited."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "As the terrestrial data collection lead for the SHIFT campaign, the nominator coordinated a large team of primarily early career scientists to gather the in situ measurements essential for interpreting high-frequency airborne hyperspectral data. Over 13 weeks, they captured detailed vegetation and ecosystem metrics across Santa Barbara County, linking AVIRIS-NG imagery to ground truth. SHIFT built an exceptional community of emerging scientists, providing graduate students, postdocs, and undergraduates with hands-on experience in field methods, data management, and remote sensing analysis. Workshops, virtual meetings, and rotating field teams enabled participants to develop independent research projects and lasting professional networks. Scientifically, SHIFT exemplifies Earth science across scales and disciplines, integrating terrestrial and airborne data to reveal sub-seasonal dynamics of plant physiology, phenology, and ecosystem function—insights unattainable with traditional approaches. The nominator's role combined scientific leadership, mentorship, and cross-scale integration, ensuring high-quality data collection while fostering the next generation of Earth scientists. SHIFT’s lasting impact lies in both its unique dataset and the thriving community now leveraging hyperspectral observations for ecological discovery, conservation, and applied management."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Chadwick, K. D., Davis, F., Miner, K. R., Pavlick, R., Reynolds, M., Townsend, P. A., Brodrick, P. G., Ade, C., Allen, J., Anderegg, L., Angel, Y., Boving, I., Byrd, K. B., Campbell, P., Carberry, L., Cavanaugh, K. C., Cavanaugh, K. C., Easterday, K., Eckert, R., … Schimel, D. (2025). Unlocking ecological insights from sub‐seasonal visible‐to‐shortwave infrared imaging spectroscopy: The SHIFT campaign. Ecosphere, 16(3), e70194. https://doi.org/10.1002/ecs2.70194"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Angel, Y., Raiho, A., Kathuria, D., Chadwick, K. D., Brodrick, P. G., Lang, E., Ochoa, F., & Shiklomanov, A. N. (2025). Deciphering the spectra of flowers to map landscape‐scale blooming dynamics. Ecosphere, 16(2), e70127. https://doi.org/10.1002/ecs2.70127 \n\nSilva, G. D., Roberts, D. A., Byrd, K. B., Chadwick, K. D., Walker, I. J., & King, J. Y. (2025). Using imaging spectroscopy and elevation in machine learning to estimate soil salinity in intermittently tidal wetlands. Ecosphere, 16(8), e70356. https://doi.org/10.1002/ecs2.70356 \n\nCawse‐Nicholson, K., Chadwick, K. D., Brodrick, P. G., Kiper, M., Thompson, D. R., Schimel, D., Miller, C. E., Townsend, P. A., Alves, L. F., Shiklomanov, A. N., Cho, M. A., Ramoelo, A., Tsele, P., Majozi, N., Pierrat, Z. A., & Ferrier, S. (2025). Intrinsic dimensionality as a metric for temporal plant diversity evaluation: Case study from the SHIFT campaign. Ecosphere, 16(4), e70213. https://doi.org/10.1002/ecs2.70213 \n\nBoving, I., Allen, J., Brodrick, P. G., Chadwick, K. D., Trugman, A., & Anderegg, L. D. L. (2025). The unstable relationship between drought status and leaf water content complicates the remote sensing of tree drought stress. Global Change Biology, 31(4), e70188. https://doi.org/10.1111/gcb.70188"
        },
