          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "Alexandru T. Codilean and Henry Munack"
        }
      ],
      "description": "OCTOPUS is an open, web-based geochronological database that makes published Quaternary and landscape-evolution data easier to find, query, visualise and reuse. It brings together cosmogenic radionuclide measurements, especially Be-10 and Al-26, luminescence ages, radiocarbon ages and denudation-rate data from studies of erosional landscapes, fluvial basins, glacial landforms, depositional environments, palaeoenvironmental archives and archaeological records.\n\nThe database is compliant with Open Geospatial Consortium standards, so its data can be accessed through the OCTOPUS web interface and compatible desktop GIS software. In addition to analytical results, OCTOPUS stores metadata such as bibliographic details, sample context, preparation and measurement information, and associated geospatial layers. For cosmogenic-nuclide denudation datasets, it also provides information and input files required to recalculate basin-wide denudation rates using published code and web-enabled calculators.\n\nThis makes OCTOPUS more than a simple data catalogue: it is a curated infrastructure for reanalysing legacy datasets, comparing records across regions, testing geomorphic and palaeoenvironmental hypotheses, and improving transparency and reproducibility in geochronology. It follows FAIR principles, aiming to make geochronological data findable, accessible, interoperable and reusable for researchers, students, landform modellers and others working across Earth-surface processes, Quaternary science and archaeology.",
