        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Neotoma is supported by a community of dozens of Data Stewards, who upload and curate data in Neotoma.\n\nData governance and management is handled by Neotoma's network of Constituent Databases, each of which curates a particular kind of paleoenvironmental proxy data, organized by proxy type, region, and/or geological time work. Within this network, dozens of Data Stewards, upload and curate data in Neotoma."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The Neotoma Paleoecology Database supports global-change research by providing an open, high-quality, and community-curated resource for paleoecological and paleoenvironmental data. Neotoma comprises three interwoven components: the data itself (time series of fossil abundances or proxies of past environmental conditions, from sites distributed worldwide), the software services that make these data available to a variety of user communities, and the community of people who steward and use these data."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "In this era of rapidly changing climates, studying the past is essential for understanding how species adapt to large and rapidly changing environments, including rates of change and states of the Earth system never experienced by human society. Neotoma provides a large, well-curated, and open data resource for fossil datasets that have been widely used to study terrestrial species responses to the large, rapid, and no-modern-analog climate states of the last several hundred thousand years. Neotoma data are highly used and cited, with an H-factor of 145, and Neotoma data volumes continue to rapidly grow. Examples of major scientific papers include estimates of global rates of vegetation change over the past 20,000 years and demonstrations that current rates of change are as fast or faster than the climate-driven ecosystem transformations at the end of the last ice age. Another foundational challenge addressed by Neotoma is how to manage the global data commons; for data commons to have value avoid exploitation, there must be community-led systems for data curation and ethical open data use that meets the FAIR, TRUST, and CARE Principles. Neotoma has created innovative new structures for distributed data governance, through its system of Constituent Databases. Each Constituent Database is charged with curating a particular kind of paleoecological data and appoints its own Data Stewards. This approach has had a high success rate in encouraging community buy-in and engagement, helping advance open-science and open-data efforts from the grassroots up."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator is one of the leaders of the Neotoma Paleoecology Database. They personally contribute data to Neotoma, serve as a Data Steward, and use Neotoma data for their own research."
        },
