    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:082",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "CALCOFI fish larvae at 66 standard stations, 1966 - ongoing",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:082",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:global-environmental-change-paleoceanography-and-paleoclimatology-biogeoscience"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "NOAA SWFSC"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/tabledap/erdCalCOFIlrvcnt.html",
        "https://portal.edirepository.org/nis/metadataviewer?packageid=edi.109.2",
        "https://obis.org/dataset/0e223f55-c826-4513-ae9a-b04cbf2e189c"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:edi",
        "id:repository:errdap",
        "id:repository:obis"
      ],
      "agu:repositoryIdentifier": [
        "coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov",
        "edirepository.org",
        "obis.org"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "Lynn deWitt, NOAA & Margaret O'Brien"
        }
      ],
      "description": "The CalCOFI larval fish dataset is one of the world’s longest and most comprehensive biological ocean time series, extending from the 1940s to the present off the coast of California. It originated in response to the devastating collapse of the Pacific sardine fishery once among the largest in the world to understand how oceanographic conditions influenced fish populations and to prevent similar collapses in the future. The dataset provides continuous, standardized observations of 100s of ichthyoplankton (fish larvae) across the California Current Ecosystem (CCE), alongside concurrent measurements of physical, chemical, and biological ocean properties. This uniquely integrated dataset enables researchers to study ecosystem structure, fisheries recruitment, climate variability, and long-term environmental change. The larval fish time series has become a cornerstone for ecological and fisheries research, forming the empirical basis for management of key California and federal fisheries.",
      "reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "The CalCOFI larval fish dataset has been widely reused in fisheries, ecosystem, and climate research for over seven decades. It underpins numerous stock assessments and Fisheries Management Plans. Examples of reuse and derivative products are available through the CalCOFI publication archive (https://calcofi.org/publications) and stakeholder applications such as the Stakeholder Valuation of CalCOFI Data Usage StoryMap (https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/b0a64c5dc84946aa869895caa229b41e) and at https://calcofi.org/data-products/ and shiny.calcofi.io/int-app",
          "schema:url": [
            "https://calcofi.org/publications",
            "https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/b0a64c5dc84946aa869895caa229b41e",
            "https://calcofi.org/data-products/"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0177-7770",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Erin Satterthwaite",
      "identifier": {
        "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
        "propertyID": "ORCID",
        "value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0177-7770"
      },
      "schema:email": "esatterthwaite@ucsd.edu",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:scripps-institution-of-oceanography-ucsd-8622-kennel-way-la-jolla-ca-92037"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:082",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:082",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:082",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0177-7770"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The CalCOFI larval fish dataset was nominated because it showcases how sustained, open-access ocean observations advance both science and society. Founded in the aftermath of the catastrophic Pacific sardine collapse of the 1940s, CalCOFI was created to understand the complex interplay between environmental variability and fishery productivity. Over nearly eight decades, this dataset has evolved from a response to a devastating economic and cultural collapse into a globally recognized foundation for ecosystem-based research and management, education, and conservation. \n\nProsperity --\nCalCOFI data are essential for sustainable fisheries and ecosystem-based management along California and the U.S. West Coast. They inform state Fisheries Management Plans and provide key insights into distribution, recruitment, and links between ocean conditions and larval abundance. CalCOFI larval indices have contributed to stock assessments for species such as Sheephead, Halibut, Cowcod, Bocaccio, and Chilipepper Rockfish, and underpin spawning biomass estimates for sardine and anchovy. Its temperature and salinity time series also inform the Pacific Coast Fishery Ecosystem Plan and sardine harvest control rules. Without this long-term dataset, managers would lack the foundation needed to detect ecological change, assess recruitment, and apply adaptive, ecosystem-based management.\n\nPlanet -- \nThe CalCOFI larval fish dataset provides unparalleled insight into how ocean ecosystems respond to environmental change. It has revealed multi-decadal patterns in species composition tied to El Niño events, marine heatwaves, and long-term ocean change. The integration of the CalCOFI larval fish data with the other biological, chemical, and physical CalCOFI data enables scientists to identify early indicators of ecosystem shifts, guiding conservation of vulnerable species and habitats.\n\nPeople -- \nCalCOFI data are integral to educating generations of ocean scientists and managers. Students, early-career researchers, and community partners use the larval fish dataset to learn time series analysis, understand population dynamics, and connect environmental changes to human outcomes such as food security and livelihoods. The sardine collapse remains a cultural touchstone in California’s coastal history — memorialized in literature, art, and policy — and CalCOFI stands as the scientific legacy that transformed crisis into understanding and resilience."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "As a marine scientist working with the CalCOFI program, the nominator uses the larval fish dataset to evaluate long-term ecosystem change and its implications for fisheries management and conservation planning. Their work focuses on integrating CalCOFI observations with state and federal management priorities. \nThey collaborate with CalCOFI researchers and state partners to translate these data into applied tools, such as visualizations, data serving, and other tools. They also contribute to efforts that assess how CalCOFI data are valued and used by stakeholders — including resource managers, NGOs, and Indigenous and community organizations. Additionally, they use this dataset in the development of educational and interactive tools and talk about it at public events (e.g., public maritime museum talks)."
        }
      ]
    },
