    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:084",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:084",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:084",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7898-8122"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "Tropical cyclones have been found to increase the salinity of the freshwater lens of anchialine aquifers due to increased mixing between the two water masses (i.e., freshwater and marine water), which negatively impacts its potability. Of particular concern is that tropical cyclones have been increasing in both intensity and frequency in the Atlantic region, although there is still some uncertainty and debate regarding the role of climate change in these changes, hindering future projections.\n\nOne of the problems in examining the relationship between climate change and tropical cyclones is that the observational record is very limited: As experts agree that it is difficult to examine the relationship between climate change and recent trends in hurricane activity given that temperatures have only shown strong increases in the last few decades, one approach is to examine the paleoclimate record.\n\nBetter understanding past climate events has been stated as critical for attributing causes and effects of environmental changes in the future as well as understanding modern environmental processes, which is particularly relevant for tropical cyclones as how they are affected by climate change is still not fully understood; however, few if any paleoenvironmental indicators have been developed to examine how climate change, specifically tropical cyclones, affects groundwater in anchialine aquifers over longer term timescales, and instrumental monitoring is most often relied on. \n\nMoreover, evidence of tropical cyclones in the paleoclimate record is currently limited and difficult to interpret compared to most other paleoclimate extreme events, as it has been noted that more prolonged extreme weather events that cover a large area, such as droughts, are easier to discern in the paleoclimate record than extreme weather events more limited in time and scale such as tropical cyclones, which could help to account for the fact that few climate reconstructions focused only on tropical cyclone activity currently exist. It has also been noted that using the sedimentary record to examine extreme weather events of short duration is complex and thus requires clear comprehension of the natural processes involved, which was a major motivation for this dataset.\n\nHence, the diatoms included in this dataset can be used as environmental indicators to examine geophysical phenomena such as tropical cyclones and the impact they have on physical systems such as anchialine aquifers. They can also further be used as paleoenvironmental indicators to examine past hydrological changes and tropical cyclone activity, which may yield important insights into freshwater management of coastal aquifers in today’s changing climate as well as provide a comparable historical analogue for future projections of tropical cyclone activity. To this point, the diatoms included in this dataset can thus also be utilized as environmental indicators to aid in disaster prediction of future tropical cyclones."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "As the increase in the intensity and frequency of tropical cyclones has been linked to a warming climate, this provides support for the hypothesis that the ancient Maya civilization that inhabited the Yucatan Peninsula during the Terminal Classic Period (~800–950 CE)—concurrent with another period of elevated global temperatures (i.e., the Medieval Warm Period)—may have been similarly affected by climate change-driven changes in tropical cyclone activity, negatively impacting (i.e., salinizing) the peninsula’s major freshwater source. Accordingly, this dataset was used to examine diatoms as possible paleoenvironmental indicators of tropical cyclone activity as it encompasses a time period when several tropical cyclones affected the region: Ingrid, Dolly, Hanna, and Bill. The study that this dataset was used in found that the Shannon Diversity Index (SDI) values showed a general decrease after tropical cyclone activity across most of the sampling stations. In addition, the relative abundance of benthic diatoms showed a general increase following most of the tropical cyclones, implying vertical mixing of the water column and increased salinization of the meteoric water mass. These largely consistent responses to tropical cyclones provide a foundation for utilizing diatoms as paleoenvironmental indicators in order to examine a sediment core extracted from nearby one of the sampling stations so that tropical cyclone activity during the Terminal Classic Period may be explored."
        }
      ]
    },
