          "schema:value": "As the increase in the intensity and frequency of tropical cyclones has been linked to a warming climate, this provides support for the hypothesis that the ancient Maya civilization that inhabited the Yucatan Peninsula during the Terminal Classic Period (~800–950 CE)—concurrent with another period of elevated global temperatures (i.e., the Medieval Warm Period)—may have been similarly affected by climate change-driven changes in tropical cyclone activity, negatively impacting (i.e., salinizing) the peninsula’s major freshwater source. Accordingly, this dataset was used to examine diatoms as possible paleoenvironmental indicators of tropical cyclone activity as it encompasses a time period when several tropical cyclones affected the region: Ingrid, Dolly, Hanna, and Bill. The study that this dataset was used in found that the Shannon Diversity Index (SDI) values showed a general decrease after tropical cyclone activity across most of the sampling stations. In addition, the relative abundance of benthic diatoms showed a general increase following most of the tropical cyclones, implying vertical mixing of the water column and increased salinization of the meteoric water mass. These largely consistent responses to tropical cyclones provide a foundation for utilizing diatoms as paleoenvironmental indicators in order to examine a sediment core extracted from nearby one of the sampling stations so that tropical cyclone activity during the Terminal Classic Period may be explored."
        },
