          "name": "Liu, E Jen"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Alden, Kyle"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "de Sherbinin, Alex"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://dataverse.harvard.edu/dataset.xhtml?persistentId=doi:10.7910/DVN/33JJXT",
        "https://doi.org/10.7910/DVN/33JJXT"
      ],
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "DOI",
          "value": "https://doi.org/10.7910/DVN/33JJXT"
        }
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:harvard-dataverse"
      ],
      "description": "EnvClim Grids is a novel, global geospatial dataset characterizing current environmental vulnerability and future climate risk for urban populations. The dataset covers 11,422 urban centers worldwide, including all cities with ≥50,000 residents. \n\nThe dataset integrates 17 global public datasets into 7 indicators of current environmental vulnerability (unsafe air pollution, warm spells, extreme heat, water scarcity, hurricane-force winds, storm surge flooding, seasonal river/surface flooding) and 6 indicators of projected future climate risk (2050, SSP2-RCP4.5) (extreme heat, water scarcity, hurricane-force winds, storm surge flooding, seasonal river flooding, and landslide susceptibility). A novel socioeconomic vulnerability layer is included for cities in 91 LMICs, identifying more vulnerable neighborhood-scale areas based on higher building density and lower road connectivity relative to similar-sized cities nationally. \n\nAll indicators are harmonized to a ≈500×500 m grid for comparability across cities, enabling analysis of intersecting socioeconomic and environmental vulnerabilities at the sub-city level where data coverage allows.\n\n(https://dataverse.harvard.edu/dataset.xhtml?persistentId=doi:10.7910/DVN/33JJXT)",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "Publication currently under review (Thomson, D. et al. Global cities dataset reveals urban poor are exposed to compounding social, environmental and climate risks, in review at npj Urban Sustainability)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8495-6402",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Lindsay Barbieri",
      "identifier": {
        "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
        "propertyID": "ORCID",
