      },
      "schema:email": "lbarbieri@glp.earth",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:global-land-programme-and-department-of-geographical-sciences-university-of-mary"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:global-land-programme-and-department-of-geographical-sciences-university-of-mary",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "Global Land Programme and Department of Geographical Sciences, University of Maryland, 2181 Samuel J. LeFrak Hall, 7251 Preinkert Drive, College Park, MD 20742, USA"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:085",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:085",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:085",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8495-6402"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The EnvClim Grids dataset is an important new resource for understanding how cities around the world are being shaped by environmental and climate risks. With more than half of the world’s population already living in urban areas, and nearly two billion more people expected to join them by 2050, cities are becoming the frontline of climate change. Many of these new urban residents are settling in informal or poorly serviced neighborhoods that face overlapping environmental stresses like floods, heatwaves, and air pollution. These compounding challenges are made worse by limited infrastructure, weak land-use regulation, and social inequality, leaving poor and marginalized communities most exposed to environmental hazards.\n\nEnvClim Grids helps address a global data gap by providing a harmonized, fine-scale (500×500 m) view of environmental vulnerability and future climate risk for 11,422 urban centers worldwide. Unlike previous national-level datasets that miss city-level differences, EnvClim Grids makes it possible to identify vulnerable neighborhoods within cities where social and environmental risks intersect. The dataset integrates 17 publicly available global datasets to produce seven key indicators of current environmental vulnerability (e.g. unsafe air pollution, extreme heat, flooding, and water scarcity) and six indicators of projected climate risk for 2050 (e.g. storm surge, landslides, and hurricane-force winds). It also includes a socioeconomic vulnerability layer for 91 LMICs, mapping areas with higher building density and lower road connectivity as proxies for low socioeconomic status.\n\nFor people, EnvClim Grids supports better disaster preparedness by showing where and what types of environmental hazards are currently present, as well as which climate risks are likely to occur in the future. It also provides a spatial understanding of where health and safety risks from environmental hazards are likely to be amplified by underlying socioeconomic vulnerability. Decision-makers, humanitarian organizations, and local planners can use this information to target resources, design context-sensitive climate adaptation strategies, and strengthen public awareness in communities at risk. \n\nFor the planet, the dataset provides an integrated view of key environmental and climatic processes shaping cities, from geophysical hazards to changing temperature and water patterns. By integrating the most current global data into environmental indicators with socioeconomic data, it enables a deeper understanding of how urbanization and climate change interact to affect ecosystems and human settlements. \n\nFor prosperity, EnvClim Grids is intended to support more equitable and resilient development. By making spatial data on environmental and socioeconomic vulnerability and risk openly available and integrated into easy-to-use indicators, it can enable urban planning across different scales and resource levels to prioritize interventions that promote community resilience. The dataset can also help guide climate investment and policy decisions such that adaptation strategies not only protect people and ecosystems but are also aimed at helping to reduce poverty over time."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "As a former geospatial analyst with the U.S. Agency for International Development, the nominator was asking for the creation of this dataset, and then subsequently using this dataset, to help fill an urgent need for proactive, inclusive urban planning to mitigate climate risks and strengthen resilience. The spatially-explicit urban environmental and climate vulnerability and risk profiles that can be created with this dataset offer really impactful tools for governments, donors, and development partners to support urban resilience, particularly for the most at-risk populations in a rapidly urbanizing world. While the dataset could not continue to be used by the nominator for program planning and implementation following the closure of USAID, it is sincerely believed that it can be extremely beneficial to other organizations, governments, decision-makers, as well as researchers and educators. There is hope that its availability will be widely shared with all who may benefit from it."
        }
      ]
    },
