    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:086",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:086",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:086",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5196-3112"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "Research cores from the Eagle Ford Group and bounding formations were made available for collaborative research across numerous international academic, government and industry institutions. The dataset has substantial educational and outreach impact. The research supported five student internships, twelve doctorates, three post-doctorates and several early career scientists, leading to over 25 publications and the successful employment of numerous researchers in Earth Sciences. The dataset is currently being utilized and added to by a new cohort of enthusiastic earth Science students and researchers.\n\nThe dataset has a positive impact on physical systems, including improved understanding of local, regional and global paleoenvironmental processes that influence climate and marine conditions during Greenhouse periods of high atmospheric carbon dioxide and temperatures. One of the two research cores (Iona-1) contains perhaps the most complete Cenomanian-Turonian section compared to most others that contain significant unconformities, providing a stratigraphic temporal framework not available elsewhere. The integrated dataset provides a “rosetta stone” for interpreting the dynamics of environmental change throughout the most profound global perturbation in the carbon cycle of the past 140 Ma, Oceanic Anoxic Event 2 (OAE 2).\n\nRhythmic alteration in limestone-marlstone couplets (resolved at 10-100 cm scales), interspersed with over 200 volcanic ash (bentonite) beds document expansion of oceanic anoxia driven by variation in solar insolation over Milankovitch periodicities. At higher resolution, individual pairs of light-dark laminae (mm-thick) represent pulses of water-column mixing and oxygenation at centennial (80–100 yr, 200–230 yr, 350–500 yr)- to millennial-scale (1650 yr, and 4843 yr), coinciding with DeVries and Gleisberg solar cycles that are reminiscent of those found in Holocene. The integration of sedimentology with bio-assemblage, isotopes (C, N, Cd, Zn, Mo) and element data constrain variations in redox state linked with the incursion of water masses; whilst analyses of Sr, Cr, Fe, and Os isotope and Hg and Platinum group element data demonstrate that three distinct LIP pulses occurred during OAE 2, that weathering initiated the Carbon Isotope Excursion, and that LIP magmatism followed the onset of the Mid-Cenomanian OAE, dated 96.4 Ma.\n\nThe dataset has also had a large impact on the economy, largely supporting the characterization of lateral heterogeneities in mudrocks systems supporting hydrocarbon production from the Eagle Ford Group Texas, and globally in the prediction of source rock facies. The dataset also provides an integrated understanding of Earth systems control on marine anoxia; and we hope this may lead to improved awareness of the current environmental degradation and potential mitigations."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominators view the Iona-1 and Innes-1 core datasets as a “Rosetta Stone” for understanding the greenhouse conditions of the Cenomanian-Turonian and have collaborated with dozens of academic and government researchers to build and use the data. Core samples are currently being analyzed, and this dataset is being shared and will provide the framework for multi-disciplinary integration. They welcome additional future collaborative research studies on the cores and dataset."
        }
      ]
    },
