    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:087",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "GOSIF and GOSIF GPP (two sister products)",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:087",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:global-environmental-change-paleoceanography-and-paleoclimatology-biogeoscience"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Xing Li, Jingfeng Xiao"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://data.globalecology.unh.edu/data/"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:global-ecology-data-repository"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "Jingfeng Xiao"
        }
      ],
      "description": "GOSIF is a global, gridded dataset of solar-induced chlorophyll fluorescence (SIF) produced by the Global Ecology Group at University of New Hampshire. SIF, an energy flux re-emitted by plants a few nanoseconds after light absorption, exhibits a strong linear relationship with gross primary production (GPP) at the ecosystem scale and is a strong proxy of GPP. The GOSIF dataset provides 8-day SIF data globally at 0.05-degree spatial resolution for 2000-2024. This product is derived from discrete SIF observations based on the Orbiting Carbon Observatory-2 (OCO-2) using a machine learning method. The generation of GOSIF also makes use of spatially and temporally continuous data on enhanced vegetation index (EVI) derived from the Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) and meteorological reanalysis data from MERRA-2. \n\nGOSIF also has a companion product - GOSIF GPP that has the same spatial and temporal coverage and resolutions. GOSIF GPP is a global, gridded GPP product that is based on the GOSIF dataset and established relationships between GPP and SIF. The companion product is also available on the Global Ecology Repository. GOSIF GPP includes an uncertainty estimate on GPP on a per-pixel basis. For a particular application, whether GOSIF or its companion product should be used depends on whether the user intends to use data on SIF, an energy flux emitted by plants and a proxy of photosynthesis, or GPP, the amount of carbon absorbed by plants via photosynthesis.",
      "isReferencedBy": [
