        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "GOSIF is a global, gridded dataset of solar-induced chlorophyll fluorescence (SIF) produced by the Global Ecology Group at University of New Hampshire. SIF, an energy flux re-emitted by plants a few nanoseconds after light absorption, exhibits a strong linear relationship with gross primary production (GPP) at the ecosystem scale and is a strong proxy of GPP. The GOSIF dataset provides 8-day SIF data globally at 0.05-degree spatial resolution for 2000-2024. This product is derived from discrete SIF observations based on the Orbiting Carbon Observatory-2 (OCO-2) using a machine learning method. The generation of GOSIF also makes use of spatially and temporally continuous data on enhanced vegetation index (EVI) derived from the Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) and meteorological reanalysis data from MERRA-2. \n\nGOSIF also has a companion product - GOSIF GPP that has the same spatial and temporal coverage and resolutions. GOSIF GPP is a global, gridded GPP product that is based on the GOSIF dataset and established relationships between GPP and SIF. The companion product is also available on the Global Ecology Repository. GOSIF GPP includes an uncertainty estimate on GPP on a per-pixel basis. For a particular application, whether GOSIF or its companion product should be used depends on whether the user intends to use data on SIF, an energy flux emitted by plants and a proxy of photosynthesis, or GPP, the amount of carbon absorbed by plants via photosynthesis."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "GOSIF and its companion product GOSIF GPP are transformative global datasets whose broad accessibility, consistency, and scientific impact make them outstanding candidates for AGU commentary. Their availability has opened new avenues of inquiry and application across the Earth and environmental sciences—advancing discovery, prediction, and societal benefit in ways that would be severely constrained or impossible without them. The justification below is organized around the dimensions of People, Planet, and Prosperity.\n\n1. People \n\nGOSIF and GOSIF GPP have become cornerstone “teaching datasets” in courses and training programs on remote sensing, ecosystem ecology, and the carbon cycle. Their spatially and temporally continuous representations of solar-induced chlorophyll fluorescence (SIF) and gross primary productivity (GPP) allow students and trainees to engage directly with near-operational global carbon-cycle data, fostering data literacy and scientific capacity building. Graduate students worldwide have adopted these datasets in master’s theses and doctoral research.\nBeyond education, GOSIF has proven valuable for environmental prediction and response. It has been used to develop and validate drought indicators that detect drought onset and recovery—offering early warning capability critical for water and agricultural management.\n\n2. Planet \n\nGOSIF and GOSIF GPP have become indispensable for monitoring and assessing ecosystem functioning, vegetation productivity, carbon fluxes, and phenological dynamics from regional to global scales. They provide direct observational constraints for quantifying terrestrial carbon sinks and sources, understanding responses to climate variability and extremes, and evaluating ecosystem resilience and resistance. The datasets have been used to evaluate terrestrial biosphere, land surface, and Earth system models, advancing the predictive understanding of the coupled carbon–climate system.\n\n3. Prosperity \n\nApplications of these datasets extend to food security and agricultural monitoring. They have been employed to assess crop yield and productivity, evaluate effects of drought and air pollution on agriculture, and define the timing of agricultural wet seasons. \n\nGOSIF has been featured by NCAR’s Climate Data Guide as a widely used and trusted dataset (https://climatedataguide.ucar.edu/climate-data/global-dataset-solar-induced-chlorophyll-fluorescence-gosif), underscoring its scientific credibility and reach. Since their release in 2019, GOSIF and GOSIF GPP have been cited 625 and 274 times, respectively, and together have supported ~450 journal publications.\n\nIn summary, GOSIF and GOSIF GPP have transformed how scientists, educators, and practitioners observe, understand, and model terrestrial carbon and water cycles. Their broad adoption and demonstrated impact across the dimensions of People, Planet, and Prosperity exemplify the kind of high-value, community-enabling datasets that merit recognition in an AGU commentary."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator is the principal investigator (PI) of both the GOSIF and GOSIF GPP datasets, which were developed by their student, Xing Li, under their supervision. They also founded and maintain the Global Ecology Data Repository (http://data.globalecology.unh.edu), where these datasets are archived and made publicly available. Since their initial release, they have continued to update and improve GOSIF and GOSIF GPP on an annual basis in collaboration with their former student to ensure their accuracy, consistency, and long-term continuity. In addition to leading their development and maintenance, they regularly assist scientists, students, and data users around the world in applying these datasets to diverse research and educational objectives, helping to maximize their scientific and societal impact."
        },
