    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:088",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:088",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:088",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1833-3239"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The Land Surface Disturbance Alert (DIST-ALERT) dataset delivers the first global, near-real-time monitoring of vegetation disturbance across all terrestrial ecosystems, filling a critical gap in how we detect and respond to land-use change. It expands global alert coverage beyond tropical forests to include grasslands, savannas, shrublands, wetlands, and croplands, enabling rapid detection of deforestation, degradation, and other disturbances anywhere in the world. DIST-ALERT also serves as the foundation for the Land Disturbance Alert Classification System (LDACS), which classifies each disturbance as likely natural or human-driven. Together, these tools allow users not only to detect change as it happens but also to interpret its likely causes—an essential step for targeted conservation, enforcement, and policy action. \n\nDIST-ALERT is particularly important for monitoring and protecting high-priority conservation areas, including protected areas, biodiversity hotspots, and Indigenous lands. These landscapes are critical for maintaining global biodiversity and storing carbon, making their protection essential for both climate mitigation and conservation goals. DIST-ALERT data is updated for most areas every 2–4 days, providing governments, Indigenous and local communities, land managers, and supply-chain actors with timely information on emerging threats in these areas. \n\nThe data supports early detection of illegal activities, ecosystem degradation, and climate-driven impacts such as droughts and wildfires. Communities have used similar alerts within forests to halt illegal logging and protect threatened ecosystems but DIST-ALERT extends these benefits globally and across all ecosystem types. By providing timely information on threats, DIST-ALERT can help contribute to international conservation targets like the Global Biodiversity Framework’s “30x30” commitment to protect 30% of the planet’s land and water by 2030. LDACS enhances this impact by helping users distinguish between human- and natural-driven events, improving the speed and effectiveness of their responses. \n\nDIST-ALERT also supports sustainable economic development and governance by providing reliable data for supply-chain monitoring, regulatory compliance, and conservation planning. Companies can use it to track no-conversion commitments and de-risk supply chains in order to meet regulations like the EU Deforestation Regulation. Governments and civil society organizations can also use the data to respond to illegal activities and target humanitarian efforts more efficiently. LDACS builds on these alerts to distinguish the likely causes of disturbances, helping decision-makers focus resources where they are most needed. \n\nWithout DIST-ALERT, the global community would lack near-real-time visibility into disturbances across most ecosystems. Its global coverage, high temporal frequency, and integration with LDACS make it a foundational dataset for environmental science, policy, and action."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator’s team makes the DIST-ALERT dataset available through Global Forest Watch (GFW), a data platform that reaches millions of users worldwide. By hosting and visualizing the data, GFW enables users to overlay DIST-ALERT with other layers such as primary forests, protected areas, and Indigenous lands, providing critical context for understanding where disturbances are happening and why they matter. This integration makes the dataset a powerful tool for decision-making in conservation, enforcement, and land-use planning. \n\nThe DIST-ALERT layer is toggled on more than 1,000 times each month, reflecting strong and growing engagement from a wide range of users, including community forest monitors, companies, and governments. The data expands near real-time monitoring beyond the tropics, enabling users to track forest loss worldwide. It has also been crucial for developing the Land Disturbance Alert Classification System (LDACS), which builds on DIST-ALERT by classifying disturbance causes, allowing users to tailor responses for more effective management, enforcement, and conservation action. \n\nThe team is currently developing new GFW features to make DIST-ALERT even more useful, including on-the-fly analysis, email subscriptions, and downloadable alert coordinates. The data will also be integrated into Forest Watcher, enabling offline use for communities and enforcement agencies."
        }
      ]
    },
