        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "This gridded global merged multi-satellite geostationary infrared (IR) brightness temperature product (1998-present) originates from the NOAA Climate Prediction Center (CPC), the National Centers for Environmental Prediction (NCEP), and the National Weather Service (NWS) and are hosted by NASA’s Goddard Earth Sciences Data and Information Services Center (GES DISC).\n\nThe 4-km datasets contain IR brightness temperature data (equivalent blackbody temps), merged from the European, Japanese, and U.S. geostationary satellites that have been active over the product’s period of record (METEOSAT-5/7/8/9/10, GMS-5/MTSat-1R/2/Himawari-8, and GOES-8/9/10/11/12/13/14/15/16), and gridded to 4-km every half hour on the latitude band 60°S-60°N.\n\nThe NASA GES DISC converts the original binary data into a self-documenting, CF-compliant, netCDF-4 format, which allows a broad range of applications to access the data directly, without the need to cope with the original binary data format.\n\nThe creation of the original data at NOAA/NCEP is supported by funding from the NOAA Office of Global Programs for the Global Precipitation Climatology Project (GPCP) and by NASA via the Tropical Rainfall Measuring Mission (TRMM). The permanent archive at GES DISC is supported by NASA's HQ Earth Science Data Systems (ESDS) Program.\n\nReference links:\n\nhttps://disc.gsfc.nasa.gov/datasets/GPM_MERGIR_1/summary\n\nhttps://gpm.nasa.gov/data/directory/ncepcpc-level-3-merged-infrared-brightness-temperatures-0\n\nhttps://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/global_precip/html/wpage.merged_IR.html"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Infrared (IR) data plays an important role in weather forecasting and environmental monitoring. Geostationary IR data provide continuous, real-time data for tracking weather patterns, such as hurricanes, fronts, and atmospheric rivers, for short-term forecasting. Some examples are found in Janowiak et al. (2001) and Liu et al. (2011). IR data is used for monitoring cloud cover, ocean currents, volcanic ash, and vegetation, helping scientists study climate change and environmental phenomena. IR data is widely used in disaster management, such as tracking wildfires and providing crucial information for emergency response efforts. Historical IR data is equally important for many activities, such as case studies, algorithm development, and climate data records.\n\nIR data is used to derive physical parameters. IR data is an important input for deriving IR-based and/or multi-sensor, multi-satellite precipitation. The abundance of IR data from geostationary satellites distributed around the globe fills in many data gaps for other satellite sensors. An example is NASA's Integrated Multi-satEllite Retrievals for Global Precipitation Mission (IMERG), providing global 10 km precipitation estimates every half hour. IR data is used to derive atmospheric motion vectors, which play a critical role in many research and applications, such as weather forecasting models and algorithms. \n\nAlthough there is an abundance of IR data around the world, collecting the data from different agencies (e.g., NOAA, ESA, JAXA) can be a challenge for many, especially for users who are not familiar with data repositories and access methods. Stitching IR data together from different geostationary satellites in a user-friendly format is another challenge. The merged IR product (1998-present), developed by the National Centers for Environmental Prediction (NCEP)/the Climate Prediction Center (CPC) and hosted by NASA’s Goddard Earth Sciences Data and Information Services Center (GES DISC), is a product that merges zenith angle corrected IR observations from geostationary satellites on a 4 km grid every half hour on the latitude band 60 N-S, greatly facilitating IR data-related research and applications. Because of the agreements with the European community, the data latency of the merged IR product is roughly 3 days. \n\nGlobal merged IR data can also support weather and natural disaster education for everyone. IR data animations frequently appear in local and national TV weather news. Similarly, the merged IR product can create animations to follow the development of storms and weather systems, as well as environmental disasters like ash clouds from volcanoes. The merged IR data can be added to user-friendly tools such as NASA’s Giovanni (https://giovanni.gsfc.nasa.gov/giovanni/), a free online tool to explore over 2000 satellite- and model-based physical parameters, without downloading data and software.\n\nReferences:\n\nJanowiak J. E., R. J. Joyce, and Y. Yarosh, 2001: A Real-Time Global Half-hourly Pixel-Resolution Infrared Dataset and Its Applications. (Bull. Amer. Meteor. Soc., vol. 82, No.3., 205-217.)\n\nLiu, Z., D. Ostrenga, and G. Leptoukh, 2011: Online Visualization and Analysis of Global Half-Hourly Infrared Satellite Data. Bull. Amer. Meteor. Soc., 92, 429–432, https://doi.org/10.1175/2010BAMS2976.1."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "I have been involved in the curation work since 2000. The merged IR product has been a popular product at GES DISC. The merged IR data is essential for a wide range of research, application, and education activities. It takes a lot of effort for NOAA’s National Centers for Environmental Prediction (NCEP)/the Climate Prediction Center (CPC) to develop, maintain, and distribute this product. Similarly, NASA GES DISC reformats the product from binary to NetCDF, facilitating the use of the product by popular tools and software packages. The GES DISC also provides different methods for data access, including HTTPS, OPeNDAP, and subsetting services. The merged IR data has been frequently used in case studies at GES DISC to showcase its usefulness in weather events and natural hazards (e.g., ash clouds from volcanic eruptions).\n\nIn 2011, the GES DISC developed an online tool to analyze and visualize this merged IR data (Liu et al. 2011). With this tool, users could generate and download animations in their areas of interest, without downloading data and software. The merged IR data can be added to NASA Giovanni in the future since Giovanni has the capability to generate animations. With more than 2000 environmental parameters in Giovanni, adding the merged IR data will help case studies.\n\nIn short, we need to continue supporting such an impactful dataset merged IR product. Ideally, it would be expanded to the entire globe by integrating additional satellite observations."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Janowiak, J. E., Joyce, R. J., & Yarosh, Y. (2001). A real–time global half–hourly pixel–resolution infrared dataset and its applications. Bulletin of the American Meteorological Society, 82(2), 205–217. https://doi.org/10.1175/1520-0477(2001)082<0205:ARTGHH>2.3.CO;2 \n\nLiu, Z., Ostrenga, D., & Leptoukh, G. (2011). Online visualization and analysis of global half-hourly infrared satellite data. Bulletin of the American Meteorological Society, 92(4), 429–432. https://doi.org/10.1175/2010BAMS2976.1"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Eldrett, J.S., Bergman, S.C. and Minisini, D., 2024. Statistical approaches for improved definition of carbon isotope excursions. Earth-Science Reviews, 255, p.104851."
        },
        {
