        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "ABCFlux v2 is a synthesis of terrestrial and freshwater CO2 and CH4 fluxes from the Arctic-Boreal region aggregated to monthly timesteps. The dataset contains 23,656 flux site-months and 198 seasonal flux observations from 1,024 unique sites, spanning 1984–2024, with most observations collected after 1999. ABCFlux v2 includes surface-atmosphere CO2 fluxes of net ecosystem exchange (NEE), gross primary production (GPP), and ecosystem respiration (Reco) alongside CH4 fluxes. For aquatic ecosystems, we differentiate surface-atmosphere CH4 fluxes between diffusive and ebullitive fluxes and include potential emissions from transient storage in the water column, alongside CO2 and CH4 concentrations dissolved in the surface water (8,182 concentration site-months). Fluxes are measured through a variety of methods including chamber and eddy covariance techniques alongside bubble traps, and concentration-based turbulence-driven modeling in aquatic ecosystems. Supporting variables include methodological metadata (e.g., gap-filling methods, number of chamber measurement days), environmental measurements (e.g., air, water, and soil temperatures), and site-level attributes (e.g., permafrost thaw status, disturbance history). (https://doi.org/10.3334/ORNLDAAC/2448)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "ABCFlux v2 represents a substantial advancement in our ability to understand carbon fluxes across the rapidly changing Arctic-Boreal region. This dataset provides the most comprehensive synthesis to date of site-level ecosystem-atmosphere fluxes from northern latitudes. \nABCFlux v2 captures data from over 1000 sites across various ecosystems including boreal forests, wetlands, tundras, and freshwater systems. By providing detailed flux measurements of CO₂, CH₄, and over 70 associated supporting environmental and meteorological variables, ABCFlux v2 provides a comprehensive synthesis of flux data from this region. These data provide essential inputs for evaluating and constraining regional and global carbon budgets, validating Earth system models, and improving understanding of ecosystem responses to climate feedbacks. Additionally, many sites in ABCFlux v2 indicate that permafrost thaw and other disturbances are actively reshaping landscapes and ecosystem functions, underscoring the critical need for this dataset in the context of a rapidly changing climate and vulnerability of Arctic-Boreal ecosystems. The dataset directly extends ABCFlux v1, which has been cited 55 times since 2021 and used to create outputs that have informed assessments such as the Arctic Report Card 2024, thereby demonstrating its scientific relevance and policy significance.\nThe collaborative scope of ABCFlux v2 is unprecedented, integrating contributions from 269 coauthors across 185 institutions in 26 countries. Beyond its scientific value, the dataset stands as a model of international and scientific cooperation within the Arctic research community that has strengthened connections among early-career and established scientists. ABCFlux v2 enables scientific analyses that support data-driven decision-making and inform the development of climate policies and mitigation strategies. Its open, harmonized framework allows researchers to investigate environmental trends, and model development, thereby enhancing the capacity of communities and institutions to respond effectively to environmental change. \nWhile flux data repositories and their processing pipelines have marked a major advance in data synthesis, their coverage across the Arctic-Boreal region remains limited. Without ABCFlux v2, data on carbon dioxide and methane fluxes from this region would remain fragmented, limiting our progress in understanding the role of these northern ecosystems in the global carbon climate system. Its comprehensiveness, accessibility, and collaborative spirit make ABCFlux v2 a cornerstone dataset for studying the future of the planet’s northern ecosystems and their role in Earth’s carbon cycle."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator has been directly involved in the synthesis and development of the ABCFlux v2 dataset as part of a collaborative research team. Over the past several years, their role has focused on bringing together flux data from across the Arcti-boreal region by reaching out to research groups and principal investigators, organizing and cleaning the data, and ensuring that the dataset is reliable and usable for others. This process has involved a lot of collaboration and communication, as well as detailed quality checking to make sure the dataset truly reflects the best available measurements.\nIn addition to dataset assembly, they’ve also contributed to early analyses using the dataset, including estimating annual carbon emissions across different ecosystem types. The next stage of this work will leverage ABCFlux v2 to investigate spatial and temporal patterns of carbon exchange across arctic boreal regions in order to improve constraints on regional and global carbon budgets. This process has provided valuable experience in data management, collaboration, and synthesis within the Earth system science community. It’s been rewarding to see the dataset grow into a resource that others can now use to ask new questions about our changing planet."
        },
