    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:091",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "International Soil Carbon Network (ISCN)",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:091",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:global-environmental-change-paleoceanography-and-paleoclimatology-biogeoscience"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Katherine Todd-Brown (Data Coordinator) for ISCN data in general."
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://iscn.fluxdata.org/data/"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:edi-data-portal"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "Luke Nave (lead author of ISCN3 database) et al"
        }
      ],
      "description": "The International Soil Carbon Network (ISCN) is a global scientific community dedicated to advancing soil carbon research and fostering collaboration across institutions and regions. ISCN manages an open-access, community-driven soil carbon database that aggregates standardized, high-quality soil data to support research on carbon stocks, turnover, and ecosystem feedbacks to climate change. Version 3‑1 of the ISCN Database (December 2015) consolidates 38 individual datasets, encompassing 67,112 sites, 71,198 soil profiles, and 431,324 soil layers, spanning diverse ecosystems, soil types, and climatic regions worldwide. Each dataset entry includes detailed pedon and horizon information, depth intervals, bulk density, carbon and nitrogen content, moisture, and other key physico-chemical parameters, accompanied by standardized metadata such as site identifier, geographic coordinates, vegetation, hydrologic context, sampling date, and analytical protocol. The ISCN is guided by a Scientific Steering Group, including representatives from leading soil science institutions, which defines scientific priorities, promotes standardized protocols, and facilitates network activities. The database and associated resources support cross-site comparisons, long-term change detection, meta-analyses, and integration with other global soil carbon initiatives. The data are fully accessible with licensing that permits reuse, modeling, and synthesis, ensuring provenance and traceability. More information on ISCN, its scientific community, leadership, datasets, data policies, and partner networks can be found at http://iscn.fluxdata.org/.",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "Nave, L., Johnson, K., Van Ingen, C., Agarwal, D., Humphrey, M., & Beekwilder, N. (2022). International soil carbon network version 3 database(Iscn3) [Dataset]. Environmental Data Initiative. https://doi.org/10.6073/PASTA/CC751923C5576B95A6D6A227D5AFE8BA",
          "identifier": [
            {
              "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
              "propertyID": "DOI",
              "value": "https://doi.org/10.6073/PASTA/CC751923C5576B95A6D6A227D5AFE8BA"
            }
          ],
          "schema:url": [
            "https://doi.org/10.6073/PASTA/CC751923C5576B95A6D6A227D5AFE8BA"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5513-8862",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Debjani Sihi",
      "identifier": {
        "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
        "propertyID": "ORCID",
        "value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5513-8862"
      },
      "schema:email": "dsihi@ncsu.edu",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:north-carolina-state-university-2101-hillsborough-st-raleigh-nc-27695"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:north-carolina-state-university-2101-hillsborough-st-raleigh-nc-27695",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "North Carolina State University, 2101 Hillsborough St, Raleigh, NC 27695"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:091",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:091",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:091",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5513-8862"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "Justification for AGU Commentary\nISCN database is a great example of global data harmonization, catalyzing innovation, collaboration, and stewardship. It aligns with AGU’s mission to advance Earth and space sciences for societal benefit. Its inclusion in AGU commentary highlights the indispensable role of data infrastructure in addressing climate challenges and advancing scientific frontiers. ISCN Database is a fundamental resource for global soil carbon science, integrating 38 datasets across 67,000+ sites, 71,000 profiles, and 431,000 soil layers. Its scale and standardization solve the long-standing problem of fragmented, site-specific data, enabling integrated, global, and long-term analyses of soil organic carbon (SOC) that were previously impossible. \n\nISCN provides the empirical foundation for modeling soil carbon, evaluating ecosystem vulnerability, and refining ecosystem and Earth system models (ESMs). It can be used to test emerging frameworks like the Microbial Efficiency-Matrix Stabilization (MEMS) model and theories of SOM formation and persistence. By harmonizing data across biomes, ISCN advances soil organic matter science and improves representation of soil processes in climate simulations.\n\nPeople: ISCN empowers students, early-career scientists, and interdisciplinary teams through hands-on access to harmonized data, fostering skills in data synthesis, geospatial analysis, and soil carbon modeling. It supports education, training, and capacity-building, while informing disaster preparedness in climate-sensitive regions. In wetlands and coastal zones, ISCN data help assess SOC loss and guide resilience strategies.\n\nPlanet: As the largest terrestrial carbon reservoir, soil plays a vital role in climate regulation. ISCN enables tracking of environmental indicators, assessment of ecosystem vulnerabilities, and evaluation of conservation strategies. It supports nature-based solutions and enhances predictive modeling of carbon–climate feedbacks. ISCN could also provide empirical grounding for global carbon budgets and mitigation planning. \n\nProsperity: ISCN links SOC to ecosystem services, agricultural productivity, and food and water security. It informs climate-smart land management, supports carbon accounting for markets, and contributes to sustainable development goals. By enabling equitable climate action and humanitarian planning, ISCN promotes social equity and community resilience.",
          "impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "People",
              "schema:text": "ISCN empowers students, early-career scientists, and interdisciplinary teams through hands-on access to harmonized data, fostering skills in data synthesis, geospatial analysis, and soil carbon modeling. It supports education, training, and capacity-building, while informing disaster preparedness in climate-sensitive regions. In wetlands and coastal zones, ISCN data help assess SOC loss and guide resilience strategies."
            },
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "Planet",
              "schema:text": "As the largest terrestrial carbon reservoir, soil plays a vital role in climate regulation. ISCN enables tracking of environmental indicators, assessment of ecosystem vulnerabilities, and evaluation of conservation strategies. It supports nature-based solutions and enhances predictive modeling of carbon–climate feedbacks. ISCN could also provide empirical grounding for global carbon budgets and mitigation planning."
            },
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "Prosperity",
              "schema:text": "ISCN links SOC to ecosystem services, agricultural productivity, and food and water security. It informs climate-smart land management, supports carbon accounting for markets, and contributes to sustainable development goals. By enabling equitable climate action and humanitarian planning, ISCN promotes social equity and community resilience."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "In the nominator’s recent study (https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1029/2023JG007702), the ISCN Database was foundational to developing a scalable framework for digital soil mapping of SOC across the United States. The team extracted over 49,000 SOC observations (0–30 cm and 0–100 cm depths), spanning more than a century of sampling. This extensive and harmonized dataset enabled them to train region-specific Machine Learning models that captured SOC variability across diverse landscapes, climates, and land-use regimes. ISCN’s structure was critical for identifying continuous soil profiles and integrating them with geospatial predictors, allowing them to delineate 20 SOC regions using multivariate geographic clustering. While some overlap existed with gNATSGO-derived soil properties, ISCN provided the primary response variable, measured SOC, which grounded their models in empirical reality. Their gridded SOC data-product is currently being used . \nCurrently, they are using ISCN data as independent validation dataset for high-resolution SOC maps they are generating using NEON (National Ecological Observatory Network)'s SOC and remote sensing (hyperspectral and LiDAR data) data in co-located regions. Thus, its depth, diversity, and accessibility make it indispensable to the nominator’s work in scalable soil carbon science."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
