    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:092",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "NOAA Climate Data Records",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:092",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:global-environmental-change-paleoceanography-and-paleoclimatology-biogeoscience"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "National Centers for Environmental Information"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/products/climate-data-records"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:noaa-national-centers-for-environmental-information"
      ],
      "description": "NOAA Climate Data Records (CDRs) can be used to manage natural resources and agriculture, measure environmental impacts on human health and community preparedness, and inform policy development and decision making for other sectors and interest groups.\n\nCDRs are robust, sustainable, and scientifically sound climate records that provide trustworthy information on how, where, and to what extent the land, oceans, atmosphere and ice sheets are changing. These datasets are thoroughly vetted time series measurements with the longevity, consistency, and continuity to assess and measure climate variability and change. NOAA CDRs are vetted using standards established by the National Research Council (NRC).\n\nNOAA CDRs are systematically generated and routinely assessed for quality. The first step in establishing an operational CDR includes public posting of the source code that generated the CDR dataset, the dataset itself, and supporting documentation through a six-phase Research-to-Operations process that is described in the Developers Guidelines.\n\nNOAA CDRs are derived from a variety of satellites and instruments. As satellites or instruments change, there will be changes to how CDR data is derived and provided. Information on the impact of each individual CDR will be updated as resources allow. \nThe launch, maintenance and decommissioning of satellites can be found on the WMO's Observing Systems Capability Analysis and Review Tool (OSCAR) website. Additionally, NOAA Satellite status and performance of JPSS, Metop, GOES, and POES spacecraft are also available.\n\nURL: https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/products/climate-data-records",
      "reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "Minisini, D; Eldrett, JS & Bergman, SC."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "2025. The value of integrated high-resolution studies: The Eagle Ford example. https://doi.org/10.5724/gcs.38.177",
          "schema:url": [
            "https://doi.org/10.5724/gcs.38.177"
          ],
          "identifier": [
            "https://doi.org/10.5724/gcs.38.177"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6850-3403",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Yuhan \"Douglas\" Rao",
      "identifier": {
        "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
        "propertyID": "ORCID",
        "value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6850-3403"
      },
      "schema:email": "yrao5@ncsu.edu",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:north-carolina-state-university-2200-hillsborough-st-raleigh-nc-27607"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:north-carolina-state-university-2200-hillsborough-st-raleigh-nc-27607",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "North Carolina State University, 2200 Hillsborough St, Raleigh, NC 27607"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:092",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:092",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:092",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6850-3403"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "NOAA Climate Data Records (CDR) are a collection of nearly 40 datasets that provide unique insight of how the Earth system has changed across different components, including atmosphere, ocean, cryosphere, hydrosphere, and ecosphere.\n\nThese datasets include dozens of essential climate variables (ECVs) derived from long-term environmental satellite observations through rigorous calibration to remove the impact of satellite platform and sensor changes. They contribute directly to a global network of satellite-based ECVs for climate monitoring that is demanded by the Global Climate Observing Systems (GCOS).\n\nThese datasets are unique for global climate system monitoring due to its near global coverage. The value of these datasets cannot be replaced with other in situ measurements due to the lack of global representation. Additionally, these CDR datasets are critical to verify the historical climate model simulations ensuring the validity of climate models. \n\nSome high-impact use cases of NOAA CDRs include, \n1) operational ENSO monitoring using the optimum-interpolated sea surface temperature (OISST), \n2) monitoring of Arctic sea ice and the publication of the annual Arctic report card using the sea ice concentration data,\n3) annual state of the climate summary using global ocean heat content, upper tropospheric humidity, mean layer atmospheric temperature (across different pressure levels),\n4) ecosystem productivity and drought monitoring using leaf area index (LAI) and normalized difference vegetation index (NDVI) data,\n5) evaluation of CMIP historical simulation experiment.\n6) empowering AI-based direct observation-to-prediction (AI-DOP) model for weather and climate prediction by both ECMWF and Google DeepMind.\n\nThe CDR collection is an embodiment of robust public-academic partnership between NOAA and different academic institutions. The CDR program was set up where academic researchers can propose and demonstrate the potential of different satellite-based CDR dataset through research activities. NOAA then evaluates the contribution of newly proposed satellite-based CDRs to transition them into operational production and maintenance for routine climate monitoring applications. \n\nAs NOAA transitions towards next generation satellite sensors, NOAA has continued the support of the CDR data by extending the legacy satellite sensor-based product with inter-satellite calibrated new sensor observations to ensure the value of long-term CDRs for climate monitoring and impact studies."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator has spent more than 12 years working with NOAA CDR data. When they were a graduate student studying vegetation phenology, the NDVI CDR was considered the gold standard dataset. The dataset was well calibrated and ready to use by a graduate student with detailed documentation and a wealth of literature using the same dataset. \n\nAs they continued to pursue their research during their PhD and at their current position, they became a subject matter expert to help NOAA evaluate the quality of a subset of CDR datasets and manage the development of cloud-based tools and data services to maximize the impact of these valuable datasets.\n\nOne example that demonstrates the value of CDRs to understand current events is Hurricane Helene. Helene devastated western NC with 72 hours of extensive rainfall which led to severe flooding, thousands of landslides, and widespread forest damage. The forest debris on the ground quickly became a fire risk for the region compounded by the lack of road access to mitigate the risk. Using the LAI CDR data, the nominator’s team quickly analyzed the extent of the forest damage compared to 30-year climatology, which shows ~30% reduction of forest canopy in the region. This information was shared with the State Forest Services of NC which supported their argument to the NC legislature for immediate resources for wildfire risk mitigation. \n\nThis is the first time the nominator felt the direct economic impact of their own research for the local communities in North Carolina using earth science data."
        }
      ]
    },
