          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Wuhan Botanical Garden"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "Vegetation phenology can profoundly modulate the climate-biosphere interactions and thus plays a crucial role in regulating the terrestrial carbon cycle and the climate. However, most previous phenology studies rely on traditional vegetation indices, which are inadequate to characterize the seasonal activity of photosynthesis. An annual vegetation photosynthetic phenology dataset was generated with a spatial resolution of 0.05 degrees from 2001 to 2020, using the latest gross primary productivity product based on solar-induced chlorophyll fluorescence (GOSIF-GPP). Smoothing splines were combined with multiple change-point detection to retrieve the phenology metrics: start of the growing season (SOS), end of the growing season (EOS), and length of growing season (LOS) for terrestrial ecosystems above 30° N latitude (Northern Biomes). The phenology product can be used to validate and develop phenology or carbon cycle models and monitor the climate change impacts on terrestrial ecosystems. (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-023-02224-w)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "This dataset was nominated because it represents a critical advance in understanding and monitoring how ecosystems respond to ongoing climate variability and change. Unlike traditional greenness-based metrics that primarily reflect structural shifts in vegetation canopy, this dataset is built on photosynthetic activity derived from solar-induced chlorophyll fluorescence (SIF)–based gross primary productivity (GPP). This functional perspective allows researchers and decision-makers to more directly track when and where plants are actually fixing carbon. \nThis dataset provides a transformative perspective on how ecosystems respond to climate change by capturing photosynthetic phenology rather than relying solely on greenness indices. Derived from GOSIF-GPP, it offers a continuous, high-resolution record (2001–2020) of the start, end, and length of growing seasons across northern terrestrial ecosystems, including the ability to detect both single and double growing seasons. Such functional indicators are critical for quantifying how warming alters the carbon balance of forests, grasslands, and croplands, and they supply an essential baseline for conservation planning, ecosystem monitoring, and biodiversity protection, especially in high-latitude regions most sensitive to climate change. Without this dataset, the scientific community would lack a consistent, two-decade, spatially comprehensive record of photosynthetic seasonality, leaving major gaps in the ability to attribute climate impacts and project future ecosystem trajectories."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "This dataset was formatted by NETCDF (easy to use in all systems or programming). The nominator uses the Vegetation Photosynthetic Phenology Dataset as a foundation for studying climate–ecosystem interactions in northern regions. Their work focuses on understanding how changes in the timing of photosynthetic activity influence ecosystem carbon dynamics and feedbacks to the climate system. The dataset’s functional basis in GOSIF-GPP allows them to move beyond traditional greenness indices and directly quantify when plants are actually fixing carbon.They typically extract start-of-season, end-of-season, and length-of-season metrics for specific regions of interest, and then compare these with climate drivers such as temperature and precipitation. Having a consistent, spatially detailed, two-decade record enables them to analyze long-term trends while also validating model outputs. This dataset has become an invaluable tool for both research and teaching: They have used it to illustrate to students how phenology reflects the “change” of ecosystems under climate change. Its openness and accessibility make it easy to integrate into workflows and to share with collaborators from different disciplines. Without it, their analyses would rely on less direct proxies of plant activity, limiting both the accuracy of their research and the clarity with which they can communicate ecosystem change to others."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Fang, J., Li, X., Xiao, J., Yan, X., Li, B., & Liu, F. (2023). Vegetation photosynthetic phenology dataset in northern terrestrial ecosystems. Scientific Data, 10(1), 300. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-023-02224-w"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Fang, J. (2022). Find phenology dates of GPP [Computer software]. Zenodo. https://doi.org/10.5281/ZENODO.7266229"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Jing Fang"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Email Address",
          "schema:value": "jingfang@mail.bnu.edu.cn"
        },
