    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4268-6322",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Ted A Scambos",
      "identifier": {
        "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
        "propertyID": "ORCID",
        "value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4268-6322"
      },
      "schema:email": "tascambos@colorado.edu",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:university-of-colorado-boulder-boulder-co-80309"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:university-of-colorado-boulder-boulder-co-80309",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "University of Colorado Boulder, Boulder, CO 80309"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:095",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:095",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:095",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4268-6322"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "ITS_LIVE is mainly about the planet's ice: how it is moving, how it is changing, and what factors affect its stability. By creating a consistently processed, long-term, global database of ice motion in glaciers and ice sheets, it provides an unmatched foundation for evaluating how ice on Earth is evolving. It has been used to assess ice mass balance for Antarctica, for High Mountain Asia, evaluate surging glaciers in High Mountain Asia and Alaska, and track seasonality of ice flow in all parts of the globe. Ancillary derived products such as the ice shelf extent tracking and Greenland outlet glacier retreat outlines provide a basis for more detailed investigations.\n\nOne of the hallmarks of ITS_LIVE is its openness, its ease of use, freedom of access, and lack of any waiting period after new data is processed. ITS_LIVE was built this way from the beginning, with very little changed as this paradigm became the norm for US -funded data. In this sense, it is a 'people' data set as well, providing students and educators with the same data used by leading researchers at the same time and in the same way. \n\nITS_LIVE is a key data set for assessing ongoing global climate change and the link between ice loss and sea level rise. In this sense, it is an important monitoring tool for what is ultimately an economic and humanitarian concern."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator has used the browse tool extensively to monitor areas of active change -- it is very easy to apply, and the selection and plotting tool is fast. Warning: you can easily find yourself spending hours exploring how global ice has changed, or is changing, what glaciers have surged, what ice shelves have new fractures and nascent icebergs about to break away with the browse and download tool. Moreover, the graphics are extractable in a form that can go directly into a presentation or publication. ITS_LIVE's ice velocity data has allowed us to track the ongoing evolution of Antarctic Peninsula glaciers (esp. those in the Larsen B embayment) and along the Amundsen Sea coast, and has led to a potentially important discovery of new acceleration of Thwaites Glacier. The data cube distribution format is netcdf, and therefore is easily incorporated into existing data analysis code.\n\nMoreover, the array of open-source shared code and tools are an impressive leg up for a researcher that wants to investigate some aspect of what ITS_LIVE provides in more detail . The data set is very well-documented and it's all there for downloading on the main site."
        }
      ]
    },
