        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "ITS_LIVE is a global, multi-satellite, near-real-time compilation of ice velocity data for all of Earth's ice areas larger than 5 km^2. The data coverage begins in 1984 with Landsat 4 and continues up to the present, incorporating Landsat 5, 7, 8 and 9, Sentinel 1 and 2, and (planned) NISAR image pairs for ice mapping. Derived products include annual mosaic compilations of major ice regions, ice edge extent maps for Greenland tidewater glaciers and Antarctica's ice shelves, and data cubes of the available image pair data for tiled areas of the globe. \n\nThe data is served from the AWS cloud, and is also available for browse and download through an interactive website that permits time-series data download for any location in the archive [https://its-live.jpl.nasa.gov/app/index.html].\n\nThe data set, in existence since 2019, has now supported dozens of studies of glacier and ice sheet processes, mass balance, and seasonal ice flux variations. The comprehensiveness and ease of access make ITS_LIVE an exceptional global change dataset for science or for education."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "ITS_LIVE is mainly about the planet's ice: how it is moving, how it is changing, and what factors affect its stability. By creating a consistently processed, long-term, global database of ice motion in glaciers and ice sheets, it provides an unmatched foundation for evaluating how ice on Earth is evolving. It has been used to assess ice mass balance for Antarctica, for High Mountain Asia, evaluate surging glaciers in High Mountain Asia and Alaska, and track seasonality of ice flow in all parts of the globe. Ancillary derived products such as the ice shelf extent tracking and Greenland outlet glacier retreat outlines provide a basis for more detailed investigations.\n\nOne of the hallmarks of ITS_LIVE is its openness, its ease of use, freedom of access, and lack of any waiting period after new data is processed. ITS_LIVE was built this way from the beginning, with very little changed as this paradigm became the norm for US -funded data. In this sense, it is a 'people' data set as well, providing students and educators with the same data used by leading researchers at the same time and in the same way. \n\nITS_LIVE is a key data set for assessing ongoing global climate change and the link between ice loss and sea level rise. In this sense, it is an important monitoring tool for what is ultimately an economic and humanitarian concern."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator has used the browse tool extensively to monitor areas of active change -- it is very easy to apply, and the selection and plotting tool is fast. Warning: you can easily find yourself spending hours exploring how global ice has changed, or is changing, what glaciers have surged, what ice shelves have new fractures and nascent icebergs about to break away with the browse and download tool. Moreover, the graphics are extractable in a form that can go directly into a presentation or publication. ITS_LIVE's ice velocity data has allowed us to track the ongoing evolution of Antarctic Peninsula glaciers (esp. those in the Larsen B embayment) and along the Amundsen Sea coast, and has led to a potentially important discovery of new acceleration of Thwaites Glacier. The data cube distribution format is netcdf, and therefore is easily incorporated into existing data analysis code.\n\nMoreover, the array of open-source shared code and tools are an impressive leg up for a researcher that wants to investigate some aspect of what ITS_LIVE provides in more detail . The data set is very well-documented and it's all there for downloading on the main site."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Gardner, Alex, Fahnestock, Mark, & Scambos, Ted. (2022). Measures its_live landsat image-pair glacier and ice sheet surface velocities, version 1 [Dataset]. NASA National Snow and Ice Data Center Distributed Active Archive Center. https://doi.org/10.5067/IMR9D3PEI28U \n\nGardner, Alex, Fahnestock, Mark, & Scambos, Ted. (2022). Measures its_live regional glacier and ice sheet surface velocities, version 1 [Dataset]. NASA National Snow and Ice Data Center Distributed Active Archive Center. https://doi.org/10.5067/6II6VW8LLWJ7 \n\nGardner, A. S., Moholdt, G., Scambos, T., Fahnstock, M., Ligtenberg, S., Van Den Broeke, M., & Nilsson, J. (2018). Increased West Antarctic and unchanged East Antarctic ice discharge over the last 7 years. The Cryosphere, 12(2), 521–547. https://doi.org/10.5194/tc-12-521-2018"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Lei, Y., Gardner, A. S., & Agram, P. (2022). Processing methodology for the ITS_LIVE Sentinel-1 ice velocity products. Earth System Science Data, 14(11), 5111–5137. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-14-5111-2022 \n\nYang, T., Liang, Q., Howat, I., Pang, A., Li, T., Zheng, L., & Cheng, X. (2026). Distinct seasonal flow pattern of byrd glacier, east antarctica. Journal of Geophysical Research: Earth Surface, 131(2), e2025JF008677. https://doi.org/10.1029/2025JF008677 \n\nGreene, C. A., Gardner, A. S., & Andrews, L. C. (2020). Detecting seasonal ice dynamics in satellite images. The Cryosphere, 14(12), 4365–4378. https://doi.org/10.5194/tc-14-4365-2020 \n\nMoon, T. A., Gardner, A. S., Csatho, B., Parmuzin, I., & Fahnestock, M. A. (2020). Rapid reconfiguration of the greenland ice sheet coastal margin. Journal of Geophysical Research: Earth Surface, 125(11), e2020JF005585. https://doi.org/10.1029/2020JF005585 \n\nMa, Y., Wang, Z., Zhang, B., An, J., Geng, H., & Li, F. (2024). The spatiotemporal surface velocity variations and analysis of the amery ice shelf from 2000 to 2022, east antarctica. Remote Sensing, 16(17), 3255. https://doi.org/10.3390/rs16173255 \n\nAlley, K. E., Wild, C. T., Luckman, A., Scambos, T. A., Truffer, M., Pettit, E. C., Muto, A., Wallin, B., Klinger, M., Sutterley, T., Child, S. F., Hulen, C., Lenaerts, J. T. M., Maclennan, M., Keenan, E., & Dunmire, D. (2021). Two decades of dynamic change and progressive destabilization on the Thwaites Eastern Ice Shelf. The Cryosphere, 15(11), 5187–5203. https://doi.org/10.5194/tc-15-5187-2021 \n\nGrinsted, A., Hvidberg, C. S., Lilien, D. A., Rathmann, N. M., Karlsson, N. B., Gerber, T., Kjær, H. A., Vallelonga, P., & Dahl-Jensen, D. (2022). Accelerating ice flow at the onset of the northeast greenland ice stream. Nature Communications, 13(1), 5589. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-022-32999-2"
        },
        {
