      "description": "The Argo dataset is the world’s most comprehensive, freely available, multidisciplinary 25-year record of the changing global ocean interior. Argo is a global array of 4,000 free-drifting profiling floats that measures the temperature and salinity of the upper 2000 m of the ocean. This allows, for the first time, continuous monitoring of the temperature, salinity, and velocity of the upper ocean, with all data being relayed and made publicly available within hours after collection. The array provides 100,000 temperature/salinity profiles per year distributed over the global oceans at an average of 3-degree spacing, as well as float-trajectory-based velocity from the parking depth.. Some floats provide additional bio-geochemical (BGC) parameters such as oxygen or chlorophyll. All data collected by Argo floats are publicly available in near real-time via the Global Data Assembly Centers (GDACs) in Brest (France) and Monterey (California) after an automated quality control (QC), and in scientifically quality controlled form, delayed mode data, via the GDACs within six months of collection. Over three million high-quality profiles, distributed through the GDACs, provide a harmonised view of how the ocean regulates weather, climate, and biogeochemical cycles, while sustaining biodiversity and the global economy.\n\nBy 2030, Argo will observe more than 90% of the ocean volume through 3 enhancing extensions: Core and Polar Argo (boundary currents and under-ice regions); Deep Argo (to the seafloor); and BGC-Argo (oxygen, pH, nitrate, chlorophyll, suspended particles, and irradiance). Emerging applications include new insights into ocean mixing, bathymetry, sediment transport, and ecosystem resilience. \n\nThe Argo dataset underpins scientific advances and societal applications, from operational forecasting and seasonal-to-decadal prediction, digital-twin development to studies of ocean circulation, stratification, water cycle intensification, deoxygenation, and acidification. It enables breakthroughs in energy, sea level, and carbon budgets and early warning systems for marine extremes, linking process understanding to prediction and long-term climate change. This cost-effective, sustained dataset equips researchers and decision-makers with the knowledge to navigate environmental change and safeguard our ocean and planet.\n\nThierry et al. (2025). Advancing ocean monitoring and knowledge for societal benefit: the urgency to expand Argo to OneArgo by 2030. Front. Mar. Sci. 12:1593904.doi:10.3389/fmars.2025.1593904\n\nArgo website: https://argo.ucsd.edu/",
