    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:096",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:096",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:096",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-5535-9362",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9144-4368",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5100-4595"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The Ocean Prediction Center is responsible for protecting life and property at sea and defending the resilience of our nation's global supply chains carrying on America's $360B+ worth of international maritime trade and fisheries. Their mission depends on their operational marine weather models that depend on forcing and energy exchange with the ocean. Argo is the most persistent, present, accurate and reliable oceanographic sensor measuring the key parameters in the ocean from the surface to a significant depth. The very ocean description that forces the atmosphere above it. Argo consistently and significantly drives more accuracy into their models which drives more accuracy into our life and property saving mission."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "Since the start of the Argo program some 25 years ago, Argo has transformed understanding of the ocean and of changing properties of the ocean. Argo has demonstrated that most of the excess heat stored by the Earth system is contained in the top 2000 m of the ocean, enabling the closure of the Earth's energy budget and clarifying the relative roles of warming and ice melt in driving sea level rise. Every day, Argo provides near-real-time information about ocean temperature and salt, as well as biogeochemical variables, supplying critical information for ocean and atmospheric forecasting. Argo information has helped drive operational forecast improvements, and at the same time, it has provided reliable information to advance understanding of the mechanisms that drive ocean physics and chemistry. The Argo program is coordinated through a visionary international coordination effort that shares responsibility for a global common good and releases data for all users."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "3",
          "schema:text": "Before Argo data, the scientific community’s view of the ocean interior resembled an old, incomplete map, biased toward ship routes and northern summers. Although it was well known that the ocean was central to Earth’s climate, only limited glimpses of its vast interior were available. Quantifying global change was an exercise in extrapolation. Forecasting was constrained by limited knowledge of the subsurface ocean, the hidden driver of weather, climate, ecosystems and biodiversity.\n\nThe Argo dataset transformed this picture. With a global fleet of robotic profiling floats, the scientific community gained a continuous, all-season, subsurface view of the changing ocean for the first time over the past 25 years. It changed what can be observed, known, and done across all three AGU dimensions of impact.\n\nPlanet – from inference to direct observation\nArgo enables more precise quantification of the ocean’s uptake of ~90% of Earth’s excess heat and ~25% of anthropogenic carbon. It supports monitoring of ocean warming (thermosteric) contributions to global sea level rise, water cycle intensification, increasing acidification, deoxygenation and marine extremes. Without Argo, it would not be possible to create detailed near real time maps of these accelerating changes or constrain our planet's energy, sea level and carbon budgets.\n\nPeople – from limited forecasts to actionable foresight\nThis planetary knowledge directly protects lives and livelihoods. Before Argo, seasonal predictions of phenomena such as ENSO suffered from poor initialisation. Assimilating Argo profiles has delivered forecast model improvements equivalent to more than a decade of research development, enhancing preparedness for droughts, floods, and heatwaves. Argo now informs predictions of hurricane intensity, sea-level rise, and early warnings for marine extremes and potential tipping points, transforming scientific insight into practical resilience. Furthermore, its freely accessible data democratises ocean science education and capacity building worldwide.\n\nProsperity – from static management to dynamic sustainability\nThis deeper knowledge of the ocean and climate underpins global prosperity. Before Argo, management of fisheries and marine resources relied on historical assumptions. Today, Argo’s continuous data reveal shifting habitats and productivity, supporting sustainable fisheries and blue economy planning. As societies explore marine carbon dioxide removal and other climate interventions, the physical and biogeochemical baselines provided by Argo are essential for assessing effectiveness and risk with scientific integrity. \n\nWithout Argo, marine conditions could not be forecast and the ocean’s role in climate could not be quantified with the confidence we have today, nor could it provide the observational foundation for the digital twins and model projections that will guide 21st century decision-making. Society would be navigating the most critical challenge of our time with a map from the last century. Argo has not just filled data gaps. It has created a living, open record of the ocean’s interior – our planet's engine room – empowering greater capacity to understand, predict, and protect our future."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "NWS Environmental Modeling Center ingests the data into their data assimilation system which then provides a critical boundary condition for global and regional marine weather models which then drive the nominator’s life and property mission saving services at sea."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "The nominator uses Argo data in their research and teaching. In their work, they use individual profiles and also take advantage of software tools and gridded products that open a variety of ways to access the data. Argo data provide a means to let students examine the impact of a hurricane in mixing the upper ocean. The data have also provided critical information for their own work to demonstrate the patterns of ocean warming, freshening, and acidification in the Southern Ocean. One aspect of their work has used modern Argo data as a reference against which to compare archived pre-Argo measurements, providing direct insight into long-term changes stretching back before Argo. Although traveling back in time is not possible, the availability of high-quality present-day data allows meaningful analysis of historical data, which in turn supplies tools that support more actionable decisions about the future."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "3",
          "schema:text": "The Argo dataset has been the cornerstone of the nominator’s science career on ocean warming, sea level rise, and the fingerprints of natural and human-driven change. Their first work (Domingues, Church et al., Nature 2008), continually updated with Argo data, has informed successive IPCC assessments (2013, 2019, 2021) and the next UNEP Global Environmental Outlook. It has also contributed to State of the Climate reports and US EPA and Climate Tracker’s Global Change Indicators, providing continuity between IPCC cycles.\n\nThe nominator is now a co-chair of the International Association for the Physical Sciences of the Oceans (IAPSO) Best-Practices community to benchmark Argo gridded products and evaluate uncertainty. Through mentoring, they help early-career scientists use Argo to track changes in water masses and circulation, and to communicate the ocean’s role in climate to the public. Their recent book chapter translates these advances into accessible narratives, bridging science, policy, and education.\n\nEven while caring for their parents and on their extended medical leave, they found themselves explaining Argo to hospital staff, drivers, and neighbours. People were amazed to learn that thousands of robotic floats silently watch over our ocean and our future.\n\nFor the nominator, Argo is more than a dataset: it is the foundation linking ocean climate science and global accountability. If one wants to understand climate change, look no further than the ocean because it holds the story of our planet’s past, present, and future."
        }
      ]
    },
