        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The Argo dataset is the world’s most comprehensive, freely available, multidisciplinary 25-year record of the changing global ocean interior. Argo is a global array of 4,000 free-drifting profiling floats that measures the temperature and salinity of the upper 2000 m of the ocean. This allows, for the first time, continuous monitoring of the temperature, salinity, and velocity of the upper ocean, with all data being relayed and made publicly available within hours after collection. The array provides 100,000 temperature/salinity profiles per year distributed over the global oceans at an average of 3-degree spacing, as well as float-trajectory-based velocity from the parking depth.. Some floats provide additional bio-geochemical (BGC) parameters such as oxygen or chlorophyll. All data collected by Argo floats are publicly available in near real-time via the Global Data Assembly Centers (GDACs) in Brest (France) and Monterey (California) after an automated quality control (QC), and in scientifically quality controlled form, delayed mode data, via the GDACs within six months of collection. Over three million high-quality profiles, distributed through the GDACs, provide a harmonised view of how the ocean regulates weather, climate, and biogeochemical cycles, while sustaining biodiversity and the global economy.\n\nBy 2030, Argo will observe more than 90% of the ocean volume through 3 enhancing extensions: Core and Polar Argo (boundary currents and under-ice regions); Deep Argo (to the seafloor); and BGC-Argo (oxygen, pH, nitrate, chlorophyll, suspended particles, and irradiance). Emerging applications include new insights into ocean mixing, bathymetry, sediment transport, and ecosystem resilience. \n\nThe Argo dataset underpins scientific advances and societal applications, from operational forecasting and seasonal-to-decadal prediction, digital-twin development to studies of ocean circulation, stratification, water cycle intensification, deoxygenation, and acidification. It enables breakthroughs in energy, sea level, and carbon budgets and early warning systems for marine extremes, linking process understanding to prediction and long-term climate change. This cost-effective, sustained dataset equips researchers and decision-makers with the knowledge to navigate environmental change and safeguard our ocean and planet.\n\nThierry et al. (2025). Advancing ocean monitoring and knowledge for societal benefit: the urgency to expand Argo to OneArgo by 2030. Front. Mar. Sci. 12:1593904.doi:10.3389/fmars.2025.1593904\n\nArgo website: https://argo.ucsd.edu/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nThe Ocean Prediction Center is responsible for protecting life and property at sea and defending the resilience of our nation's global supply chains carrying on America's $360B+ worth of international maritime trade and fisheries. Their mission depends on their operational marine weather models that depend on forcing and energy exchange with the ocean. Argo is the most persistent, present, accurate and reliable oceanographic sensor measuring the key parameters in the ocean from the surface to a significant depth. The very ocean description that forces the atmosphere above it. Argo consistently and significantly drives more accuracy into their models which drives more accuracy into our life and property saving mission. \n\nNominator 2:\nSince the start of the Argo program some 25 years ago, Argo has transformed understanding of the ocean and of changing properties of the ocean. Argo has demonstrated that most of the excess heat stored by the Earth system is contained in the top 2000 m of the ocean, enabling the closure of the Earth's energy budget and clarifying the relative roles of warming and ice melt in driving sea level rise. Every day, Argo provides near-real-time information about ocean temperature and salt, as well as biogeochemical variables, supplying critical information for ocean and atmospheric forecasting. Argo information has helped drive operational forecast improvements, and at the same time, it has provided reliable information to advance understanding of the mechanisms that drive ocean physics and chemistry. The Argo program is coordinated through a visionary international coordination effort that shares responsibility for a global common good and releases data for all users.\n\nNominator 3:\nBefore Argo data, the scientific community’s view of the ocean interior resembled an old, incomplete map, biased toward ship routes and northern summers. Although it was well known that the ocean was central to Earth’s climate, only limited glimpses of its vast interior were available. Quantifying global change was an exercise in extrapolation. Forecasting was constrained by limited knowledge of the subsurface ocean, the hidden driver of weather, climate, ecosystems and biodiversity.\n\nThe Argo dataset transformed this picture. With a global fleet of robotic profiling floats, the scientific community gained a continuous, all-season, subsurface view of the changing ocean for the first time over the past 25 years. It changed what can be observed, known, and done across all three AGU dimensions of impact.\n\nPlanet – from inference to direct observation\nArgo enables more precise quantification of the ocean’s uptake of ~90% of Earth’s excess heat and ~25% of anthropogenic carbon. It supports monitoring of ocean warming (thermosteric) contributions to global sea level rise, water cycle intensification, increasing acidification, deoxygenation and marine extremes. Without Argo, it would not be possible to create detailed near real time maps of these accelerating changes or constrain our planet's energy, sea level and carbon budgets.\n\nPeople – from limited forecasts to actionable foresight\nThis planetary knowledge directly protects lives and livelihoods. Before Argo, seasonal predictions of phenomena such as ENSO suffered from poor initialisation. Assimilating Argo profiles has delivered forecast model improvements equivalent to more than a decade of research development, enhancing preparedness for droughts, floods, and heatwaves. Argo now informs predictions of hurricane intensity, sea-level rise, and early warnings for marine extremes and potential tipping points, transforming scientific insight into practical resilience. Furthermore, its freely accessible data democratises ocean science education and capacity building worldwide.\n\nProsperity – from static management to dynamic sustainability\nThis deeper knowledge of the ocean and climate underpins global prosperity. Before Argo, management of fisheries and marine resources relied on historical assumptions. Today, Argo’s continuous data reveal shifting habitats and productivity, supporting sustainable fisheries and blue economy planning. As societies explore marine carbon dioxide removal and other climate interventions, the physical and biogeochemical baselines provided by Argo are essential for assessing effectiveness and risk with scientific integrity. \n\nWithout Argo, marine conditions could not be forecast and the ocean’s role in climate could not be quantified with the confidence we have today, nor could it provide the observational foundation for the digital twins and model projections that will guide 21st century decision-making. Society would be navigating the most critical challenge of our time with a map from the last century. Argo has not just filled data gaps. It has created a living, open record of the ocean’s interior – our planet's engine room – empowering greater capacity to understand, predict, and protect our future."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nNWS Environmental Modeling Center ingests the data into their data assimilation system which then provides a critical boundary condition for global and regional marine weather models which then drive the nominator’s life and property mission saving services at sea. \n\nNominator 2:\nThe nominator uses Argo data in their research and teaching. In their work, they use individual profiles and also take advantage of software tools and gridded products that open a variety of ways to access the data. Argo data provide a means to let students examine the impact of a hurricane in mixing the upper ocean. The data have also provided critical information for their own work to demonstrate the patterns of ocean warming, freshening, and acidification in the Southern Ocean. One aspect of their work has used modern Argo data as a reference against which to compare archived pre-Argo measurements, providing direct insight into long-term changes stretching back before Argo. Although traveling back in time is not possible, the availability of high-quality present-day data allows meaningful analysis of historical data, which in turn supplies tools that support more actionable decisions about the future.\n\nNominator 3:\nThe Argo dataset has been the cornerstone of the nominator’s science career on ocean warming, sea level rise, and the fingerprints of natural and human-driven change. Their first work (Domingues, Church et al., Nature 2008), continually updated with Argo data, has informed successive IPCC assessments (2013, 2019, 2021) and the next UNEP Global Environmental Outlook. It has also contributed to State of the Climate reports and US EPA and Climate Tracker’s Global Change Indicators, providing continuity between IPCC cycles.\n\nThe nominator is now a co-chair of the International Association for the Physical Sciences of the Oceans (IAPSO) Best-Practices community to benchmark Argo gridded products and evaluate uncertainty. Through mentoring, they help early-career scientists use Argo to track changes in water masses and circulation, and to communicate the ocean’s role in climate to the public. Their recent book chapter translates these advances into accessible narratives, bridging science, policy, and education.\n\nEven while caring for their parents and on their extended medical leave, they found themselves explaining Argo to hospital staff, drivers, and neighbours. People were amazed to learn that thousands of robotic floats silently watch over our ocean and our future.\n\nFor the nominator, Argo is more than a dataset: it is the foundation linking ocean climate science and global accountability. If one wants to understand climate change, look no further than the ocean because it holds the story of our planet’s past, present, and future."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Wong, A. P. S., et al. (2020). Argo data 1999–2019: Two million temperature-salinity profiles and subsurface velocity observations from a global array of profiling floats. Frontiers in Marine Science, 7, 700. https://doi.org/10.3389/fmars.2020.00700 \n\nThierry, V., et al. (2025). Advancing ocean monitoring and knowledge for societal benefit: The urgency to expand Argo to OneArgo by 2030. Frontiers in Marine Science, 12, 1593904. https://doi.org/10.3389/fmars.2025.1593904 \n\nRiser, S. C., et al. (2016). Fifteen years of ocean observations with the global Argo array. Nature Climate Change, 6(2), 145–153. https://doi.org/10.1038/nclimate2872 \n\nRoemmich, D., et al. (2019). On the future of argo: A global, full-depth, multi-disciplinary array. Frontiers in Marine Science, 6, 439. https://doi.org/10.3389/fmars.2019.00439 \n \nRoemmich, D., et al. (2021). The Technological, Scientific, and Sociological Revolution of GlobaSubsurface Ocean Observing. Oceanography, 34(4), 2-8. https://doi.org/10.5670/oceanog.2021.supplement.02-02"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Argo Bibliography: https://argo.ucsd.edu/outreach/publications/bibliography/\nArgo data products catalogued here: Argo data products | Argo and BGC: Biogeochemical Argo | Data Products\nArgo Data Visualizations: https://argo.ucsd.edu/data/data-visualizations/\nArgo Data Operational Uses: https://www.aoml.noaa.gov/argo/\nOceanPredict’s use of Argo data: Observations table(s) (operational centre usage) - Ocean Predict: https://oceanpredict.org/observations-use/#section-argo-profiling-floats \n\nThe Argo program maintains a bibliography demonstrating the remarkable impact of the data: https://argo.ucsd.edu/outreach/publications/bibliography/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nA.J. Reiss\n\nNominator 2:\nSarah Gille\n\nNominator 3:\nCatia M Domingues"
