    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:098",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "Water Quality Portal",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:098",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:ocean-science-hydrology-cryosphere"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "US Geological Survey, US Enviornmental Protection Agency"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://www.waterqualitydata.us/beta/"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:hosted-by-usgs-aws-hosted"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "wqp@usgs.gov"
        }
      ],
      "description": "The Water Quality Portal (https://www.waterqualitydata.us/) is the largest water quality data warehouse in the United States containing over 400 million water quality records from over one million locations nationally sourced from over 1,000 water quality data providers, including every state, territory, and over 100 tribes and Nations. The information served by the Water Quality Portal is used to make Clean Water Act decisions such as “Can I swim in the water or eat the fish?”, helps inform scientific studies and advancements in academia, and informs State and Federal actions such as compliance monitoring and targeting and the establishment of permits and water quality standards. The Water Quality Portal was launched in 2012 after a MOU was signed between the United States Environmental Protection Agency (USEPA) and United States Geological Survey (USGS) in 2008, the realization of a vision of unified data management to better support water-quality decision making nationally by delivering data in a unified format on one platform. \nThe Water Quality Portal is currently funded by both the USGS and USEPA. Both agencies also contribute to a team of domain experts that set the vision and direction of development activities. Mission-critical USEPA programs implemented at the national scale depend on the Water Quality Portal data warehouse which is owned and configured by USGS. In addition, we have built a robust user community made up of data providers and users who meet monthly to help guide the direction of development activities. This data warehouse is guided by users and data submitters at every step. \nThe Water Quality Portal has two primary audiences: those who use the data housed in the Water Quality Portal directly, and the broader public who benefits from Water Quality Portal’s data offerings through secondary applications. Environmental professionals are the most frequent users of the Water Quality Portal application and services; these users rely on the Water Quality Portal to obtain data from multiple providers, increasing data access and saving time in data manipulation. Examples of such users are State Environmental Agency employees, USEPA Clean Water Act Programs, Tribal Environmental Programs, academic researchers, and Citizen Science organizations; these users have also formed a user community that communicates regularly with both USEPA and USGS. These environmental professionals have shared multiple success stories of how the Water Quality Portal has dramatically improved workflows, freeing personnel previously involved in data aggregation to instead focus on data analysis and policy implementation. The New Jersey Department of Environmental Protection, for example, reported that data collection and aggregation that used to take staff three to five days now takes seconds, allowing staff to focus more efforts on data analysis. The success of the Water Quality Portal has resulted in many organizations committing to serve data through this platform, increasing in scale from serving data from tens of organizations at launch to over a thousand organizations currently. Perhaps most notably, some organizations have committed to resubmitting years of historic data so that they can use that data in the Water Quality Portal based reporting workflows. Enhancing the performance of the Water Quality Portal will allow more environmental professionals to use the data and services offered by this product, allowing for more comprehensive and representative studies and analyses. In addition, the states performing Clean Water Act assessments would greatly benefit from more efficient data retrievals, allowing agencies to spend less time retrieving and compiling data and more time on other important steps in the assessment process. \n The Water Quality Portal serves the broader public by making data easily findable and available in a standardized format; this enables powerful public communication tools to be built off Water Quality Portal’s services. One example is the “How’s My Waterway” application, which reaches approximately 20,000 unique users per month. The “How’s My Waterway” application (https://www.epa.gov/waterdata/hows-my-waterway) allows for members of the public to interactively explore water resources by leveraging Water Quality Portal’s web services and determine if their water is safe for drinking, recreating, and fishing. This application relies on the Water Quality Portal to provide essential context for the information provided. For example, a member of the public may use “How’s My Waterway” and see their waterbody is impaired for fishing purposes; they then can look at the data in the Water Quality Portal to see the exact results of mercury analyses to understand the context for this listing. As the Water Quality Portal becomes more usable for professionals, we expect that additional tools for the public will be created that can leverage the powerful services and nation-wide coverage offered. Currently, the Water Quality Portal’s web services support mapping and other visualizations at the community scale in “How’s My Waterway”; in the future, with these requested funds, the updated architecture should be able to support regional and national scale mapping and statistics, trends, and visualizations. \nThe nation-wide data coverage has benefitted environmental professionals focused on various scales; in addition, other users have come to benefit from the Water Quality Portal. Big data users have found ways to ingest water-quality data for novel use cases, from industry (oil and gas) to manufacturing (hair care products). We commonly hear from new users finding the Water Quality Portal with new questions and use cases for these data. We expect rearchitecting data will make them more discoverable, and that more users will continue to find the Water Quality Portal useful for their purposes and find new and innovative ways to leverage these data for various purposes.\n\nThe Water Quality Portal’s success is evident in the growth in data, users, and anecdotes of saved labor; this application needs refactoring to accommodate growth and meet growing user interest. Currently the Water Quality Portal is well utilized, with over 5,200 unique users hitting the user interface or web services each day. This application has become vitally important to a growing number of users who rely on the Water Quality Portal to access water quality data, saving them time and streamlining their workflows. As interest in the Water Quality Portal has increased, so have the amount of data being submitted. Each day, more samples are added to the database, allowing the public to better understand in real-time the conditions of their water and challenges facing our natural resources.",
      "reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "https://www.waterqualitydata.us/beta/publications/",
          "schema:url": [
