    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:099",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "Catchment Attributes and MEteorology for Large-sample Studies (CAMELS)",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:099",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:ocean-science-hydrology-cryosphere"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Part 1: A. J. Newman, M. P. Clark, K. Sampson, A. Wood, L. E. Hay, A. Bock, R. J. Viger, D. Blodgett, L. Brekke, J. R. Arnold, T. Hopson, and Q. Duan"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Part 2: N. Addor, A. Newman, M. Mizukami, and M. P. Clark"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://ral.ucar.edu/solutions/products/camels"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:gdex-geoscience-data-exchange"
      ],
      "description": "The CAMELS data is mainly aimed at enabling hydrologic research. It consists of two parts: the first part (Newman et al., 2015) contains time series of daily meteorological data, daily streamflow observations and model simulations of streamflow for 671 small- to medium-sized basins across the contiguous United States. The original focus of this work was to (1) present the data set for community use and (2) provide a model performance benchmark using the coupled Snow-17 snow model and the Sacramento Soil Moisture Accounting Model, calibrated using the shuffled complex evolution global optimization routine. The second part contains so-called catchment attributes; statistical descriptors of each basin's topography, climate, streamflow, land cover, soil, and geology. The large number of catchments, combined with the diversity of the attributes we extracted, makes the combined data set well suited for large-sample studies and comparative hydrology.\n\nText partially copied from the abstracts of the two papers describing data set development:\n\nNewman, A. J., et al. (2015). Development of a large-sample watershed-scale hydrometeorological data set for the contiguous USA: Data set characteristics and assessment of regional variability in hydrologic model performance. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 19(1), 209–223. https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-19-209-2015 \n\nAddor, N., Newman, A. J., Mizukami, N., & Clark, M. P. (2017). The CAMELS data set: Catchment attributes and meteorology for large-sample studies. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 21(10), 5293–5313. https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-21-5293-2017",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
