      "name": "University of Calgary, 2500 University Drive NW, Calgary, Alberta, T2N 1N4, Canada"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:099",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:099",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:099",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8301-3787"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The CAMELS data set is worthy of inclusion in this commentary due to its enormous impact on the hydrologic sciences in the short period of time it has been published. Its publication was a catalyst for three important changes in hydrological research: first, it shifted emphasis from the idiosyncrasies of individual basins to large-domain patterns; second, it provided a testing ground that established modern machine learning in the hydrologic sciences; third, it inspired the community to produce various follow up data sets, rapidly expanding the data available to hydrologists.\n\nThe publication of the CAMELS data coincides with an increased societal need for effective predictions of floods and droughts. This required a shift from understanding the idiosyncrasies of individual basins to understanding and prediction of hydrologic behaviour across large geographical domains. While a similar data set already existed (MOPEX) and another one was published at the same time (CANOPEX), it is the publication of the CAMELS data set that marks a rapid increase in hydrologic studies focusing on large geographical domains. \n\nPart of the success of the CAMELS data is that its publication also coincided with a rapid increase in machine learning capabilities. Such approaches require large amounts of easily ingestible data, and the CAMELS data provided this. Early hydrologic implementations of Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks used the CAMELS data as a testing ground, and have in a short time become established as incredibly good methods for flood forecasting (see for example Google Flood Hub). \n\nHowever, there are limits to what can be learned from one data set. The final piece of the CAMELS success is that it inspired numerous follow-up data releases. Initially, these data sets mimicked the original data but covered different regions: CAMELS-like data sets are now available for Chile, Brazil, Great Britain, Australia, China, Switzerland, Sweden, Germany, France, Denmark and India, as well as an aggregation of a number of these into a harmonized data set called CARAVAN. Extensions also focus on adding different data types: the CAMELS-Chem data extends the original data set with records of atmospheric deposition and common stream water chemistry constituents, whereas the QUADICA data provides large-sample water quality data for Germany. Finally, there is a growing number of data sets that expend the original concept into time and space, by providing data at higher temporal and/or spatial resolution. Examples here include two datasets for hourly streamflow across the United States, and spatially distributed CAMELS-like data sets for Central Europe, Iceland and a combined coverage of the United States and Canada. \n\nThese developments promote international collaboration, open sharing of data, and enable new research avenues. The CAMELS data set stands at the origin of this, and I believe this makes it worthy of a mention in this commentary."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator has used the CAMELS data in their own work to investigate the merits of different types of hydrologic models. The CAMELS data provided an easy-to-use way to test their models across many hundreds of basins, and provided the means to try and categorize those basins based on shared characteristics. This work was part of their PhD degree, and without the data they would not have been able to get everything done in time. In later work, they and their colleagues have revisited those results to investigate model selection under uncertainty. The CAMELS data also provided the basis of a new data set their team developed, which extends that CAMELS concept in time and space with the goal of enabling large-domain model simulations for more process-based models. \n\nOne thing the nominator particularly likes is the community of large-sample data users and developers that has emerged after the publication of the CAMELS data set. There is a recurring session at the EGU General Assembly where new developments are shown and shared, and this session is typically a highlight of the conference."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:099",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 100,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Ocean Science, Hydrology, Cryosphere"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "Catchment Attributes and MEteorology for Large-sample Studies (CAMELS)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Part 1: A. J. Newman, M. P. Clark, K. Sampson, A. Wood, L. E. Hay, A. Bock, R. J. Viger, D. Blodgett, L. Brekke, J. R. Arnold, T. Hopson, and Q. Duan\n\nPart 2: N. Addor, A. Newman, M. Mizukami, and M. P. Clark"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://ral.ucar.edu/solutions/products/camels"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "GDEX Geoscience Data Exchange"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.5065/D6MW2F4D"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The CAMELS data is mainly aimed at enabling hydrologic research. It consists of two parts: the first part (Newman et al., 2015) contains time series of daily meteorological data, daily streamflow observations and model simulations of streamflow for 671 small- to medium-sized basins across the contiguous United States. The original focus of this work was to (1) present the data set for community use and (2) provide a model performance benchmark using the coupled Snow-17 snow model and the Sacramento Soil Moisture Accounting Model, calibrated using the shuffled complex evolution global optimization routine. The second part contains so-called catchment attributes; statistical descriptors of each basin's topography, climate, streamflow, land cover, soil, and geology. The large number of catchments, combined with the diversity of the attributes we extracted, makes the combined data set well suited for large-sample studies and comparative hydrology.\n\nText partially copied from the abstracts of the two papers describing data set development:\n\nNewman, A. J., et al. (2015). Development of a large-sample watershed-scale hydrometeorological data set for the contiguous USA: Data set characteristics and assessment of regional variability in hydrologic model performance. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 19(1), 209–223. https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-19-209-2015 \n\nAddor, N., Newman, A. J., Mizukami, N., & Clark, M. P. (2017). The CAMELS data set: Catchment attributes and meteorology for large-sample studies. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 21(10), 5293–5313. https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-21-5293-2017"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The CAMELS data set is worthy of inclusion in this commentary due to its enormous impact on the hydrologic sciences in the short period of time it has been published. Its publication was a catalyst for three important changes in hydrological research: first, it shifted emphasis from the idiosyncrasies of individual basins to large-domain patterns; second, it provided a testing ground that established modern machine learning in the hydrologic sciences; third, it inspired the community to produce various follow up data sets, rapidly expanding the data available to hydrologists.\n\nThe publication of the CAMELS data coincides with an increased societal need for effective predictions of floods and droughts. This required a shift from understanding the idiosyncrasies of individual basins to understanding and prediction of hydrologic behaviour across large geographical domains. While a similar data set already existed (MOPEX) and another one was published at the same time (CANOPEX), it is the publication of the CAMELS data set that marks a rapid increase in hydrologic studies focusing on large geographical domains. \n\nPart of the success of the CAMELS data is that its publication also coincided with a rapid increase in machine learning capabilities. Such approaches require large amounts of easily ingestible data, and the CAMELS data provided this. Early hydrologic implementations of Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks used the CAMELS data as a testing ground, and have in a short time become established as incredibly good methods for flood forecasting (see for example Google Flood Hub). \n\nHowever, there are limits to what can be learned from one data set. The final piece of the CAMELS success is that it inspired numerous follow-up data releases. Initially, these data sets mimicked the original data but covered different regions: CAMELS-like data sets are now available for Chile, Brazil, Great Britain, Australia, China, Switzerland, Sweden, Germany, France, Denmark and India, as well as an aggregation of a number of these into a harmonized data set called CARAVAN. Extensions also focus on adding different data types: the CAMELS-Chem data extends the original data set with records of atmospheric deposition and common stream water chemistry constituents, whereas the QUADICA data provides large-sample water quality data for Germany. Finally, there is a growing number of data sets that expend the original concept into time and space, by providing data at higher temporal and/or spatial resolution. Examples here include two datasets for hourly streamflow across the United States, and spatially distributed CAMELS-like data sets for Central Europe, Iceland and a combined coverage of the United States and Canada. \n\nThese developments promote international collaboration, open sharing of data, and enable new research avenues. The CAMELS data set stands at the origin of this, and I believe this makes it worthy of a mention in this commentary."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator has used the CAMELS data in their own work to investigate the merits of different types of hydrologic models. The CAMELS data provided an easy-to-use way to test their models across many hundreds of basins, and provided the means to try and categorize those basins based on shared characteristics. This work was part of their PhD degree, and without the data they would not have been able to get everything done in time. In later work, they and their colleagues have revisited those results to investigate model selection under uncertainty. The CAMELS data also provided the basis of a new data set their team developed, which extends that CAMELS concept in time and space with the goal of enabling large-domain model simulations for more process-based models. \n\nOne thing the nominator particularly likes is the community of large-sample data users and developers that has emerged after the publication of the CAMELS data set. There is a recurring session at the EGU General Assembly where new developments are shown and shared, and this session is typically a highlight of the conference."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Newman, A. J.,et al. (2015). Development of a large-sample watershed-scale hydrometeorological data set for the contiguous USA: Data set characteristics and assessment of regional variability in hydrologic model performance. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 19(1), 209–223. https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-19-209-2015 \n\nAddor, N., Newman, A. J., Mizukami, N., & Clark, M. P. (2017). The CAMELS data set: Catchment attributes and meteorology for large-sample studies. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 21(10), 5293–5313. https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-21-5293-2017"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": ">>> Follow-up datasets inspired by or extending CAMELS:\n\nAlvarez-Garreton, C., et al. (2018). The CAMELS-CL dataset: Catchment attributes and meteorology for large sample studies – Chile dataset. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 22(11), 5817–5846. https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-22-5817-2018 \n\nChagas, V. B. P., et al. (2020). CAMELS-BR: Hydrometeorological time series and landscape attributes for 897 catchments in Brazil. Earth System Science Data, 12(3), 2075–2096. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-12-2075-2020 \n\nAlmagro, A., Oliveira, P. T. S., Meira Neto, A. A., Roy, T., & Troch, P. (2021). CABra: A novel large-sample dataset for Brazilian catchments. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 25(6), 3105–3135. https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-25-3105-2021 \n\nCoxon, G., et al. (2020). CAMELS-GB: Hydrometeorological time series and landscape attributes for 671 catchments in Great Britain. Earth System Science Data, 12(4), 2459–2483. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-12-2459-2020 \n\nFowler, K. J. A., Acharya, S. C., Addor, N., Chou, C., & Peel, M. C. (2021). CAMELS-AUS: Hydrometeorological time series and landscape attributes for 222 catchments in Australia. Earth System Science Data, 13(8), 3847–3867. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-13-3847-2021 \n\nHao, Z., Jin, J., Xia, R., Tian, S., Yang, W., Liu, Q., Zhu, M., Ma, T., Jing, C., & Zhang, Y. (2021). Ccam: China catchment attributes and meteorology dataset. Earth System Science Data, 13(12), 5591–5616. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-13-5591-2021 \n\nKlingler, C., Schulz, K., & Herrnegger, M. (2021). Lamah-ce: Large-sample data for hydrology and environmental sciences for central europe. Earth System Science Data, 13(9), 4529–4565. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-13-4529-2021 \n\nEbeling, P., et al. (2022). Quadica: Water quality, discharge and catchment attributes for large-sample studies in Germany. Earth System Science Data, 14(8), 3715–3741. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-14-3715-2022 \n\nKratzert, F., et al. (2023). Caravan—A global community dataset for large-sample hydrology. Scientific Data, 10(1), 61. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-023-01975-w \n\nHöge, M., et al. (2023). CAMELS-CH: Hydro-meteorological time series and landscape attributes for 331 catchments in hydrologic Switzerland. Earth System Science Data, 15(12), 5755–5784. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-15-5755-2023 \n\nTeutschbein, C. (2024). CAMELS-SE: Long-term hydroclimatic observations (1961–2020) across 50 catchments in sweden as a resource for modelling, education, and collaboration. Geoscience Data Journal, gdj3.239. https://doi.org/10.1002/gdj3.239 \n\nHelgason, H. B., & Nijssen, B. (2024). Lamah-ice: Large-sample data for hydrology and environmental sciences for iceland. Earth System Science Data, 16(6), 2741–2771. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-16-2741-2024 \n\nLoritz, R., et al. (2024). CAMELS-DE: Hydro-meteorological time series and attributes for 1582 catchments in Germany. Earth System Science Data, 16(12), 5625–5642. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-16-5625-2024 \n\nSterle, G., et al. (2024). CAMELS-Chem: Augmenting CAMELS (Catchment attributes and meteorology for large-sample studies) with atmospheric and stream water chemistry data. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 28(3), 611–630. https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-28-611-2024 \n\nDelaigue, O., Guimarães, G. M., Brigode, P., Génot, B., Perrin, C., Soubeyroux, J.-M., Janet, B., Addor, N., & Andréassian, V. (2025). CAMELS-FR dataset: A large-sample hydroclimatic dataset for France to explore hydrological diversity and support model benchmarking. Earth System Science Data, 17(4), 1461–1479. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-17-1461-2025 \n\nFowler, K. J. A., Zhang, Z., & Hou, X. (2025). CAMELS-AUS v2: Updated hydrometeorological time series and landscape attributes for an enlarged set of catchments in Australia. Earth System Science Data, 17(8), 4079–4095. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-17-4079-2025 \n\nLiu, J., et al. (2025). CAMELS-DK: Hydrometeorological time series and landscape attributes for 3330 Danish catchments with streamflow observations from 304 gauged stations. Earth System Science Data, 17(4), 1551–1572. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-17-1551-2025 \n\nMangukiya, N. K., Kumar, K. B., Dey, P., Sharma, S., Bejagam, V., Mujumdar, P. P., & Sharma, A. (2025). CAMELS-IND: Hydrometeorological time series and catchment attributes for 228 catchments in Peninsular India. Earth System Science Data, 17(2), 461–491. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-17-461-2025 \n\nFärber, C., Plessow, H., Mischel, S., Kratzert, F., Addor, N., Shalev, G., & Looser, U. (2024). GRDC-caravan: Extending caravan with data from the global runoff data centre. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-2024-427 \n\nKnoben, W. J. M.,et al. (2025). Catchment Attributes and MEteorology for Large-Sample SPATially distributed analysis (Camels-spat): Streamflow observations, forcing data and geospatial data for hydrologic studies across North America. EGUsphere [preprint; accepted] https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-2025-893 \n\n>>> Use of the CAMELS data to introduce LSTMs for hydrologic prediction:\n\nKratzert, F., Klotz, D., Brenner, C., Schulz, K., & Herrnegger, M. (2018). Rainfall–runoff modelling using Long Short-Term Memory (Lstm) networks. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 22(11), 6005–6022. https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-22-6005-2018 \n\nKratzert, F., Klotz, D., Shalev, G., Klambauer, G., Hochreiter, S., & Nearing, G. (2019). Towards learning universal, regional, and local hydrological behaviors via machine learning applied to large-sample datasets. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 23(12), 5089–5110. https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-23-5089-2019 \n\nKratzert, F., Klotz, D., Herrnegger, M., Sampson, A. K., Hochreiter, S., & Nearing, G. S. (2019). Toward improved predictions in ungauged basins: Exploiting the power of machine learning. Water Resources Research, 55(12), 11344–11354. https://doi.org/10.1029/2019WR026065 \n\nGauch, M., Kratzert, F., Klotz, D., Nearing, G., Lin, J., & Hochreiter, S. (2021). Rainfall–runoff prediction at multiple timescales with a single Long Short-Term Memory network. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 25(4), 2045–2062. https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-25-2045-2021 \n\nKratzert, F., Klotz, D., Hochreiter, S., & Nearing, G. S. (2021). A note on leveraging synergy in multiple meteorological data sets with deep learning for rainfall–runoff modeling. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 25(5), 2685–2703. https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-25-2685-2021 \n\nKlotz, D., et al. (2022). Uncertainty estimation with deep learning for rainfall–runoff modeling. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 26(6), 1673–1693. https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-26-1673-2022 \n\n>>> Further examples of the CAMELS data supporting research with a considerable number of citations:\n\nAddor, N., Nearing, G., Prieto, C., Newman, A. J., Le Vine, N., & Clark, M. P. (2018). A ranking of hydrological signatures based on their predictability in space. Water Resources Research, 54(11), 8792–8812. https://doi.org/10.1029/2018WR022606 \n\nMizukami, N., Rakovec, O., Newman, A. J., Clark, M. P., Wood, A. W., Gupta, H. V., & Kumar, R. (2019). On the choice of calibration metrics for “high-flow” estimation using hydrologic models. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences, 23(6), 2601–2614. https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-23-2601-2019 \n\nKnoben, W. J. M., Freer, J. E., Peel, M. C., Fowler, K. J. A., & Woods, R. A. (2020). A brief analysis of conceptual model structure uncertainty using 36 models and 559 catchments. Water Resources Research, 56(9), e2019WR025975. https://doi.org/10.1029/2019WR025975 \n\nMa, K., Feng, D., Lawson, K., Tsai, W., Liang, C., Huang, X., Sharma, A., & Shen, C. (2021). Transferring hydrologic data across continents – leveraging data‐rich regions to improve hydrologic prediction in data‐sparse regions. Water Resources Research, 57(5), e2020WR028600. https://doi.org/10.1029/2020WR028600 \n\nClark, M. P., et al. (2021). The abuse of popular performance metrics in hydrologic modeling. Water Resources Research, 57(9), e2020WR029001. https://doi.org/10.1029/2020WR029001 \n\nFeng, D., Liu, J., Lawson, K., & Shen, C. (2022). Differentiable, learnable, regionalized process‐based models with multiphysical outputs can approach state‐of‐the‐art hydrologic prediction accuracy. Water Resources Research, 58(10), e2022WR032404. https://doi.org/10.1029/2022WR032404 \n\nShen, H., Tolson, B. A., & Mai, J. (2022). Time to update the split‐sample approach in hydrological model calibration. Water Resources Research, 58(3), e2021WR031523. https://doi.org/10.1029/2021WR031523"
