    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:100",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:100",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:100",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0693-466X",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6396-3523",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5628-2903",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6394-7651"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "The NSIDC Sea Ice Index (SII) exemplifies the meaning of an “impactful dataset,” with influence spanning science, policy, and education. One of the most visible indicators of human-caused climate change is Arctic sea-ice loss, and the SII has become the standard for translating the satellite-derived sea ice record into a trusted data product for monitoring changes across both polar regions. The SII underpins original scientific research, supports real-time ice monitoring for shipping and other socioeconomic sectors, and serves as a foundation for science communication while also informing legislators on adaptation planning. A central strength of the SII is its deliberate design to be both rigorous and broadly accessible, with well-maintained formats, clear documentation, and extensive resources that enable nonexperts to engage with the data. It has also become a go-to dataset for educators and is widely used to teach students about timeseries analysis and the realities of climate change.\n\nThe SII directly aligns with the key dimensions of AGU impact. For the “People Dimension,” the SII is used across sectors, including education and data visualization, validation and training of sea ice prediction models (e.g. the Sea Ice Prediction Network), decision-making support for emergency managers in Alaska, and research by climate scientists. It also clearly aligns with the “Planet Dimension” as the SII is synonymous with one of the most compelling pieces of evidence for climate change. Finally, the SII fits the “Prosperity Dimension,” as the data is used by business-oriented applications to understand ice conditions, as well as in reports critical to Indigenous peoples who rely on sea ice for their livelihoods, transportation, and food. For example, in February 2018, the SII was used to document the dramatic loss of ice in the Bering Sea, which impacted marine ecosystems, increased coastal flooding, and severely affected commercial and subsistence hunting and fishing. This is just one example of how the SII has supported communities and researchers alike in documenting rapid Arctic system change."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1a",
          "schema:text": "The SII launched when research was beginning to point insistently to the Arctic as the region where the effects of climate change would first be evident on a large scale. The term “arctic amplification” appeared, and so did the phrase “canary in the coal mine” in connection with sea ice. NSIDC already had satellite-based sea ice concentration products. Developer Ken Knowles and I saw that with them, we could make a user-friendly product that served scientists as well as the public, all with a growing interest in what sea ice was doing. A patchwork of NOAA support made it possible. –F. Fetterer"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1b",
          "schema:text": "As the manager maintaining the SII, I lead developers to sustain and improve the product. This often involves being “plugged in” to the user community on improvements needed. For example, in 2013 users requested that the base period for SII anomaly analysis be updated from a 22-year to 30-year period. We made the update and provided supplemental material describing how the information conveyed by median extent lines changed as a result and documented the change in the User Guide. Being proactive to user needs is a big part of making the SII a popular and relevant data set. –A. Windnagel"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1c",
          "schema:text": "I support SII users with clear answers, careful follow-through, and a polite, constructive tone. I prevent surprises by posting plain language notices, offering alternatives, and timelines. When issues arise, I acknowledge quickly, explain next steps, and close the loop. I translate details into actions, point to the right files, and set expectations. Success means users feel heard and leave with a specific next step and link. –D. Cantarero"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "For the nominator, the Sea Ice Index (SII) has been a foundational part of inspiring and supporting their scientific journey. Their PhD thesis focused on linking Arctic amplification with changes to midlatitude extreme weather. The first step of this research was to better understand Arctic sea ice and characterize its regional variability in the passive microwave satellite record. The SII serves as the authoritative source for sea-ice data, in part due to its careful attention to creating a spatially and temporarily homogeneous record that is intended for assessing long-term trends. In the nominator’s research, their team used the SII to validate the credibility of global climate models, to characterize observations for understanding global climate change and variability, and to facilitate our transdisciplinary collaborations with community partners.\n\nThe SII has also served as a key dataset for supporting their science communication on Arctic change. To break down complex climate science, they apply accessible and open science data visualization practices as a form of storytelling. Many of their graphics use SII and can be viewed at https://zacklabe.com/arctic-sea-ice-figures/. The SII allows them to share current ice conditions on their social media feeds, reaching an audience of over 100,000 daily that includes politicians, Arctic explorers, Indigenous peoples, journalists, and others. In summary, the SII is a unique dataset that supports polar researchers, like the nominator, by providing high-quality, real-time information updated daily."
        }
      ]
    },
