        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The Sea Ice Index provides sea ice extent and area data for both hemispheres, using satellite passive microwave-derived sea ice concentration fields as input. The simple, quick-to-understand and up-to-date graphs and maps have contributed to growing awareness of how rapidly the Earth, particularly the Arctic, is changing. \n\nIt is a source for ice extent and concentration images and data values from November 1978 to the present. Sea Ice Index images also depict trends and anomalies in ice cover calculated using a 30-year reference period of 1981 through 2010. The images and data are produced in a consistent way that makes the Index timeseries appropriate for use when looking at long-term trends in sea ice cover. (From the Overview on the product landing page, https://nsidc.org/data/g02135/versions/4 ) \n\nThe Sea Ice Index was first published in 2002. It was envisioned as a website that would meet a need for readily accessible, easy-to-use information on sea ice trends and anomalies, with products that would assist in monitoring and diagnosing the ice extent minima that were gaining increasing attention in the research community. Ice conditions, trends, and anomalies presented graphically would give at-a-glance answers to general questions such as \"Is the ice extent in the Chukchi about where it usually is this time of year?\" and \"Was there less ice in the Beaufort Sea last spring than is typical?\". The Index was and still is intended for both researchers and the scientifically inclined public. (From the Product History section of the User Guide, https://nsidc.org/sites/default/files/documents/user-guide/g02135-v004-userguide.pdf)\n\nThe product has been decommissioned by NOAA. The practical impact of this is that fewer labor hours are available to respond to requests for assistance from users, and we may not be able to fix code quickly should problems arise."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Nominators 1a-1c:\nChanging sea ice extent is a potent indicator of climate change, but it only creates impact when people can interpret it. The Sea Ice Index (SII) provides a multi-decadal, trusted time series that makes change legible to experts and non-experts.\n\nPress coverage of each year’s September Arctic minimum renews public attention to polar change. The record-low 2007 minimum drew media interest and questions about mid-latitude weather. In response, the National Snow and Ice Data Center (NSIDC) launched Sea Ice Today to pair SII with interpretation, offering scientist commentary and tools that let users explore data. In the first half of 2025, Sea Ice Today had 1.13M views. Over 100,000 users engaged with SII, and roughly 30,000 downloaded files each quarter. \n\nPeople – education and public understanding\n\n• SII graphics appear widely in media and exhibits, making polar change clear to broad audiences.\n• Educators use SII for data literacy purposes. One high school teacher wrote, “I teach my students about climate change using [SII] data. My students graph the data and develop important skills…They learn the value of scientific debate as they discuss the impacts of shrinking arctic sea ice.”\n• Policy briefings incorporate SII, including WMO updates at UN climate conferences, extending SII into international public discourse.\n\nWithout SII, teaching and public briefings would be fragmented and less comparable over time, reducing clarity of communication about sea ice. \n\nPlanet – environmental indicators and scientific context\n\n• SII was designed to show change and spark discussion as a consistent indicator underpinning climate interpretation. \n• Field and synthesis studies rely on SII to contextualize observations, track basin-scale change, and compare across decades. \n• Conservation programs integrate SII in wildlife tracking and habitat studies.\n\nWithout SII, cross-year comparisons would carry higher uncertainty and reduced power to detect change. \n\nProsperity – regulation and community resilience\n\n• The US Coast Guard International Ice Patrol integrates SII figures in annual reports; reduced accuracy or frequency would hinder mandated analysis. \n• Alaska Department of Fish and Game and NOAA Fisheries use SII when assessing critical habitat for marine mammals and subsistence species.\n\nWithout SII, agencies and NGOs would lose an authoritative baseline for decisions and risk management. \n\nSupport amplifies impact\n\nOther datasets can convey similar information, but SII’s impact is amplified by NSIDC’s setting: responsive user support, documentation, and tools that make complex polar concepts understandable. Feedback reflects breadth of use: \n\n“(I) study the relationship between … snow petrels and sea ice habitats…These data are vital to my work and to most biologists who study polar marine organisms.”\n\n“I am a climate scientist who has referred to your sea ice data and graphics at least monthly for years. This data has been a powerful and effective way to communicate long-term climate trends to a wide variety of audiences, and your graphics have appeared in many of my presentations to non-scientific audiences.”\n\nSII turns a complex, evolving component of the cryosphere into a clear, comparable record. With sustained support and interpretation, it helps people learn, researchers detect change, and institutions make timely, evidence based decisions: an interpretable baseline linking science, education, and policy.\n\nNominator 2:\nThe NSIDC Sea Ice Index (SII) exemplifies the meaning of an “impactful dataset,” with influence spanning science, policy, and education. One of the most visible indicators of human-caused climate change is Arctic sea-ice loss, and the SII has become the standard for translating the satellite-derived sea ice record into a trusted data product for monitoring changes across both polar regions. The SII underpins original scientific research, supports real-time ice monitoring for shipping and other socioeconomic sectors, and serves as a foundation for science communication while also informing legislators on adaptation planning. A central strength of the SII is its deliberate design to be both rigorous and broadly accessible, with well-maintained formats, clear documentation, and extensive resources that enable nonexperts to engage with the data. It has also become a go-to dataset for educators and is widely used to teach students about timeseries analysis and the realities of climate change.\n\nThe SII directly aligns with the key dimensions of AGU impact. For the “People Dimension,” the SII is used across sectors, including education and data visualization, validation and training of sea ice prediction models (e.g. the Sea Ice Prediction Network), decision-making support for emergency managers in Alaska, and research by climate scientists. It also clearly aligns with the “Planet Dimension” as the SII is synonymous with one of the most compelling pieces of evidence for climate change. Finally, the SII fits the “Prosperity Dimension,” as the data is used by business-oriented applications to understand ice conditions, as well as in reports critical to Indigenous peoples who rely on sea ice for their livelihoods, transportation, and food. For example, in February 2018, the SII was used to document the dramatic loss of ice in the Bering Sea, which impacted marine ecosystems, increased coastal flooding, and severely affected commercial and subsistence hunting and fishing. This is just one example of how the SII has supported communities and researchers alike in documenting rapid Arctic system change."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1a:\nThe SII launched when research was beginning to point insistently to the Arctic as the region where the effects of climate change would first be evident on a large scale. The term “arctic amplification” appeared, and so did the phrase “canary in the coal mine” in connection with sea ice. NSIDC already had satellite-based sea ice concentration products. Developer Ken Knowles and I saw that with them, we could make a user-friendly product that served scientists as well as the public, all with a growing interest in what sea ice was doing. A patchwork of NOAA support made it possible. –F. Fetterer\n\nNominator 1b:\nAs the manager maintaining the SII, I lead developers to sustain and improve the product. This often involves being “plugged in” to the user community on improvements needed. For example, in 2013 users requested that the base period for SII anomaly analysis be updated from a 22-year to 30-year period. We made the update and provided supplemental material describing how the information conveyed by median extent lines changed as a result and documented the change in the User Guide. Being proactive to user needs is a big part of making the SII a popular and relevant data set. –A. Windnagel\n\nNominator 1c:\nI support SII users with clear answers, careful follow-through, and a polite, constructive tone. I prevent surprises by posting plain language notices, offering alternatives, and timelines. When issues arise, I acknowledge quickly, explain next steps, and close the loop. I translate details into actions, point to the right files, and set expectations. Success means users feel heard and leave with a specific next step and link. –D. Cantarero \n\nNominator 2:\nFor the nominator, the Sea Ice Index (SII) has been a foundational part of inspiring and supporting their scientific journey. Their PhD thesis focused on linking Arctic amplification with changes to midlatitude extreme weather. The first step of this research was to better understand Arctic sea ice and characterize its regional variability in the passive microwave satellite record. The SII serves as the authoritative source for sea-ice data, in part due to its careful attention to creating a spatially and temporarily homogeneous record that is intended for assessing long-term trends. In the nominator’s research, their team used the SII to validate the credibility of global climate models, to characterize observations for understanding global climate change and variability, and to facilitate our transdisciplinary collaborations with community partners.\n\nThe SII has also served as a key dataset for supporting their science communication on Arctic change. To break down complex climate science, they apply accessible and open science data visualization practices as a form of storytelling. Many of their graphics use SII and can be viewed at https://zacklabe.com/arctic-sea-ice-figures/. The SII allows them to share current ice conditions on their social media feeds, reaching an audience of over 100,000 daily that includes politicians, Arctic explorers, Indigenous peoples, journalists, and others. In summary, the SII is a unique dataset that supports polar researchers, like the nominator, by providing high-quality, real-time information updated daily."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1a-1c:\nMajor updates to the dataset often come with Special Reports. These are citable documents that provide information about changes to the product. The most recent is: \n\nWindnagel, A., Stafford, T., Fetterer, F., Meier, W. (2025). Sea Ice Index Version 4 Analysis. NSIDC Special Report 28. Boulder CO, USA: National Snow and Ice Data Center. https://nsidc.org/sites/default/files/documents/technical-reference/nsidc-special-report-28.pdf.\n\nNominator 2:\nFetterer, F., Knowles, K., Meier, W. N., Savoie, M., Windnagel, A. K. & Stafford, T. (2025). Sea Ice Index. (G02135, Version 4). [Data Set]. Boulder, Colorado USA. National Snow and Ice Data Center. https://doi.org/10.7265/a98x-0f50. Date Accessed 09-06-2025."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 2:\nChristensen, M., & Nilsson, A. E. (2017). Arctic sea ice and the communication of climate change. Popular Communication, 15(4), 249–268. https://doi.org/10.1080/15405702.2017.1376064\n\nFetterer, F., Windnagel, A., & Meier, W. N. (2014, March 15). A New Normal for the Sea Ice Index [Conference paper]. NASA Goddard Space Flight Center; National Snow and Ice Data Center. Document ID 20140008666. https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20140008666/downloads/20140008666.pdf \n\nMeier, Walter & National Center for Atmospheric Research Staff (Eds). Last modified 2025-04-29 \"The Climate Data Guide: NSIDC Sea Ice Index.” Retrieved from https://climatedataguide.ucar.edu/climate-data/nsidc-sea-ice-index on 2025-09-06\n\nVardy, M. (2020). Relational agility: Visualizing near-real-time Arctic sea ice data as a proxy for climate change. Social Studies of Science, 50(5), 802–820. https://doi.org/10.1177/0306312720906532 \n\nWindnagel, A., Stafford, T., Fetterer, F., Meier, W. (2025). Sea Ice Index Version 4 Analysis. NSIDC Special Report 28. Boulder CO, USA: National Snow and Ice Data Center. https://nsidc.org/sites/default/files/documents/technicalreference/nsidc-special-report-28.pdf"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1a:\nFlorence Fetterer\n\nNominator 1b:\nAnn Windnagel\n\nNominator 1c:\nDanica Linda Cantarero\n\nNominator 2: \nZachary Labe"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Email Address",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1a:\nfetterer@colorado.edu\n\nNominator 1b:\nann.windnagel@colorado.edu\n\nNominator 1c:\nDanicaLinda.Cantarero@colorado.edu\n\nNominator 2:\nzlabe@climatecentral.org"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator ORCID",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1a:\nhttps://orcid.org/0000-0002-0693-466X\n\nNominator 1b:\nhttps://orcid.org/0000-0001-6396-3523\n\nNominator 1c:\nhttps://orcid.org/0000-0002-5628-2903\n\nNominator 2:\nhttps://orcid.org/0000-0002-6394-7651"
        },
        {
