      "@id": "id:dataset:101",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "Historical daily discharge data of Italian rivers - SIREN project",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:101",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:ocean-science-hydrology-cryosphere"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Paola Mazzoglio, Miriam Bertola"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.14996262"
      ],
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "DOI",
          "value": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.14996262"
        }
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:zenodo"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "Paola Mazzoglio"
        }
      ],
      "description": "The dataset consists of digitized historical daily river flow measurements from across Italy, originally collected and published throughout the 20th century by the Italian national hydrological service in the volumes of the hydrological yearbooks. These yearbooks represent a unique and invaluable archive of hydrological observations, but until now they were only available in printed form, making their use extremely difficult for scientific, engineering, and policy applications.\nThrough the SIREN (Saving Italian hydRological mEasuremeNts) citizen science project, thousands of volunteers from around the world contributed to transcribing more than 17,000 tables of daily discharge data, each containing 365 values. Each record was digitized independently by at least two volunteers, with discrepancies resolved through manual quality control by the project team. This process has produced a high-quality, machine-readable dataset that follows the FAIR principles (Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, Reusable).\nThe dataset provides a consistent and openly available record of Italian river discharges spanning much of the 20th century. It enables the study of long-term hydrological variability, extreme events (floods and droughts), and the impacts of climate change on water resources. It also supports improved water management, infrastructure design, and climate adaptation strategies at local, national, and European levels.\nAs of today, a few time series have already been published on Zenodo. The complete datasets of about 1000 time series will be released in late 2025 – early 2026.",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-egu25-1395 and https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-egu24-2080 (a datapaper is planned to be prepared and submitted in late 2025).",
