          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:department-of-environment-land-and-infrastructure-engineering-politecnico-di-tor",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "Department of Environment, Land and Infrastructure Engineering, Politecnico di Torino, 10129 Torino, Italy"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:101",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:101",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:101",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3662-9439"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The SIREN dataset represents the first comprehensive effort to digitize and make openly available historical daily river discharge measurements across Italy. Originally collected and published in printed Hydrological Yearbooks by the former National Hydrographic and Mareographic Service (SIMN) throughout the 20th century, these data have until now been largely inaccessible to the scientific and policy communities. Their release fills a critical gap, aligning Italy with other European countries that already benefit from digitized hydrological archives.\n\nPeople dimension. The dataset enhances education and science by providing a rich resource for students, researchers, and citizens. It raises public awareness of the importance of long-term hydrological monitoring and creates opportunities for active participation in science, as thousands of volunteers contributed to its digitization. It also enhances the ability of scientists, educators, and practitioners to understand and communicate the characteristics of river flows in Italy. By enabling better water resources assessment, the dataset also supports climate change impact studies.\n\nPlanet dimension. This dataset is essential to understanding how Italian rivers have evolved under natural variability and climate change. It provides the basis for studying hydrological extremes such as floods and droughts, detecting long-term changes in seasonality and magnitude of flows, and establishing baselines for climate adaptation. Without these records, assessing the impacts of climate change on water systems in Italy would be fragmented or impossible.\n\nProsperity dimension. Access to reliable long-term hydrological data directly benefits socioeconomic systems by informing the safe design of critical infrastructure such as bridges, dams, and flood defenses."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "As part of the project team, the nominator contributed to identifying and preparing more than 17,000 scanned pages of Hydrological Yearbooks for digitization. They also coordinated the involvement of thousands of volunteers on Zooniverse, designing the workflows that allowed them to transcribe daily discharge data. Personally, they worked closely with the dataset through quality control, checking discrepancies between double transcriptions and ensuring consistency across stations and years. They also interact with the dataset in its outreach dimension, presenting it to students, citizen scientists, and policymakers."
        }
      ]
    },
