      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://doi.org/10.22008/promice/freshwater"
      ],
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "DOI",
          "value": "https://doi.org/10.22008/promice/freshwater"
        }
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:dataverse-geus-dk"
      ],
      "description": "Mankoff et al., present a catchment scale estimate of liquid discharge from Greenland. Greenland runoff, from ice mass loss and increasing rainfall, is increasing with climate change. This has many impacts on the physical, chemical, and biological properties of the adjacent fjords and coastal seas. However, where and when the discharge occurs was not previously available as an open, quality-controlled database that could be easily accessed. Mankoff's development of such a dataset was therefore extremely useful. The original dataset includes runoff from 1958 to 2019 with an updated version also available online. \n\n[Description adapted from the corresponding Earth System Science Data abstract: https://essd.copernicus.org/articles/12/2811/2020/]\n\nGreenland liquid water discharge from 1958 through 2019\nAbstract\nGreenland runoff, from ice mass loss and increasing rainfall, is increasing. That runoff, as discharge, impacts the physical, chemical, and biological properties of the adjacent fjords. However, where and when the discharge occurs is not readily available in an open database. Here we provide data sets of high-resolution Greenland hydrologic outlets, basins, and streams, as well as a daily 1958 through 2019 time series of Greenland liquid water discharge for each outlet. The data include 24 507 ice marginal outlets and upstream basins and 29 635 land coast outlets and upstream basins, derived from the 100 m ArcticDEM and 150 m BedMachine. At each outlet there are daily discharge data for 22 645 d – ice sheet runoff routed subglacially to ice margin outlets and land runoff routed to coast outlets – from two regional climate models (RCMs; MAR and RACMO). Our sensitivity study of how outlet location changes for every inland cell based on subglacial routing assumptions shows that most inland cells where runoff occurs are not highly sensitive to those routing assumptions, and outflow location does not move far. We compare RCM results with 10 gauges from streams with discharge rates spanning 4 orders of magnitude. Results show that for daily discharge at the individual basin scale the 5 % to 95 % prediction interval between modeled discharge and observations generally falls within plus or minus a factor of 5 (half an order of magnitude, or +500 % %). Results from this study are available at https://doi.org/10.22008/promice/freshwater (Mankoff, 2020a) and code is available at http://github.com/mankoff/freshwater (last access: 6 November 2020) (Mankoff, 2020b).",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "https://essd.copernicus.org/articles/12/2811/2020/ (73 listed)",
          "schema:url": [
            "https://essd.copernicus.org/articles/12/2811/2020/"
          ]
        }
      ],
