      ],
      "name": "Mark James Hopwood",
      "identifier": {
        "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
        "propertyID": "ORCID",
        "value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9743-079X"
      },
      "schema:email": "mark@sustech.edu.cn",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:southern-university-of-science-and-technology-1088-xueyuan-avenue-nanshan-distri"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:southern-university-of-science-and-technology-1088-xueyuan-avenue-nanshan-distri",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "Southern University of Science and Technology, 1088 Xueyuan Avenue, Nanshan District, Shenzhen, Guangdong Province, China, 518055"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:103",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:103",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:103",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9743-079X"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "Knowing the volume and distribution of freshwater discharge from the Greenland Ice Sheet is essential for understanding its implications for global sea level rise, regional and global ocean circulation, coastal hazards, and marine ecosystems. Before Mankoff et al. developed a quality-controlled dataset that provided an estimate of discharge from every catchment in Greenland, there was nothing close to such data product and so all estimates of discharge volume and its distribution were crude and disharmonious across the literature. In most cases, it simply was not possible to deduce the effects of meltwater in the ocean. The development of a high-quality, high-resolution estimate of runoff allowed a step forward in multiple fields, concerning how this freshwater drives changes in the Earth System. We know that landslides around Greenland, and tidal tsunamis are triggered by discharge events, we know that fisheries -which support 95% of Greenland's economy- respond to glacier discharge, and it's thought that increasing discharge will contribute to a slowdown of the AMOC. All these hypotheses are longstanding but were extremely difficult to quantitatively work on without a data product that described exactly when, where and how much the Greenland Ice Sheet discharged meltwater into the ocean. Mankoff not only developed a liquid data product, but also a corresponding solid ice discharge product. These datasets have become standard tools across multiple disciplines. They allow researchers and Greenlandic stakeholders alike to quickly visualize where 'hotspots' of discharge are, how these rates of discharge are changing with time, and to model and understand the effects on the ocean. A major strength of the dataset is how widely it has been used, from understanding the changing dynamics of Greenland's fisheries, to forecasting how ice melt will affect AMOC, to modeling whether or not 'geoengineering' of Greenland's glaciers might limit future sea level rise. All of these research areas have advanced very quickly in the past 6 years since the Mankoff data products were released. (See for example the selection of references below which used the dataset and range from paleo-fields to future projections)"
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "Mankoff's datasets around Greenland have underpinned much of the nominator’s work over the past 6 years. From selecting field sites prior to the two research cruises they led, to deriving annual fluxes, to assessing how regional oceanography has changed around Greenland over the past century, to critically evaluating whether or not installation of underwater curtains might stem future ice melt and sea level rise. Mankoff's work has been absolutely essential to multiple avenues of investigation. Without them, we would not have advanced as quickly in our understanding of ice-ocean interaction, or have anywhere close to our understanding of how meltwater feedbacks may operate in the future. \n\nBy viewing the Mankoff et al. dataset, the nominator was able to quickly constrain the largest 'hotspots' of meltwater discharge around Greenland and plan 2 very successful research cruises; one as a postdoc to Disko Bay in 2022 and one as an associate professor to SE Greenland in 2024. These cruises were inter-disciplinary aiming to understand the effects of increasing discharge on marine biology, chemistry and physics. Without datasets to quantify where the largest outflows of meltwater were occurring, it would have been extremely difficult to plan the cruises and they would not have been so successful. Post-cruises we were also able to utilize the Mankoff et al., datasets to produce flux calculations and to overlay meltwater discharge with satellite-derived measurements which revealed new trends we hadn't expected."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:103",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 104,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Ocean Science, Hydrology, Cryosphere"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
