        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.22008"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "Mankoff et al., present a catchment scale estimate of liquid discharge from Greenland. Greenland runoff, from ice mass loss and increasing rainfall, is increasing with climate change. This has many impacts on the physical, chemical, and biological properties of the adjacent fjords and coastal seas. However, where and when the discharge occurs was not previously available as an open, quality-controlled database that could be easily accessed. Mankoff's development of such a dataset was therefore extremely useful. The original dataset includes runoff from 1958 to 2019 with an updated version also available online. \n\n[Description adapted from the corresponding Earth System Science Data abstract: https://essd.copernicus.org/articles/12/2811/2020/]\n\nGreenland liquid water discharge from 1958 through 2019\nAbstract\nGreenland runoff, from ice mass loss and increasing rainfall, is increasing. That runoff, as discharge, impacts the physical, chemical, and biological properties of the adjacent fjords. However, where and when the discharge occurs is not readily available in an open database. Here we provide data sets of high-resolution Greenland hydrologic outlets, basins, and streams, as well as a daily 1958 through 2019 time series of Greenland liquid water discharge for each outlet. The data include 24 507 ice marginal outlets and upstream basins and 29 635 land coast outlets and upstream basins, derived from the 100 m ArcticDEM and 150 m BedMachine. At each outlet there are daily discharge data for 22 645 d – ice sheet runoff routed subglacially to ice margin outlets and land runoff routed to coast outlets – from two regional climate models (RCMs; MAR and RACMO). Our sensitivity study of how outlet location changes for every inland cell based on subglacial routing assumptions shows that most inland cells where runoff occurs are not highly sensitive to those routing assumptions, and outflow location does not move far. We compare RCM results with 10 gauges from streams with discharge rates spanning 4 orders of magnitude. Results show that for daily discharge at the individual basin scale the 5 % to 95 % prediction interval between modeled discharge and observations generally falls within plus or minus a factor of 5 (half an order of magnitude, or +500 % %). Results from this study are available at https://doi.org/10.22008/promice/freshwater (Mankoff, 2020a) and code is available at http://github.com/mankoff/freshwater (last access: 6 November 2020) (Mankoff, 2020b)."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Knowing the volume and distribution of freshwater discharge from the Greenland Ice Sheet is essential for understanding its implications for global sea level rise, regional and global ocean circulation, coastal hazards, and marine ecosystems. Before Mankoff et al. developed a quality-controlled dataset that provided an estimate of discharge from every catchment in Greenland, there was nothing close to such data product and so all estimates of discharge volume and its distribution were crude and disharmonious across the literature. In most cases, it simply was not possible to deduce the effects of meltwater in the ocean. The development of a high-quality, high-resolution estimate of runoff allowed a step forward in multiple fields, concerning how this freshwater drives changes in the Earth System. We know that landslides around Greenland, and tidal tsunamis are triggered by discharge events, we know that fisheries -which support 95% of Greenland's economy- respond to glacier discharge, and it's thought that increasing discharge will contribute to a slowdown of the AMOC. All these hypotheses are longstanding but were extremely difficult to quantitatively work on without a data product that described exactly when, where and how much the Greenland Ice Sheet discharged meltwater into the ocean. Mankoff not only developed a liquid data product, but also a corresponding solid ice discharge product. These datasets have become standard tools across multiple disciplines. They allow researchers and Greenlandic stakeholders alike to quickly visualize where 'hotspots' of discharge are, how these rates of discharge are changing with time, and to model and understand the effects on the ocean. A major strength of the dataset is how widely it has been used, from understanding the changing dynamics of Greenland's fisheries, to forecasting how ice melt will affect AMOC, to modeling whether or not 'geoengineering' of Greenland's glaciers might limit future sea level rise. All of these research areas have advanced very quickly in the past 6 years since the Mankoff data products were released. (See for example the selection of references below which used the dataset and range from paleo-fields to future projections)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "Mankoff's datasets around Greenland have underpinned much of the nominator’s work over the past 6 years. From selecting field sites prior to the two research cruises they led, to deriving annual fluxes, to assessing how regional oceanography has changed around Greenland over the past century, to critically evaluating whether or not installation of underwater curtains might stem future ice melt and sea level rise. Mankoff's work has been absolutely essential to multiple avenues of investigation. Without them, we would not have advanced as quickly in our understanding of ice-ocean interaction, or have anywhere close to our understanding of how meltwater feedbacks may operate in the future. \n\nBy viewing the Mankoff et al. dataset, the nominator was able to quickly constrain the largest 'hotspots' of meltwater discharge around Greenland and plan 2 very successful research cruises; one as a postdoc to Disko Bay in 2022 and one as an associate professor to SE Greenland in 2024. These cruises were inter-disciplinary aiming to understand the effects of increasing discharge on marine biology, chemistry and physics. Without datasets to quantify where the largest outflows of meltwater were occurring, it would have been extremely difficult to plan the cruises and they would not have been so successful. Post-cruises we were also able to utilize the Mankoff et al., datasets to produce flux calculations and to overlay meltwater discharge with satellite-derived measurements which revealed new trends we hadn't expected."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "https://essd.copernicus.org/articles/12/2811/2020/ (73 listed)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "A selection of different topics: \n\nKanna, N., Sugiyama, S., Ando, T., Wang, Y., Sakuragi, Y., Hazumi, T., Matsuno, K., Yamaguchi, A., Nishioka, J., & Yamashita, Y. (2022). Meltwater discharge from marine‐terminating glaciers drives biogeochemical conditions in a greenlandic fjord. Global Biogeochemical Cycles, 36(11), e2022GB007411. https://doi.org/10.1029/2022GB007411 \n\nLindeman, M. R., Straneo, F., Adams, H. M., Nelson, M. J. S., & Schartup, A. T. (2024). Low mercury concentrations in a Greenland glacial fjord attributed to oceanic sources. Communications Earth & Environment, 5(1), 320. https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-024-01474-9 \n\nWood, M., Carroll, D., Fenty, I., Bertin, C., Darby, B., Dutkiewicz, S., Hopwood, M., Khazendar, A., Meire, L., Oliver, H., Parker, T., & Willis, J. (2025). Increased melt from Greenland’s most active glacier fuels enhanced coastal productivity. Communications Earth & Environment, 6(1), 626. https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-025-02599-1 \n\nHansen, K., Karlsson, N. B., How, P., Poulsen, E., Mortensen, J., & Rysgaard, S. (2025). Winter subglacial meltwater detected in a Greenland fjord. Nature Geoscience, 18(3), 219–225. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41561-025-01652-0 \n\nWekerle, C., McPherson, R., Von Appen, W.-J., Wang, Q., Timmermann, R., Scholz, P., Danilov, S., Shu, Q., & Kanzow, T. (2024). Atlantic Water warming increases melt below Northeast Greenland’s last floating ice tongue. Nature Communications, 15(1), 1336. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-45650-z \n\nMcPherson, R. A., Wekerle, C., Kanzow, T., Ionita, M., Heukamp, F. O., Zeising, O., & Humbert, A. (2024). Atmospheric blocking slows ocean-driven melting of Greenland’s largest glacier tongue. Science, 385(6715), 1360–1366. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.ado5008 \n\nCowton, T. R., Slater, D. A., & Inall, M. E. (2023). Subglacial‐discharge plumes drive widespread subsurface warming in northwest greenland’s fjords. Geophysical Research Letters, 50(15), e2023GL103801. https://doi.org/10.1029/2023GL103801 \n\nKvorning, A. B., Heikkilä, M., Pearce, C., Seidenkrantz, M.-S., Simpson, G. L., Meire, L., Kuijpers, A., Larsen, N. K., & Ribeiro, S. (2025). A Holocene fjord record from Greenland reveals exceptional Atlantic water influence during minimum ice-sheet extent. Communications Earth & Environment, 6(1), 326. https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-025-02282-5 \n\nOliver, H., Slater, D., Carroll, D., Wood, M., Morlighem, M., & Hopwood, M. J. (2023). Greenland subglacial discharge as a driver of hotspots of increasing coastal chlorophyll since the early 2000s. Geophysical Research Letters, 50(10), e2022GL102689. https://doi.org/10.1029/2022GL102689 \n\nAndresen, C. S., Karlsson, N. B., Straneo, F., Schmidt, S., Andersen, T. J., Eidam, E. F., Bjørk, A. A., Dartiguemalle, N., Dyke, L. M., Vermassen, F., & Gundel, I. E. (2024). Sediment discharge from Greenland’s marine-terminating glaciers is linked with surface melt. Nature Communications, 15(1), 1332. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-45694-1"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Mark James Hopwood"
        },
