        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The TIARES (Tomographic Investigation by seafloor ARray Experiment for the Society hotspot) network, deployed on the French Polynesian seafloor in the Pacific Ocean from 2009 to 2010, collected multi-sensor geophysical data to investigate the mantle structure beneath the Society hotspot. The dataset includes continuous records from broadband ocean-bottom seismometers, ocean-bottom electromagnetometers, and differential pressure gauges. These data provide information on the seismic and electrical conductivity structures of the mantle beneath the Society hotspot. In addition, the TIARES network captured unprecedented records of pressure and electromagnetic (EM) signals generated by tsunamis associated with large Pacific Ocean earthquakes, including the 2010 Chilean earthquake (Mw 8.8)."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The dataset was nominated because it provides a unique record of simultaneous seafloor observations of tsunami-induced water pressure and three-component electromagnetic fields. It includes data from the 2009 Samoa and 2010 Chile tsunamis, offering rare and valuable case studies of how tsunami-driven ocean motion generates corresponding magnetic field signals. To the best of current knowledge, this is the only dataset in the world that contains co-located and synchronized measurements of tsunami pressure and tsunami magnetic fields.\n\nThe availability of this dataset makes it possible to directly study the physical relationship between ocean dynamics and electromagnetic variations during tsunamis, which cannot be achieved through pressure or magnetic data alone. It opens new avenues for advancing tsunami science, validating coupled hydrodynamic–electromagnetic models, and exploring the feasibility of electromagnetic methods for rapid tsunami detection and early warning"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator has used this dataset to investigate the relationship between tsunami-induced sea level changes, flow direction, flow velocity, and the corresponding magnetic field signals. By analyzing the simultaneous records of water pressure and three-component electromagnetic fields, they were able to study how tsunami dynamics are expressed in the magnetic environment of the ocean. The uniqueness and quality of these observations made it possible to validate theoretical models and reveal new insights into the physical coupling between ocean motion and electromagnetic variations."
        },
