    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:105",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "Greenland Ecosystem Monitoring (GEM) – MarineBasis",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:105",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:ocean-science-hydrology-cryosphere"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Aarhus University & Greenland Institute of Natural Resources"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://data.g-e-m.dk/datasets?theme=marine"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:greenland-ecosystem-monitoring"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "Greenland Ecosystem Monitoring"
        }
      ],
      "description": "The Greenland Ecosystem Monitoring (GEM) program is a long-term integrated monitoring initiative that collects, curates, and disseminates environmental data from across Greenland’s ecosystems. Within GEM, the MarineBasis program provides unique, continuous, and high-quality time series of physical, chemical, and biological observations in high and low Arctic fjord systems in Greenland. Since the early 2000s, the program has documented seasonal and interannual variability in sea ice cover, water column structure, primary production, nutrient cycling, and biodiversity. The data are openly available through the GEM Dataverse and support fundamental research on Arctic ecosystems, climate change impacts, and biogeochemical processes.\nReference: https://data.g-e-m.dk/"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1370-7887",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Henry C. Henson",
      "identifier": {
        "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
        "propertyID": "ORCID",
        "value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1370-7887"
      },
      "schema:email": "hch@ecos.au.dk",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:department-of-ecoscience-aarhus-university-c-f-m-llers-all-3-bygning-1131-8000-a"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:department-of-ecoscience-aarhus-university-c-f-m-llers-all-3-bygning-1131-8000-a",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "Department of Ecoscience, Aarhus University, C. F. Møllers Allé 3, Bygning 1131, 8000 Aarhus, Denmark"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:105",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:105",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:105",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1370-7887"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The MarineBasis dataset is impactful because it provides one of the most comprehensive and continuous records of Arctic marine ecosystem change. As the Arctic undergoes rapid warming, the long-term time series collected by MarineBasis serve as an irreplaceable foundation for understanding how physical and biological processes respond to climate-driven pressures. Without MarineBasis, our ability to study the cascading impacts of sea ice retreat, altered hydrology, and ecosystem shifts on Arctic fjord and coastal systems would be severely limited.\n\n- People dimension: MarineBasis data are used in capacity-building, training, and education for a new generation of Arctic scientists. The program also contributes to local and Indigenous community awareness by documenting environmental changes that directly affect fisheries, sea ice safety, and livelihoods.\n- Planet dimension: The dataset tracks key indicators of marine system health, including sea ice extent, nutrient cycling, productivity, and biodiversity. These long-term records are essential for understanding ecosystem resilience and for improving climate and ecosystem models.\n- Prosperity dimension: MarineBasis provides the foundation for policy-relevant science in Greenland and the Arctic, informing fisheries management, conservation strategies, and sustainable resource use. The dataset contributes to resilience planning at local and regional levels, and supports international climate assessments (e.g., Arctic Council, IPCC).\n\nThe open availability and quality of MarineBasis data make it widely reused in peer-reviewed research, syntheses, and global reporting. Its integration with other GEM subprograms (ClimateBasis, GeoBasis, GlacioBasis, and BioBasis) ensures that it contributes to a holistic understanding of Greenland’s ecosystems under change."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "As a PhD student, the nominator has been directly involved in the collection of MarineBasis data in Young Sound for the past two years, gaining first-hand experience in Arctic fieldwork and the stewardship of long-term datasets. Their connection to GEM began earlier, during their Master’s studies, when they used GEM data in courses to understand Arctic coastal ecosystems and how they respond to a changing climate. Since then, MarineBasis has been integral to their research: they have published two articles in their PhD that relied on GEM data, both as a foundation for analyses and as essential context for experimental studies conducted in the same environment. Even when their work does not directly analyze MarineBasis measurements, the ancillary data (e.g., hydrography, nutrient concentrations, sea ice conditions) provide invaluable context, enabling more robust interpretation of results. Beyond publications, GEM has shaped their training and growth as an Arctic researcher, serving as both a scientific resource and a learning platform."
        }
      ]
    },
