    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:107",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "United States Groundwater Well Database (USGWD)",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:107",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:ocean-science-hydrology-cryosphere"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Chung-Yi Lin"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Alexandra Miller"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Musab Waqar"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Landon T. Marston"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://www.hydroshare.org/resource/8b02895f02c14dd1a749bcc5584a5c55/"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:hydroshare"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "CUAHSI"
        }
      ],
      "description": "Groundwater wells are critical infrastructure that enable the monitoring, extraction, and use of groundwater, which has important implications for the environment, water security, and economic development. Despite the importance of wells, a unified database collecting and standardizing information on the characteristics and locations of these wells across the United States has been lacking. To bridge this gap, a comprehensive database of groundwater well records was created using data collected from state and federal agencies. This database is called the United States Groundwater Well Database (USGWD). Presented in both tabular form and as vector points, the USGWD comprises over 14.2 million well records with attributes such as well purpose, location, depth, and capacity for wells constructed as far back as 1763 to 2023. Rigorous cross-verification steps have been applied to ensure the accuracy of the data. The USGWD stands as a valuable tool for improving our understanding of how groundwater is accessed and managed across various regions and sectors within the United States. [Copied from link above]",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "Lin, C.-Y., Miller, A., Waqar, M., & Marston, L. T. (2024). A database of groundwater wells in the United States. Scientific Data, 11(1), 335. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-03186-3",
          "identifier": [
            {
              "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
              "propertyID": "DOI",
              "value": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-03186-3"
            }
          ],
          "schema:url": [
            "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-03186-3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "Although published in mid-2024, the United States Groundwater Well Database (USGWD) has already gained substantial traction within the hydrologic and environmental science community. The dataset has been viewed nearly 9,000 times and downloaded more than 1,700 times in its first year of release—clear evidence of its broad utility for research, education, and data integration. It has already been cited in multiple peer-reviewed publications, including studies examining groundwater access inequality, well-monitoring coverage, aquifer depletion, and infrastructure mapping. Several research groups have begun building derived products that link the USGWD to national and global datasets, including cropland extent, aquifer boundaries, and census demographics, to evaluate water access and vulnerability at regional to national scales."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9116-1691",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Landon Marston",
      "identifier": {
        "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
        "propertyID": "ORCID",
        "value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9116-1691"
      },
      "schema:email": "lmarston@vt.edu",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:virginia-tech-925-prices-fork-road-blacksburg-va-24060"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:virginia-tech-925-prices-fork-road-blacksburg-va-24060",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "Virginia Tech, 925 Prices Fork Road, Blacksburg, VA 24060"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:107",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:107",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:107",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9116-1691"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The dataset was nominated because the United States Groundwater Well Database (USGWD) is the first openly available, standardized, nation-wide compilation of groundwater well locations, purposes, and attributes for the U.S., assembled from state and federal sources, harmonized to a common schema, and delivered as both tabular (CSV) and geospatial (SHP) products with rigorous QA/QC flags. Spanning wells constructed from the 18th century through 2023 and totaling ~14.2 million records, it transforms fragmented state records into a coherent national resource that enables comparable analyses across jurisdictions and disciplines. Without USGWD, many cross-state assessments of groundwater access, monitoring coverage, and infrastructure trends would remain infeasible or irreproducible.\n\nPeople — education, training, disaster response and prediction\nUSGWD lowers barriers for educators, students, and local practitioners by providing a clean, consistent foundation for curricula, capstone projects, and community training on well stewardship, private-well safety, and groundwater literacy. Emergency managers and health departments can rapidly locate domestic and public-supply wells, identify areas with uncertain coordinates or statuses, and prioritize outreach, testing, or emergency water supply during contamination events or drought. The status and purpose classifications (e.g., domestic, irrigation, monitoring) and location-verification flags help target where data are strong versus where ground verification is needed—turning the database into a practical decision aid, not just a map.\n\nPlanet — geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate indicators\nBy georeferencing wells and linking them to aquifer and HUC-12 boundaries, USGWD provides the missing infrastructure layer for basin-scale and national groundwater assessments. Researchers can quantify well densities over major/minor aquifers, illuminate historical drilling patterns, and evaluate monitoring coverage relative to ecosystems and streams vulnerable to depletion. The explicit, standardized attributes and QA/QC flags enable robust coupling with climate and hydrologic models, advancing studies of groundwater–surface water interactions, drought exposure, and environmental flow protection under a changing climate.\n\nProsperity — economic good, equity, humanitarian relief, resilience\nGroundwater supports households, farms, and industry; yet policy and investment decisions often lack a reliable picture of where and how wells are used. USGWD reveals spatial inequalities in access and reporting, informs permitting and infrastructure planning, and helps utilities and agencies modernize programs for well construction, decommissioning, and monitoring. For humanitarian and resilience planning, the dataset guides where to bolster monitoring networks, protect vulnerable domestic-well communities, and target investments in recharge, treatment, or alternative supplies. Because the data and standardization code are open, states and stakeholders can iteratively improve completeness and accuracy—building a durable public good that supports equitable prosperity. \n\nWithout USGWD, the U.S. would lack a public, reproducible baseline of groundwater-well infrastructure. The database turns groundwater access points from “invisible infrastructure” into analyzable public data, thereby enabling better science, smarter policy, and faster response."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator’s research group developed and maintains the United States Groundwater Well Database (USGWD), integrating over 14 million well records from state and federal repositories into a standardized, open-access national resource. Because of their direct role in building the dataset, they have deep familiarity with its structure, metadata, and quality-control procedures, and are actively using it as the foundation for several forthcoming analyses.\n\nCurrent projects include quantifying the share of the U.S. population that relies on self-supplied groundwater versus public water systems, identifying at the field scale whether irrigated croplands depend on surface water, groundwater, or both, and evaluating the socioeconomic and demographic characteristics of populations reliant on private wells. The database also underpins their peer-reviewed study, Connecting Inequalities in Land Ownership and Groundwater Access: The Case of California’s Central Valley, which links the USGWD to parcel-level ownership data to quantify disparities in groundwater access and well capacity.\n\nThrough these applications, they use the USGWD to reveal how groundwater access shapes patterns of equity, sustainability, and resilience—transforming scattered administrative records into actionable insights for policy, research, and education."
        }
      ]
    },
