nitoring coverage relative to ecosystems and streams vulnerable to depletion. The explicit, standardized attributes and QA/QC flags enable robust coupling with climate and hydrologic models, advancing studies of groundwater–surface water interactions, drought exposure, and environmental flow protection under a changing climate.\n\nProsperity — economic good, equity, humanitarian relief, resilience\nGroundwater supports households, farms, and industry; yet policy and investment decisions often lack a reliable picture of where and how wells are used. USGWD reveals spatial inequalities in access and reporting, informs permitting and infrastructure planning, and helps utilities and agencies modernize programs for well construction, decommissioning, and monitoring. For humanitarian and resilience planning, the dataset guides where to bolster monitoring networks, protect vulnerable domestic-well communities, and target investments in recharge, treatment, or alternative supplies. Because the data and standardization code are open, states and stakeholders can iteratively improve completeness and accuracy—building a durable public good that supports equitable prosperity. \n\nWithout USGWD, the U.S. would lack a public, reproducible baseline of groundwater-well infrastructure. The database turns groundwater access points from “invisible infrastructure” into analyzable public data, thereby enabling better science, smarter policy, and faster response."
