    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:108",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "Global Reservoir Inventory of Lost Storage by Sedimentation (GRILSS)",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:108",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:ocean-science-hydrology-cryosphere"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Sanchit Minocha and Faisal Hossain"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://doi.org/10.17605/OSF.IO/W4UG8"
      ],
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "DOI",
          "value": "https://doi.org/10.17605/OSF.IO/W4UG8"
        }
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:open-science-framework-osf"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "Sanchit Minocha and Faisal Hossain"
        }
      ],
      "description": "The Global Reservoir Inventory of Lost Storage by Sedimentation (GRILSS) dataset provides open-source data on sedimentation and capacity loss for 1,013 reservoirs in 75 major river basins across 54 countries. This dataset serves as a critical resource for studying reservoir sedimentation, which affects the storage capacity of artificial reservoirs, with implications for water management, environmental sustainability, and infrastructure planning. The dataset consists of two main components: sedimentation data and spatial vector data, allowing for detailed geospatial analysis of reservoirs, dams, and catchments worldwide. The data is intended to support sediment management strategies, research, and future contributions from the community. (reference: https://osf.io/w4ug8/)",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "Minocha, S., & Hossain, F. (2025). GRILSS: Opening the gateway to global reservoir sedimentation data curation. Earth System Science Data, 17(4), 1743–1759. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-17-1743-2025",
          "identifier": [
            {
              "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
              "propertyID": "DOI",
              "value": "https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-17-1743-2025"
            }
          ],
          "schema:url": [
            "https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-17-1743-2025"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "The GRILSS dataset has already been reused in several independent studies, demonstrating its broad applicability and scientific value. For example, Suresh and Hossain (2025) used GRILSS to investigate links between sedimentation loss rates and hydropower dams classified as flood-inducing, identifying global hotspots of reservoir capacity loss, particularly across developing regions (https://doi.org/10.1029/2025EF006648). Similarly, Yang et al. (2025) utilized GRILSS to generate a global map of 57 large reservoirs (storage capacity > 0.1 km³) that have lost more than 30% of their capacity, revealing that episodic flooding and drawdown events may pose widespread risks to downstream river environments (https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-025-02666-7).",
          "schema:url": [
            "https://doi.org/10.1029/2025EF006648",
            "https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-025-02666-7"
          ],
          "identifier": [
            "https://doi.org/10.1029/2025EF006648",
            "https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-025-02666-7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "Within just a few months of its release, GRILSS has been cited in multiple peer-reviewed publications, reflecting its growing role as a foundational dataset for global research on reservoir sedimentation, sustainability, and flood risk assessment."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-1969-7521",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Sanchit Minocha",
      "identifier": {
        "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
        "propertyID": "ORCID",
        "value": "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-1969-7521"
      },
      "schema:email": "msanchit@uw.edu",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:university-of-washington-1400-ne-campus-parkway-seattle-wa-98195"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:university-of-washington-1400-ne-campus-parkway-seattle-wa-98195",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "University of Washington, 1400 NE Campus Parkway, Seattle, WA 98195"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:108",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:108",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:108",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-1969-7521"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "Reservoirs are the backbone of global water security. They store water for drinking, farming, and energy generation, and help control floods and droughts. But across the world, many reservoirs are slowly losing their storage capacity as sediments carried by rivers settle inside them. This process, called reservoir sedimentation, quietly reduces how much water a reservoir can hold. The problem has been known for decades, yet the world has lacked a reliable, open dataset to understand how serious it is, where it is happening fastest, and how it is changing over time.\nThe Global Reservoir Inventory of Lost Storage by Sedimentation (GRILSS) dataset fills this critical gap. It is the first global, openly available dataset that measures how much water storage has been lost in reservoirs due to sedimentation. \nImpact on People:\nWhen reservoirs lose storage, people face water shortages, higher flood risks, and power disruptions. Without knowing how much storage has already been lost, planners and communities make decisions based on incomplete information. GRILSS helps fill that gap. It provides water managers, researchers, and local agencies with the data they need to identify which reservoirs are most at risk and to plan accordingly. It also serves as an open learning resource for students and professionals who want to understand how sedimentation affects daily life and water security.\nImpact on the Planet:\nSedimentation connects what happens on land to what happens in rivers and reservoirs. GRILSS helps scientists and policymakers see how changes in rainfall, deforestation, or land use affect reservoir health across different regions of the world. By identifying where sediment builds up fastest, it guides conservation and watershed management efforts that can slow down storage loss, protect ecosystems, and sustain freshwater resources. In severe cases, when reservoirs are flushed to remove accumulated sediment, downstream regions can face sudden ecological and water quality impacts — insights from GRILSS can help anticipate and reduce such risks.\nImpact on Prosperity:\nEvery cubic meter of storage lost reduces the economic value of a reservoir — it means less water for crops, less power generation, and higher maintenance costs. GRILSS helps decision-makers see where these losses are greatest and where investments in sediment management can have the biggest impact. It also supports the development of globally scalable, cost-effective techniques that use satellite data and machine learning to pinpoint reservoirs suffering the most from sedimentation. This can enhance social equity, as countries with limited monitoring capacity can benefit from insights derived from well-surveyed regions. By enabling such data-driven prioritization, GRILSS helps strengthen water infrastructure, reduce the need for costly new dams, and ensure that the benefits of surface water storage are shared more fairly across regions.\nWithout GRILSS, our understanding of how fast the world is losing water storage would remain fragmented and incomplete. By bringing together accurate, well-documented data from around the world, GRILSS enables a shared understanding of a global challenge. It provides the basis needed to plan for a more secure water future — one where reservoirs continue to serve people, the planet, and economies for generations to come."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "For the nominator, working with the GRILSS dataset was a turning point in developing their tool, RECLAIM (Reservoir Estimation of Capacity Loss using AI-based Methods). Before GRILSS, the biggest barrier to building a global model for reservoir sedimentation was the lack of consistent and verifiable data. GRILSS changed that. It provided the reliable foundation and validation data that made RECLAIM possible — not just as a research idea, but as a working, globally scalable framework.\n\nThe dataset’s detailed, georeferenced records of dams, reservoir polygons, and catchment boundaries allowed them to link physical, climatic, and satellite-derived features to real observations of capacity loss with confidence. For the first time, they could see how sedimentation behaves differently across regions and climates, revealing patterns that simple models and fragmented studies had long overlooked. These insights directly shaped RECLAIM’s design — a machine learning framework that uses 88 predictor variables, from satellite-based hydrological indicators to catchment and reservoir attributes, to estimate sedimentation rates and identify their dominant drivers. \n\nIn essence, GRILSS turned RECLAIM from concept to capability. It bridged the gap between local observations and global prediction, enabling a tool that can now identify reservoirs most at risk of losing capacity and help prioritize where detailed surveys or management interventions are needed the most."
        }
      ]
    },
