    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:111",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "CalCOFI SIO Hydrographic CTD Bottle/Cast Data",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:111",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:ocean-science-hydrology-cryosphere"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "UCSD SIO"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/tabledap/siocalcofiHydroCast.html",
        "https://calcofi.org/data/oceanographic-data/bottle-database/"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:erddap",
        "id:repository:calcofi"
      ],
      "agu:repositoryIdentifier": [
        "coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "Lynn DeWitt"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "NOAA ERD"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "Rasmus Swalethorp"
        }
      ],
      "description": "The California Cooperative Oceanic Fisheries Investigations (CalCOFI) Bottle Database contains one of the longest and most comprehensive oceanographic time series in the world, spanning from 1949 to the present. It includes chemical and physical measurements from seawater samples collected at fixed stations throughout the California Current System off of California. Parameters include temperature, salinity, dissolved oxygen, chlorophyll-a, nutrients, and other oceanographic variables derived from bottle samples collected at discrete depths using Niskin bottles on a CTD-Rosette system. These data allow users to explore changes in water column structure, biogeochemistry, and productivity over time and are foundational for understanding long-term ocean variability, ecosystem change, and fisheries dynamics off the coast of California.",
      "reuseExample": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:ReuseExample",
          "schema:text": "The CalCOFI Bottle Database has been widely used in scientific research, management applications, and educational initiatives. Examples of its reuse and derivative products can be found through the CalCOFStakeholder I publication archive (https://calcofi.org/publications) and stakeholder applications such as the Valuation of CalCOFI Data Usage StoryMap (https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/b0a64c5dc84946aa869895caa229b41e) and at https://calcofi.org/data-products/ and shiny.calcofi.io/int-app",
          "schema:url": [
            "https://calcofi.org/publications",
            "https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/b0a64c5dc84946aa869895caa229b41e",
            "https://calcofi.org/data-products/"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:111",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:111",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:111",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0177-7770"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "CalCOFI Bottle Data was nominated because it represents one of the most enduring and influential ocean observing datasets in the world. Established in 1949 in response to the collapse of the Pacific sardine fishery, CalCOFI has sustained, quality-controlled record of ocean temperature, salinity, oxygen, nutrients, and chlorophyll from discrete seawater samples provides unparalleled insight into how the California Current Ecosystem has changed over time. Without this 76 year record, the scientific community would lack a coherent picture of ocean ecosystem variability in one of the world’s most productive and economically significant coastal regions. It can be regarded as the scientific community’s “eyes and ears” beneath the surface and continues to shape our understanding of the ocean and our collective capacity to sustain it.\n\nPlanet --\nCalCOFI’s Bottle Data has served as the foundation for understanding how ocean climate variability and anthropogenic change shape ecosystems. The CalCOFI Bottle data helped reveal El Niño’s global impact by detecting unusual warm-water conditions, shifts in currents, and combined with observed changes in plankton and fish populations off California during the 1957–58 event. These systematic observations showed that the anomalies were linked to a broader climate pattern originating in the tropical Pacific, establishing El Niño as a basin-wide phenomenon. The dataset also helps validate essential models of ocean circulation, validates satellite and autonomous sensor observations, and provides essential baselines for new methods such as environmental DNA analysis. It has illuminated global-scale linkages between ocean physics and biology—revealing how climate-driven fluctuations cascade through marine food webs.\n\nPeople & Prosperity -- \nCalCOFI has trained generations of oceanographers, fisheries scientists, and data analysts, serving as a living classroom aboard research vessels and through open data access for educators, students, and early-career scientists. The program also hosts a volunteer ship-based apprenticeship program, providing hands-on experience in ocean data collection and mentoring the next generation of marine scientists and technicians. Volunteer apprentices gain direct exposure to the full research process, from deploying CTDs and collecting bottle samples to processing and quality-controlling data. The dataset underpins research and decision-making at state, federal, and academic institutions. CalCOFI’s long record has shaped predictive models of marine heatwaves, hypoxia, and El Niño events—contributing to disaster preparedness and improved resource management along the U.S. West Coast.\nCalCOFI data has also helped to inform the development of the sustainable blue economy by guiding marine spatial planning and offshore renewable energy siting."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "As a CalCOFI coordinator and researcher, the nominator’s role is to actively share and use it to advance understanding of the California Current ecosystem. They also draw on the dataset to integrate it with other datasets like the larval fish data and environmental DNA analyses. Beyond data, they train and mentor students to use the data. They also work closely with partner institutions and resource managers, finding new ways to share the data with users to support marine management, conservation efforts, and policy decisions. They also write publications and conduct outreach to highlight the ongoing value of these sustained ocean observations."
        }
      ]
    },
