    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:112",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:112",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:112",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7470-6602"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The NEWTS database was nominated because it addresses critical challenges in managing energy wastewater, fostering advancements across multiple dimensions.\n\nNEWTS significantly enhances educational and training opportunities by providing a readily accessible, standardized dataset for researchers and students. This facilitates deeper understanding of wastewater characteristics and treatment processes, specifically giving student researchers an opportunity to understand realistic energy wastewater parameters without conducting time and resource limited data collection. \n\nBy consolidating and standardizing diverse energy water stream data, NEWTS also enables more accurate assessment of geophysical phenomena related to water contamination and resource availability. It supports conservation efforts by facilitating the design of effective treatment and reuse strategies, reducing environmental discharge and preserving freshwater resources. The comprehensive data on environmental indicators, such as the composition of various wastewater streams, allows for more precise monitoring of environmental health and the impact of energy production.\n\nEconomically, NEWTS drives prosperity by streamlining research and development in wastewater treatment, reducing the time and cost associated with data gathering and preprocessing. This accelerates innovation in areas like critical mineral recovery from wastewater, creating new economic opportunities. For instance, the ability to identify and quantify lithium and other critical minerals in produced water streams, even with initial data gaps filled by CoDaRT's machine learning, directly supports a burgeoning industry. The database fosters community resilience by enabling more effective and economical management of water resources in energy-producing regions, mitigating environmental risks and promoting sustainable practices.\n\nWithout NEWTS, researchers and industry would continue to face disparate, unstructured, and often incomplete data, hindering accurate modeling and efficient design of water treatment solutions. This would result in slower progress in critical mineral recovery, increased environmental risks due to less optimized wastewater management, and a less informed approach to water resource planning in the energy sector. Consolidated data enables robust predictive models and assessment of regional variations in wastewater composition, thereby facilitating advanced research and practical application."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator regularly uses the NEWTS database to target specific regions and energy wastewater types for further economic analysis. The NEWTS Economic Data Dashboard: Critical Materials for Energy - Submissions - EDX includes the value of critical minerals contained in energy wastewaters per meter cubed by region. For produced water, it also adds volumetric data to calculate the total value per region. This dashboard can help inform which region and wastewater source is a high priority for future process analysis on recovery of critical minerals. \nThey also frequently use the NEWTS database for data science analyses on energy wastewater. For example, they have used the NEWTS USGS Produced Water Database to develop a cluster analysis of representative produced water composition by region. This cluster analysis informs understanding of different types of produced water that can be treated and valorized for recovery of byproducts."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:112",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 113,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Ocean Science, Hydrology, Cryosphere"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "National Energy Water Treatment and Speciation (NEWTS) Database"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Rachel Yesenchak, Devin Justman, Lucy Romeo, Isabelle Pfander, Justin Mackey, Sophia Bauer, Michael Sabbatino, Burt Thomas, Zineb Belarbi, Madison Wenzlick, Kathryn Smith, Chad Able, Alison Fritz, Nicholas Siefert"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://edx.netl.doe.gov/group/newts"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "Energy Data eXchange (EDX)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "https://edx.netl.doe.gov/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "National Energy Technology Laboratory (NETL)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The NEWTS database serves as a centralized digital resource offering cleaned, standardized, and reformatted geochemical data essential for evaluating fossil energy wastewater treatment, beneficial reuse strategies, and critical minerals recovery. This resource significantly enhances the accessibility and usability of fossil energy wastewater chemistry data. Hosted on NETL's Energy Data eXchange (EDX), the NEWTS group comprises 171 individual resources, including tabular geochemical datasets from over 400,000 wastewater samples, chemical modeling software templates and input files for OLI Studio and Geochemist's Workbench (GWB), geospatial data, and case studies. Additionally, it provides data-based tools such as the NEWTS Federal Database Dashboard, the NEWTS State-Level Database Dashboard, and the Constituent Data Replacement Tool (CoDaRT) for imputing missing constituent data, along with video tutorials to guide users."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The NEWTS database was nominated because it addresses critical challenges in managing energy wastewater, fostering advancements across multiple dimensions.\n\nNEWTS significantly enhances educational and training opportunities by providing a readily accessible, standardized dataset for researchers and students. This facilitates deeper understanding of wastewater characteristics and treatment processes, specifically giving student researchers an opportunity to understand realistic energy wastewater parameters without conducting time and resource limited data collection. \n\nBy consolidating and standardizing diverse energy water stream data, NEWTS also enables more accurate assessment of geophysical phenomena related to water contamination and resource availability. It supports conservation efforts by facilitating the design of effective treatment and reuse strategies, reducing environmental discharge and preserving freshwater resources. The comprehensive data on environmental indicators, such as the composition of various wastewater streams, allows for more precise monitoring of environmental health and the impact of energy production.\n\nEconomically, NEWTS drives prosperity by streamlining research and development in wastewater treatment, reducing the time and cost associated with data gathering and preprocessing. This accelerates innovation in areas like critical mineral recovery from wastewater, creating new economic opportunities. For instance, the ability to identify and quantify lithium and other critical minerals in produced water streams, even with initial data gaps filled by CoDaRT's machine learning, directly supports a burgeoning industry. The database fosters community resilience by enabling more effective and economical management of water resources in energy-producing regions, mitigating environmental risks and promoting sustainable practices.\n\nWithout NEWTS, researchers and industry would continue to face disparate, unstructured, and often incomplete data, hindering accurate modeling and efficient design of water treatment solutions. This would result in slower progress in critical mineral recovery, increased environmental risks due to less optimized wastewater management, and a less informed approach to water resource planning in the energy sector. Consolidated data enables robust predictive models and assessment of regional variations in wastewater composition, thereby facilitating advanced research and practical application."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator regularly uses the NEWTS database to target specific regions and energy wastewater types for further economic analysis. The NEWTS Economic Data Dashboard: Critical Materials for Energy - Submissions - EDX includes the value of critical minerals contained in energy wastewaters per meter cubed by region. For produced water, it also adds volumetric data to calculate the total value per region. This dashboard can help inform which region and wastewater source is a high priority for future process analysis on recovery of critical minerals. \nThey also frequently use the NEWTS database for data science analyses on energy wastewater. For example, they have used the NEWTS USGS Produced Water Database to develop a cluster analysis of representative produced water composition by region. This cluster analysis informs understanding of different types of produced water that can be treated and valorized for recovery of byproducts."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Smith, K., Z. Belarbi, I. Pfander, R. Yesenchak, J. Mackey, L. Romeo, D. Justman, M. Wenzlick, R. Thomas, H. Hadjeres, C. Able, A. Fritz, N. Siefert. (2025). National Energy Water Treatment and Speciation (NEWTS) Database and Dashboards: New Resources for Locating and Assessing Energy-Related Wastewater Composition and Volumes in the United States. [Manuscript submitted for publication]."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "These data have been applied to develop average compositions to inform process models for treatment of energy wastewaters. An example of this use case is shown in the table below:\n\nAble, C., Schmitt, T., Siefert, N., & Fritz, A. (2025). Baseline cost analysis of energy wastewater treatment with preliminary feasibility analysis of critical mineral recovery. Minerals, 15(3), 213. https://doi.org/10.3390/min15030213 \n\nThe database was also used in the following manuscript to complete a resource assessment of critical minerals in produced water:\n\nSmith, K. H., Mackey, J. E., Wenzlick, M., Thomas, B., & Siefert, N. S. (2024). Critical mineral source potential from oil & gas produced waters in the United States. Science of The Total Environment, 929, 172573. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2024.172573 \n\nFinally, these data have been applied for development of synthetic compositions of energy wastewaters that are used in experiments for treatment and mineral recovery technologies."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Alison Fritz"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Email Address",
          "schema:value": "Alison.Fritz@netl.doe.gov"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator ORCID",
          "schema:value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7470-6602"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Affiliation or Organization",
          "schema:value": "National Energy Technology Laboratory"
        }
      ]
