        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The following description is adapted from the ArcticDEM and REMA websites: (https://www.pgc.umn.edu/data/arcticdem/, https://www.pgc.umn.edu/data/rema/)\n\nThe ArcticDEM and REMA projects are a response to the need for high quality elevation data in remote locations and the need for accurate measurement of topographic change at the Earth’s dynamic polar regions. They provide the first, high resolution (2-meter) terrain maps of the Arctic and Antarctica.\n\nThe polar DEM projects include hundreds of thousands of individual stereoscopic Digital Elevation Models (DEM) extracted from pairs of submeter (0.32 to 0.5 m) resolution Maxar satellite imagery, including data from WorldView-1, WorldView-2, and WorldView-3, and a small number from GeoEye-1, acquired between 2007 and the present.\n\nOutput DEM raster files are made available as both “strip” DEMs that preserve the original source material’s temporal component, as well as mosaic tiles that incorporate the full stack of strips that cover any given point on the ground into a fusion product. Files are available to researchers as widely-accessible GeoTIFFs and as Cloud-Optimized GeoTIFFS (COGs) that permit access and analyses of these data without being constrained by storage and processing limitations.\n\nThe time-dependent nature of the strip DEM files allows users to perform change detection analysis and to compare observations of topographic data acquired in different seasons or years. The mosaic DEM tiles are assembled from multiple strip DEMs with the intention of providing a more consistent and comprehensive product over larger areas while also providing a per-pixel minimum and maximum date range to enable change detection."
        },
