          "schema:value": "ArcticDEM and REMA have transformed science capabilities in polar regions, which were among the least-mapped regions on Earth only a few years ago. These data enable a broad range of science activities, from measuring changes in glaciers and ice sheets to river hydrology and ecology. They also enable field work that would be otherwise impossible by allowing researchers to plan deployments and operations. The data’s practical applications impact the safety and prosperity of human communities. As of July 2025, the journal article describing REMA and the data citations for ArcticDEM and REMA have collectively been cited in 915 publications.\n\nSurface topography is among the most fundamental Earth Science data sets, essential to numerous research activities, including hazard assessment and mitigation, hydrologic modeling, solid earth dynamics, and many others. Change in surface topography provides critical information about surface processes, including plate tectonics, erosion and subsidence, glacier mass balance, and mass balance of woody vegetation. Accurate topography is also necessary for basic human activities such as urban planning, building transportation infrastructure, flood mapping, and water resource conservation. Over most areas of the globe, however, the spatial resolution (10s of m) and accuracy ( >10m) of openly available topographic data sets preclude many of these research activities. Even fewer areas have repeated high-precision elevation measurements for observing and quantifying change. \n\nThis need for global, repeat, high-resolution topographic data was highlighted in the National Academies’ 2007 and 2018 Decadal Surveys, stating “the coarse resolution of topographic mapping has been a major impediment to understanding the forces and dynamic processes that shape the Earth’s surface”. Among the few existing data sources that can fulfill this science requirement in polar regions at continental-scale are Digital Elevation Models (DEMs) created from repeat, submeter-resolution imagery collected by the Maxar WorldView constellation of three Earth-imaging satellites. \n\nArcticDEM and REMA use the Maxar archive to provide complete coverage and 14 years of repeat measurements in Antarctica and the Arctic. The DEM data are publicly available and have been used in 915 verified scientific publications and numerous international mapping efforts.\n\nAntarctic logistics have been revolutionized by REMA which delivers more detail and accuracy than previously-available products. Air operation safety relies on accurate topographic maps for assessment of novel landing sites. Detailed understanding of terrain in remote locations enabled advance planning of field operations. REMA has enabled Antarctic field campaigns that would have been far more costly or logistically impossible. At the other pole, ArcticDEM has been used by Canadian, Icelandic, and Greenlandic mapping agencies as a component of their national mapping strategy, especially in remote regions and in areas of rapid change.\n\nArcticDEM and REMA’s repeated measurements over 14 years illuminate surface processes in both time and space. The datasets shed light on volcano dynamics, ice movement and volume changes, erosion patterns, permafrost degradation, and vegetation shifts. They have been used for landslide and flood risk assessment and mitigation as well as quantification of coastal and riverine erosion near vulnerable infrastructure, among many other applications."
        },
        {
