      "description": "The Global Extreme Sea Level Analysis (GESLA) project was initiated in 2009 through a collaborative effort to better understand changes in the frequency and magnitude of extreme sea levels. Its original goal was to compile as many high-frequency (hourly or more frequent) sea-level records as possible into a unified format with consistent quality control, enabling researchers to easily, quickly and freely access data and analyze tides, storm surges, and other extreme sea-level processes on a global scale.\n\nSince its inception, several versions of the dataset have been released, culminating in GESLA-4 (July 2025), which provides the most extensive temporal and spatial coverage to date. GESLA-4 includes 127,623 station-years of data from 6,474 records contributed by over 40 data providers worldwide.\n\nEach record in the dataset offers sea-level measurements with at least hourly resolution, accompanied by detailed metadata describing the contributor, location, geographical classification (coastal, lake, or river), datum, and record length. Additionally, each individual observation includes a quality control flag to ensure data reliability.",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
