    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:114",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:114",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:114",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7802-2662"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "Sea-level measurements are fundamental for understanding and managing the interactions between the ocean and coastal environments. Accurate, high-resolution sea-level data underpin a wide range of scientific, operational, and societal applications. From assessing coastal flood risk and informing the design of defenses to studying erosion and protecting ecosystems such as coral reefs, mangroves, and wetlands, these observations are critical for decision-making from local to global scales.\n\nFor decades, the Permanent Service for Mean Sea Level (PSMSL) has provided invaluable long-term records of monthly and annual means, crucial for tracking climate-driven sea-level rise. However, many impactful coastal processes, particularly extreme sea-level events, occur over much shorter timescales of hours to days. Capturing these dynamics requires high-frequency observations that mean sea-level datasets cannot provide.\n\nThe Global Extreme Sea Level Analysis (GESLA) dataset addresses this gap by providing harmonized, high-frequency (hourly or higher) sea-level records from thousands of tide gauges worldwide. Now in its fourth release (GESLA-4, July 2025), it includes over 127,000 station-years of data from more than 6,400 records contributed by 40+ international agencies. Its standardized format, rigorous quality control, and comprehensive metadata make it uniquely suited for analyzing tides, storm surges, and extreme sea levels across global and regional scales.\n\nGESLA underpins nearly all global extreme sea-level assessments and has been used in the three most recent IPCC reports and more than 200 peer-reviewed studies. Despite its global reach, it remains a volunteer-driven initiative maintained through open collaboration among researchers and data providers, ensuring continued accessibility and scientific integrity.\n\nBeyond research, GESLA plays a key role in education and capacity-building, providing accessible, high-quality data for students, early-career scientists, and practitioners worldwide. Its open access supports disaster risk reduction by enabling event analysis, storm surge model validation, and improved early-warning systems, directly benefiting coastal communities and decision-makers.\n\nBy offering harmonized and continuous high-frequency records, GESLA enhances understanding of the geophysical processes driving sea-level variability and extremes, including climate-induced changes, storm clustering, and interactions between tides and meteorological forcing. This knowledge is essential for conserving coastal ecosystems and developing environmental indicators that inform long-term monitoring and adaptation.\n\nGESLA also strengthens socioeconomic resilience by informing flood-risk assessments, infrastructure design, and sustainable coastal planning. It enables evidence-based decisions that protect lives, property, and livelihoods, supporting equitable adaptation and long-term prosperity in vulnerable coastal regions. Freely accessible through a user-friendly interface, GESLA democratizes access to sea-level data and promotes reproducible science and collaboration. As a trusted reference, it has become a cornerstone for ocean and climate research, connecting people, advancing planetary understanding, and supporting global resilience in a changing climate."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator has used the GESLA dataset extensively throughout their research, beginning with her master’s degree, where they validated hydrodynamic model outputs of storm surges generated by tropical cyclones in the Caribbean using GESLA observations. During her PhD, they expanded this work to a global analysis of the temporal clustering of storm surges, an effort that would not have been possible without harmonized, high-frequency sea-level records from thousands of tide gauges worldwide. Since tide gauges are managed by different national agencies, hosted in diverse repositories, and provided in varying formats and datums, compiling such a dataset independently would have required an enormous preprocessing effort. \n\nUsing GESLA, they have identified hotspots of storm surge clustering and analyzed inter-arrival times between consecutive events, key indicators of recovery potential and community resilience. They also contribute to the SurgeMIP project (Storm Surges and Extreme Sea Levels: Review, Model Intercomparison, and Projection Coordination), which uses GESLA as the primary observational reference for validating storm surge models. By selecting ~500 tide gauges from GESLA, SurgeMIP enables consistent model intercomparison and validation across studies, bringing together nearly 40 scientists from over 35 institutions, demonstrating how accessible, high-quality data can unite researchers worldwide toward advancing climate resilience and societal preparedness."
        }
      ]
    },
