      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The EStreams dataset and catalogue mark a major step forward for hydrological science in Europe, bridging the gap between fragmented national archives and global hydrological initiatives. While global repositories provide invaluable resources, they often lack detailed catchment metadata, consistent temporal coverage, and dense spatial representation across Europe. National-scale CAMELS-type datasets have revolutionized regional hydrology but remain confined within borders, limiting cross-country comparisons. EStreams overcomes these barriers through a harmonized, continental-scale framework integrating streamflow indices, hydro-climatic signatures, meteorological time series, and landscape descriptors from 1900 to 2022.\nCovering more than 17,000 catchments across 41 countries, EStreams enables exploration of streamflow variability, hydrological extremes, and long-term climate-driven trends at unprecedented scales. Standardized, quality-controlled indices and meteorological time series provide a ready-to-use dataset supporting both process-based and machine-learning modeling, offering a robust basis for testing hypotheses and benchmarking models across diverse climatic and physiographic settings.\nA defining feature is its dual structure: a harmonized dataset paired with a catalogue of original sources and retrieval scripts, bridging open data availability and usability. EStreams is dynamic, reproducible, and extensible, allowing updates and expansion as new information emerges, functioning as a living infrastructure for European hydrology.\nEStreams has been instrumental in advancing large-sample hydrology and climate impact research across Europe. Studies such as Brunner et al. (2025) on compound drought-flood dynamics, Berghuijs et al. (2025) on streamflow seasonality, and Brunner et al. (2025) on dry-to-wet transitions show how Estreams supports novel methodological and conceptual developments. Additionally, do Nascimento et al. (2025) and Klotz et al. (2025) use it to evaluate geological controls and validate runoff reconstructions, while Hemshorn de Sánchez et al. (2025) assess climate sensitivities of European rivers. Without Estreams, such integrative, continental-scale assessments would remain fragmented or unattainable.\nIn terms of broader impacts, EStreams advances understanding of Europe’s hydrology, with clear benefits for the planet by enabling analysis of floods, droughts, and climate-sensitive water systems. It also support individuals, for example students and early-career researchers can use the dataset to practice hydrological modeling, statistical analysis, and climate impact studies without needing to collect raw streamflow observations themselves. By providing reproducible, high-resolution streamflow data at continental scales, Estreams allows research and policy decisions that would otherwise be limited, strengthening knowledge of water resources and environmental resilience."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "As one of the dataset’s developers, the nominator has worked on integrating, harmonizing, and validating multi-source data into the EStreams database. Their research uses EStreams to analyze relationships between climate drivers, streamflow behavior, and catchment characteristics across Europe with the overarching goal of improving the prediction in ungauged basins. They also use the dataset for benchmarking hydrological models, developing machine learning prediction frameworks, and exploring hydro-climatic variability at continental and regional scales. EStreams has become a core component of their work on environmental data integration and large-sample hydrology. Beyond research, they collaborate with colleagues to ensure EStreams remain open, transparent, and reproducible, contributing to the broader community of scientists seeking to understand and manage European water systems under climate change."
        }
      ]
    },
