 and Klotz et al. (2025) use it to evaluate geological controls and validate runoff reconstructions, while Hemshorn de Sánchez et al. (2025) assess climate sensitivities of European rivers. Without Estreams, such integrative, continental-scale assessments would remain fragmented or unattainable.\nIn terms of broader impacts, EStreams advances understanding of Europe’s hydrology, with clear benefits for the planet by enabling analysis of floods, droughts, and climate-sensitive water systems. It also support individuals, for example students and early-career researchers can use the dataset to practice hydrological modeling, statistical analysis, and climate impact studies without needing to collect raw streamflow observations themselves. By providing reproducible, high-resolution streamflow data at continental scales, Estreams allows research and policy decisions that would otherwise be limited, strengthening knowledge of water resources and environmental resilience."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "As one of the dataset’s developers, the nominator has worked on integrating, harmonizing, and validating multi-source data into the EStreams database. Their research uses EStreams to analyze relationships between climate drivers, streamflow behavior, and catchment characteristics across Europe with the overarching goal of improving the prediction in ungauged basins. They also use the dataset for benchmarking hydrological models, developing machine learning prediction frameworks, and exploring hydro-climatic variability at continental and regional scales. EStreams has become a core component of their work on environmental data integration and large-sample hydrology. Beyond research, they collaborate with colleagues to ensure EStreams remain open, transparent, and reproducible, contributing to the broader community of scientists seeking to understand and manage European water systems under climate change."
        },
        {
