        }
      ],
      "description": "The CAMELS-IND (Catchment Attributes and Meteorology for Large-sample Studies in India) dataset provides hydrometeorological time series and catchment attributes for 472 catchments across Peninsular India, with observed streamflow available for 228 catchments between 1980 and 2020. CAMELS-IND standardizes and curates data from diverse sources such as streamflow records, meteorological forcings, remote sensing, reanalysis products, and geospatial datasets into an analysis-ready format. It includes 19 meteorological variables such as precipitation, temperature, radiation, evapotranspiration, and soil moisture, along with 211 catchment attributes representing hydroclimatic conditions, land cover, and human influences. The dataset also provides predicted streamflow from a long short-term memory (LSTM) based hydrological model to complement observed data. CAMELS-IND follows the structure of existing CAMELS datasets from the United States, Chile, Brazil, Great Britain, Australia, Switzerland, and Germany, enabling cross-comparisons with global hydrological datasets while addressing regional challenges in Indian water science. It supports studies of climate impacts, water resources management, hydrologic extremes, and human influences on river basins, making it an essential foundation for advancing hydrological research and decision-making in South Asia.",
