    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:116",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:116",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:116",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7622-8306"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The CAMELS-IND dataset addresses a critical gap in hydrological data for India, a region with catchments spanning arid, temperate, and tropical climates and subject to diverse human influences. Prior to CAMELS-IND, researchers relied on scattered, inconsistent datasets that required extensive pre-processing and lacked standardized metadata. CAMELS-IND provides hydrometeorological time series, observed streamflow, LSTM-modeled streamflow predictions, and 211 catchment attributes for 472 catchments across Peninsular India. This analysis-ready dataset enables large-sample studies of Indian catchments that were previously infeasible and facilitates global comparative hydrology by following the standards of other CAMELS datasets.\n\nCAMELS-IND empowers research, education, and practical applications in water management. Students, early-career researchers, and practitioners can use the dataset to develop hydrological models, test data-driven methods, and explore catchment behavior under climate variability and human influences. The dataset supports disaster preparedness and risk reduction by providing the high-quality inputs necessary for flood forecasting, drought monitoring, and water resource planning, which benefit communities that depend on rivers across Peninsular India.\n\nThe dataset also enables detailed analysis of environmental and geophysical processes. Its catchment attributes and meteorological time series allow examination of climate impacts on hydrological extremes, evaluation of soil moisture and evapotranspiration patterns, and assessment of human interventions such as dam regulation and land-use change. By providing consistent, standardized information at the catchment scale, CAMELS-IND advances understanding of how natural and anthropogenic processes shape hydrological systems and supports sustainable management of freshwater resources.\n\nIn addition, CAMELS-IND contributes to socioeconomic resilience and informed policy-making. Reliable hydrological data underpin agriculture, hydropower, and water supply management. The inclusion of modeled streamflow for ungauged or data-scarce catchments ensures that decisions can be informed even where observations are limited. This facilitates equitable water allocation, planning for climate-related risks, and long-term regional resilience.\n\nBy making these comprehensive data openly accessible, CAMELS-IND removes barriers that previously limited large-scale hydrological research in India. It provides a foundation for new insights into hydrologic processes, extreme events, and human impacts while fostering global collaboration. Without this dataset, systematic, reproducible, and large-sample studies of Indian catchments would remain extremely difficult, and opportunities for integrating India into global hydrological research would remain limited."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator’s group uses the CAMELS-IND dataset as a foundational resource for large-sample hydrological studies across Peninsular India. It allows them to investigate how climate variability, land-use changes, and human interventions influence hydrological processes. They leverage the dataset to develop and benchmark hydrological models, including studies in ungauged or data-scarce catchments, which would be difficult without this standardized and analysis-ready resource. It is also a valuable data for catchment classification and regional frequency analysis studies.\n\nCAMELS-IND significantly reduces the time and effort required to gather, clean, and preprocess data from multiple sources, enabling the nominator to focus on modeling, comparative studies, and understanding drivers of hydrological extremes. Its alignment with international CAMELS standards allows them to compare Indian catchments with those in other countries, facilitating insights into global hydrology. They also use the dataset to support research with direct societal relevance, including flood forecasting, drought risk assessment, and water resources planning, which informs decision-making and enhances resilience in communities dependent on rivers. Overall, CAMELS-IND provides a reliable, comprehensive foundation that accelerates research, improves reproducibility, and expands the scope of hydrological studies in India and beyond."
        }
      ]
    },
