        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "NCEI Data Management Team"
        }
      ],
      "description": "INCOIS_TA data product is a high-resolution (1/12°), spatio-temporally varying, gap-free, monthly product of surface total alkalinity (TA) for the North Indian Ocean spanning 1993–2020. Developed using an ensemble machine-learning approach trained on ship-based observations (from open sources and the EEZ regions) and reanalysis variables such as sea surface temperature, salinity, and mixed-layer depth, it represents the first long-term regional TA reconstruction of its kind. INCOIS_TA reveals significant spatial and temporal patterns in ocean buffering capacity, including rising alkalinity trends linked to salinity increases and climate modes like ENSO and the Indian Ocean Dipole. By providing both TA values and uncertainty estimates, it serves as a benchmark for model validation, bias correction, and carbonate system projections. This openly available data product advances understanding of ocean acidification, carbon cycling, and climate feedback, while supporting training, forecasting, and conservation in the Indian Ocean region. (https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/access/metadata/landing-page/bin/iso?id=gov.noaa.nodc:0307789)",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "Joshi, A. P., Ghoshal, P. K., Chakraborty, K., Roy, R., Jayaram, C., Sridevi, B., & Sarma, V. V. S. S. (2025). Long‐term changes of surface total alkalinity and its driving mechanisms in the north Indian Ocean. Global Biogeochemical Cycles, 39(8), e2024GB008344."
        }
      ]
    },
