        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "INCOIS_TA data product is a high-resolution (1/12°), spatio-temporally varying, gap-free, monthly product of surface total alkalinity (TA) for the North Indian Ocean spanning 1993–2020. Developed using an ensemble machine-learning approach trained on ship-based observations (from open sources and the EEZ regions) and reanalysis variables such as sea surface temperature, salinity, and mixed-layer depth, it represents the first long-term regional TA reconstruction of its kind. INCOIS_TA reveals significant spatial and temporal patterns in ocean buffering capacity, including rising alkalinity trends linked to salinity increases and climate modes like ENSO and the Indian Ocean Dipole. By providing both TA values and uncertainty estimates, it serves as a benchmark for model validation, bias correction, and carbonate system projections. This openly available data product advances understanding of ocean acidification, carbon cycling, and climate feedback, while supporting training, forecasting, and conservation in the Indian Ocean region. (https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/access/metadata/landing-page/bin/iso?id=gov.noaa.nodc:0307789)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "INCOIS_TA represents a significant advancement in understanding the carbonate system dynamics of the North Indian Ocean (NIO). This high-resolution, spatio-temporally varying data product, spanning the period from 1993 to 2020, is a machine learning-based product that integrates total alkalinity (TA) observations from both publicly available sources and the Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ) regions along the Indian coast. It is the first data product of its kind to provide comprehensive coverage of both open-ocean and coastal regions of the NIO. INCOIS_TA empowers ocean scientists, students, and modelers with an open, high-quality data product for research, education, and training in marine carbonate chemistry and climate interactions. It enables researchers to learn from real-world data while encouraging interdisciplinary integration between data science and oceanography. Moreover, by providing a long-term reference baseline for the buffering capacity of the surface ocean, it enhances predictive capabilities for ocean acidification and could support early warning systems relevant to coral reef health and fisheries in coastal nations. \n\nINCOIS_TA strengthens scientific understanding of the ocean’s role in carbon cycling and climate regulation. TA is a key component governing oceanic CO₂ uptake and acidification potential. However, NIO, despite having its critical role in the global carbonate budget, has historically been significantly under sampled. Thus, no long-term evolving information on TA to measure model performance in these regions was available. By quantifying statistically significant positive trends in TA and linking them to physical drivers such as salinity, stratification, and climate oscillations like ENSO and IOD, the data product sheds new light on the dynamic feedback shaping the ocean’s buffering capacity under climate change. This comprehensive spatial and temporal depiction of TA trends provides an invaluable benchmark for validating, bias-correcting, and improving global ocean and Earth System models. \n\nINCOIS_TA contributes directly to the socioeconomic and environmental resilience of Indian Ocean rim nations. Its open accessibility ensures equitable availability of advanced scientific information, empowering local institutions, policymakers, and coastal communities to make data-driven decisions for sustainable marine resource management. For example, identifying regions of stronger natural buffering capacity can guide aquaculture planning, reef protection, and coastal adaptation strategies. The data product’s alignment with UN Sustainable Development Goals (like Goal 13: Climate Action and Goal 14: Life Below Water) underlines its global relevance. This data product not only deepens our scientific comprehension of the changing ocean but also enhances the capacity of nations and communities to respond to and mitigate those changes. In essence, INCOIS_TA is not just a data product; it is an enabling infrastructure for knowledge, resilience, and sustainability in a rapidly changing climate system."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "INCOIS_TA data product is used to analyze the spatio-temporal variability and long-term trends of surface total alkalinity (TA) in the North Indian Ocean (NIO) from 1993 to 2020. We examine how TA varies regionally across the Arabian Sea and the Bay of Bengal, and how it responds to climatic and physical drivers. The study reveals statistically significant positive trends in TA in three sub regions of North Indian Ocean; south of 7° N, along the southeastern Arabian Sea, and the southwestern Bay of Bengal. Thus, indicating a gradual enhancement in the ocean’s buffering capacity in these three sub-regions. These increases are closely linked to rising sea surface salinity, suggesting the dominant role of freshwater fluxes and evaporation–precipitation balance in shaping alkalinity patterns. By analyzing interannual variations, we identify climate modes such as the El Niño–Southern Oscillation (ENSO) and the Indian Ocean Dipole (IOD) as key modulators of TA variability. ENSO exerts a stronger influence in the Arabian Sea, while both ENSO and IOD impacting the TA in the Bay of Bengal. We further assess salinity-normalized alkalinity anomalies to separate physical from biogeochemical controls, showing differences in calcification potential between the two basins. Through these applications, INCOIS_TA provides new insights into regional carbon cycle processes, ocean acidification vulnerability, and climate-driven changes in ocean total alkalinity."
        },
