    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:118",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "Global Network of Isotopes in Precipitation - The GNIP Database",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:118",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:ocean-science-hydrology-cryosphere"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "IAEA - many"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://nucleus.iaea.org/wiser"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:iaea"
      ],
      "description": "GNIP is a worldwide observation network of stable isotope and tritium in monthly precipitation samples initiated in 1960 by the IAEA and the World Meteorological Organization (WMO) and is operated in cooperation with numerous partner institutions in IAEA Member States."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9966-5915",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Person",
        "foaf:Person"
      ],
      "name": "Nina Welti",
      "identifier": {
        "@type": "schema:PropertyValue",
        "propertyID": "ORCID",
        "value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9966-5915"
      },
      "schema:email": "nina.welti@csiro.au",
      "affiliation": [
        "id:organization:csiro-clunies-ross-street-black-mountain-canberra-act-2601"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:csiro-clunies-ross-street-black-mountain-canberra-act-2601",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "CSIRO, Clunies Ross Street, Black Mountain, Canberra, ACT 2601"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:118",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:118",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:118",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9966-5915"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "This database provides a long-term global record of the isotopic composition of rainfall and snowfall. Its establishes a worldwide collection network that creates a global community around stable isotopes applications in hydrology and other domains. This dataset connects climate science, hydrology, ecology, and environmental monitoring into a cohesive understanding of Earth’s water and climate systems. This long-term record allows us to create global, continental, and regional models of the water cycle. IGNIP data underpin early-warning systems for droughts and floods, as isotopic hydrology reveals precursors and water-source origins critical for forecasting extreme weather events, such as drought.In Australia, where regional climate variability and water-cycle changes are increasing the occurrence and duration of drought, such data is invaluable to compare changes happening at multiple scale. This scientific understanding is translated to policy makers at multiple levels to inform water allocation and drought resilience policy and action. \n\nIf the IAEA’s Global Network of Isotopes in Precipitation (GNIP) dataset were unavailable, the world would lose a uniquely powerful tool for understanding, predicting, and managing global water and climate systems. Without it, prediction models would lose accuracy in tracing moisture trajectories that drive these phenomena, weakening planning at the national, state, and local levels in Australia. Economically and socially, the lack of GNIP data would weaken efforts to secure sustainable water management. Additionally, isotope-based verification of food, plant, and water origins—vital for trade integrity and environmental law enforcement—would be hindered, leading to weaker regulation of water scarcity and fraud in agricultural exports."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator generally interacts with this dataset through isoscape models which are built using the dataset. Additionally, when undertaking field measurements, they use GNIP to create the global meteoric water line and validate the local meteoric water line of their study area. \n\nThey not only interact with the dataset, but also the measurement network, as they are establishing more GNIP monitoring locations in Australia. The purpose of increasing the number of monitoring locations is to increase the local resolution of the data and improve our models based here."
        }
      ]
    },
