        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The Sofar Spotter Data Archive is a globally distributed, open-access collection of ocean wave, wind, and temperature observations gathered by Sofar’s Spotter buoys — a network of low-cost, solar-powered ocean sensors. The archive aggregates years of continuous measurements from thousands of locations across the world’s oceans, spanning coastal and open-ocean environments. \n\nThis dataset provides an unprecedented view of surface ocean dynamics, enabling research in air-sea interaction, climate variability, extreme wave prediction, and coastal resilience. By openly sharing these data through GitHub, Sofar Ocean aims to accelerate scientific discovery, foster reproducible oceanography, and support equitable access to ocean data for scientists, policymakers, and educators worldwide."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The Sofar Spotter Data Archive exemplifies an impactful dataset across all three AGU impact dimensions — People, Planet, and Prosperity.\n\nPeople:\nThis dataset democratizes access to high-quality ocean observations. By removing financial and logistical barriers to ocean monitoring, it supports training, education, and global research collaboration. The data have been used in university labs, graduate theses, and by government agencies. The open-source analysis examples and tutorials available on GitHub further enhance its educational impact, providing hands-on training in data processing, spectral analysis, and oceanographic interpretation using real-world datasets.\n\nPlanet:\nThe archive contributes directly to our understanding of ocean-climate interactions and environmental change. With wave and wind data from both under-observed and remote ocean regions, researchers can use these observations to validate global wave models, monitor marine heatwaves, and study tropical cyclones. The global coverage and consistency of this dataset make it uniquely suited for climate monitoring and model improvement. \n\nProsperity:\nBy underpinning applications in renewable energy siting, coastal infrastructure design, and navigation safety, this dataset can contribute to economic and community resilience. The open-access model also supports equitable participation in the blue economy — from startups to nations — ensuring that environmental intelligence benefits all.\n\nWithout the Spotter Data Archive, the ocean science community would lack one of the few truly global and openly accessible datasets on surface ocean dynamics. Its design reflects a philosophy of stewardship and transparency — each record is versioned, permanently archived, and citable — allowing the community to build upon it with confidence."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "As a member of Sofar Ocean’s science team, the nominator works directly with the Spotter Data Archive both as a contributor and as a user. They use the dataset to explore the status of their Spotter network, identify under-sampled regions to target future deployments, and assess the durability of Spotters in extreme conditions.\n\nBeyond internal research, they engage with the open-science community to expand the dataset’s reach — responding to questions from external researchers, continuing to build examples and analysis notebooks, and helping maintain clear documentation for public use."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Houghton, I. A., Smit, P. B., Clark, D., Dunning, C., Fisher, A., Nidzieko, N. J., Chamberlain, P., & Janssen, T. T. (2021). Performance statistics of a real-time pacific ocean weather sensor network. Journal of Atmospheric and Oceanic Technology, 38(5), 1047–1058. https://doi.org/10.1175/JTECH-D-20-0187.1"
