        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The Global Land Data Assimilation System (GLDAS) project estimates land surface states and fluxes with global coverage and high resolution (up to 0.25 degrees x 3 hourly). GLDAS data products have been generated using several meteorological forcing inputs (see LDAS website for details) and land surface models (LSMs), including the Variable Infiltration Capacity (VIC) model (Liang et al., 1994), the Noah model (Ek et al., 2003), and the Catchment Land Surface Model (CLSM; Koster et al., 2000). GLDAS outputs include water balance states and fluxes such as runoff, soil moisture, and snow water equivalent, as well as energy balance states and fluxes, e.g. land surface temperature and latent and sensible heat flux.\n\nGLDAS-2.2, the newest data collection released under the GLDAS umbrella, contributes a new and important piece of water budget information that most LSMs do a poor job estimating: groundwater storage. GLDAS-2.2 assimilates terrestrial water storage (TWS) from the Gravity Recovery and Climate Experiment (GRACE) and GRACE Follow-On (GRACE-FO) into CLSM to produce groundwater storage estimates. GLDAS 2.2 data are available in two production streams – a “main product” released every 2-6 months for research studies and an “early product” for operational use that is released on a shorter timeframe, but without GRACE assimilation. GLDAS-2.2 data are available at 0.25 degree spatial resolution and three-hourly temporal resolution."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "GLDAS uses land surface models (LSMs) to estimate hydrologic variables such as runoff, evaporation, and soil moisture. LSMs are complex models that integrate a plethora of data sources, including meteorological inputs from numerical weather prediction systems (which themselves integrate atmospheric measurements from satellites, ground-based radars, and weather balloons), and land characteristics such as vegetation cover, albedo, and soil properties.\n\nLSM frameworks like GLDAS are important because they provide estimates of variables that are otherwise difficult to observe, but which have profound implications for our society and our food supply. GLDAS runoff estimates, for example, enable hydrologists to make streamflow forecasts, which inform reservoir storage/release decisions, floodplain mapping, and drought monitoring.\n\nGLDAS-2.2, in particular, addresses a key challenge that other LSM frameworks struggle with. Most land surface models do not explicitly represent groundwater processes (Scanlon et al., 2018). CLSM, for example, calculates groundwater as the difference between total soil profile water storage and soil moisture stored in the root zone (first 100 cm of soil depth). There is no explicit accounting for water table depth or aquifer characteristics. Additionally, and crucially for understanding the impacts of using groundwater for irrigation, none of the GLDAS LSMs account for anthropogenic groundwater withdrawals. \n\nGLDAS-2.2 data have been used to detect and quantify groundwater change in locations around the world, such as India (Dubey et al., 2022), California’s Central Valley (Yazbeck and Rundle, 2023), and the Sahel region in Africa (Fatolazadeh and Goita, 2025). GLDAS-2.2 has also served as a useful validation and training dataset for machine learning models, which have been used to characterize groundwater depletion in urban settings (Uddin et al., 2025) and generate a 60+ year TWS anomaly dataset (Mandal et al., 2025)."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator is a data curator for this dataset at GES DISC. They field questions from data users about a variety of hydrological datasets. GLDAS-2.2 is a popular source of global, high-resolution groundwater storage change data, along with many other water and energy balance variables with implications for understanding human and climate impacts on our water supply.\n\nWhile they have not used GLDAS-2.2 in their research work specifically, they have used GLDAS runoff data (from GLDAS_VIC10_3H_2.1) for comparison with a land surface model setup in the Indus River Basin, as in-situ streamflow observations are not widely available in that region."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Li, B., et al. (2019). Global grace data assimilation for groundwater and drought monitoring: Advances and challenges. Water Resources Research, 55(9), 7564–7586. https://doi.org/10.1029/2018WR024618 \n\nRodell, M., Houser, P. R., Jambor, U., Gottschalck, J., Mitchell, K., Meng, C.-J., Arsenault, K., Cosgrove, B., Radakovich, J., Bosilovich, M., Entin, J. K., Walker, J. P., Lohmann, D., & Toll, D. (2004). The global land data assimilation system. Bulletin of the American Meteorological Society, 85(3), 381–394. https://doi.org/10.1175/BAMS-85-3-381"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Eldrett JS, Ma C, Bergman S, Ozkan A, Minisini D, Lutz B, Jackett S-J, Macaulay C, Kelly AE. 2015b. Origin of limestone–marlstone cycles: Astronomic forcing of organic-rich sedimentary rocks from the Cenomanian to early Coniacian of the Cretaceous Western Interior Seaway, USA. Earth and Planetary Science Letters 423:98–113. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.epsl.2015.04.026"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Jacob Schaperow"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Email Address",
          "schema:value": "jacob.r.schaperow@nasa.gov"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator ORCID",
          "schema:value": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7012-5472"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Affiliation or Organization",
          "schema:value": "NASA Goddard Earth Sciences Data and Information Services Center (GES DISC), NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, 8800 Greenbelt Rd, Greenbelt, MD 20771, USA"
        }
      ]
    },
