          "value": "https://doi.org/10.25921/x46c-3620"
        }
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:noaa-national-centers-for-environmental-information"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "NOAA National Centers for Environmental Information"
        }
      ],
      "description": "This dataset includes hourly estimates of sea surface temperature and near-surface ocean currents derived from the data collected by satellite-tracked surface drifting buoys, or drifters, of the NOAA Global Drifter Program. These estimates are currently derived by the Drifter Data Assembly Center (DAC) of the NOAA’s Atlantic Oceanographic and Meteorological Laboratory (AOML) in Miami, following the methodologies described in two peer-reviewed papers which were led by the nominator.\n\nThe principal data consist of 19 different variables, including positions (latitude and longitude), ocean velocities (eastward, northward), and sea surface temperatures (SST; total, diurnal, and non-diurnal components) with accompanying uncertainty estimates, along the trajectories of drifters, at hourly intervals. The associated metadata consist of 39 variables, including identification and experiment numbers, deployment and death dates and times, and various specific information related to drifter types, characteristics, and manufacturers. The latest release of the dataset (version 2.01.1) contains the data from 19396 trajectories, totaling 197, 214, 787 hourly observations from October 1987 to October 2022.\n\nThis dataset is built from a larger historical database of drifter data as part of the NOAA Global Drifter Program. The historical observations, with the earliest ones from 1979, have been processed in incremental steps to generate a 6-hour joint dataset of drifter position, velocity, and SST estimates, along with their uncertainty estimates (a separate data product from the one being nominated here). Because the frequency of drifter observations has increased since the onset of the array, an hourly product of drifter velocity estimates with uncertainties has been generated following a new estimation methodology since 2016, and the accompanying hourly product of temperature estimates with uncertainties since 2022 (this dataset).",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
