    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:121",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:121",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:121",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6051-5426"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The hourly drifter dataset is, to this date, the only in situ dataset of total near-surface ocean velocity and sea surface temperature that can be used to achieve a global statistical description of the complete spectrum of oceanic near-surface variability. Since its inception, ocean scientists such as myself and others have used this dataset to describe and characterize oceanic motions at a wide range of spatial and temporal scales. These characterizations are not only useful to further test our theoretical understanding of the mechanics of the climate system, but also to evaluate global numerical simulations of this system. These simulations in turn, feed into predictions of the future state of our climate.\n\nBeyond the phenomenology of ocean currents for the study of the ocean and the climate as physical systems, this dataset has human, societal, and economic impacts. For example, it form the basis of statistical models which can be used for the prediction of the spread at the ocean surface of drifting organisms, pollutants, plastics, and even human lives lost at sea. \n\nThis dataset is impactful in the ways described above, but I believe also has the potential to broaden further its impacts by supporting the calibration and validation of multiple upcoming and planned remote sensing datasets from satellites. For example, the latest surface water and ocean topography mission (SWOT) is producing various estimates of near-surface ocean velocities from both the effects of the large-scale circulation and of tides, which can be compared globally to no other dataset than the hourly drifter dataset.\n\nFinally, another strength of this dataset lies not only in its regular updates as new observations become available, but also in its potential to be refined and expanded. The hourly estimate of near-surface ocean properties (velocity, temperature) are obtained by fitting mathematical models of the temporal evolution of oceanic properties to the original drifter observations. As such, it is potentially feasible to further refine these mathematical models to extract even further information from the original observations, as well as further calibrate satellite retrieval algorithm of sea surface temperature.\n\nAnother potential expansion of this dataset resides in the possibility of the NOAA Global Drifter Program to become a new observing system to monitor the changes of global mean sea level. This technological capacity simply stems from the fact that surface drifters are now tracked by the Global Positioning System (GPS)—which will continuously provide the geographical positions and the height of the sea surface along the buoys’ trajectories. The sea-level height data collected in this way, averaged over regional basins and the global ocean, can theoretically provide daily measures of regional and global mean sea levels."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator, having designed the hourly dataset in two steps with their colleagues, in 2016 for ocean velocity and in 2022 for ocean temperature, they continue to work closely with the scientists of the GDP Data Assembly Center to refine, expand, and update the dataset. The dataset is also at the core of a project called Clouddrift, funded by the National Science Foundation, which aims at facilitating the use of Lagrangian data for ocean and climate sciences. Notably, the Clouddrift project has enabled the distribution of the hourly drifter dataset as a analysis-ready cloud-optimized dataset in the cloud in an AWS S3 bucket, in collaboration with the NOAA Open Data Dissemination Program (see https://registry.opendata.aws/noaa-oar-hourly-gdp/). In terms of scientific investigations, the nominator uses this dataset to study the full spectrum of oceanic near-surface variability, from the general circulation to internal oceanic tides, as well as validate various models. For example, they have used this dataset with co-authors to evaluate global oceanic numerical simulations and global barotropic tidal models."
        }
      ]
    },
