    {
      "@id": "id:organization:university-of-colorado-1111-engineering-drive-campus-box-428-boulder-co-usa-8030",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "University of Colorado, 1111 Engineering Drive, Campus Box 428,, Boulder, CO, USA 80309"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:122",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:122",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:122",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6883-7240"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The major contribution/strengths of this data set are - (i) long-term, spanning multiple decades, (ii) inclusive of many stations, requiring a minimum of 20-years of data for stations to be included from CONUS and S. Canada, or 1-year of data for Mexico, and (iii) spatially continuous, with a consistent gridding approach across international boundaries that avoids discontinuities present in other datasets. All of these are crucial for hydroclimate studies and diagnostics. They continue to be invaluable in uncovering new insights. To highlight some-\nThe data set has been instrumental in attribution studies of meteorological versus land-surface moisture states on historical droughts (Livneh and Hoerling 2016), flooding (Badger et al., 2018), sediment transport (Stewart et al., 2017) and snowpack evolution (Houle et al., 2017)."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "Recently, The nominator’s group has been investigating the role of winter snow (precipitation) and antecedent Fall land conditions on the subsequent spring season streamflows in the Colorado River. To obtain a robust estimate of the basin winter precipitation, this dataset is crucial. Other data sets are either coarse in space and/or in time. On these aspects the Livneh dataset stands out."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:122",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 123,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Ocean Science, Hydrology, Cryosphere"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "Livneh Hydrometeorological dataset"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Ben Livneh"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://psl.noaa.gov/data/gridded/data.livneh.html \n\nand https://doi.org/10.7289/v5x34vf6"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "NOAA Physical Sciences Laboratory (PSL) \n\nand NOAA National Centers for Environmental Information (NCEI)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.7289/v5x34vf6 \n\nand https://doi.org/10.7289/v5x34vf6"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Ben Livneh"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "This nomination covers two related, publicly available hydrometeorological datasets. The original dataset, covering the conterminous U.S. (CONUS) from 1915-2011, provides a long-term, hydrologically consistent set of land surface fluxes and states derived from daily observations. It increased the spatial resolution to 1/16° and extended the temporal range of previous work, making it valuable for historical climate and water budget studies.\nThe newer dataset expands on this, with a spatial coverage including all of Mexico, CONUS, and southern Canada for the period 1950-2013 (with extensions to 2021). A key improvement is the implementation of orographic precipitation adjustments for Mexico and Canada, which significantly impacts precipitation totals in mountainous regions and improves the continuity of data across international borders. Both datasets are gridded at a 1/16° (~6km) resolution and are widely used for hydrologic modeling, climate change assessment, and drought reconstruction"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The major contribution/strengths of this data set are - (i) long-term, spanning multiple decades, (ii) inclusive of many stations, requiring a minimum of 20-years of data for stations to be included from CONUS and S. Canada, or 1-year of data for Mexico, and (iii) spatially continuous, with a consistent gridding approach across international boundaries that avoids discontinuities present in other datasets. All of these are crucial for hydroclimate studies and diagnostics. They continue to be invaluable in uncovering new insights. To highlight some-\nThe data set has been instrumental in attribution studies of meteorological versus land-surface moisture states on historical droughts (Livneh and Hoerling 2016), flooding (Badger et al., 2018), sediment transport (Stewart et al., 2017) and snowpack evolution (Houle et al., 2017)."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "Recently, The nominator’s group has been investigating the role of winter snow (precipitation) and antecedent Fall land conditions on the subsequent spring season streamflows in the Colorado River. To obtain a robust estimate of the basin winter precipitation, this dataset is crucial. Other data sets are either coarse in space and/or in time. On these aspects the Livneh dataset stands out."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Livneh, B., Bohn, T. J., Pierce, D. W., Muñoz-Arriola, F., Nijssen, B., Vose, R., Cayan, D. R., & Brekke, L. (2015). A spatially comprehensive, meteorological data set for Mexico, the U.S., and southern Canada (NCEI Accession 0129374) [Dataset]. NOAA National Centers for Environmental Information. https://doi.org/10.7289/V5X34VF6"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "332:138–155. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gca.2022.06.016"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Balaji Rajagopalan"
