        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "This data set provides daily 4 km snow water equivalent (SWE) and snow depth over the conterminous United States. It was developed by consistently assimilating PRISM daily 4 km precipitation and temperature data, SWE and snow depth data from thousands of in-situ snow stations from the SNOTEL network, and snow depth data from the COOP network. The assimilation of the SWE and snow depth measurements in this data set was achieved by using the key idea in Broxton et al. (2016a) along with the new snow density model described in Dawson et al. (2017). A summary of how the method was additionally refined, as well as a trend/driver analysis of the data set, are provided in Zeng et al. (2018).\n\nThe ratio between observed SWE, which is normalized by the accumulated snowfall, and modeled ablation (based on a temperature index snow model forced with PRISM data) is interpolated between the station locations. The results are then used to correct a background SWE field generated using a gridded version of the same PRISM-based snow model. The assimilation includes a new snow density parameterization, which is used to combine SWE and snow depth measurements from hundreds of SNOTEL sites with the snow depth measurements from thousands of COOP sites. In addition, snowfall is separated from rainfall using a temperature threshold, which is based on the occurrence of snow and rain at individual stations; the snow ablation is also estimated as a function of temperature, which is based on station data.\n\nFrom https://nsidc.org/sites/default/files/documents/user-guide/nsidc-0719-v001-userguide.pdf"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The Daily 4 km Gridded SWE and Snow Depth from Assimilated In-Situ and Observation-Based Data over the Conterminous US [more commonly known as the University of Arizona (UA) snowpack data] is a widely used snow product over the conterminous US. The dataset utilizes a data fusion approach, which combines observation-based gridded temperature and precipitation data with snowpack data from thousands of in-situ stations across the US. The inclusion of a snow density model (Dawson et al., 2017), a proven snow ablation model (Broxton et al., 2016b), and innovative interpolation methodology (Broxton et al., 2016a) allows for the best- possible SWE data in areas with in-situ SWE measurements, in-situ snow depth measurements, as well as areas where there are substantial gaps between stations. The UA product has been shown to produce high quality SWE and snow depth estimates that are superior to many other model and satellite-based SWE estimates.\n\nDespite being first produced nearly a decade ago, the UA snowpack data is still unique in that it is the only observationally based snowpack dataset that provides daily snowpack data going back to October 1981 over the entire conterminous US. This long temporal record, complete spatial coverage, and consistent algorithm make it ideal for a variety of applications. In particular, it is well suited for basin scale water use by water managers who need long-consistent datasets for training streamflow and water supply forecasting models. The UA snowpack data, along with a real-time version, known as the Snow Water Artificial Neural Network (SWANN), enables operationally relevant snowpack monitoring and historical contextualization, and water supply forecasting by agencies across the western US, including the Salt River Project in Arizona, the California Department of Water Resources, and the Colorado Basin River Forecast Center.\n\nIn addition, the UA snowpack data also provides critical estimates of snow water equivalent (SWE) for studying the health and sustainability of the environment. Its long temporal record enables the study of changes in snowpack patterns across the US since the 1980s. In particular, while the hundreds of SNOTEL sites have been extremely useful for a variety of snowpack-related applications at those sites over the western U.S., Zeng et al. (2018) found that their SWE trends and interannual variability do not represent those over the whole western U.S. – which can only be quantified by the UA snowpack data (which assimilates SNOTEL and other snowpack measurements). It is also an ideal dataset for the assessment and improvement of Land Surface and Earth System Models.\n\nNote that the first paper (Broxton et al. 2016a) describing the fusion of gridded and in situ data was published in an AGU journal, while the last paper (Zeng et al. 2018) on the UA snowpack data description was also published in an AGU journal and was highlighted at the AGU Fall Meeting 2018 Press Conference: The changing U.S. snowpack."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The UA snowpack data is used extensively in the nominator’s research. For example, they have looked at trends in snowpack variables through time, done extensive comparisons with other remotely sensed snowpack datasets, used the data to evaluate model results, and used the data for data assimilation for improved regional weather forecasting. The long temporal record, complete spatial coverage, and high resolution also make it ideal for use in basin-scale hydrological applications. For example, they have used it in research about water supply forecasting for individual river basins, subseasonal to seasonal forecasting of other hydrological variables across the western US, and featured the dataset in an online map viewer (https://snowview.arizona.edu) that aims to help water managers to put current snowpack and other hydrometeorological conditions into a historical context."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Broxton, P. D., Dawson, N., & Zeng, X. (2016). Linking snowfall and snow accumulation to generate spatial maps of SWE and snow depth. Earth and Space Science, 3(6), 246–256. https://doi.org/10.1002/2016EA000174 \n\nBroxton, P. D., Zeng, X., & Dawson, N. (2016). Why do global reanalyses and land data assimilation products underestimate snow water equivalent? Journal of Hydrometeorology, 17(11), 2743–2761. https://doi.org/10.1175/JHM-D-16-0056.1 \n\nDawson, N., Broxton, P., & Zeng, X. (2017). A new snow density parameterization for land data initialization. Journal of Hydrometeorology, 18(1), 197–207. https://doi.org/10.1175/JHM-D-16-0166.1 \n\nZeng, X., Broxton, P., & Dawson, N. (2018). Snowpack change from 1982 to 2016 over conterminous united states. Geophysical Research Letters, 45(23). https://doi.org/10.1029/2018GL079621"
