        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "10.7914/SN/XA_1969"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "This is a new archive of the Apollo Passive Seismic data. The archive contains timing information.\nFor more details, please see: Nunn, C., Nakamura, Y., Kedar, S., & Panning, M. P. (2022). A new archive of apollo’s lunar seismic data. The Planetary Science Journal, 3(9), 219. https://doi.org/10.3847/PSJ/ac87af\n\nIt is also recommended that the following Getting Started page be read:\nhttps://github.com/cerinunn/pdart/blob/master/getting_started.ipynb \n\nhttps://www.fdsn.org/networks/detail/XA_1969/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "This dataset has a long history. It was originally collected on the Moon during the Apollo missions, and sent directly to Earth, in real time. It was one of the first examples of a digital seismic network. It covers a network of seismic stations which were placed on the Moon by Apollo astronauts, and operated from 1969 until the stations were turned off in 1977. The nominator is recommending a version which was reprocessed for modern seismologists, and rearchived in 2022. \nThe dataset was used to discover 22,0000 previously unidentified moonquakes. The paper, which was published in AGU’s JGR Planets, reanalyzed the seismic data using the new archive (Onodera, 2024). Headlines included “New Moonquakes from Old Data” (Montési, 2024), “Moonquakes are much more common than thought, Apollo data suggest” (Grossman, 2024), “Number of known moonquakes tripled with discovery in Apollo archive” (Voosen, 2024). \nAs Dr. Keisuke Onodera wrote in his paper, “I utilized the newly archived Apollo lunar seismic data by Nunn et al. (2022), which is available via the Incorporated Research Institutions for Seismology Data Management Center (IRIS/DMC). Their thorough corrections of errors - such as variable sampling rate with temperature, data gaps, and incorrect timestamps—made it much easier to process the Apollo seismic data.” (Onodera, 2024). The team was very pleased that a researcher was able to take their work and use it in a way that would have been very difficult before.\nThe team is also using this dataset to prepare to collect new seismic data on the Moon, with the Farside Seismic Suite (Panning et al., 2021) and with Artemis. The data have been used to support the development of new lunar habitats. \n\nGrossman, L. (2024). Moonquakes are much more common than thought, Apollo data suggest. Science News. https://www.sciencenews.org/article/moonquakes-common-apollo-data-suggest \n\nMontési, L.G.J. (2024). New Moonquakes from Old Data. EOS. https://eos.org/editor-highlights/new-moonquakes-from-old-data \n\nNunn, C., Nakamura, Y., Kedar, S., & Panning, M. P. (2022). A new archive of apollo’s lunar seismic data. The Planetary Science Journal, 3(9), 219. https://doi.org/10.3847/PSJ/ac87af \n\nOnodera, K. (2024). New views of lunar seismicity brought by analysis of newly discovered moonquakes in Apollo short‐period seismic data. Journal of Geophysical Research: Planets, 129(7), e2023JE008153. https://doi.org/10.1029/2023JE008153 \n\nPanning, M.P., Kedar, S., Bowles, N., Calcutt, S., Cutler, J., Elliott, J.O., Garcia, R.F., Kawamura, T., Lognonné, P.H., Miller, E.A., Nunn, C., Pike, W.T., Pont, G., De Raucourt, S., Standley, I.M., Walsh, W., Weber, R.C., Yana, C., 2021. Farside Seismic Suite (FSS): First seismic data from the farside of the Moon delivered by a commercial lander, in: AGU Fall Meeting.\n\nVoosen, P (2024). Number of known moonquakes tripled with discovery in Apollo archive. Science. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.zcitjc1"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator originally developed the dataset to support the analysis of Apollo seismic data. They’re currently using it to support the return to the Moon, and to understand the requirements for new seismic missions, such as the Farside Seismic Suite (FSS), the Artemis Lunar Environment Monitoring Station (LEMS) and the Artemis South Pole Seismic Station (SPSS)."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Nunn, C., Nakamura, Y., Kedar, S., & Panning, M. P. (2022). A new archive of apollo’s lunar seismic data. The Planetary Science Journal, 3(9), 219. https://doi.org/10.3847/PSJ/ac87af"
        },
