      "@id": "id:dataset:128",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "International Sunspot Number",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:128",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:space-and-planetary-science"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Clette Frédéric, Lefèvre Laure"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://sidc.be/SILSO/home"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:https-sidc-be-silso-datafiles"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "WDC-SILSO - Royal Observatory of Belgium"
        }
      ],
      "description": "The International Sunspot Number (SN) computed by the World Data Center for the Sunspot Index and Long-term Solar Observations (WDC-SILSO) measures the number of spots (Ns) and groups of spots (Ng) on the surface of the Sun every day through a worldwide network of observing stations according to a formula developed by Professor Rudolf Wolf from the Zürich Observatory in 1849 (SN = Ns + 10x Ng; Wolf, R.: 1851, Sonnenflecken-Beobachtungen in der zweiten Hälfte des Jahres 1850. Mitt. Nat.forsch. Ges. Bern 207, 89.). It is the oldest experiment still ongoing since its first measurements date back to the beginning of the seventeenth century (Owens, 2013). It is a measure of solar activity on the very long-term and thus an invaluable dataset for e.g. climate studies. Its first version was called the Zürich sunspot number series (or Sunspot Number version 1.0) and was replaced in 2015 by a new improved version (version 2.0) that includes several corrections of past inhomogeneities in the time series.",
      "isReferencedBy": [
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "http://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007%2F978-1-4939-2584-1_3",
          "schema:url": [
            "http://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007%2F978-1-4939-2584-1_3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "@type": [
            "dct:BibliographicResource",
            "schema:CreativeWork"
          ],
          "schema:citation": "The production of version 1.0 is described in Clette, F., Berghmans, D., Vanlommel, P., Van der Linden, R.A.M., Koeckelenbergh, A., and Wauters, L.: 2007, Advances in Space Research, 40, 919. doi:10.1016/j.asr.2006.12.045.\nWhile the recalibration leading to version 2.0 is described in:\n1. Clette, F., Svalgaard, L., Vaquero, J. M., & Cliver, E. W. (2014). Revisiting the sunspot number: A 400-year perspective on the solar cycle. Space Science Reviews, 186(1–4), 35–103. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-014-0074-2 \n2. Clette, F., & Lefèvre, L. (2016). The new sunspot number: Assembling all corrections. Solar Physics, 291(9–10), 2629–2651. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11207-016-1014-y \n3. Clette, F., Lefèvre, L., Cagnotti, M., Cortesi, S., & Bulling, A. (2016). The revised brussels–locarno sunspot number(1981 – 2015). Solar Physics, 291(9–10), 2733–2761. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11207-016-0875-4 \n4. Clette, F., Cliver, E. W., Lefèvre, L., Svalgaard, L., Vaquero, J. M., & Leibacher, J. W. (2016). Preface to topical issue: Recalibration of the sunspot number. Solar Physics, 291(9–10), 2479–2486. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11207-016-1017-8",
