    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:128",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:128",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:128",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4052-9462",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8306-2500",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1005-7353",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6715-7166",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8754-1509"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The sunspot number is THE parameter characterizing solar activity, including the solar wind and space weather. The sunspot number therefore characterizes the state of the whole heliosphere with impacts on topics as diverse as planetary ionospheres, satellite orbitography and possibly even an influence on climatology (Maunder minimum, etc)."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "The International Sunspot Number (SN) is the only index that covers a large range of solar activity. It supports our space assets by providing the data needed for understanding satellite drag and navigation/communication outages. The solar flares and coronal mass ejections that correlate with SN can harm satellites, astronauts, and even power systems on the Earth's surface. It also provides the only long-term, verifiable, quantitative assessment of the solar dynamo that generates the magnetic responsible for those flares and ejections. The knowledge gained from the Sun through SN then guides our understanding of stellar activity."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "3",
          "schema:text": "The international Sunspot Number is a highly impactful dataset because it represents the variation of activity over several centuries of our main source of light and heat, i.e. the Sun. It is one of the oldest scientific experiments still ongoing since its first measurements date back to the beginning of the seventeenth century with observations from well-known figures of science like Galileo Galilei. If it did not exist, we would be severely limited in our understanding of how the variability of the Sun on the short and long-term can impact the Earth and its surroundings (the heliosphere). We would have no dataset to study detailed solar variability on the long-term, thus no clear way of assessing the solar forcing on the Earth’s atmosphere before humanity became the main driver of climate change. Cosmogenic nuclides series like 14C and 10Be can help assessing longer term variations but they remain indirect measurements and their time-resolution is limited. Only the Sunspot Number series links the active Sun that we witnessed in modern times (and for which we have multiple datasets, but limited in time), to a very quiet Sun that was observed in the 17th and the beginning of the 18th century (i.e. during the Maunder minimum). SN helps understanding the Sun’s past and current behavior as well as predict its future behavior. Having reliable solar data helps protect our economy by predicting catastrophic events, thus preserving communication, navigation, and energy systems from disruptions caused by space weather or disasters related to climate change. It allows for early warnings, which can help reduce damage and keep societies running smoothly. Plus, it’s a key tool for education — teaching people about solar physics, climate and space weather — and for disaster response, ultimately helping to save lives and infrastructures. In essence, the sunspot number dataset underpins scientific research, societal safety, environmental understanding, and economic stability, making it a vital resource for sustainable development and resilience."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "4",
          "schema:text": "The International Sunspot Number is one of the longest time data series in history (going back up to Galileo Galilei's times). It is a reference for vast amounts of solar physics papers and statistical analysis."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "5",
          "schema:text": "The Sunspot Number (SN) stands as one of the most influential and enduring datasets in the history of Earth and Space Sciences. It is the cornerstone for understanding solar activity and its manifold effects on our planet. For more than four centuries, this record has provided a continuous measure of solar variability, linking the dynamics of our star to environmental, climatic, and technological processes on Earth.\n\nWithout the Sunspot Number, our ability to investigate the Sun–Earth connection would be severely limited. Many foundational advances in geophysics, climatology, and space weather research rely on this dataset. From reconstructing past solar cycles and understanding long-term solar variability to assessing its role in climate change and geomagnetic storms, the SN serves as a universal benchmark. Its absence would have dramatically slowed the progress of heliophysics and Earth system science over the past century.\n\nThe Sunspot Number embodies the spirit of citizen science. Anyone equipped with a modest telescope can count sunspots and contribute to this global effort, an experiment that has been running unbroken for over 400 years. Today, this activity continues to engage amateur astronomers, educators, and students worldwide, making it one of the most accessible tools for scientific education and training in solar and space physics. It fosters awareness of our planet’s dependence on solar activity and builds capacity for public engagement with science.\n\nAs a global indicator of solar behavior, the Sunspot Number provides an essential reference for studying geophysical and climatic phenomena. Its long-term record has allowed scientists to demonstrate, for example, that the high solar activity of the mid-20th century was comparable to that of the late 18th century —evidence that helped confirm that recent global warming is anthropogenic. Moreover, it underpins models of the solar cycle, geomagnetic activity, and cosmic ray modulation (key drivers of space weather that directly affect the Earth’s environment and technological systems).\n\nThe Sunspot Number contributes to global economic and social resilience by informing space weather prediction and risk mitigation. Since solar storms can disrupt satellite operations, communications, power grids, and navigation systems, accurate monitoring of solar activity is vital for the protection of critical infrastructure. The SN supports this by providing the historical context and statistical foundation upon which forecasting models are built. Its impact extends far beyond academic research. It is fundamental to ensuring the stability and prosperity of our technological society.\n\nIn summary, the Sunspot Number is not just a dataset: it is a scientific heritage of humanity. It bridges professional science and citizen observation, past and future, Earth and space. Its continued refinement and accessibility are essential for advancing knowledge, protecting our world, and inspiring future generations."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "Images of the solar corona are significantly different in solar minimum as compared to solar maximum. As a science planner of solar space telescope observations, this influences eg directly the exposure times and targets of interest. For historical images, the observation date with respect to the solar cycle determines what the image look like."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "The nominator predicts SN for space agencies and studied other phenomena that are in phase of out of phase with sunspots."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "3",
          "schema:text": "The nominator's interaction with the Sunspot Number dataset is very special as they are responsible for the production and curation of this invaluable series at the WDC-SILSO. They lead the center that gathers data from every corner of the world in order to compute it every month. They also work at the observing station associated to the WDC-SILSO where they observe the Sun and draw sunspots regularly. But they remain a scientist first and foremost, and their mission is to scrutinize every aspect of it to see where it can be improved. Maintaining and improving the historical part of the series entails putting on an archeologist hat and, with the help of many colleagues, unearthing solar observations from dusty archives all over the world, and then, at the opposite, using very modern AI driven methods to extract data from them efficiently. Producing the modern part of the series, on the other hand, means using the most recent statistical tools and software at our disposal for the most accurate data possible. The variety of tasks associated to caring for this very special dataset is what makes it so captivating and inspiring."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "4",
          "schema:text": "The nominator interacts by verifying solar radio observations performed at the Humain Radio Astronomy Station."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "5",
          "schema:text": "The nominator earned their Ph.D. with a dissertation on the history of physics and astronomy. Afterward, they began teaching and conducting research in a university physics department—yet they found themself drawn to historical documents rather than laboratory experiments. Having been an amateur astronomer since their teenage years, they have been counting sunspots daily with a small telescope since they were 17 (a practice they still continue today).\n\nBringing together these two passions, they began to wonder whether historical documents could be used to improve the Sunspot Number series and, at the same time, to enhance understanding of the Earth’s past climate. These two interests have guided their scientific career for more than 25 years.\n\nEven now, they still feel a thrill when they uncover forgotten solar observations hidden in dusty archives. Throughout their career, they have had the privilege of working within an enthusiastic international community devoted to refining the Sunspot Number. Together, remarkable progress has been made: today, the Sun’s past activity is understood far better than it was a quarter of a century ago. This shared achievement continues to inspire them and fuels their passion for research."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:128",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 129,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Space and Planetary Science"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "International Sunspot Number"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Clette Frédéric, Lefèvre Laure"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://sidc.be/SILSO/home"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "https://sidc.be/SILSO/datafiles"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "https://doi.org/10.24414/qnza-ac80"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "WDC-SILSO - Royal Observatory of Belgium"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The International Sunspot Number (SN) computed by the World Data Center for the Sunspot Index and Long-term Solar Observations (WDC-SILSO) measures the number of spots (Ns) and groups of spots (Ng) on the surface of the Sun every day through a worldwide network of observing stations according to a formula developed by Professor Rudolf Wolf from the Zürich Observatory in 1849 (SN = Ns + 10x Ng; Wolf, R.: 1851, Sonnenflecken-Beobachtungen in der zweiten Hälfte des Jahres 1850. Mitt. Nat.forsch. Ges. Bern 207, 89.). It is the oldest experiment still ongoing since its first measurements date back to the beginning of the seventeenth century (Owens, 2013). It is a measure of solar activity on the very long-term and thus an invaluable dataset for e.g. climate studies. Its first version was called the Zürich sunspot number series (or Sunspot Number version 1.0) and was replaced in 2015 by a new improved version (version 2.0) that includes several corrections of past inhomogeneities in the time series."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nThe sunspot number is THE parameter characterizing solar activity, including the solar wind and space weather. The sunspot number therefore characterizes the state of the whole heliosphere with impacts on topics as diverse as planetary ionospheres, satellite orbitography and possibly even an influence on climatology (Maunder minimum, etc).\n\nNominator 2:\nThe International Sunspot Number (SN) is the only index that covers a large range of solar activity. It supports our space assets by providing the data needed for understanding satellite drag and navigation/communication outages. The solar flares and coronal mass ejections that correlate with SN can harm satellites, astronauts, and even power systems on the Earth's surface. It also provides the only long-term, verifiable, quantitative assessment of the solar dynamo that generates the magnetic responsible for those flares and ejections. The knowledge gained from the Sun through SN then guides our understanding of stellar activity.\n\nNominator 3:\nThe international Sunspot Number is a highly impactful dataset because it represents the variation of activity over several centuries of our main source of light and heat, i.e. the Sun. It is one of the oldest scientific experiments still ongoing since its first measurements date back to the beginning of the seventeenth century with observations from well-known figures of science like Galileo Galilei. If it did not exist, we would be severely limited in our understanding of how the variability of the Sun on the short and long-term can impact the Earth and its surroundings (the heliosphere). We would have no dataset to study detailed solar variability on the long-term, thus no clear way of assessing the solar forcing on the Earth’s atmosphere before humanity became the main driver of climate change. Cosmogenic nuclides series like 14C and 10Be can help assessing longer term variations but they remain indirect measurements and their time-resolution is limited. Only the Sunspot Number series links the active Sun that we witnessed in modern times (and for which we have multiple datasets, but limited in time), to a very quiet Sun that was observed in the 17th and the beginning of the 18th century (i.e. during the Maunder minimum). SN helps understanding the Sun’s past and current behavior as well as predict its future behavior. Having reliable solar data helps protect our economy by predicting catastrophic events, thus preserving communication, navigation, and energy systems from disruptions caused by space weather or disasters related to climate change. It allows for early warnings, which can help reduce damage and keep societies running smoothly. Plus, it’s a key tool for education — teaching people about solar physics, climate and space weather — and for disaster response, ultimately helping to save lives and infrastructures. In essence, the sunspot number dataset underpins scientific research, societal safety, environmental understanding, and economic stability, making it a vital resource for sustainable development and resilience.\n\nNominator 4:\nThe International Sunspot Number is one of the longest time data series in history (going back up to Galileo Galilei's times). It is a reference for vast amounts of solar physics papers and statistical analysis.\n\nNominator 5:\nThe Sunspot Number (SN) stands as one of the most influential and enduring datasets in the history of Earth and Space Sciences. It is the cornerstone for understanding solar activity and its manifold effects on our planet. For more than four centuries, this record has provided a continuous measure of solar variability, linking the dynamics of our star to environmental, climatic, and technological processes on Earth.\n\nWithout the Sunspot Number, our ability to investigate the Sun–Earth connection would be severely limited. Many foundational advances in geophysics, climatology, and space weather research rely on this dataset. From reconstructing past solar cycles and understanding long-term solar variability to assessing its role in climate change and geomagnetic storms, the SN serves as a universal benchmark. Its absence would have dramatically slowed the progress of heliophysics and Earth system science over the past century.\n\nThe Sunspot Number embodies the spirit of citizen science. Anyone equipped with a modest telescope can count sunspots and contribute to this global effort, an experiment that has been running unbroken for over 400 years. Today, this activity continues to engage amateur astronomers, educators, and students worldwide, making it one of the most accessible tools for scientific education and training in solar and space physics. It fosters awareness of our planet’s dependence on solar activity and builds capacity for public engagement with science.\n\nAs a global indicator of solar behavior, the Sunspot Number provides an essential reference for studying geophysical and climatic phenomena. Its long-term record has allowed scientists to demonstrate, for example, that the high solar activity of the mid-20th century was comparable to that of the late 18th century —evidence that helped confirm that recent global warming is anthropogenic. Moreover, it underpins models of the solar cycle, geomagnetic activity, and cosmic ray modulation (key drivers of space weather that directly affect the Earth’s environment and technological systems).\n\nThe Sunspot Number contributes to global economic and social resilience by informing space weather prediction and risk mitigation. Since solar storms can disrupt satellite operations, communications, power grids, and navigation systems, accurate monitoring of solar activity is vital for the protection of critical infrastructure. The SN supports this by providing the historical context and statistical foundation upon which forecasting models are built. Its impact extends far beyond academic research. It is fundamental to ensuring the stability and prosperity of our technological society.\n\nIn summary, the Sunspot Number is not just a dataset: it is a scientific heritage of humanity. It bridges professional science and citizen observation, past and future, Earth and space. Its continued refinement and accessibility are essential for advancing knowledge, protecting our world, and inspiring future generations."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nImages of the solar corona are significantly different in solar minimum as compared to solar maximum. As a science planner of solar space telescope observations, this influences eg directly the exposure times and targets of interest. For historical images, the observation date with respect to the solar cycle determines what the image look like.\n\nNominator 2:\nThe nominator predicts SN for space agencies and studied other phenomena that are in phase of out of phase with sunspots.\n\nNominator 3:\nThe nominator's interaction with the Sunspot Number dataset is very special as they are responsible for the production and curation of this invaluable series at the WDC-SILSO. They lead the center that gathers data from every corner of the world in order to compute it every month. They also work at the observing station associated to the WDC-SILSO where they observe the Sun and draw sunspots regularly. But they remain a scientist first and foremost, and their mission is to scrutinize every aspect of it to see where it can be improved. Maintaining and improving the historical part of the series entails putting on an archeologist hat and, with the help of many colleagues, unearthing solar observations from dusty archives all over the world, and then, at the opposite, using very modern AI driven methods to extract data from them efficiently. Producing the modern part of the series, on the other hand, means using the most recent statistical tools and software at our disposal for the most accurate data possible. The variety of tasks associated to caring for this very special dataset is what makes it so captivating and inspiring. \n\nNominator 4:\nThe nominator interacts by verifying solar radio observations performed at the Humain Radio Astronomy Station.\n\nNominator 5:\nThe nominator earned their Ph.D. with a dissertation on the history of physics and astronomy. Afterward, they began teaching and conducting research in a university physics department—yet they found themself drawn to historical documents rather than laboratory experiments. Having been an amateur astronomer since their teenage years, they have been counting sunspots daily with a small telescope since they were 17 (a practice they still continue today).\n\nBringing together these two passions, they began to wonder whether historical documents could be used to improve the Sunspot Number series and, at the same time, to enhance understanding of the Earth’s past climate. These two interests have guided their scientific career for more than 25 years.\n\nEven now, they still feel a thrill when they uncover forgotten solar observations hidden in dusty archives. Throughout their career, they have had the privilege of working within an enthusiastic international community devoted to refining the Sunspot Number. Together, remarkable progress has been made: today, the Sun’s past activity is understood far better than it was a quarter of a century ago. This shared achievement continues to inspire them and fuels their passion for research."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "http://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007%2F978-1-4939-2584-1_3 \n\nThe production of version 1.0 is described in Clette, F., Berghmans, D., Vanlommel, P., Van der Linden, R.A.M., Koeckelenbergh, A., and Wauters, L.: 2007, Advances in Space Research, 40, 919. doi:10.1016/j.asr.2006.12.045.\nWhile the recalibration leading to version 2.0 is described in:\n1. Clette, F., Svalgaard, L., Vaquero, J. M., & Cliver, E. W. (2014). Revisiting the sunspot number: A 400-year perspective on the solar cycle. Space Science Reviews, 186(1–4), 35–103. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-014-0074-2 \n2. Clette, F., & Lefèvre, L. (2016). The new sunspot number: Assembling all corrections. Solar Physics, 291(9–10), 2629–2651. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11207-016-1014-y \n3. Clette, F., Lefèvre, L., Cagnotti, M., Cortesi, S., & Bulling, A. (2016). The revised brussels–locarno sunspot number(1981 – 2015). Solar Physics, 291(9–10), 2733–2761. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11207-016-0875-4 \n4. Clette, F., Cliver, E. W., Lefèvre, L., Svalgaard, L., Vaquero, J. M., & Leibacher, J. W. (2016). Preface to topical issue: Recalibration of the sunspot number. Solar Physics, 291(9–10), 2479–2486. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11207-016-1017-8 \n\nVaquero, J. M. (2007). Historical sunspot observations: A review. Advances in Space Research, 40(7), 929–941. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asr.2007.01.087 \n\nClette, F., Svalgaard, L., Vaquero, J. M., & Cliver, E. W. (2014). Revisiting the sunspot number: A 400-year perspective on the solar cycle. Space Science Reviews, 186(1–4), 35–103. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-014-0074-2 \n\nClette, F., & Lefèvre, L. (2016). The new sunspot number: Assembling all corrections. Solar Physics, 291(9–10), 2629–2651. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11207-016-1014-y \n \nClette, F., Lefèvre, L., Cagnotti, M., Cortesi, S., & Bulling, A. (2016). The revised brussels–locarno sunspot number(1981 – 2015). Solar Physics, 291(9–10), 2733–2761. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11207-016-0875-4 \n\nClette, F., Cliver, E. W., Lefèvre, L., Svalgaard, L., Vaquero, J. M., & Leibacher, J. W. (2016). Preface to topical issue: Recalibration of the sunspot number. Solar Physics, 291(9–10), 2479–2486. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11207-016-1017-8 \n\nVaquero, J. M., et al. (2016). A revised collection of sunspot group numbers. Solar Physics, 291(9–10), 3061–3074. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11207-016-0982-2 \n\nMuñoz-Jaramillo, A., & Vaquero, J. M. (2018). Visualization of the challenges and limitations of the long-term sunspot number record. Nature Astronomy, 3(3), 205–211. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41550-018-0638-2 \n\nArlt, R., & Vaquero, J. M. (2020). Historical sunspot records. Living Reviews in Solar Physics, 17(1), 1. https://doi.org/10.1007/s41116-020-0023-y \n\nClette, F., et al. (2023). Recalibration of the sunspot-number: Status report. Solar Physics, 298(3), 44. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11207-023-02136-3"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nhttps://www.nature.com/articles/495300a \n\nNominator 2:\nRoughly 1700 publications at https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/search/fq=%7B!type%3Daqp%20v%3D%24fq_database%7D&fq_database=(database%3Aastronomy%20OR%20database%3Aphysics)&p_=0&q=%20full%3A%22silso%22%20year%3A2013-&sort=date%20desc%2C%20bibcode%20desc\n\nNominator 3:\nThis dataset is reused in many different contexts from Space Weather (e.g. forecasting solar wind speed:https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4365/adf436) to Climate research (e.g. ENSO climate variability: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jastp.2025.106490) to marine biology (e.g. flowering of seagrass: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecss.2025.109483) or technical maintenance of satellites or measurements (e.g. F10.7 :https://doi.org/10.1029/2024SW004060). And this is just a few examples, the Sunspot Number is used in many different fields including the stock markets (e.g. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.techfore.2007.07.001). \n\nNominator 4:\nThis dataset is reused in many different contexts from Space Weather (e.g. forecasting solar wind speed :https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4365/adf436) to Climate research (e.g. ENSO climate variability : https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jastp.2025.106490) to marine biology (e.g. flowering of seagrass : https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecss.2025.109483) or technical maintenance of satellites or measurements (e.g. F10.7 :https://doi.org/10.1029/2024SW004060). And this is just a few examples, the Sunspot Number is used in many different fields including the stock markets (e.g. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.techfore.2007.07.001). \n\nNominator 5: \nThe Sunspot Number is one of the most widely used datasets in the geosciences and space sciences. It serves as a fundamental reference for studies on solar activity, heliophysics, space weather, and Sun–Earth interactions, as well as for research in climate science and long-term variability of the Earth’s atmosphere.\n\nEvidence of its extensive reuse can be found in the vast number of scientific papers that reference or rely on it. For example, a search on the SciX platform (https://scixplorer.org/search?n=10&p=1&q=abs%3A%22Sunspot+Number%22&sort=score+desc&sort=date+desc\n) for publications containing “Sunspot Number” in the abstract returns hundreds of records, revealing continuous scientific interest in this dataset for more than a century.\n\nFurthermore, the citation record of the main papers describing the dataset highlights its exceptional impact. The key reference, Clette et al. (2014, Space Science Reviews), currently has 542 citations in SciX and 822 in Google Scholar (as of 9 October 2025), confirming the dataset’s central role in solar and geophysical research.\n\nThe Sunspot Number has also inspired a number of derived products and complementary datasets, such as sunspot catalogs, group sunspot numbers, and reconstructed solar activity indices. These derivatives, together with the International Sunspot Number Version 2.0, form the backbone of solar activity reconstructions spanning the past four centuries and are essential for multidisciplinary studies linking solar variability with terrestrial and planetary processes."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Nominator Name",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nDavid Berghmans\n\nNominator 2:\nDean Pesnell\n\nNominator 3:\nLaure Lefevre\n\nNominator 4:\nAntonio Martínez Picar\n\nNominator 5:\nJosé Manuel Vaquero"
