    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:130",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:130",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:130",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3859-058X"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The dataset was nominated because it helped renew interest in the Moon, its history, and the processes currently changing the lunar surface. The high resolution NAC observations (50m per pixel), with nearly global coverage, allow an unprecedented view of the surface, both geographically as well as temporally. When acquired, stereo observations can be processed into orthorectified images and digital terrain models, helping us to visualize the surface and make interpretations. Regional mosaics of NAC observations (including the impressive megapixel North Pole mosaic) provides insights that were not possible prior to the data acquisition. Over the sixteen years of operations, repeat coverage allows temporal studies of the surface, helping to constrain the actual change in surface due to impacts and space weathering. The LROC team has also been instrumental in identifying the location of recent lunar missions, successful or not, allowing those teams to review and refine their ability to land on the Moon. Given the large volume of data, both from the NAC and especially from the WAC, which provides global coverage monthly, providing a unique dataset of the Moon that has not been available before, there are numerous discoveries waiting to be found in the data. The archive of derived products is 63 Tbytes of information, while the calibrated NAC/WAC data set has a volume over 1 Petabyte, a truly staggering amount of planetary data."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator was the original LROC SOC manager, responsible for verification and validation of all PDS products generated by the SOC, and bundling that for delivery to the SOC hosted data node (part of the PDS nodes), during the first 7 years of operations. In those 7 years, they were contributors on several papers that used the data to describe new features found in high resolution NAC images, helped catalog polar permanently shadowed craters and analysis of what could be seen in NAC image in the PSRs, collaborating with other LRO instrument (LAMP, Diviner) to better understand the lunar surface, its geologic history, and most importantly how the lunar surface was changing over time (due to impacts)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:130",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 131,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
