      "name": "Sandia National Laboratories, 1515 Eubank Blvd SE, Albuquerque, NM 87123, USA"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:131",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:131",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:131",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4778-1409"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "This dataset is uniquely impactful because it rescues, curates, and makes accessible an irreplaceable record of Earth's interaction with near-Earth objects (NEOs) from an era (1960-1972) that predates modern space-based and digital monitoring. Without this fully curated dataset, researchers could not independently verify historical bolide energy estimates, compare legacy analog instrumentation with modern digital networks, or access the underlying calibrated waveform data from these ten major events.\n\nPlanet Dimension: The dataset provides a fundamental baseline for planetary science, capturing ground-truth measurements of bolide activity during a period with sparse observations. It fills a critical gap in the NEO influx database, allowing for refined meteoroid flux models and long-term comparisons of bolide frequencies and magnitudes. It directly supports the study of geophysical phenomena, including meteoroid fragmentation and the propagation of shock waves through Earth's atmosphere.\n\nPeople Dimension: This release transforms previously inaccessible artifacts (analog strip-charts and limited raw scans) into a research-ready, FAIR-compliant archive. For the first time, it provides cleaned images, geometrically straightened traces, calibrated pressure-time files, and the digitized instrument-response function. This empowers a new generation of researchers by enabling independent study, reproducible reanalysis, and new applications in atmospheric acoustics that were previously impossible.\n\nProsperity Dimension: The data directly supports planetary defense and community resilience. By establishing a historical baseline for meter-to-tens-of-meter scale impacts, the dataset provides important data points for refining impact hazard evaluations and bolide energy-scaling laws. This improved understanding of NEO impact frequency and energy is essential for assessing and mitigating future impact risks, which carry profound socioeconomic consequences.",
          "impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "Planet",
              "schema:text": "The dataset provides a fundamental baseline for planetary science, capturing ground-truth measurements of bolide activity during a period with sparse observations. It fills a critical gap in the NEO influx database, allowing for refined meteoroid flux models and long-term comparisons of bolide frequencies and magnitudes. It directly supports the study of geophysical phenomena, including meteoroid fragmentation and the propagation of shock waves through Earth's atmosphere."
            },
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "People",
              "schema:text": "This release transforms previously inaccessible artifacts (analog strip-charts and limited raw scans) into a research-ready, FAIR-compliant archive. For the first time, it provides cleaned images, geometrically straightened traces, calibrated pressure-time files, and the digitized instrument-response function. This empowers a new generation of researchers by enabling independent study, reproducible reanalysis, and new applications in atmospheric acoustics that were previously impossible."
            },
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "Prosperity",
              "schema:text": "The data directly supports planetary defense and community resilience. By establishing a historical baseline for meter-to-tens-of-meter scale impacts, the dataset provides important data points for refining impact hazard evaluations and bolide energy-scaling laws. This improved understanding of NEO impact frequency and energy is essential for assessing and mitigating future impact risks, which carry profound socioeconomic consequences."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "As a scientist, the nominator uses this dataset to study old fireball events and better understand their energy. Their process starts by picking an event folder. They look at the images, comparing the raw scan to the cleaned-up and straightened images. This helps them see where the data came from.The main part is using the files in the waveform folder. They can open these easily in their analysis program, as they do not require special software. They show the processed signal data, including its timing and strength in pressure units. This dataset makes it possible to connect these old records to modern-day work on planetary defense."
        }
      ]
    },
