      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The Astromaterials Data System curates and preserves unique data that have been acquired by the study of extremely rare and precious material samples brought back from space. Billions of dollars have been invested into flying space crafts to the Moon and to asteroids billions of miles away, to be able to extract fundamental knowledge from them that expands humanity’s understanding of our solar system and the origin of life. Astromat ensures the long-term impact and Return on Investment of these costly sample-return missions. Astromaterials samples have been studied in laboratories around the globe to determine their chemical, physical, mineralogical, and structural properties. The data generated by these studies are unique and invaluable because measurements cannot be repeated due to the extremely limited quantities of material available. Astromat ensures that the data is carefully and professionally curated and archived so it can be reused with confidence to foster new scientific inquiry and inspire new data-driven research paradigms. Open and trusted access to these data not only leverages the gigantic investments of the USA in NASA’s sample-return missions and sample curation, but it also broadens participation in extraterrestrial samples research and provides new opportunities to learn from the samples not only for planetary science researchers, but also for researchers from other disciplines, students and their instructors, as well as the general public."
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator oversees the operation and development of the Astromaterials Data System"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:132",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 133,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Space and Planetary Science"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "Astromaterials Data Synthesis"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Kerstin Lehnert"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://www.astromat.org"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "Astromaterials Data System"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Geoinformatics Research Group, Lamont-Doherty Earth Observatory"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "The Astromaterials Data System (Astromat) is a comprehensive data collection of laboratory analyses of samples returned from space, including samples collected on the Moon during the Apollo missions, samples collected from asteroids Bennu and Ryuku, and samples of solar wind and cosmic dust. It also contains meteorite data, including the entire content of the MetBase database. With funding from NASA, Astromat has restored a vast volume of legacy data generated since the 1970s and now is NASA’s designated archive of laboratory analytical data from recent and future sample-return missions (OSIRIS-REx, Artemis, MARS2020). Astromat compiles and curates both legacy and new compositional data of returned samples into the Astromat Data Synthesis that can be explored via an interactive search interface and subsets of the data can be extracted, fully customized to the needs of the user. The Astromat Data Archive is a CoreTrustSeal certified data repository with thousands of well-documented datasets archived for long-term preservation and access."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "The Astromaterials Data System curates and preserves unique data that have been acquired by the study of extremely rare and precious material samples brought back from space. Billions of dollars have been invested into flying space crafts to the Moon and to asteroids billions of miles away, to be able to extract fundamental knowledge from them that expands humanity’s understanding of our solar system and the origin of life. Astromat ensures the long-term impact and Return on Investment of these costly sample-return missions. Astromaterials samples have been studied in laboratories around the globe to determine their chemical, physical, mineralogical, and structural properties. The data generated by these studies are unique and invaluable because measurements cannot be repeated due to the extremely limited quantities of material available. Astromat ensures that the data is carefully and professionally curated and archived so it can be reused with confidence to foster new scientific inquiry and inspire new data-driven research paradigms. Open and trusted access to these data not only leverages the gigantic investments of the USA in NASA’s sample-return missions and sample curation, but it also broadens participation in extraterrestrial samples research and provides new opportunities to learn from the samples not only for planetary science researchers, but also for researchers from other disciplines, students and their instructors, as well as the general public."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "The nominator oversees the operation and development of the Astromaterials Data System"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "Lehnert, K. A., J. Mays, R. R. Downs, L. Profeta, M. Celnick, G. Danninger, P. Ji, and J. D. Figueroa. \"ASTROMAT: OPTIMIZING REUSE & IMPACT OF ASTROMATERIALS SAMPLES DATA.\" In METEORITICS & PLANETARY SCIENCE, vol. 59, pp. A246-A246. 111 RIVER ST, HOBOKEN 07030-5774, NJ USA: WILEY, 2024.\n\nHezel, D. C., Lehnert, K. A., Elangovan, P., Ji, P., Mays, J., & Koblitz, J. (2025). The MetBase database has been merged into Astromat. Meteoritics & Planetary Science, 60(1), 143–148. https://doi.org/10.1111/maps.14293 \n\nLehnert, Kerstin, Lucia Profeta, and Jennifer Mays. \"Managing Analytical Data from Pristine Returned Samples in Compliance with NASA’S Data Strategy: The Astromaterials Data System.\" Authorea Preprints (2022)."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "Nana Yobo, L., Brandon, A. D., Lauckner, L. M., Eldrett, J. S., Bergman, S. C., & Minisini, D. (2022). Enhanced continental weathering activity at the onset of the mid-Cenomanian Event (Mce). Geochemical Perspectives Letters, 23, 17–22. https://doi.org/10.7185/geochemlet.2231"
        },
