    {
      "@id": "id:dataset:133",
      "@type": [
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "schema:Dataset"
      ],
      "title": "OMNI",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:133",
      "theme": [
        "id:theme:space-and-planetary-science"
      ],
      "creator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Multiple sources"
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:CreatorStatement",
          "name": "Joseph H. King & Natalia E. Papitashvili"
        }
      ],
      "landingPage": [
        "https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/"
      ],
      "inCatalog": [
        "id:repository:omniweb",
        "id:repository:space-physics-data-facility"
      ],
      "agu:repositoryIdentifier": [
        "Multiple (see Short Description submission)"
      ],
      "curator": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:CuratorStatement",
          "name": "Natalia E. Papitashvili & SPDF"
        }
      ],
      "description": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "All the data to which OMNIWeb and its multiple underlying interfaces provide access have in common that they are relevant to heliospheric studies and/or provide solar wind input data for studies of solar wind - magnetosphere coupling, and that they are resident at GSFC/Space Physics Data Facility.\nMost important among of them are Low and High res. OMNI:\n(a) Low res OMNI (LRO) is Hourly \"Near-Earth\" solar wind magnetic field and plasma data, energetic proton fluxes (>1 to >60 MeV), and geomagnetic and solar activity indices. There are Daily, 27-day, and Yearly resolution derived from hourly data also. All details about that data descriptions and about the data access user may find at https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/ow.html.\n(b) High 1-min and 5-min resolution OMNI(HRO) : Solar wind magnetic field and plasma data at Earth's Bow Shock Nose, and geomagnetic activity indices. 5-min resolution derived from 1-min data with added 5-min energetic proton fluxes. All details about high res. OMNi data descriptions and about data access user may find at https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/ow_min.html.\nIn addition, some data sets are as reformatted or have parameters transformed to a different coordinate system to facilitate multi-point heliospheric studies (cf.COHOWeb at https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/coho/).\nMost data sets are from specific instruments carried on specific spacecraft and are as provided by spacecraft instrument teams."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "The OMNI Database is a collection of highly curated datasets that have been serving the Heliophysics community for nearly 50 years. It is one of the many products offered by the Space Physics Data Facility (SPDF) at NASA’s Goddard Space Flight Center (GSFC). OMNI provides magnetic field, plasma, energetic particle, and solar & geomagnetic index data in various resolutions and coordinate systems. However, “OMNI” is not an acronym and simply meant to construe a “variety.” The data in OMNI is collected & aggregated from multiple sources and updated/maintained to provide the highest quality possible.\n \nOMNI is rooted in the Catalogs of Particles & Fields Data and Interplanetary Medium Data Books that were disseminated by NASA’s National Space Science Data Center (NSSDC) in the mid-1970s. Around 1989, the NSSDC started digitizing their datasets, and by the mid-1990s, the OMNI datasets were available in a variety of formats – paper supplement, magnetic tape (binary & ASCII), CD-ROM, and even online (NODIS & WWW). In 1992, the SPDF was founded and began transferring many of the space physics data archiving responsibilities from the NSSDC. Around 2005, a web-based interface for accessing OMNI data – OMNIWeb – was developed. It was further upgraded in 2008 to its current configuration – OMNIWeb Plus (OMNIWeb+) – which integrated various other interfaces such as COHOWeb, FTPBrowser, CDAWeb, and HelioWeb.\n\nThe most popular datasets are as follows:\n\n Low-Resolution OMNI (LRO)\n • LRO is based on hourly-averaged, “near-Earth” solar wind magnetic field & plasma data, energetic particle fluxes, and geomagnetic & solar activity indices. Any data from spacecraft at L1 have been time shifted to their expected magnetospheric arrival times, prior to taking the hourly averages. The hourly data has also been further averaged into daily, 27-day, and yearly resolutions. The time range for the LRO dataset dates all the way back from 1963 to the present, covering multiple solar cycles. \n • More detailed info: https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/html/ow_data.html \n • DOI(s):\n o Hourly LRO: https://doi.org/10.48322/1shr-ht18 \n o Daily LRO: https://doi.org/10.48322/5fmx-hv56 \n o 27-Day LRO: https://doi.org/10.48322/nmh3-jf75 \n\n High-Resolution OMNI (HRO)\n • HRO is based on 1-min solar wind magnetic field & plasma data and geomagnetic indices that have been time shifted to Earth's Bow Shock nose. This 1-min data has also been used to derive a 5-min resolution version that includes 5-min energetic particle fluxes. Each HRO dataset is further divided into non-definitive and definitive variations, with the latter sacrificing a slight decrease in coverage & latency for improved accuracy. The time range for these HRO datasets begins around 1981 and continues to the present, covering a few solar cycles. \n • More detailed info: https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/html/HROdocum.html \n • DOI(s):\n o 1-min HRO: \n • https://doi.org/10.48322/45bb-8792 (non-definitive)\n • https://doi.org/10.48322/mj0k-fq60 (definitive)\n o 5-min HRO: \n • https://doi.org/10.48322/gbpg-5r77 (non-definitive)\n • https://doi.org/10.48322/hkaw-ff03 (definitive)\n\nThe OMNI datasets are accessible via its web-based interface – OMNIWeb+. Some of the more popular datasets have also been added to SPDF’s web-based Coordinated Data Analysis tool – CDAWeb – which can be accessed via HTTPS, FTPS, REST/SOAP web services, HAPI protocol, or third-party software (IDL, Python, SPEDAS/PySPEDAS, Autoplot, etc.)."
        }
      ],
