    },
    {
      "@id": "id:organization:nasa-gsfc-spdf",
      "@type": [
        "schema:Organization",
        "foaf:Organization"
      ],
      "name": "NASA/GSFC/SPDF"
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:nomination:133",
      "@type": [
        "agu:Nomination",
        "prov:Entity"
      ],
      "nominates": "id:dataset:133",
      "wasDerivedFrom": "id:sourcerow:133",
      "nominator": [
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0764-9569",
        "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5032-524X"
      ],
      "justification": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "Dating back to November 1963, the OMNI (not an acronym) dataset provides a comprehensive description of the near-Earth solar wind plasma and magnetic environment invaluable to the heliophysics and geospace communities. The spacecraft data contributing to the OMNI dataset include a number of former and current space missions, such as IMP-8, ACE, Wind, ISEE-3, and Geotail, in geocentric orbit and at the Earth's first Lagrange (L1) point. Thorough analysis is performed in compiling the extensive dataset that is easily accessible via the NASA GSFC/SPDF OMNIWeb interface. OMNI data preserves a history of the Sun-Earth relationship for more than five solar cycles and serves as the \"ground truth\" for solar wind and energetic particle models, enabling more reliable predictions of hazardous conditions for future human exploration in deep space. OMNI data also serves as the primary input for geospace models for space weather predictions that play an important role in protecting commercial and military assets both on the ground and in space. Furthermore, OMNI data is crucial in understanding the solar variability and the Sun's influence on Earth and throughout the solar system, as well as the Sun's interaction with the interstellar medium."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:JustificationStatement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "The OMNI Database is an “unsung hero” that has been supporting the Heliophysics and Space Science communities for nearly 50 years. Its datasets appear across a breadth of areas including education, research, analysis, and operations. It impacts society both directly and indirectly, with the latter often get overlooked. Direct impacts are easier to quantify, as publications, citations, and references are tracked and counted. However, indirect impacts are trickier to identify. For example, parameters from a dataset used early on in an analysis or project – where that specific data is not directly presented in any of the results – typically go unacknowledged. I believe such is likely the case with OMNI data, which is the primary motivation behind this nomination. \n\nPeople Dimension:\n\nOMNI data directly impacts individuals in a multitude of ways. Notably, it is used to educate and train the next generations of space scientists and heliophysicists. In my experience, OMNI data is often required to not only answer homework questions but also complete semester projects in core science programs such as astrophysics, planetary science, space physics, and aerospace engineering – at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. OMNI data is also used in specialized programs such as the NASA Heliophysics Summer School, which exposes graduate students and early-career scientists to an array of tools & resources available for space weather analysis. I also expect that there are advanced high/secondary school courses that use or reference OMNI data in their curricula. I would also venture to say that every graduate student in space science has almost certainly accessed or used data from the OMNI Database at some point in their academic careers. \n\nOMNI also abides by NASA’s FAIR principles – findable, accessible, interoperable, and reusable. Its data is often linked with instructional exercises in finding, accessing, and downloading data from NASA’s systems. More advanced usage typically involves some level of programming, as data retrieval from interfaces like OMNIWeb regularly require recursive processing. These are fundamental skills that space science researchers tend to develop early in their studies. The OMNI data can also be downloaded in many different formats and coordinate systems, heavily leaning into the interoperability principles. Lastly, once a user has the OMNI data, it can be easily reused for many different analyses, as it can be quickly appended or updated whenever necessary. \n\nSPDF tracks access to OMNIWeb+, downloads of OMNI data on CDAWeb (FTPS, HTTPS, and custom files), and references to OMNI/OMNIWeb in publications. Unfortunately, these statistics fail to capture the full extent of OMNI’s impacts, as visualization & analysis and the number of data files used to create custom downloads are not easily traceable. However, SPDF’s statistics clearly demonstrates that OMNI is one of their most popular datasets, and OMNI’s monthly usage has been exponentially increasing over the past few decades (see SPDF Usage [https://spdf.gsfc.nasa.gov/spdf-secaa_usage.html] for more info). \n\nThe two most common methods of accessing OMNI data are through OMNIWeb+ or CDAWeb. OMNIWeb+ has had roughly 800k and 1.1M users in 2024 and 2025, respectively. Of all these users, 90-95% of them accessed at least one of the OMNI datasets (see OMNIWeb Stats [https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/html/stat.html] for more info). In terms of monthly usage, the average users of OMNI data on OMNIWeb+ came out to approximately 64k and 80k for 2024 and 2025, respectively. In terms of CDAWeb, the total number of OMNI data downloads for 2024 and 2025 are 1.49M and 3.40M files, respectively. Over half of these downloads were via HTTPS, while the creation of custom files via the CDAWeb system accounted for roughly 42% (see the Use of SPDF Services [https://spdf.gsfc.nasa.gov/spdf-secaa_usage.html] for more info). As previously mentioned, CDAWeb only tracks downloads from its system, so the actual usage is likely higher, especially with the current shift towards cloud computing. \n\nSPDF conducts a yearly review of articles that mention OMNI in major scientific journals. In recent years, the Heliophysics community acknowledged SPDF in roughly half of articles published in the Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics and Space Weather (see SPDF Acknowledgements [https://spdf.gsfc.nasa.gov/Acknowledgements.html] for more info). In terms of OMNI, either “OMNI” or “OMNIWeb” was specifically referenced in 602 and 691 articles for the years 2024 and 2025, respectively. There is clearly an upward trend in acknowledgments, however, these figures are still likely an underrepresentation of SPDF and OMNI’s impact. Similarly, OMNI’s sole published article, King & Papitashvili (2005) [https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1029/2004JA010649], has only been cited 1,114 times. Given its large number of acknowledgements, this implies that the OMNI paper is not cited as often as it should be. Please see the recommendation for citing the OMNI datasets [https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/html/citing.html]. \n\nPlanet Dimension:\n\nThe OMNI Database plays a crucial role in investigating, analyzing, modeling, and validating many different space weather phenomena. As with its impact on people discussed above, this data has both direct and indirect contributions to these efforts. For example, empirical models normally perform best with a wide coverage of data. Datasets like OMNI lend themselves perfectly to this type of modeling because their data spans multiple decades and contains multiple solar cycles. Likewise, predictive models not only require historical datasets but also key metrics that are used to drive their forecasting capabilities. Validation of these models demands a precise & accurate history of these key metrics. Both empirical & predictive modeling are ideal applications for parameters such as the solar wind magnetic field & plasma data, energetic particle fluxes, and geomagnetic & solar activity indices contained in OMNI. \n\nDuring investigation or analysis, OMNI data is often used to correlate phenomena with increased activity levels, whether it be solar or geomagnetic. For example, a researcher may be interested in a particular phenomenon that is known to appear during intense geomagnetic storms. A typical starting point is to define criteria that identifies these types of storms. Then, researchers query databases such as OMNI for events that meet the criteria. The result is the identification of timeframes for further analysis. This example demonstrates how OMNI is used to support these investigations. Similarly, the inverse is true when analyzing results, where any newly detected phenomena is correlated back to [or potentially explained by] key parameters found in datasets like OMNI. \nProsperity Dimension:\n\nA current paradigm in space weather research & operations (R2O2R) is the development of machine learning (ML) models, or artificial intelligence (AI), specifically with the capability to forecast. Like predictive modeling, ML models require a lot of data, but that data also needs to be high quality to obtain good results. Datasets like OMNI, that are highly curated to the highest of scientific standards, are both powerful and practical when applied in these settings. Many machine learning models are currently being run operationally at NASA’s Community Coordinated Modeling Center (CCMC). For example. the Space Weather Modeling Framework (SWMF [https://ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/models/SWMF~2023/]) integrates & couples various space weather models together into a single, coherent system. SWMF is driven by solar wind plasma and magnetic field data, and CCMC has even developed a get_omni routine [https://ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/ror/requests/GetInput/get_omni.php] that pulls high-resolution OMNI data directly from OMNIWeb. Models such as SMWF at CCMC are primary used for space physics or space weather research but have also been applied to other areas such as alerts/warning of spacecraft charging, solar energetic particle (SEP), or satellite conjunction events. Another key consideration is that OMNI data is not only used to train & drive these ML-based predictive models, but it has also been employed to inform their early development stages & later validation processes.",
          "impactDimension": [
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "People",
              "schema:text": "OMNI data directly impacts individuals in a multitude of ways. Notably, it is used to educate and train the next generations of space scientists and heliophysicists. In my experience, OMNI data is often required to not only answer homework questions but also complete semester projects in core science programs such as astrophysics, planetary science, space physics, and aerospace engineering – at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. OMNI data is also used in specialized programs such as the NASA Heliophysics Summer School, which exposes graduate students and early-career scientists to an array of tools & resources available for space weather analysis. I also expect that there are advanced high/secondary school courses that use or reference OMNI data in their curricula. I would also venture to say that every graduate student in space science has almost certainly accessed or used data from the OMNI Database at some point in their academic careers. \n\nOMNI also abides by NASA’s FAIR principles – findable, accessible, interoperable, and reusable. Its data is often linked with instructional exercises in finding, accessing, and downloading data from NASA’s systems. More advanced usage typically involves some level of programming, as data retrieval from interfaces like OMNIWeb regularly require recursive processing. These are fundamental skills that space science researchers tend to develop early in their studies. The OMNI data can also be downloaded in many different formats and coordinate systems, heavily leaning into the interoperability principles. Lastly, once a user has the OMNI data, it can be easily reused for many different analyses, as it can be quickly appended or updated whenever necessary. \n\nSPDF tracks access to OMNIWeb+, downloads of OMNI data on CDAWeb (FTPS, HTTPS, and custom files), and references to OMNI/OMNIWeb in publications. Unfortunately, these statistics fail to capture the full extent of OMNI’s impacts, as visualization & analysis and the number of data files used to create custom downloads are not easily traceable. However, SPDF’s statistics clearly demonstrates that OMNI is one of their most popular datasets, and OMNI’s monthly usage has been exponentially increasing over the past few decades (see SPDF Usage [https://spdf.gsfc.nasa.gov/spdf-secaa_usage.html] for more info). \n\nThe two most common methods of accessing OMNI data are through OMNIWeb+ or CDAWeb. OMNIWeb+ has had roughly 800k and 1.1M users in 2024 and 2025, respectively. Of all these users, 90-95% of them accessed at least one of the OMNI datasets (see OMNIWeb Stats [https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/html/stat.html] for more info). In terms of monthly usage, the average users of OMNI data on OMNIWeb+ came out to approximately 64k and 80k for 2024 and 2025, respectively. In terms of CDAWeb, the total number of OMNI data downloads for 2024 and 2025 are 1.49M and 3.40M files, respectively. Over half of these downloads were via HTTPS, while the creation of custom files via the CDAWeb system accounted for roughly 42% (see the Use of SPDF Services [https://spdf.gsfc.nasa.gov/spdf-secaa_usage.html] for more info). As previously mentioned, CDAWeb only tracks downloads from its system, so the actual usage is likely higher, especially with the current shift towards cloud computing. \n\nSPDF conducts a yearly review of articles that mention OMNI in major scientific journals. In recent years, the Heliophysics community acknowledged SPDF in roughly half of articles published in the Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics and Space Weather (see SPDF Acknowledgements [https://spdf.gsfc.nasa.gov/Acknowledgements.html] for more info). In terms of OMNI, either “OMNI” or “OMNIWeb” was specifically referenced in 602 and 691 articles for the years 2024 and 2025, respectively. There is clearly an upward trend in acknowledgments, however, these figures are still likely an underrepresentation of SPDF and OMNI’s impact. Similarly, OMNI’s sole published article, King & Papitashvili (2005) [https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1029/2004JA010649], has only been cited 1,114 times. Given its large number of acknowledgements, this implies that the OMNI paper is not cited as often as it should be. Please see the recommendation for citing the OMNI datasets [https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/html/citing.html]."
            },
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "Planet",
              "schema:text": "The OMNI Database plays a crucial role in investigating, analyzing, modeling, and validating many different space weather phenomena. As with its impact on people discussed above, this data has both direct and indirect contributions to these efforts. For example, empirical models normally perform best with a wide coverage of data. Datasets like OMNI lend themselves perfectly to this type of modeling because their data spans multiple decades and contains multiple solar cycles. Likewise, predictive models not only require historical datasets but also key metrics that are used to drive their forecasting capabilities. Validation of these models demands a precise & accurate history of these key metrics. Both empirical & predictive modeling are ideal applications for parameters such as the solar wind magnetic field & plasma data, energetic particle fluxes, and geomagnetic & solar activity indices contained in OMNI. \n\nDuring investigation or analysis, OMNI data is often used to correlate phenomena with increased activity levels, whether it be solar or geomagnetic. For example, a researcher may be interested in a particular phenomenon that is known to appear during intense geomagnetic storms. A typical starting point is to define criteria that identifies these types of storms. Then, researchers query databases such as OMNI for events that meet the criteria. The result is the identification of timeframes for further analysis. This example demonstrates how OMNI is used to support these investigations. Similarly, the inverse is true when analyzing results, where any newly detected phenomena is correlated back to [or potentially explained by] key parameters found in datasets like OMNI."
            },
            {
              "@type": "agu:ImpactDimension",
              "prefLabel": "Prosperity",
              "schema:text": "A current paradigm in space weather research & operations (R2O2R) is the development of machine learning (ML) models, or artificial intelligence (AI), specifically with the capability to forecast. Like predictive modeling, ML models require a lot of data, but that data also needs to be high quality to obtain good results. Datasets like OMNI, that are highly curated to the highest of scientific standards, are both powerful and practical when applied in these settings. Many machine learning models are currently being run operationally at NASA’s Community Coordinated Modeling Center (CCMC). For example. the Space Weather Modeling Framework (SWMF [https://ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/models/SWMF~2023/]) integrates & couples various space weather models together into a single, coherent system. SWMF is driven by solar wind plasma and magnetic field data, and CCMC has even developed a get_omni routine [https://ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/ror/requests/GetInput/get_omni.php] that pulls high-resolution OMNI data directly from OMNIWeb. Models such as SMWF at CCMC are primary used for space physics or space weather research but have also been applied to other areas such as alerts/warning of spacecraft charging, solar energetic particle (SEP), or satellite conjunction events. Another key consideration is that OMNI data is not only used to train & drive these ML-based predictive models, but it has also been employed to inform their early development stages & later validation processes."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "interactionStatement": [
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "1",
          "schema:text": "The nominator has been using OMNI data for solar wind model validation at 1 AU and also to drive outer heliosphere models starting at 1 AU on an everyday basis since they were a graduate student. Thanks to OMNI, they can simply focus on modeling without having to assemble the dataset themselves."
        },
        {
          "@type": "agu:Statement",
          "sequence": "2",
          "schema:text": "OMNI was utilized in two different capacities of the nominator’s research. The first was in the development of a novel discrete sampling methodology that was used to quell the unbalanced dataset problem often faced in space weather research. This is where datasets containing rare events are significantly outnumbered by the normal, or “quiescent”, conditions. This methodology leverages techniques from Latin hypercube sampling and 21 years of OMNI data to identify random events that sufficiently capture the range of solar activity and ring current responses. The result was a list of 30 weekly intervals, showcasing yet another example where OMNI played a pivotal role in the early development of a long-term project that often gets overlooked. \n\nThe nominator’s research utilized long short-term memory (LSTM) neural networks for the dynamical modeling of a reduced-order probabilistic emulator (ROPE) of RAM-SCB particle flux. LSTMs require a unique input structure that contains key parameters used to drive its predictive capabilities. They chose to train an ensemble of LSTMs using the same OMNI data that powered the discrete sampling methodology. Basically, their ROPE cannot make any predictions without the OMNI data. It is exactly this type of dependency that makes the preservation of datasets like OMNI paramount to the future of the Space Science and Heliophysics communities."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "id:sourcerow:133",
      "@type": "agu:SourceRow",
      "agu:rowNumber": 134,
      "sourceValue": [
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Discipline Group",
          "schema:value": "Space and Planetary Science"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Name",
          "schema:value": "OMNI"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset Authors or Creators",
          "schema:value": "Multiple sources; Joseph H. King & Natalia E. Papitashvili"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Dataset link or location (Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL of the landing page for the dataset)",
          "schema:value": "https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Name where Dataset is hosted",
          "schema:value": "OMNIWeb; Space Physics Data Facility"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Persistent Identifier (RRID, DOI, other)",
          "schema:value": "Multiple (see Short Description submission)"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Repository Data managers or curators that support the dataset. ",
          "schema:value": "Natalia E. Papitashvili & SPDF"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description (1500 characters) of the dataset being nominated. ",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nAll the data to which OMNIWeb and its multiple underlying interfaces provide access have in common that they are relevant to heliospheric studies and/or provide solar wind input data for studies of solar wind - magnetosphere coupling, and that they are resident at GSFC/Space Physics Data Facility.\nMost important among of them are Low and High res. OMNI:\n(a) Low res OMNI (LRO) is Hourly \"Near-Earth\" solar wind magnetic field and plasma data, energetic proton fluxes (>1 to >60 MeV), and geomagnetic and solar activity indices. There are Daily, 27-day, and Yearly resolution derived from hourly data also. All details about that data descriptions and about the data access user may find at https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/ow.html.\n(b) High 1-min and 5-min resolution OMNI(HRO) : Solar wind magnetic field and plasma data at Earth's Bow Shock Nose, and geomagnetic activity indices. 5-min resolution derived from 1-min data with added 5-min energetic proton fluxes. All details about high res. OMNi data descriptions and about data access user may find at https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/ow_min.html.\nIn addition, some data sets are as reformatted or have parameters transformed to a different coordinate system to facilitate multi-point heliospheric studies (cf.COHOWeb at https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/coho/).\nMost data sets are from specific instruments carried on specific spacecraft and are as provided by spacecraft instrument teams.\n\nNominator 2: \nThe OMNI Database is a collection of highly curated datasets that have been serving the Heliophysics community for nearly 50 years. It is one of the many products offered by the Space Physics Data Facility (SPDF) at NASA’s Goddard Space Flight Center (GSFC). OMNI provides magnetic field, plasma, energetic particle, and solar & geomagnetic index data in various resolutions and coordinate systems. However, “OMNI” is not an acronym and simply meant to construe a “variety.” The data in OMNI is collected & aggregated from multiple sources and updated/maintained to provide the highest quality possible.\n \nOMNI is rooted in the Catalogs of Particles & Fields Data and Interplanetary Medium Data Books that were disseminated by NASA’s National Space Science Data Center (NSSDC) in the mid-1970s. Around 1989, the NSSDC started digitizing their datasets, and by the mid-1990s, the OMNI datasets were available in a variety of formats – paper supplement, magnetic tape (binary & ASCII), CD-ROM, and even online (NODIS & WWW). In 1992, the SPDF was founded and began transferring many of the space physics data archiving responsibilities from the NSSDC. Around 2005, a web-based interface for accessing OMNI data – OMNIWeb – was developed. It was further upgraded in 2008 to its current configuration – OMNIWeb Plus (OMNIWeb+) – which integrated various other interfaces such as COHOWeb, FTPBrowser, CDAWeb, and HelioWeb.\n\nThe most popular datasets are as follows:\n\n Low-Resolution OMNI (LRO)\n • LRO is based on hourly-averaged, “near-Earth” solar wind magnetic field & plasma data, energetic particle fluxes, and geomagnetic & solar activity indices. Any data from spacecraft at L1 have been time shifted to their expected magnetospheric arrival times, prior to taking the hourly averages. The hourly data has also been further averaged into daily, 27-day, and yearly resolutions. The time range for the LRO dataset dates all the way back from 1963 to the present, covering multiple solar cycles. \n • More detailed info: https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/html/ow_data.html \n • DOI(s):\n o Hourly LRO: https://doi.org/10.48322/1shr-ht18 \n o Daily LRO: https://doi.org/10.48322/5fmx-hv56 \n o 27-Day LRO: https://doi.org/10.48322/nmh3-jf75 \n\n High-Resolution OMNI (HRO)\n • HRO is based on 1-min solar wind magnetic field & plasma data and geomagnetic indices that have been time shifted to Earth's Bow Shock nose. This 1-min data has also been used to derive a 5-min resolution version that includes 5-min energetic particle fluxes. Each HRO dataset is further divided into non-definitive and definitive variations, with the latter sacrificing a slight decrease in coverage & latency for improved accuracy. The time range for these HRO datasets begins around 1981 and continues to the present, covering a few solar cycles. \n • More detailed info: https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/html/HROdocum.html \n • DOI(s):\n o 1-min HRO: \n • https://doi.org/10.48322/45bb-8792 (non-definitive)\n • https://doi.org/10.48322/mj0k-fq60 (definitive)\n o 5-min HRO: \n • https://doi.org/10.48322/gbpg-5r77 (non-definitive)\n • https://doi.org/10.48322/hkaw-ff03 (definitive)\n\nThe OMNI datasets are accessible via its web-based interface – OMNIWeb+. Some of the more popular datasets have also been added to SPDF’s web-based Coordinated Data Analysis tool – CDAWeb – which can be accessed via HTTPS, FTPS, REST/SOAP web services, HAPI protocol, or third-party software (IDL, Python, SPEDAS/PySPEDAS, Autoplot, etc.)."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Justification for inclusion in AGU commentary. Why is the nominated dataset impactful “in your own words” or “in your opinion”.\nPeople: education, training, disaster response and prediction\nPlanet: geophysical phenomena, conservation, climate, environmental indicators\nProsperity: economic good, social equity, humanitarian relief, community resilience",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1: \nDating back to November 1963, the OMNI (not an acronym) dataset provides a comprehensive description of the near-Earth solar wind plasma and magnetic environment invaluable to the heliophysics and geospace communities. The spacecraft data contributing to the OMNI dataset include a number of former and current space missions, such as IMP-8, ACE, Wind, ISEE-3, and Geotail, in geocentric orbit and at the Earth's first Lagrange (L1) point. Thorough analysis is performed in compiling the extensive dataset that is easily accessible via the NASA GSFC/SPDF OMNIWeb interface. OMNI data preserves a history of the Sun-Earth relationship for more than five solar cycles and serves as the \"ground truth\" for solar wind and energetic particle models, enabling more reliable predictions of hazardous conditions for future human exploration in deep space. OMNI data also serves as the primary input for geospace models for space weather predictions that play an important role in protecting commercial and military assets both on the ground and in space. Furthermore, OMNI data is crucial in understanding the solar variability and the Sun's influence on Earth and throughout the solar system, as well as the Sun's interaction with the interstellar medium.\n\nNominator 2:\nThe OMNI Database is an “unsung hero” that has been supporting the Heliophysics and Space Science communities for nearly 50 years. Its datasets appear across a breadth of areas including education, research, analysis, and operations. It impacts society both directly and indirectly, with the latter often get overlooked. Direct impacts are easier to quantify, as publications, citations, and references are tracked and counted. However, indirect impacts are trickier to identify. For example, parameters from a dataset used early on in an analysis or project – where that specific data is not directly presented in any of the results – typically go unacknowledged. I believe such is likely the case with OMNI data, which is the primary motivation behind this nomination. \n\nPeople Dimension:\n\nOMNI data directly impacts individuals in a multitude of ways. Notably, it is used to educate and train the next generations of space scientists and heliophysicists. In my experience, OMNI data is often required to not only answer homework questions but also complete semester projects in core science programs such as astrophysics, planetary science, space physics, and aerospace engineering – at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. OMNI data is also used in specialized programs such as the NASA Heliophysics Summer School, which exposes graduate students and early-career scientists to an array of tools & resources available for space weather analysis. I also expect that there are advanced high/secondary school courses that use or reference OMNI data in their curricula. I would also venture to say that every graduate student in space science has almost certainly accessed or used data from the OMNI Database at some point in their academic careers. \n\nOMNI also abides by NASA’s FAIR principles – findable, accessible, interoperable, and reusable. Its data is often linked with instructional exercises in finding, accessing, and downloading data from NASA’s systems. More advanced usage typically involves some level of programming, as data retrieval from interfaces like OMNIWeb regularly require recursive processing. These are fundamental skills that space science researchers tend to develop early in their studies. The OMNI data can also be downloaded in many different formats and coordinate systems, heavily leaning into the interoperability principles. Lastly, once a user has the OMNI data, it can be easily reused for many different analyses, as it can be quickly appended or updated whenever necessary. \n\nSPDF tracks access to OMNIWeb+, downloads of OMNI data on CDAWeb (FTPS, HTTPS, and custom files), and references to OMNI/OMNIWeb in publications. Unfortunately, these statistics fail to capture the full extent of OMNI’s impacts, as visualization & analysis and the number of data files used to create custom downloads are not easily traceable. However, SPDF’s statistics clearly demonstrates that OMNI is one of their most popular datasets, and OMNI’s monthly usage has been exponentially increasing over the past few decades (see SPDF Usage [https://spdf.gsfc.nasa.gov/spdf-secaa_usage.html] for more info). \n\nThe two most common methods of accessing OMNI data are through OMNIWeb+ or CDAWeb. OMNIWeb+ has had roughly 800k and 1.1M users in 2024 and 2025, respectively. Of all these users, 90-95% of them accessed at least one of the OMNI datasets (see OMNIWeb Stats [https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/html/stat.html] for more info). In terms of monthly usage, the average users of OMNI data on OMNIWeb+ came out to approximately 64k and 80k for 2024 and 2025, respectively. In terms of CDAWeb, the total number of OMNI data downloads for 2024 and 2025 are 1.49M and 3.40M files, respectively. Over half of these downloads were via HTTPS, while the creation of custom files via the CDAWeb system accounted for roughly 42% (see the Use of SPDF Services [https://spdf.gsfc.nasa.gov/spdf-secaa_usage.html] for more info). As previously mentioned, CDAWeb only tracks downloads from its system, so the actual usage is likely higher, especially with the current shift towards cloud computing. \n\nSPDF conducts a yearly review of articles that mention OMNI in major scientific journals. In recent years, the Heliophysics community acknowledged SPDF in roughly half of articles published in the Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics and Space Weather (see SPDF Acknowledgements [https://spdf.gsfc.nasa.gov/Acknowledgements.html] for more info). In terms of OMNI, either “OMNI” or “OMNIWeb” was specifically referenced in 602 and 691 articles for the years 2024 and 2025, respectively. There is clearly an upward trend in acknowledgments, however, these figures are still likely an underrepresentation of SPDF and OMNI’s impact. Similarly, OMNI’s sole published article, King & Papitashvili (2005) [https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1029/2004JA010649], has only been cited 1,114 times. Given its large number of acknowledgements, this implies that the OMNI paper is not cited as often as it should be. Please see the recommendation for citing the OMNI datasets [https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/html/citing.html]. \n\nPlanet Dimension:\n\nThe OMNI Database plays a crucial role in investigating, analyzing, modeling, and validating many different space weather phenomena. As with its impact on people discussed above, this data has both direct and indirect contributions to these efforts. For example, empirical models normally perform best with a wide coverage of data. Datasets like OMNI lend themselves perfectly to this type of modeling because their data spans multiple decades and contains multiple solar cycles. Likewise, predictive models not only require historical datasets but also key metrics that are used to drive their forecasting capabilities. Validation of these models demands a precise & accurate history of these key metrics. Both empirical & predictive modeling are ideal applications for parameters such as the solar wind magnetic field & plasma data, energetic particle fluxes, and geomagnetic & solar activity indices contained in OMNI. \n\nDuring investigation or analysis, OMNI data is often used to correlate phenomena with increased activity levels, whether it be solar or geomagnetic. For example, a researcher may be interested in a particular phenomenon that is known to appear during intense geomagnetic storms. A typical starting point is to define criteria that identifies these types of storms. Then, researchers query databases such as OMNI for events that meet the criteria. The result is the identification of timeframes for further analysis. This example demonstrates how OMNI is used to support these investigations. Similarly, the inverse is true when analyzing results, where any newly detected phenomena is correlated back to [or potentially explained by] key parameters found in datasets like OMNI. \nProsperity Dimension:\n\nA current paradigm in space weather research & operations (R2O2R) is the development of machine learning (ML) models, or artificial intelligence (AI), specifically with the capability to forecast. Like predictive modeling, ML models require a lot of data, but that data also needs to be high quality to obtain good results. Datasets like OMNI, that are highly curated to the highest of scientific standards, are both powerful and practical when applied in these settings. Many machine learning models are currently being run operationally at NASA’s Community Coordinated Modeling Center (CCMC). For example. the Space Weather Modeling Framework (SWMF [https://ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/models/SWMF~2023/]) integrates & couples various space weather models together into a single, coherent system. SWMF is driven by solar wind plasma and magnetic field data, and CCMC has even developed a get_omni routine [https://ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/ror/requests/GetInput/get_omni.php] that pulls high-resolution OMNI data directly from OMNIWeb. Models such as SMWF at CCMC are primary used for space physics or space weather research but have also been applied to other areas such as alerts/warning of spacecraft charging, solar energetic particle (SEP), or satellite conjunction events. Another key consideration is that OMNI data is not only used to train & drive these ML-based predictive models, but it has also been employed to inform their early development stages & later validation processes."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Short description of how you interact with this dataset.  ",
          "schema:value": "Nominator 1:\nThe nominator has been using OMNI data for solar wind model validation at 1 AU and also to drive outer heliosphere models starting at 1 AU on an everyday basis since they were a graduate student. Thanks to OMNI, they can simply focus on modeling without having to assemble the dataset themselves.\n\nNominator 2:\nOMNI was utilized in two different capacities of the nominator’s research. The first was in the development of a novel discrete sampling methodology that was used to quell the unbalanced dataset problem often faced in space weather research. This is where datasets containing rare events are significantly outnumbered by the normal, or “quiescent”, conditions. This methodology leverages techniques from Latin hypercube sampling and 21 years of OMNI data to identify random events that sufficiently capture the range of solar activity and ring current responses. The result was a list of 30 weekly intervals, showcasing yet another example where OMNI played a pivotal role in the early development of a long-term project that often gets overlooked. \n\nThe nominator’s research utilized long short-term memory (LSTM) neural networks for the dynamical modeling of a reduced-order probabilistic emulator (ROPE) of RAM-SCB particle flux. LSTMs require a unique input structure that contains key parameters used to drive its predictive capabilities. They chose to train an ensemble of LSTMs using the same OMNI data that powered the discrete sampling methodology. Basically, their ROPE cannot make any predictions without the OMNI data. It is exactly this type of dependency that makes the preservation of datasets like OMNI paramount to the future of the Space Science and Heliophysics communities."
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Reference publication that describes the dataset.",
          "schema:value": "King, J. H., & Papitashvili, N. E. (2005). Solar wind spatial scales in and comparisons of hourly wind and ace plasma and magnetic field data. Journal of Geophysical Research, 110(A2). https://doi.org/10.1029/2004JA010649 \n\nKing, J., & Papitashvili, N. (2006). One minute and five minute solar wind data sets at the earth’s bow shock nose. NASA Goddard Space Flight Center/Space Physics Data Facility. https://omniweb.gsfc.nasa.gov/html/HROdocum.html \n\nCruz, A. A., Siddalingappa, R., Mehta, P. M., Morley, S. K., Godinez, H. C., & Jordanova, V. K. (2024). Reduced‐order probabilistic emulation of physics‐based ring current models: Application to ram‐scb particle flux. Space Weather, 22(6), e2023SW003706. https://doi.org/10.1029/2023SW003706"
        },
        {
          "sourceColumn": "Examples of how this dataset has been reused, any bibliographies listing instances of reuse, or any references to derived data products/datasets. ",
          "schema:value": "See yearly Acknowledgements/Bibliography links in https://spdf.gsfc.nasa.gov/Acknowledgements.html"
        },
        {
